raph --><p>Our latest job openings are on our Instagram.<br>Follow our posts to find new work opportunities in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->','Jobs','','inherit','closed','closed','','22200-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 10:37:58','2026-09-25 09:37:58','',22200,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22217',0,'revision','',0),(22218,283039558,'2026-08-25 16:24:34','2026-08-25 15:24:34','<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At Conexão BR Irlanda, we share new job opportunities every day — and finding openings near you just got even easier! 📍</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">👉 In our Instagram Highlights, the openings are grouped by County, making it easier to search the region where you live or plan to work.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">📌 Update your résumé<br>📌 Check the Highlights by County<br>📌 Follow the new openings daily<br>📌 Browse our free Guides to find out where else to look for work in Ireland</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">🌐 More information on our Instagram — <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dcd0YXqMrEK/?igsi=M3VrZm04aHV4czN6\">click here</a></p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">💚 Save this post and share it with a friend who is also looking for an opportunity in Ireland.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">#JobsInIreland #JobOpeningsIreland #BraziliansInIreland #WorkInIreland #ConexaoBRIrlanda #JobVacancies #IrelandJobs #BraziliansInEurope</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>','Opportunities','At Conexão BR Irlanda, we share new job opportunities every day — and finding openings near you just got even easier! In our Instagram Highlights, the openings are grouped by County…','publish','closed','closed','','opportunities','','','2026-08-25 16:24:34','2026-08-25 15:24:34','',0,'http://localhost:8080/empregos/opportunities/',0,'job','',0),(22311,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:47','2026-08-15 09:03:47','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the PPS Number?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>PPS Number (Personal Public Service Number)</strong> is the most important personal identification number in Ireland. It is a unique number with 7 digits followed by 1 or 2 letters (example: 1234567A). It is used to access public services, work, pay tax, receive social welfare payments and access the health system.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-a-personal-public-service-pps-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information about the PPS Number</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs a PPS Number?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You need a PPS Number if you are going to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Work in Ireland (employed or self-employed)</li>\n<li>Pay tax (Income Tax, USC, PRSI)</li>\n<li>Access health services (Medical Card, GP Visit Card)</li>\n<li>Apply for social welfare payments (Child Benefit, Jobseeker\'s, etc.)</li>\n<li>Open a bank account (some banks require it)</li>\n<li>Buy or rent a property (in some cases)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for the PPS Number on MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To apply for the PPS Number, you need:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong>: valid passport (Brazilian or another country)</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address in Ireland</strong>: an electricity, water or internet bill, rental contract or a letter from a public body</li>\n<li><strong>Proof that you need the PPS Number</strong>: a job offer letter, a letter from the Department of Social Protection, or another document showing why you need it</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The process is run by the <strong>Department of Social Protection (DSP)</strong> through MyWelfare. For people over 18, the official service allows you to apply for the PPS Number online; if you cannot use the online system, there is the REG1 form.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Make the application through <strong>MyWelfare</strong> and follow the Department of Social Protection instructions</li>\n<li>Provide proof of identity, address and the reason you need the PPSN</li>\n<li>If you cannot use the online service, use the <strong>REG1</strong> form</li>\n<li>The DSP will inform you of the outcome and next steps</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The PPS Number is <strong>free</strong>. There is no fee to apply.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How long does it take?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Processing can take from <strong>a few days to a few weeks</strong>, depending on application volumes and whether you need an interview. The DSP does not publish a guaranteed fixed turnaround time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a PPS Number:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-a-personal-public-service-pps-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get a PPS Number</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The PPS Number is <strong>personal and non-transferable</strong>. Never share it with third parties.</li>\n<li>You can only apply if <strong>you need</strong> the number for a specific service. You cannot apply “just in case”.</li>\n<li>If you have had a PPS Number before, use the same number.</li>\n<li>Keep the card or letter with your PPS Number in a safe place.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I work without a PPS Number?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have started work and have not received your PPSN yet, talk to your employer and to Revenue about registering the job correctly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a visa to apply for a PPS Number?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You need to be legally in Ireland. The PPSN is allocated when there is a valid reason to have the number. The right to work depends on your immigration status.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the PPS Number expire?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The PPS Number is permanent and does not expire, even if you leave Ireland and come back later.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-a-personal-public-service-pps-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get a PPS Number</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/social-welfare/irish-social-welfare-system/personal-public-service-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - PPS Number</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare - social services portal</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to get a PPS Number in Ireland','What the PPS Number is, who needs it, which documents to prepare, how to apply, what it costs and how long it takes.','publish','closed','closed','','pps-number-ireland','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/pps-number-ireland/',0,'guide','',0),(22312,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:47','2026-08-15 09:03:47','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Medical Card?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Medical Card</strong> is a Health Service Executive (HSE) card that can give free access to certain health services. Eligibility normally involves a means test, and there are also specific situations in which a card can be granted on a discretionary basis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/medical-cards/applying/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for a Medical Card or GP Visit Card online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is entitled?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To qualify for the Medical Card, you must be <strong>ordinarily resident</strong> in Ireland (living here and intending to live here for at least one year) and pass the income test. The test looks at the family’s weekly <strong>net</strong> income (after tax, PRSI and USC).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Income limits and assessment rules</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Medical Card (up to 66)</th><th>Medical Card (66-70)</th><th>GP Visit Card</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Single person living alone</td><td>€184</td><td>€201.50</td><td>€418</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Single person living with family</td><td>€164</td><td>€173.50</td><td>€373</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Couple (or lone parent with children)</td><td>€266.50</td><td>€298</td><td>€607</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extra for a child under 16 (1st and 2nd)</td><td>€38</td><td>€38</td><td>€57</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extra for a child under 16 (3rd+)</td><td>€41</td><td>€41</td><td>€61.50</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What does the Medical Card cover?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Free</strong> GP (family doctor) appointments</li>\n<li>Prescribed medicines at a reduced charge (€2 per item, with a monthly cap of €80 per family)</li>\n<li><strong>Free</strong> public hospital care (including surgery and inpatient stays)</li>\n<li>Tests and treatment in public hospitals</li>\n<li>Maternity services</li>\n<li>Dental, eye and hearing services (in some cases)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Download the <strong>MC1</strong> form on the HSE website or request it by post</li>\n<li>Fill it in with your details and your family’s details</li>\n<li>Send the form with the supporting documents (identity, proof of residence, proof of income)</li>\n<li>The HSE reviews your application and sends the decision by post</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no fee to apply for the Medical Card. Check the income criteria and the applicable rules on the current HSE page.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a Medical Card:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/medical-cards/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE – Medical Cards</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The Medical Card is <strong>free</strong> for those who pass the income test</li>\n<li>If you do not pass the Medical Card test, you may qualify for the <strong>GP Visit Card</strong>, which covers free GP visits (but not medicines or hospital care)</li>\n<li>The card must be <strong>renewed</strong> periodically</li>\n<li>People with specific medical conditions may qualify even above the income limit (a <em>discretionary medical card</em>)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians get a Medical Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are ordinarily resident in Ireland (living here and intending to stay for at least 1 year) and pass the income test. A valid Employment Permit or other valid immigration status helps prove residence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the GP Visit Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The GP Visit Card covers free family-doctor (GP) visits, but not medicines, tests or hospital care. The income limits are higher than for the Medical Card.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You will need to provide the PPS Numbers of the people included in the application, plus dates of birth and income and expenses information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/medical-cards/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE – Medical Cards</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/medical-cards-and-gp-visit-cards/medical-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information – Medical Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/medical-cards-and-gp-visit-cards/medical-card-means-test-under-70s/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information – Medical Card Means Test</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Medical Card in Ireland: Complete Guide','What the Medical Card is, who qualifies, the income limits, what it covers and how to apply through the HSE.','publish','closed','closed','','medical-card-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/medical-card-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22313,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:47','2026-08-15 09:03:47','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is a GP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A <strong>GP (General Practitioner)</strong> is the family doctor in Ireland. It is the first point of contact for any health matter. For non-emergency problems the GP is usually the first place to go, but there are other services. In an emergency you can go straight to an Emergency Department or call 112/999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to register with a GP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no general rule saying every resident must register with one specific GP. Having access to a GP is, however, very useful for non-emergency care and ongoing health follow-up.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Routine medical appointments</li>\n<li>Prescriptions</li>\n<li>Referrals to specialists</li>\n<li>Medical certificates</li>\n<li>Vaccinations</li>\n<li>Screening tests</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to find a GP</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Use <strong>HSE Find a GP</strong> to search for practices in your area</li>\n<li>Ask friends, colleagues or neighbours</li>\n<li>Check whether the practice is <strong>taking new patients</strong> (some have a waiting list)</li>\n<li>Call the practice and ask about the registration process</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Choose a practice (GP practice) near you</li>\n<li>Call or visit the practice to check whether they are taking new patients</li>\n<li>Fill in the practice registration form</li>\n<li>Bring your <strong>PPS Number</strong> and identity document</li>\n<li>If you have a Medical Card or GP Visit Card, tell the practice</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The price of a private consultation varies by practice. Do not rely on a single figure as the rule: confirm directly with the GP. If you have a Medical Card or GP Visit Card and the visit is covered by the card, the scheme conditions may allow a free appointment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to find one</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Find a GP:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE &#8211; Find a GP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find a GP through the official HSE finder</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>You need to find a practice taking new patients; availability, registration criteria and distance vary</li>\n<li>If you have a Medical Card, the GP is <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>In an emergency, call <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong></li>\n<li>Outside opening hours, many practices have an out-of-hours service</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to register with a GP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, a PPS Number is required to register with a GP in Ireland, especially if you want to use the Medical Card or GP Visit Card.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I go straight to hospital without a GP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In an emergency, yes &#8211; call 112/999 or go to the Emergency Department. For non-urgent matters you should see a GP first.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE &#8211; Find a GP</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/health-services/gp-and-hospital-services/gp-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information &#8211; GP Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/medical-cards/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE &#8211; Medical Cards</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to register with a GP (family doctor) in Ireland','How to find a GP, how to register, what it costs and what to do out of hours or in an emergency.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-register-with-a-gp-in-ireland','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-register-with-a-gp-in-ireland/',0,'guide','',0),(22314,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:47','2026-08-15 09:03:47','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is an Irish bank account?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Having an Irish bank account is essential to receive salary, pay bills, receive social welfare payments and manage your finances. Most employers pay salary by bank transfer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs a bank account?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A bank account is useful for many people, but it is not a general legal requirement for all residents. Whether you need one depends on your situation and on the services you use.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Receiving salary</li>\n<li>Paying bills (rent, electricity, internet, etc.)</li>\n<li>Receiving social welfare payments (Child Benefit, etc.)</li>\n<li>Receiving tax refunds from Revenue</li>\n<li>Making online purchases and payments</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to open an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong>: valid passport</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address in Ireland</strong>: an electricity, water or internet bill, rental contract, or a letter from a public body</li>\n<li><strong>PPS Number</strong>: some banks ask for it, but the required documents vary by institution</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of income</strong> (in some cases): employment contract, recent payslips</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to open an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Choose a bank (AIB, Bank of Ireland, Permanent TSB, etc.)</li>\n<li>Book a branch appointment or start the process online</li>\n<li>Bring your documents (identity, proof of address, PPS Number)</li>\n<li>Complete the account opening form</li>\n<li>The bank reviews your application and activates the account</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/consumers/money/banking/current-accounts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Compare and understand bank accounts - CCPC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fees and charges vary between accounts and institutions. Do not treat a fixed €4&#8211;€6 range as a current rule: check the bank&#8217;s charges and compare the options before opening the account.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to open one</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The main banks in Ireland include: <strong>AIB</strong>, <strong>Bank of Ireland</strong>, <strong>Permanent TSB</strong>, <strong>Revolut</strong> (digital bank). The <strong>Central Bank of Ireland</strong> regulates banks in the country.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Some banks may require you to already have a job or income in Ireland</li>\n<li>The Irish <strong>IBAN</strong> starts with &#8220;IE&#8221;</li>\n<li>You may need a PPS Number to open an account &#8211; if you do not have one yet, some banks allow you to open with a passport and proof of address</li>\n<li>Digital banks such as Revolut and N26 can be easier for new arrivals</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to open an account?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Most banks require a PPS Number, but some may allow you to open an account with a passport and proof of address. Check with the bank you choose.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I open an account without a job?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, it is possible, but some banks may ask for proof of income or require an opening deposit.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/consumer/financial-services/bank-accounts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information &#8211; Bank Accounts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/consumers/money/bank-accounts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CCPC &#8211; Bank Accounts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank of Ireland</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Opening a Bank Account in Ireland','Why you need an Irish bank account, which documents to bring, how to apply and what it costs.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-open-a-bank-account-in-ireland','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-open-a-bank-account-in-ireland/',0,'guide','',0),(22315,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:47','2026-08-15 09:03:47','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish rental market?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish property market is <strong>competitive</strong>, especially in Dublin and other large cities. Demand is high and prices are high. It is important to understand the rules and your rights as a tenant.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to rent?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Almost everyone who has just arrived in Ireland starts by renting. The property purchase market is expensive and requires a credit history.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to rent</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong>: valid passport</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of income</strong>: employment contract, recent payslips (usually 3 months)</li>\n<li><strong>References</strong>: from an employer and/or previous landlords</li>\n<li><strong>PPS Number</strong>: in some cases</li>\n<li><strong>Deposit</strong>: cannot exceed 1 month&#8217;s rent; as a rule the landlord also cannot charge more than 1 month of rent in advance</li>\n<li><strong>First month&#8217;s rent in advance</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to look</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>daft.ie</strong> &#8211; Ireland&#8217;s largest property portal</li>\n<li><strong>Rent.ie</strong> &#8211; rental portal</li>\n<li><strong>MyHome.ie</strong> &#8211; property portal</li>\n<li>Local letting agents</li>\n<li>Brazilian community Facebook groups in Ireland (with caution)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to rent</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Search the property portals and agents</li>\n<li>Contact them quickly &#8211; good properties are rented within days</li>\n<li>Book a viewing</li>\n<li>Prepare your documents in advance</li>\n<li>If approved, sign the <strong>tenancy agreement</strong></li>\n<li>Pay the deposit and the first month</li>\n<li>The landlord must register the tenancy with the <strong>RTB (Residential Tenancies Board)</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Prices vary widely by location. Rents change quickly and differ greatly by city, property and date. Avoid treating old ranges as current averages: look at recent listings and the RTB information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Your rights as a tenant</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The <strong>RTB (Residential Tenancies Board)</strong> is the body that regulates renting in Ireland</li>\n<li>The deposit cannot be more than <strong>1 month&#8217;s rent</strong>; the rule about rent paid in advance is separate</li>\n<li>The landlord must provide a <strong>receipt</strong> for the deposit</li>\n<li>The tenancy must be <strong>registered with the RTB</strong> within 1 month</li>\n<li>The landlord must give <strong>notice</strong> to end the tenancy (between 90 and 152 days depending on how long you have lived there)</li>\n<li>Since <strong>1 March 2026</strong> there is a national rent control scheme for private tenancies and Student Specific Accommodation, with specific rules and exceptions</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Beware of scams &#8211; never pay a deposit before viewing the property</li>\n<li>Check that the property has an <strong>Eircode</strong> (postcode)</li>\n<li>Read the contract carefully before signing</li>\n<li>Keep all payment receipts</li>\n<li>If you have problems with the landlord, contact the <strong>RTB</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to rent?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not always, but many landlords ask for it. Having a PPS Number makes the process easier.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the RTB?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The RTB (Residential Tenancies Board) is the official body that regulates residential renting in Ireland. It registers tenancies, resolves disputes and provides information about tenant and landlord rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much deposit can I pay?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The landlord cannot ask for a deposit above <strong>1 month&#8217;s rent</strong> and, as a rule, cannot ask for more than 1 month of rent in advance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://rtb.ie/renting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check renting rules and rights - RTB</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rtb.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RTB &#8211; Residential Tenancies Board</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/housing/renting-a-home/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information &#8211; Renting a Home</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-housing-local-government-and-heritage/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Housing</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Renting a House in Ireland: Complete Guide','Where to look for a rental, what documents you need, what it costs and your rights as a tenant in Ireland.','publish','closed','closed','','renting-a-house-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/renting-a-house-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22316,0,'2026-09-25 14:20:53','2026-09-25 13:20:53','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the PPS Number?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>PPS Number (Personal Public Service Number)</strong> is the most important personal identification number in Ireland. It is a unique number with 7 digits followed by 1 or 2 letters (example: 1234567A). It is used to access public services, work, pay tax, receive social welfare payments and access the health system.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-a-personal-public-service-pps-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information about the PPS Number</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs a PPS Number?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You need a PPS Number if you are going to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Work in Ireland (employed or self-employed)</li>\n<li>Pay tax (Income Tax, USC, PRSI)</li>\n<li>Access health services (Medical Card, GP Visit Card)</li>\n<li>Apply for social welfare payments (Child Benefit, Jobseeker\'s, etc.)</li>\n<li>Open a bank account (some banks require it)</li>\n<li>Buy or rent a property (in some cases)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for the PPS Number on MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To apply for the PPS Number, you need:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong>: valid passport (Brazilian or another country)</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address in Ireland</strong>: an electricity, water or internet bill, rental contract or a letter from a public body</li>\n<li><strong>Proof that you need the PPS Number</strong>: a job offer letter, a letter from the Department of Social Protection, or another document showing why you need it</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The process is run by the <strong>Department of Social Protection (DSP)</strong> through MyWelfare. For people over 18, the official service allows you to apply for the PPS Number online; if you cannot use the online system, there is the REG1 form.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Make the application through <strong>MyWelfare</strong> and follow the Department of Social Protection instructions</li>\n<li>Provide proof of identity, address and the reason you need the PPSN</li>\n<li>If you cannot use the online service, use the <strong>REG1</strong> form</li>\n<li>The DSP will inform you of the outcome and next steps</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The PPS Number is <strong>free</strong>. There is no fee to apply.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How long does it take?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Processing can take from <strong>a few days to a few weeks</strong>, depending on application volumes and whether you need an interview. The DSP does not publish a guaranteed fixed turnaround time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a PPS Number:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-a-personal-public-service-pps-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get a PPS Number</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The PPS Number is <strong>personal and non-transferable</strong>. Never share it with third parties.</li>\n<li>You can only apply if <strong>you need</strong> the number for a specific service. You cannot apply “just in case”.</li>\n<li>If you have had a PPS Number before, use the same number.</li>\n<li>Keep the card or letter with your PPS Number in a safe place.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I work without a PPS Number?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have started work and have not received your PPSN yet, talk to your employer and to Revenue about registering the job correctly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a visa to apply for a PPS Number?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You need to be legally in Ireland. The PPSN is allocated when there is a valid reason to have the number. The right to work depends on your immigration status.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the PPS Number expire?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The PPS Number is permanent and does not expire, even if you leave Ireland and come back later.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-a-personal-public-service-pps-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get a PPS Number</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/social-welfare/irish-social-welfare-system/personal-public-service-number/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - PPS Number</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare - social services portal</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to get a PPS Number in Ireland','What the PPS Number is, who needs it, which documents to prepare, how to apply, what it costs and how long it takes.','inherit','closed','closed','','22311-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:20:53','2026-09-25 13:20:53','',22311,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22316',0,'revision','',0),(22317,0,'2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Medical Card?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Medical Card</strong> is a Health Service Executive (HSE) card that can give free access to certain health services. Eligibility normally involves a means test, and there are also specific situations in which a card can be granted on a discretionary basis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/medical-cards/applying/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for a Medical Card or GP Visit Card online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is entitled?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To qualify for the Medical Card, you must be <strong>ordinarily resident</strong> in Ireland (living here and intending to live here for at least one year) and pass the income test. The test looks at the family’s weekly <strong>net</strong> income (after tax, PRSI and USC).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Income limits and assessment rules</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Medical Card (up to 66)</th><th>Medical Card (66-70)</th><th>GP Visit Card</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Single person living alone</td><td>€184</td><td>€201.50</td><td>€418</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Single person living with family</td><td>€164</td><td>€173.50</td><td>€373</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Couple (or lone parent with children)</td><td>€266.50</td><td>€298</td><td>€607</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extra for a child under 16 (1st and 2nd)</td><td>€38</td><td>€38</td><td>€57</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extra for a child under 16 (3rd+)</td><td>€41</td><td>€41</td><td>€61.50</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What does the Medical Card cover?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Free</strong> GP (family doctor) appointments</li>\n<li>Prescribed medicines at a reduced charge (€2 per item, with a monthly cap of €80 per family)</li>\n<li><strong>Free</strong> public hospital care (including surgery and inpatient stays)</li>\n<li>Tests and treatment in public hospitals</li>\n<li>Maternity services</li>\n<li>Dental, eye and hearing services (in some cases)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Download the <strong>MC1</strong> form on the HSE website or request it by post</li>\n<li>Fill it in with your details and your family’s details</li>\n<li>Send the form with the supporting documents (identity, proof of residence, proof of income)</li>\n<li>The HSE reviews your application and sends the decision by post</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no fee to apply for the Medical Card. Check the income criteria and the applicable rules on the current HSE page.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a Medical Card:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/medical-cards/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE – Medical Cards</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The Medical Card is <strong>free</strong> for those who pass the income test</li>\n<li>If you do not pass the Medical Card test, you may qualify for the <strong>GP Visit Card</strong>, which covers free GP visits (but not medicines or hospital care)</li>\n<li>The card must be <strong>renewed</strong> periodically</li>\n<li>People with specific medical conditions may qualify even above the income limit (a <em>discretionary medical card</em>)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians get a Medical Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are ordinarily resident in Ireland (living here and intending to stay for at least 1 year) and pass the income test. A valid Employment Permit or other valid immigration status helps prove residence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the GP Visit Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The GP Visit Card covers free family-doctor (GP) visits, but not medicines, tests or hospital care. The income limits are higher than for the Medical Card.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You will need to provide the PPS Numbers of the people included in the application, plus dates of birth and income and expenses information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/medical-cards/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE – Medical Cards</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/medical-cards-and-gp-visit-cards/medical-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information – Medical Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/medical-cards-and-gp-visit-cards/medical-card-means-test-under-70s/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information – Medical Card Means Test</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Medical Card in Ireland: Complete Guide','What the Medical Card is, who qualifies, the income limits, what it covers and how to apply through the HSE.','inherit','closed','closed','','22312-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','',22312,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22317',0,'revision','',0),(22318,0,'2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is a GP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A <strong>GP (General Practitioner)</strong> is the family doctor in Ireland. It is the first point of contact for any health matter. For non-emergency problems the GP is usually the first place to go, but there are other services. In an emergency you can go straight to an Emergency Department or call 112/999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to register with a GP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no general rule saying every resident must register with one specific GP. Having access to a GP is, however, very useful for non-emergency care and ongoing health follow-up.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Routine medical appointments</li>\n<li>Prescriptions</li>\n<li>Referrals to specialists</li>\n<li>Medical certificates</li>\n<li>Vaccinations</li>\n<li>Screening tests</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to find a GP</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Use <strong>HSE Find a GP</strong> to search for practices in your area</li>\n<li>Ask friends, colleagues or neighbours</li>\n<li>Check whether the practice is <strong>taking new patients</strong> (some have a waiting list)</li>\n<li>Call the practice and ask about the registration process</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Choose a practice (GP practice) near you</li>\n<li>Call or visit the practice to check whether they are taking new patients</li>\n<li>Fill in the practice registration form</li>\n<li>Bring your <strong>PPS Number</strong> and identity document</li>\n<li>If you have a Medical Card or GP Visit Card, tell the practice</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The price of a private consultation varies by practice. Do not rely on a single figure as the rule: confirm directly with the GP. If you have a Medical Card or GP Visit Card and the visit is covered by the card, the scheme conditions may allow a free appointment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to find one</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Find a GP:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE &#8211; Find a GP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find a GP through the official HSE finder</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>You need to find a practice taking new patients; availability, registration criteria and distance vary</li>\n<li>If you have a Medical Card, the GP is <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>In an emergency, call <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong></li>\n<li>Outside opening hours, many practices have an out-of-hours service</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to register with a GP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, a PPS Number is required to register with a GP in Ireland, especially if you want to use the Medical Card or GP Visit Card.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I go straight to hospital without a GP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In an emergency, yes &#8211; call 112/999 or go to the Emergency Department. For non-urgent matters you should see a GP first.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE &#8211; Find a GP</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/health-services/gp-and-hospital-services/gp-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information &#8211; GP Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/medical-cards/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE &#8211; Medical Cards</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to register with a GP (family doctor) in Ireland','How to find a GP, how to register, what it costs and what to do out of hours or in an emergency.','inherit','closed','closed','','22313-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','',22313,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22318',0,'revision','',0),(22319,0,'2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is an Irish bank account?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Having an Irish bank account is essential to receive salary, pay bills, receive social welfare payments and manage your finances. Most employers pay salary by bank transfer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs a bank account?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A bank account is useful for many people, but it is not a general legal requirement for all residents. Whether you need one depends on your situation and on the services you use.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Receiving salary</li>\n<li>Paying bills (rent, electricity, internet, etc.)</li>\n<li>Receiving social welfare payments (Child Benefit, etc.)</li>\n<li>Receiving tax refunds from Revenue</li>\n<li>Making online purchases and payments</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to open an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong>: valid passport</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address in Ireland</strong>: an electricity, water or internet bill, rental contract, or a letter from a public body</li>\n<li><strong>PPS Number</strong>: some banks ask for it, but the required documents vary by institution</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of income</strong> (in some cases): employment contract, recent payslips</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to open an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Choose a bank (AIB, Bank of Ireland, Permanent TSB, etc.)</li>\n<li>Book a branch appointment or start the process online</li>\n<li>Bring your documents (identity, proof of address, PPS Number)</li>\n<li>Complete the account opening form</li>\n<li>The bank reviews your application and activates the account</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/consumers/money/banking/current-accounts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Compare and understand bank accounts - CCPC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fees and charges vary between accounts and institutions. Do not treat a fixed €4&#8211;€6 range as a current rule: check the bank&#8217;s charges and compare the options before opening the account.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to open one</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The main banks in Ireland include: <strong>AIB</strong>, <strong>Bank of Ireland</strong>, <strong>Permanent TSB</strong>, <strong>Revolut</strong> (digital bank). The <strong>Central Bank of Ireland</strong> regulates banks in the country.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Some banks may require you to already have a job or income in Ireland</li>\n<li>The Irish <strong>IBAN</strong> starts with &#8220;IE&#8221;</li>\n<li>You may need a PPS Number to open an account &#8211; if you do not have one yet, some banks allow you to open with a passport and proof of address</li>\n<li>Digital banks such as Revolut and N26 can be easier for new arrivals</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to open an account?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Most banks require a PPS Number, but some may allow you to open an account with a passport and proof of address. Check with the bank you choose.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I open an account without a job?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, it is possible, but some banks may ask for proof of income or require an opening deposit.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/consumer/financial-services/bank-accounts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information &#8211; Bank Accounts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/consumers/money/bank-accounts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CCPC &#8211; Bank Accounts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank of Ireland</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Opening a Bank Account in Ireland','Why you need an Irish bank account, which documents to bring, how to apply and what it costs.','inherit','closed','closed','','22314-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','',22314,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22319',0,'revision','',0),(22320,0,'2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish rental market?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish property market is <strong>competitive</strong>, especially in Dublin and other large cities. Demand is high and prices are high. It is important to understand the rules and your rights as a tenant.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to rent?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Almost everyone who has just arrived in Ireland starts by renting. The property purchase market is expensive and requires a credit history.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to rent</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong>: valid passport</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of income</strong>: employment contract, recent payslips (usually 3 months)</li>\n<li><strong>References</strong>: from an employer and/or previous landlords</li>\n<li><strong>PPS Number</strong>: in some cases</li>\n<li><strong>Deposit</strong>: cannot exceed 1 month&#8217;s rent; as a rule the landlord also cannot charge more than 1 month of rent in advance</li>\n<li><strong>First month&#8217;s rent in advance</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to look</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>daft.ie</strong> &#8211; Ireland&#8217;s largest property portal</li>\n<li><strong>Rent.ie</strong> &#8211; rental portal</li>\n<li><strong>MyHome.ie</strong> &#8211; property portal</li>\n<li>Local letting agents</li>\n<li>Brazilian community Facebook groups in Ireland (with caution)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to rent</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Search the property portals and agents</li>\n<li>Contact them quickly &#8211; good properties are rented within days</li>\n<li>Book a viewing</li>\n<li>Prepare your documents in advance</li>\n<li>If approved, sign the <strong>tenancy agreement</strong></li>\n<li>Pay the deposit and the first month</li>\n<li>The landlord must register the tenancy with the <strong>RTB (Residential Tenancies Board)</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Prices vary widely by location. Rents change quickly and differ greatly by city, property and date. Avoid treating old ranges as current averages: look at recent listings and the RTB information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Your rights as a tenant</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The <strong>RTB (Residential Tenancies Board)</strong> is the body that regulates renting in Ireland</li>\n<li>The deposit cannot be more than <strong>1 month&#8217;s rent</strong>; the rule about rent paid in advance is separate</li>\n<li>The landlord must provide a <strong>receipt</strong> for the deposit</li>\n<li>The tenancy must be <strong>registered with the RTB</strong> within 1 month</li>\n<li>The landlord must give <strong>notice</strong> to end the tenancy (between 90 and 152 days depending on how long you have lived there)</li>\n<li>Since <strong>1 March 2026</strong> there is a national rent control scheme for private tenancies and Student Specific Accommodation, with specific rules and exceptions</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Beware of scams &#8211; never pay a deposit before viewing the property</li>\n<li>Check that the property has an <strong>Eircode</strong> (postcode)</li>\n<li>Read the contract carefully before signing</li>\n<li>Keep all payment receipts</li>\n<li>If you have problems with the landlord, contact the <strong>RTB</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to rent?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not always, but many landlords ask for it. Having a PPS Number makes the process easier.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the RTB?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The RTB (Residential Tenancies Board) is the official body that regulates residential renting in Ireland. It registers tenancies, resolves disputes and provides information about tenant and landlord rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much deposit can I pay?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The landlord cannot ask for a deposit above <strong>1 month&#8217;s rent</strong> and, as a rule, cannot ask for more than 1 month of rent in advance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://rtb.ie/renting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check renting rules and rights - RTB</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rtb.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RTB &#8211; Residential Tenancies Board</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/housing/renting-a-home/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information &#8211; Renting a Home</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-housing-local-government-and-heritage/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Housing</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Renting a House in Ireland: Complete Guide','Where to look for a rental, what documents you need, what it costs and your rights as a tenant in Ireland.','inherit','closed','closed','','22315-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:20:54','2026-09-25 13:20:54','',22315,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22320',0,'revision','',0),(22321,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:47','2026-08-15 09:03:47','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What do you need to buy a car in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Buying a car in Ireland involves several steps: choosing the vehicle, checking its history, transferring ownership, paying the motor tax and getting insurance. This guide explains the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can buy a car?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Anyone aged 18 or over with a valid driving licence can buy a car in Ireland. You do not need to be an Irish citizen.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>A valid <strong>driving licence</strong> (Irish or foreign)</li>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Car insurance</strong> (compulsory in Ireland)</li>\n<li><strong>Motor Tax</strong> (the annual vehicle tax)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to buy</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Set your budget (including insurance, tax and maintenance)</li>\n<li>Search on sites such as <strong>DoneDeal</strong> and <strong>CarsIreland</strong>, or at dealerships</li>\n<li>Check the vehicle history (mileage, accidents, etc.)</li>\n<li>Take a test drive</li>\n<li>Check that the car has a valid <strong>NCT</strong> (roadworthiness test)</li>\n<li>Complete the change of ownership</li>\n<li>Pay the <strong>Motor Tax</strong> and take out <strong>insurance</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/vrt/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information on VRT - Revenue</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the NCT?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>NCT (National Car Test)</strong> is the compulsory roadworthiness test for cars over 4 years old. A car must pass the NCT to be driven legally. The inspection checks brakes, tyres, lights, emissions, etc.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Motor Tax?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Motor Tax</strong> is the annual tax to keep a vehicle on the road. The amount varies with engine size and CO2 emissions. You can pay online on the <strong>motortax.ie</strong> website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pay Motor Tax online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Car insurance</strong> is compulsory in Ireland</li>\n<li>The <strong>NCT</strong> is compulsory for cars over 4 years old</li>\n<li><strong>Motor Tax</strong> must be paid every year</li>\n<li>Check that the car has no <strong>pending fines</strong> or motor tax debt</li>\n<li>Consider the cost of insurance - it can be high for new arrivals with no Irish driving history</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an Irish licence to buy a car?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. You can buy a car with a valid foreign licence, but you need insurance and motor tax. To drive for more than 1 year in Ireland you must exchange your licence for an Irish one.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Eircode is the Irish postcode (7 characters, e.g. D01 F5P2). Every address in Ireland has a unique Eircode. You can look up the Eircode for an address on eircode.ie.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - National Car Test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - Road Safety Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/buying-or-selling-a-vehicle/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Buying a Vehicle</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Buying a Car in Ireland: Complete Guide','How to buy a car in Ireland: what you need, the buying steps, the NCT, Motor Tax and insurance.','publish','closed','closed','','buying-a-car-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/buying-a-car-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22322,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:47','2026-08-15 09:03:47','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish driving licence?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish driving licence is issued by the <strong>NDLS (National Driver Licence Service)</strong>. Brazilians can drive in Ireland with their Brazilian licence for up to <strong>12 months</strong> after arriving in the country. After that period you must exchange it for an Irish licence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/licensed-driver/exchange-my-foreign-driving-licence.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Exchange a foreign licence through the NDLS</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to exchange their licence?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who intend to live in Ireland for more than 12 months need to exchange their Brazilian licence for the Irish one. The exchange is possible because Brazil has a <strong>reciprocity agreement</strong> with Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to exchange it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Valid Brazilian licence</strong> (it must not be suspended or revoked)</li>\n<li>Valid <strong>passport</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address in Ireland</strong> (less than 6 months old)</li>\n<li><strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of residence in Ireland</strong> (at least 185 days in the year)</li>\n<li><strong>Certified translation</strong> of your licence into English (if required)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to exchange it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Complete the online form on the <strong>NDLS</strong> website</li>\n<li>Book a visit to your nearest <strong>NDLS</strong> centre</li>\n<li>Bring your original documents</li>\n<li>Pay the fee</li>\n<li>Once the application is processed, the licence is sent according to the NDLS procedure; the timeframe can vary</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fees and validity periods depend on the licence type and the current NDLS rules. Confirm the amount directly with the official service before you pay.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a licence exchange:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS - National Driver Licence Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The rules on driving with a foreign licence depend on your residence status and on the validity of the licence. Check the current NDLS/RSA rules instead of assuming a universal time limit.</li>\n<li>After 12 months it is <strong>mandatory</strong> to have an Irish licence</li>\n<li>If your Brazilian licence expires you cannot renew it in Ireland &#8211; you must exchange it for an Irish one</li>\n<li><strong>Car insurance</strong> can be more expensive for holders of a foreign licence</li>\n<li>If you cannot exchange your licence (for example, because it expired), you will need to do the full process: <strong>theory test, lessons and a practical test</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I drive in Ireland with a Brazilian licence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, for up to 12 months after you arrive in Ireland. After that you must exchange it for the Irish licence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a test to exchange my licence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. If your licence is valid and Brazil has an agreement with Ireland, you can exchange it without a theory or practical test.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take to receive the licence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Usually 5 to 10 working days after the application, but it can vary.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS - National Driver Licence Service</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - Road Safety Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/driver-licensing/exchanging-foreign-driving-permit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Exchanging a Foreign Driving Permit</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Driving Licence in Ireland: How to Exchange Your CNH','How Brazilians exchange their Brazilian driving licence for an Irish one through the NDLS, and what it requires.','publish','closed','closed','','driving-licence-in-ireland-how-to-exchange-your-cnh','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/driving-licence-in-ireland-how-to-exchange-your-cnh/',0,'guide','',0),(22323,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the tax system in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish tax system is run by <strong>Revenue (Office of the Revenue Commissioners)</strong>. If you work in Ireland, your tax is deducted automatically from your salary through the <strong>PAYE (Pay As You Earn)</strong> system.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has to pay tax?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers in Ireland pay tax. If you are an employee the tax is deducted automatically. If you are self-employed you must file a return and pay your own tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main taxes</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Income Tax</strong>: progressive tax on your income</li>\n<li><strong>USC (Universal Social Charge)</strong>: universal social charge</li>\n<li><strong>PRSI (Pay Related Social Insurance)</strong>: social insurance contribution</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How Income Tax works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Income Tax is progressive. For 2026 the rates are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>20%</strong> on income up to the standard rate band</li>\n<li><strong>40%</strong> on income above that limit</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The standard rate band depends on your status (single, married, etc.). For a single person the limit is approximately <strong>€42,000</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How USC works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>USC is a social tax charged on gross income. Rates range from <strong>0.5% to 8%</strong> depending on your income. People with annual income below <strong>€13,000</strong> do not pay USC.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How PRSI works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>PRSI is the contribution to Irish social insurance. The rate is <strong>4%</strong> for most workers (with some exceptions). PRSI gives access to benefits such as Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit, Illness Benefit and the old-age pension.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How PAYE works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>PAYE (Pay As You Earn)</strong> is the system through which your employer automatically deducts Income Tax, USC and PRSI from your salary. You do not need to do anything &#8211; the tax is withheld at source.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Tax credits</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You are entitled to <strong>tax credits</strong> that reduce the tax you pay. The main one is the <strong>Personal Tax Credit</strong>, which for 2026 is approximately <strong>€1,875</strong> per year for single people.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register with Revenue</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Create a <strong>Revenue MyAccount</strong> account (using your PPS Number)</li>\n<li>Register your employment or activity</li>\n<li>Check that your tax credits are correct</li>\n<li>If you paid too much tax, claim a refund</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/online-services/services/myaccount/register-for-myaccount.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign in or register for Revenue myAccount</a> · <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access Revenue</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Access Revenue:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>If you worked for more than one employer you may have paid too much tax and be entitled to a <strong>refund</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>tax year</strong> in Ireland runs from January to December</li>\n<li>If you are self-employed you must file an annual return (Form 11)</li>\n<li>Revenue can <strong>fine</strong> people who do not file their tax correctly</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to file an income tax return in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are an employee (PAYE) the tax is deducted automatically. You may need to file if you have other sources of income, are self-employed, or want to claim a refund.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I claim a tax refund?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can claim a refund through <strong>Revenue MyAccount</strong>. If you paid too much tax, Revenue refunds it automatically or you can claim it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the PPS Number in a tax context?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The PPS Number is your tax identification in Ireland. It is the equivalent of the Brazilian CPF for tax purposes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/money-and-tax/tax/income-tax/how-your-tax-is-calculated/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - How Your Tax is Calculated</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/money-and-tax/tax/income-tax/universal-social-charge/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - USC</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Taxes in Ireland: Complete Guide (Income Tax, USC, PRSI)','How Income Tax, USC, PRSI and PAYE work in Ireland, what tax credits you get and how to register with Revenue.','publish','closed','closed','','taxes-in-ireland-complete-guide-income-tax-usc-prsi','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/taxes-in-ireland-complete-guide-income-tax-usc-prsi/',0,'guide','',0),(22324,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Irish citizenship?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Irish citizenship can be obtained by <strong>birth</strong>, by <strong>descent</strong> (Foreign Birth Registration) or by <strong>naturalisation</strong>. For most Brazilians the route is <strong>naturalisation</strong> after a period of legal residence in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/become-an-irish-citizen-by%20-naturalisation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply online for citizenship by naturalisation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official citizenship and residence guide</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can apply for citizenship by naturalisation if:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>You have <strong>legal residence</strong> in Ireland for a continuous period</li>\n<li>You <strong>intend to continue residing</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li>You are of <strong>good character</strong> (no relevant criminal convictions)</li>\n<li>You intend to remain in Ireland after obtaining citizenship</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Residence requirements</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The residence requirements for naturalisation are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>1 year of continuous residence</strong> immediately before the application</li>\n<li>Plus <strong>3 years of residence</strong> in the previous 8 years (4 years in the last 8 in total)</li>\n<li>Or <strong>3 years of continuous residence</strong> if you are married to an Irish citizen</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid <strong>passport</strong></li>\n<li>Valid <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of residence</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li><strong>Birth certificate</strong> (translated if necessary)</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of good character</strong> (criminal record certificate)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Complete the naturalisation form (Form 8)</li>\n<li>Gather all required documents</li>\n<li>Send the application with the fee to the <strong>Department of Justice</strong></li>\n<li>Wait for the assessment (it can take several months)</li>\n<li>If approved, attend the <strong>citizenship ceremony</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The naturalisation application fee is <strong>€175</strong> (not refundable). If approved, the certificate fee is <strong>€950</strong> for adults.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How long does it take?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Processing can take from <strong>6 to 12 months</strong> or more, depending on application volumes. The Department of Justice does not publish a guaranteed fixed timeframe.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for citizenship:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - How to Become a Citizen</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Ireland <strong>allows dual citizenship</strong> &#8211; you do not have to give up Brazilian citizenship</li>\n<li>Residence time is counted from your <strong>immigration registration</strong> (IRP)</li>\n<li>Student (Stamp 2) residence periods count only in part</li>\n<li>If you have Irish ancestry (grandparents or great-grandparents) you may be entitled to citizenship by <strong>Foreign Birth Registration</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to give up my Brazilian citizenship?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Ireland allows dual citizenship, and so does Brazil. You can hold both citizenships.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much residence is required?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Generally 4 years of legal residence in the last 8 years, including at least 1 continuous year immediately before the application. Specific categories have their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the citizenship ceremony?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is an official ceremony where you take the oath of fidelity to Ireland and receive your naturalisation certificate. It is a formal and mandatory event.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - How to Become a Citizen</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/moving-country/irish-citizenship/becoming-an-irish-citizen-through-naturalisation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Naturalisation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Irish Citizenship: Complete Naturalisation Guide','Who can apply for Irish citizenship by naturalisation, the residence requirements, fees and the process.','publish','closed','closed','','irish-citizenship-complete-naturalisation-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/irish-citizenship-complete-naturalisation-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22325,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish passport?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish passport is issued by the <strong>Passport Service</strong> (Department of Foreign Affairs) to Irish citizens. It allows travel to many countries without a visa.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ireland.ie/en/dfa/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Passport Online - Department of Foreign Affairs</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can apply for an Irish passport if you are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>An <strong>Irish citizen</strong> by birth</li>\n<li>An <strong>Irish citizen</strong> by naturalisation</li>\n<li>An <strong>Irish citizen</strong> by Foreign Birth Registration</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Naturalisation certificate</strong> (if naturalised)</li>\n<li><strong>Birth certificate</strong> (if a citizen by birth)</li>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong> with photo</li>\n<li><strong>Digital photo</strong> to the required standard</li>\n<li><strong>Witness</strong> to sign the form</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>Passport Service</strong> website</li>\n<li>Create an account or sign in</li>\n<li>Complete the online form</li>\n<li>Upload the digital photo</li>\n<li>Pay the fee</li>\n<li>Track the application status online</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Irish passport fees (online application) are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Adult passport (10 years)</strong>: €75</li>\n<li><strong>Child passport (5 years)</strong>: €20</li>\n<li><strong>Large passport (66 pages)</strong>: €105</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How long does it take?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Online passport processing usually takes <strong>10 to 20 working days</strong> for straightforward applications. Postal applications can take longer. The Passport Service publishes current turnaround times on its website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a passport:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ireland.ie/en/dfa/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Passport Service - Ireland.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>An Irish passport is valid for <strong>10 years</strong> for adults</li>\n<li>You can apply for the passport <strong>immediately</strong> after receiving your naturalisation certificate</li>\n<li>The Irish passport allows <strong>visa-free travel</strong> to many countries, including the United Kingdom and the European Union</li>\n<li>If you hold both Brazilian and Irish citizenship, you can use the Irish passport to travel around Europe</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I have both a Brazilian and an Irish passport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Ireland and Brazil both allow dual citizenship. You can hold both passports.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take to receive the passport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For straightforward online applications it is usually 10 to 20 working days. Check the current turnaround times on the Passport Service website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ireland.ie/en/dfa/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Passport Service - Ireland.ie</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Passports</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Irish Passport: How to Apply','Who can apply for an Irish passport, what you need, the fees and how long it takes.','publish','closed','closed','','irish-passport-how-to-apply','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/irish-passport-how-to-apply/',0,'guide','',0),(22326,0,'2026-09-25 14:25:36','2026-09-25 13:25:36','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What do you need to buy a car in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Buying a car in Ireland involves several steps: choosing the vehicle, checking its history, transferring ownership, paying the motor tax and getting insurance. This guide explains the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can buy a car?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Anyone aged 18 or over with a valid driving licence can buy a car in Ireland. You do not need to be an Irish citizen.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>A valid <strong>driving licence</strong> (Irish or foreign)</li>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Car insurance</strong> (compulsory in Ireland)</li>\n<li><strong>Motor Tax</strong> (the annual vehicle tax)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to buy</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Set your budget (including insurance, tax and maintenance)</li>\n<li>Search on sites such as <strong>DoneDeal</strong> and <strong>CarsIreland</strong>, or at dealerships</li>\n<li>Check the vehicle history (mileage, accidents, etc.)</li>\n<li>Take a test drive</li>\n<li>Check that the car has a valid <strong>NCT</strong> (roadworthiness test)</li>\n<li>Complete the change of ownership</li>\n<li>Pay the <strong>Motor Tax</strong> and take out <strong>insurance</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/vrt/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information on VRT - Revenue</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the NCT?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>NCT (National Car Test)</strong> is the compulsory roadworthiness test for cars over 4 years old. A car must pass the NCT to be driven legally. The inspection checks brakes, tyres, lights, emissions, etc.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Motor Tax?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Motor Tax</strong> is the annual tax to keep a vehicle on the road. The amount varies with engine size and CO2 emissions. You can pay online on the <strong>motortax.ie</strong> website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pay Motor Tax online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Car insurance</strong> is compulsory in Ireland</li>\n<li>The <strong>NCT</strong> is compulsory for cars over 4 years old</li>\n<li><strong>Motor Tax</strong> must be paid every year</li>\n<li>Check that the car has no <strong>pending fines</strong> or motor tax debt</li>\n<li>Consider the cost of insurance - it can be high for new arrivals with no Irish driving history</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an Irish licence to buy a car?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. You can buy a car with a valid foreign licence, but you need insurance and motor tax. To drive for more than 1 year in Ireland you must exchange your licence for an Irish one.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Eircode is the Irish postcode (7 characters, e.g. D01 F5P2). Every address in Ireland has a unique Eircode. You can look up the Eircode for an address on eircode.ie.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - National Car Test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - Road Safety Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/buying-or-selling-a-vehicle/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Buying a Vehicle</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Buying a Car in Ireland: Complete Guide','How to buy a car in Ireland: what you need, the buying steps, the NCT, Motor Tax and insurance.','inherit','closed','closed','','22321-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:25:36','2026-09-25 13:25:36','',22321,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22326',0,'revision','',0),(22327,0,'2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish driving licence?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish driving licence is issued by the <strong>NDLS (National Driver Licence Service)</strong>. Brazilians can drive in Ireland with their Brazilian licence for up to <strong>12 months</strong> after arriving in the country. After that period you must exchange it for an Irish licence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/licensed-driver/exchange-my-foreign-driving-licence.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Exchange a foreign licence through the NDLS</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to exchange their licence?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who intend to live in Ireland for more than 12 months need to exchange their Brazilian licence for the Irish one. The exchange is possible because Brazil has a <strong>reciprocity agreement</strong> with Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need to exchange it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Valid Brazilian licence</strong> (it must not be suspended or revoked)</li>\n<li>Valid <strong>passport</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address in Ireland</strong> (less than 6 months old)</li>\n<li><strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of residence in Ireland</strong> (at least 185 days in the year)</li>\n<li><strong>Certified translation</strong> of your licence into English (if required)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to exchange it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Complete the online form on the <strong>NDLS</strong> website</li>\n<li>Book a visit to your nearest <strong>NDLS</strong> centre</li>\n<li>Bring your original documents</li>\n<li>Pay the fee</li>\n<li>Once the application is processed, the licence is sent according to the NDLS procedure; the timeframe can vary</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fees and validity periods depend on the licence type and the current NDLS rules. Confirm the amount directly with the official service before you pay.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a licence exchange:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS - National Driver Licence Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The rules on driving with a foreign licence depend on your residence status and on the validity of the licence. Check the current NDLS/RSA rules instead of assuming a universal time limit.</li>\n<li>After 12 months it is <strong>mandatory</strong> to have an Irish licence</li>\n<li>If your Brazilian licence expires you cannot renew it in Ireland &#8211; you must exchange it for an Irish one</li>\n<li><strong>Car insurance</strong> can be more expensive for holders of a foreign licence</li>\n<li>If you cannot exchange your licence (for example, because it expired), you will need to do the full process: <strong>theory test, lessons and a practical test</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I drive in Ireland with a Brazilian licence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, for up to 12 months after you arrive in Ireland. After that you must exchange it for the Irish licence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a test to exchange my licence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. If your licence is valid and Brazil has an agreement with Ireland, you can exchange it without a theory or practical test.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take to receive the licence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Usually 5 to 10 working days after the application, but it can vary.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS - National Driver Licence Service</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - Road Safety Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/driver-licensing/exchanging-foreign-driving-permit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Exchanging a Foreign Driving Permit</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Driving Licence in Ireland: How to Exchange Your CNH','How Brazilians exchange their Brazilian driving licence for an Irish one through the NDLS, and what it requires.','inherit','closed','closed','','22322-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','',22322,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22327',0,'revision','',0),(22328,0,'2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the tax system in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish tax system is run by <strong>Revenue (Office of the Revenue Commissioners)</strong>. If you work in Ireland, your tax is deducted automatically from your salary through the <strong>PAYE (Pay As You Earn)</strong> system.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has to pay tax?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers in Ireland pay tax. If you are an employee the tax is deducted automatically. If you are self-employed you must file a return and pay your own tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main taxes</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Income Tax</strong>: progressive tax on your income</li>\n<li><strong>USC (Universal Social Charge)</strong>: universal social charge</li>\n<li><strong>PRSI (Pay Related Social Insurance)</strong>: social insurance contribution</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How Income Tax works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Income Tax is progressive. For 2026 the rates are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>20%</strong> on income up to the standard rate band</li>\n<li><strong>40%</strong> on income above that limit</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The standard rate band depends on your status (single, married, etc.). For a single person the limit is approximately <strong>€42,000</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How USC works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>USC is a social tax charged on gross income. Rates range from <strong>0.5% to 8%</strong> depending on your income. People with annual income below <strong>€13,000</strong> do not pay USC.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How PRSI works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>PRSI is the contribution to Irish social insurance. The rate is <strong>4%</strong> for most workers (with some exceptions). PRSI gives access to benefits such as Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit, Illness Benefit and the old-age pension.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How PAYE works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>PAYE (Pay As You Earn)</strong> is the system through which your employer automatically deducts Income Tax, USC and PRSI from your salary. You do not need to do anything &#8211; the tax is withheld at source.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Tax credits</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You are entitled to <strong>tax credits</strong> that reduce the tax you pay. The main one is the <strong>Personal Tax Credit</strong>, which for 2026 is approximately <strong>€1,875</strong> per year for single people.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register with Revenue</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Create a <strong>Revenue MyAccount</strong> account (using your PPS Number)</li>\n<li>Register your employment or activity</li>\n<li>Check that your tax credits are correct</li>\n<li>If you paid too much tax, claim a refund</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/online-services/services/myaccount/register-for-myaccount.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign in or register for Revenue myAccount</a> · <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access Revenue</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Access Revenue:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>If you worked for more than one employer you may have paid too much tax and be entitled to a <strong>refund</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>tax year</strong> in Ireland runs from January to December</li>\n<li>If you are self-employed you must file an annual return (Form 11)</li>\n<li>Revenue can <strong>fine</strong> people who do not file their tax correctly</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to file an income tax return in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are an employee (PAYE) the tax is deducted automatically. You may need to file if you have other sources of income, are self-employed, or want to claim a refund.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I claim a tax refund?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can claim a refund through <strong>Revenue MyAccount</strong>. If you paid too much tax, Revenue refunds it automatically or you can claim it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the PPS Number in a tax context?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The PPS Number is your tax identification in Ireland. It is the equivalent of the Brazilian CPF for tax purposes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/money-and-tax/tax/income-tax/how-your-tax-is-calculated/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - How Your Tax is Calculated</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/money-and-tax/tax/income-tax/universal-social-charge/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - USC</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Taxes in Ireland: Complete Guide (Income Tax, USC, PRSI)','How Income Tax, USC, PRSI and PAYE work in Ireland, what tax credits you get and how to register with Revenue.','inherit','closed','closed','','22323-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','',22323,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22328',0,'revision','',0),(22329,0,'2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Irish citizenship?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Irish citizenship can be obtained by <strong>birth</strong>, by <strong>descent</strong> (Foreign Birth Registration) or by <strong>naturalisation</strong>. For most Brazilians the route is <strong>naturalisation</strong> after a period of legal residence in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/become-an-irish-citizen-by%20-naturalisation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply online for citizenship by naturalisation</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official citizenship and residence guide</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can apply for citizenship by naturalisation if:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>You have <strong>legal residence</strong> in Ireland for a continuous period</li>\n<li>You <strong>intend to continue residing</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li>You are of <strong>good character</strong> (no relevant criminal convictions)</li>\n<li>You intend to remain in Ireland after obtaining citizenship</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Residence requirements</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The residence requirements for naturalisation are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>1 year of continuous residence</strong> immediately before the application</li>\n<li>Plus <strong>3 years of residence</strong> in the previous 8 years (4 years in the last 8 in total)</li>\n<li>Or <strong>3 years of continuous residence</strong> if you are married to an Irish citizen</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid <strong>passport</strong></li>\n<li>Valid <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of residence</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li><strong>Birth certificate</strong> (translated if necessary)</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proof of good character</strong> (criminal record certificate)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Complete the naturalisation form (Form 8)</li>\n<li>Gather all required documents</li>\n<li>Send the application with the fee to the <strong>Department of Justice</strong></li>\n<li>Wait for the assessment (it can take several months)</li>\n<li>If approved, attend the <strong>citizenship ceremony</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The naturalisation application fee is <strong>€175</strong> (not refundable). If approved, the certificate fee is <strong>€950</strong> for adults.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How long does it take?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Processing can take from <strong>6 to 12 months</strong> or more, depending on application volumes. The Department of Justice does not publish a guaranteed fixed timeframe.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for citizenship:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - How to Become a Citizen</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Ireland <strong>allows dual citizenship</strong> &#8211; you do not have to give up Brazilian citizenship</li>\n<li>Residence time is counted from your <strong>immigration registration</strong> (IRP)</li>\n<li>Student (Stamp 2) residence periods count only in part</li>\n<li>If you have Irish ancestry (grandparents or great-grandparents) you may be entitled to citizenship by <strong>Foreign Birth Registration</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to give up my Brazilian citizenship?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Ireland allows dual citizenship, and so does Brazil. You can hold both citizenships.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much residence is required?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Generally 4 years of legal residence in the last 8 years, including at least 1 continuous year immediately before the application. Specific categories have their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the citizenship ceremony?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is an official ceremony where you take the oath of fidelity to Ireland and receive your naturalisation certificate. It is a formal and mandatory event.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/how-to-become-a-citizen/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - How to Become a Citizen</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/moving-country/irish-citizenship/becoming-an-irish-citizen-through-naturalisation/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Naturalisation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Irish Citizenship: Complete Naturalisation Guide','Who can apply for Irish citizenship by naturalisation, the residence requirements, fees and the process.','inherit','closed','closed','','22324-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','',22324,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22329',0,'revision','',0),(22330,0,'2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish passport?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish passport is issued by the <strong>Passport Service</strong> (Department of Foreign Affairs) to Irish citizens. It allows travel to many countries without a visa.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ireland.ie/en/dfa/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Passport Online - Department of Foreign Affairs</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can apply for an Irish passport if you are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>An <strong>Irish citizen</strong> by birth</li>\n<li>An <strong>Irish citizen</strong> by naturalisation</li>\n<li>An <strong>Irish citizen</strong> by Foreign Birth Registration</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Naturalisation certificate</strong> (if naturalised)</li>\n<li><strong>Birth certificate</strong> (if a citizen by birth)</li>\n<li><strong>Identity document</strong> with photo</li>\n<li><strong>Digital photo</strong> to the required standard</li>\n<li><strong>Witness</strong> to sign the form</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>Passport Service</strong> website</li>\n<li>Create an account or sign in</li>\n<li>Complete the online form</li>\n<li>Upload the digital photo</li>\n<li>Pay the fee</li>\n<li>Track the application status online</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Irish passport fees (online application) are:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Adult passport (10 years)</strong>: €75</li>\n<li><strong>Child passport (5 years)</strong>: €20</li>\n<li><strong>Large passport (66 pages)</strong>: €105</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How long does it take?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Online passport processing usually takes <strong>10 to 20 working days</strong> for straightforward applications. Postal applications can take longer. The Passport Service publishes current turnaround times on its website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a passport:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ireland.ie/en/dfa/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Passport Service - Ireland.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>An Irish passport is valid for <strong>10 years</strong> for adults</li>\n<li>You can apply for the passport <strong>immediately</strong> after receiving your naturalisation certificate</li>\n<li>The Irish passport allows <strong>visa-free travel</strong> to many countries, including the United Kingdom and the European Union</li>\n<li>If you hold both Brazilian and Irish citizenship, you can use the Irish passport to travel around Europe</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I have both a Brazilian and an Irish passport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Ireland and Brazil both allow dual citizenship. You can hold both passports.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take to receive the passport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For straightforward online applications it is usually 10 to 20 working days. Check the current turnaround times on the Passport Service website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ireland.ie/en/dfa/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Passport Service - Ireland.ie</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/passports/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Passports</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Irish Passport: How to Apply','Who can apply for an Irish passport, what you need, the fees and how long it takes.','inherit','closed','closed','','22325-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:25:37','2026-09-25 13:25:37','',22325,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22330',0,'revision','',0),(22331,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Child Benefit?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Child Benefit</strong> is a monthly payment from the Irish government to families with children under 16 (or under 18 if they are in full-time education or have a disability).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/topics/parents-children-family/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for Child Benefit on MyWelfare</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information about Child Benefit</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is entitled?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Habitual residents</strong> in Ireland (living here and intending to stay for at least 1 year)</li>\n<li>Families with children <strong>under 16</strong></li>\n<li>Children <strong>under 18</strong> if they are in full-time education or have a disability</li>\n<li>The applicant must have a <strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much is it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Child Benefit is <strong>€140 per month</strong> for each child. Payment is made on the <strong>first working day of each month</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Sign in to <strong>MyWelfare</strong> with your MyGovID</li>\n<li>Complete the Child Benefit application form</li>\n<li>Enter the child&#8217;s details (birth certificate)</li>\n<li>Submit the application</li>\n<li>Track the status on MyWelfare</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for Child Benefit:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Child Benefit is paid <strong>monthly</strong> on the first working day of the month</li>\n<li>Payment is made to <strong>one parent</strong> (usually the mother)</li>\n<li>If you have children in Brazil, you may qualify for Child Benefit if they live with you in Ireland</li>\n<li>The payment is <strong>not affected</strong> by family income</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a visa to receive Child Benefit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You need to be a habitual resident of Ireland. Having a valid immigration status (such as an Employment Permit) helps prove residence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does my child have to live in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Child Benefit is paid for children who reside in Ireland with you.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/service/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Child Benefit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/social-welfare/families-and-children/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Child Benefit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Child Benefit in Ireland: Complete Guide','What Child Benefit is, who qualifies, how much it pays and how to apply through MyWelfare.','publish','closed','closed','','child-benefit-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/child-benefit-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22332,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Social Welfare?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Social Welfare</strong> is the Irish social security system, run by the <strong>Department of Social Protection</strong>. It offers a range of payments for workers, families and people in vulnerable situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/en/explore-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Explore and start Social Welfare claims on MyWelfare</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/collections/social-welfare/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official collection of social payments and services</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can access it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Social Welfare payments are available to <strong>habitual residents</strong> of Ireland. Some payments require PRSI contributions, while others are means-tested (allowances).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main payments</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit</strong>: unemployment insurance for those who have paid PRSI</li>\n<li><strong>Jobseeker&#8217;s Allowance</strong>: means-tested unemployment assistance</li>\n<li><strong>Illness Benefit</strong>: payment if you cannot work for health reasons</li>\n<li><strong>Maternity Benefit</strong>: paid maternity leave</li>\n<li><strong>Paternity Benefit</strong>: paid paternity leave</li>\n<li><strong>Child Benefit</strong>: monthly payment for families with children</li>\n<li><strong>State Pension</strong>: the state pension</li>\n<li><strong>Working Family Payment</strong>: income top-up for working families</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Sign in to <strong>MyWelfare</strong> with your MyGovID</li>\n<li>Find the payment you need</li>\n<li>Complete the online form</li>\n<li>Send the required documents</li>\n<li>Track the status on MyWelfare</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for payments:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For PRSI-based payments (such as Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit) you must have <strong>contributed</strong> for a minimum period</li>\n<li>For means-tested payments (such as Jobseeker&#8217;s Allowance) you must pass a <strong>means test</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>PPS Number</strong> is required to apply for any payment</li>\n<li>You must be a <strong>habitual resident</strong> of Ireland</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians claim Social Welfare?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are a habitual resident of Ireland and meet the requirements for each payment. Having an Employment Permit or another valid immigration status is important.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is MyGovID?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>MyGovID is the Irish government&#8217;s authentication system. You need it to access MyWelfare and other online government services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Social Protection</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/social-welfare/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Social Welfare</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Social Welfare in Ireland: Benefits Guide','The main Irish social welfare payments, who qualifies and how to apply through MyWelfare.','publish','closed','closed','','social-welfare-in-ireland-benefits-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/social-welfare-in-ireland-benefits-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22333,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What do you need to open a company in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Opening a company in Ireland is a relatively simple process. Registration is done with the <strong>CRO (Companies Registration Office)</strong>, and you need to register with <strong>Revenue</strong> for tax purposes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can open a company?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Anyone aged 18 or over can open a company in Ireland, regardless of nationality. Brazilians with a valid immigration status can open companies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Types of company</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Sole Trader</strong>: you trade in your own name. Simpler, but you are personally liable for the debts.</li>\n<li><strong>Limited Company</strong>: a company separate from you. More protection, but more paperwork.</li>\n<li><strong>Partnership</strong>: two or more partners trading together.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to open one</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Choose the type of company</li>\n<li>Register the company with the <strong>CRO</strong> (for a Limited Company)</li>\n<li>Register with <strong>Revenue</strong> for tax purposes</li>\n<li>Get a <strong>PPS Number</strong> (if you do not have one yet)</li>\n<li>Open a <strong>business bank account</strong></li>\n<li>Appoint an <strong>accountant</strong> (recommended)</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Registering a Limited Company with the CRO costs <strong>€50</strong> (online registration). Registering as a Sole Trader with Revenue is <strong>free</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to register</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Register a company:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CRO - Companies Registration Office</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://cro.ie/services-and-help/core/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register a company on CORE (CRO)</a> · <a href=\"https://cro.ie/Registration/Company/Registration-Methods/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register a company - official CRO page</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>As a <strong>Sole Trader</strong> you are personally liable for the company&#8217;s debts</li>\n<li>As a <strong>Limited Company</strong> the company is a separate entity &#8211; your personal assets are protected</li>\n<li>You must file tax returns every year (Form 11 for a Sole Trader, CT1 for a Limited Company)</li>\n<li>Appoint an <strong>accountant</strong> to handle the tax obligations</li>\n<li><strong>Revenue</strong> can fine anyone who files tax incorrectly</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians open a company in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, as long as they have a valid immigration status. Brazilians on Stamp 1 (work) or Stamp 4 (residence) can open companies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an entrepreneur visa?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It depends on your immigration status. If you already have Stamp 4 you can open a company. If you are on Stamp 1 you may need a specific permission. Check with the Department of Justice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CRO - Companies Registration Office</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/employment/types-of-employment/self-employment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Self-Employment</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Opening a Company in Ireland: Complete Guide','Types of company in Ireland, how to register with the CRO and Revenue, and what it costs.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-open-a-company-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-open-a-company-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22334,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish immigration system?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish immigration system is run by the <strong>Department of Justice</strong> and the <strong>Irish Immigration Service (ISD)</strong>. Brazilians <strong>do not need a visa</strong> to enter Ireland as tourists (up to 90 days), but they do need a permission to work or to reside for longer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official immigration portal</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs a visa?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians <strong>do not need a visa</strong> to enter Ireland as tourists for up to 90 days. However, to <strong>work</strong>, <strong>study</strong> or <strong>reside</strong> for more than 90 days you need the right permission.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Types of permission (Stamps)</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Stamp 1</strong>: work permission (Employment Permit)</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 2</strong>: student</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 2A</strong>: student not entitled to work</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 3</strong>: visitor/family member (cannot work)</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 4</strong>: residence (can work without a separate permit)</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 5</strong>: residence without a time limit</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 6</strong>: Irish citizen with dual citizenship</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to get work permission</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To work in Ireland you generally need an <strong>Employment Permit</strong>. The process is:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Find a job with an Irish employer</li>\n<li>The employer applies for the <strong>Employment Permit</strong> to the Department of Enterprise</li>\n<li>With the permit approved you can enter Ireland</li>\n<li>Register at the <strong>Immigration Office</strong> and receive your <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the IRP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong> is the document that proves your legal residence in Ireland. It is a card with your details and your immigration status (Stamp). You must renew it periodically.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register with immigration</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Book a visit to the <strong>Immigration Office</strong> (in Dublin) or the <strong>Garda National Immigration Bureau (GNIB)</strong> (outside Dublin)</li>\n<li>Bring your passport, Employment Permit, proof of address and photo</li>\n<li>Pay the registration fee</li>\n<li>Receive your IRP</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The immigration registration (IRP) fee is <strong>€300</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Immigration information:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>You must register with immigration <strong>within 90 days</strong> of arriving in Ireland (if you are staying more than 90 days)</li>\n<li>The IRP must be <strong>renewed annually</strong></li>\n<li>Do not work without the right permission &#8211; this can lead to <strong>deportation</strong></li>\n<li>If you lose your job, notify the Department of Justice immediately</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 4</strong> allows you to work without a separate permit</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do Brazilians need a visa for Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not for tourism (up to 90 days). To work or study you need the right permission (an Employment Permit or a Student visa).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is Stamp 4?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Stamp 4 is a residence permission that allows you to work without a separate Employment Permit. It is granted in several situations, such as after 2 years on Stamp 1 or for family members of Irish citizens.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does the IRP cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The IRP registration fee is €300 per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/moving-country/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Moving to Ireland</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Visas and Immigration in Ireland: Complete Guide','Which permissions (Stamps) exist in Ireland, who needs a visa and how to register with immigration.','publish','closed','closed','','visas-and-immigration-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:47','2026-09-25 13:46:47','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/visas-and-immigration-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22335,283039560,'2026-08-15 09:03:48','2026-08-15 09:03:48','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the IRP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong> is the document that proves your legal residence in Ireland. It is a card with your personal details, photo and immigration status (Stamp). You must renew it <strong>annually</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to renew it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All non-EEA residents in Ireland with a residence permission need to renew their IRP. Brazilians on Stamp 1, Stamp 2, Stamp 3 or Stamp 4 need to renew.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid <strong>passport</strong></li>\n<li>Your <strong>current IRP</strong> (if you have it)</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of status</strong>: Employment Permit, school letter, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Photo</strong> to the required standard</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to renew</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>Irish Immigration Service</strong> website</li>\n<li>Complete the online renewal form</li>\n<li>Send the required documents</li>\n<li>Pay the €300 fee</li>\n<li>Track the application status</li>\n<li>Receive your new IRP by post</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The IRP renewal fee is <strong>€300</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to renew</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Renew your IRP:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registration-renewal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - Registration Renewal</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Renew your IRP <strong>before</strong> it expires</li>\n<li>If your IRP has expired you may be <strong>illegally</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li>The IRP is <strong>personal and non-transferable</strong></li>\n<li>Keep your IRP in a safe place &#8211; it is your residence document</li>\n<li>If you lose your job, notify the Department of Justice immediately</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take to renew the IRP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Processing can take from a few weeks to a few months, depending on application volumes. Apply in advance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What happens if my IRP expires?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You may be illegally in Ireland. Request the renewal as soon as possible and explain your situation to the Department of Justice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-renew-your-current-permission/renewing-your-registration-permission-if-you-live-in-the-republic-of-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Renew your IRP online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registration-renewal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - Registration Renewal</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - Registration</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','IRP Renewal in Ireland: Complete Guide','Who has to renew the Irish Residence Permit, what you need and how to renew it online.','publish','closed','closed','','irp-renewal-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/irp-renewal-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22336,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is MyGovID?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>MyGovID</strong> is the Irish government&#8217;s authentication system. It works like a &#8220;single sign-on&#8221; that lets you access several public services online with one account. It is essential for <strong>MyWelfare</strong>, <strong>Revenue</strong> and other government services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mygovid.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access MyGovID</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs MyGovID?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Anyone who needs to access public services online in Ireland, including to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Apply for social welfare payments (MyWelfare)</li>\n<li>Access Revenue (tax)</li>\n<li>Apply for a PPS Number</li>\n<li>Access health services</li>\n<li>Renew documents</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>MyGovID levels</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Basic</strong>: basic account with an email and password. It gives access to some services.</li>\n<li><strong>Verified</strong>: verified account with an identity document. It gives access to most services.</li>\n<li><strong>Mobile</strong>: additional verification with the mobile app for more sensitive services.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to create an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>MyGovID</strong> website</li>\n<li>Click &#8220;Create Account&#8221;</li>\n<li>Enter your email and create a password</li>\n<li>Verify your email</li>\n<li>To verify your identity you will need your <strong>PPS Number</strong> and an identity document</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to create it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Create a MyGovID account:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mygovid.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyGovID</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>MyGovID is <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>You need a <strong>PPS Number</strong> to verify your account</li>\n<li>Keep your password in a safe place &#8211; it is the key to all your public services</li>\n<li>Never share your password with third parties</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to create MyGovID?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. To verify your account and access most services you need your PPS Number.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is MyGovID free?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, creating and using MyGovID is free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mygovid.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyGovID</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/government-in-ireland/how-government-works/egovernment/mygovid/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - MyGovID</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','MyGovID in Ireland: How to Create and Use It','What MyGovID is, which levels exist and how to create a verified account.','publish','closed','closed','','mygovid-in-ireland-how-to-create-and-use','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/mygovid-in-ireland-how-to-create-and-use/',0,'guide','',0),(22337,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are employment rights in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Employment rights in Ireland are protected by law and administered by the <strong>Workplace Relations Commission (WRC)</strong>. All workers, including Brazilians with work permission, have guaranteed rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has employment rights?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers in Ireland have employment rights regardless of nationality. This includes the minimum wage, annual leave, working hours and protection against discrimination.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Minimum wage</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish national minimum wage is <strong>€13.50 per hour</strong> (from January 2026). Reduced rates apply to under-20s.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Annual leave</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>All workers are entitled to <strong>4 weeks of paid annual leave</strong></li>\n<li>Leave accrues in proportion to time worked</li>\n<li>The employer must pay out unused leave at the end of the contract</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Working hours</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The maximum working week is <strong>48 hours</strong> (on average)</li>\n<li>You are entitled to <strong>11 hours of rest</strong> between shifts</li>\n<li>You are entitled to <strong>1 rest day</strong> per week</li>\n<li>Overtime must be paid or time off in lieu</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Contract of employment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Your employer must give you a <strong>written contract of employment</strong> within 5 days of starting work. The contract must include pay, hours, leave and the notice period.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Notice period</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The notice period depends on length of service:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>1-2 years: 1 week</li>\n<li>2-5 years: 2 weeks</li>\n<li>5-10 years: 4 weeks</li>\n<li>10+ years: 8 weeks</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to get help</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have a problem at work, contact the <strong>Workplace Relations Commission (WRC)</strong>:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>WRC:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">workplacerelations.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The minimum wage applies to <strong>all</strong> workers, including non-nationals</li>\n<li>You are entitled to detailed <strong>payslips</strong></li>\n<li>It is <strong>illegal</strong> to discriminate on the basis of nationality, race, gender, etc.</li>\n<li>If you were unfairly dismissed you can take the case to the WRC</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the minimum wage in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The national minimum wage is €13.50 per hour (from January 2026). Check the current rate on the government website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much annual leave am I entitled to?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers are entitled to 4 weeks of paid annual leave per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the WRC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The WRC (Workplace Relations Commission) is the official body dealing with workplace disputes, inspections and information on employment rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make a complaint to the WRC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Workplace Relations Commission</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/employment/employment-rights-and-conditions/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Employment Rights</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/publication/3a4d5-national-minimum-wage/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - National Minimum Wage</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Employment Rights in Ireland: Complete Guide','Minimum wage, annual leave, working hours, written contracts and where to get help.','publish','closed','closed','','employment-rights-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/employment-rights-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22338,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is public transport in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Public transport in Ireland is administered by the <strong>National Transport Authority (NTA)</strong> and operated by several companies. The system includes buses, trains (DART, Luas, Intercity) and regional services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who uses public transport?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Public transport is used by residents and visitors all over Ireland. In Dublin the system is the most complete, with buses, Luas (light rail) and DART (coastal train).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check transport fares and services - TFI</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main services</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Dublin Bus</strong>: buses in Dublin</li>\n<li><strong>Luas</strong>: light rail in Dublin</li>\n<li><strong>DART</strong>: coastal train in Dublin</li>\n<li><strong>Irish Rail</strong>: intercity trains</li>\n<li><strong>Bus Éireann</strong>: intercity and rural buses</li>\n<li><strong>Local Link</strong>: rural buses</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to pay</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Leap Card</strong>: rechargeable transport card with reduced fares</li>\n<li><strong>TFI Go</strong>: mobile payment app</li>\n<li>Cash payment (higher fares)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Leap Card?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Leap Card</strong> is Ireland&#8217;s public transport card. You can buy and top it up in stations, participating shops and online. With a Leap Card the fares are cheaper than paying cash.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to buy a Leap Card</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Buy a Leap Card:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.leapcard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Leap Card</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information about the Leap Card</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>A Leap Card costs <strong>€5</strong> (one-off card fee)</li>\n<li>You can top it up online, at stations or in participating shops</li>\n<li>Students may get <strong>discounts</strong> with the Student Leap Card</li>\n<li><strong>TFI</strong> (Transport for Ireland) is the official transport information portal</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Leap Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is Ireland&#8217;s rechargeable public transport card. It offers reduced fares on buses, trains and the Luas.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a Leap Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is not compulsory, but fares are cheaper with a Leap Card than paying cash.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Transport for Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nationaltransport.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">National Transport Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.leapcard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Leap Card</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Public Transport in Ireland: Complete Guide','Buses, trains, Luas and DART in Ireland, and how to pay with the Leap Card.','publish','closed','closed','','public-transport-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/public-transport-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22339,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is SUSI?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>SUSI (Student Universal Support Ireland)</strong> is the official body that administers student grants for further-education students in Ireland. The grants help pay fees and living costs.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/start-an-application/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Start a SUSI application</a> · <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for or check a SUSI grant</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To apply for SUSI you need:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>To be a <strong>habitual resident</strong> of Ireland (living here for at least 3 of the last 5 years)</li>\n<li>To be enrolled in an <strong>eligible course</strong> (usually at third level)</li>\n<li>To pass the <strong>income assessment</strong></li>\n<li>To have a <strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Types of grant</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Maintenance Grant</strong>: for living costs (rent, food)</li>\n<li><strong>Fee Grant</strong>: to pay tuition fees</li>\n<li><strong>Field Trip Grant</strong>: for study trips</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>SUSI</strong> website</li>\n<li>Create an account</li>\n<li>Complete the online form</li>\n<li>Send the required documents (proof of income, residence, etc.)</li>\n<li>Track the application status</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a SUSI grant:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SUSI</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The application period is usually <strong>April to November</strong> for the following academic year</li>\n<li>The income test considers parental income (if you are dependent) or your own income</li>\n<li>The income limits vary according to family size</li>\n<li>You must be a <strong>habitual resident</strong> &#8211; newly arrived Brazilians may not be eligible immediately</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians receive a SUSI grant?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are a habitual resident of Ireland (usually 3 of the last 5 years) and meet the income and course requirements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The main window is April to November for the following academic year. Apply as early as possible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/how-to-apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for a SUSI grant</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SUSI - Student Universal Support Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/education/third-level-education/fees-and-supports-for-third-level-education/student-grant-scheme/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Student Grant Scheme</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','SUSI in Ireland: How to Apply for a Grant','Who can apply for a SUSI student grant in Ireland, the types of grant and how to apply.','publish','closed','closed','','susi-in-ireland-how-to-apply-for-a-grant','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/susi-in-ireland-how-to-apply-for-a-grant/',0,'guide','',0),(22340,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are emergency services in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In Ireland, emergency services are reached on <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong>. Both are free and work 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to know?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents and visitors in Ireland should know how to reach emergency services. For a medical emergency, fire, crime or accident, call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Emergency numbers</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>112</strong>: European emergency number (works across the EU)</li>\n<li><strong>999</strong>: Irish emergency number</li>\n<li><strong>112/999</strong>: Garda (police), ambulance, fire brigade, sea rescue</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to call</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Call <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong></li>\n<li>Say which service you need (police, ambulance, fire brigade)</li>\n<li>Give your location (address or Eircode)</li>\n<li>Describe the emergency</li>\n<li>Do not hang up until the operator tells you to</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/contact-us/useful-contact-numbers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Garda emergency numbers and guidance</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE guidance on urgent care</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Eircode?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Eircode</strong> is the Irish postcode (7 characters, e.g. D01 F5P2). Every address in Ireland has a unique Eircode. In an emergency, giving your Eircode helps services find you quickly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Calls to 112/999 are <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>You can call from any phone, including a mobile with no credit</li>\n<li>The operator may speak <strong>English</strong> &#8211; have the basics ready</li>\n<li>If you do not speak English, say the name of your language and ask for an interpreter</li>\n<li>For non-urgent medical problems, call your <strong>GP</strong> or the <strong>HSE</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Which number should I call in an emergency?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Call 112 or 999. Both work and are free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the Irish postcode. Every address has a unique 7-character Eircode. You can look up the Eircode for an address on eircode.ie.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.hse.ie/eng/services/list/4/healthservices/emergency/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Emergency Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/health-services/emergency-health-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Emergency Health Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eircode</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Emergency Services in Ireland: 112 and 999','How to reach emergency services in Ireland, what to say and why your Eircode matters.','publish','closed','closed','','emergency-services-in-ireland-112-and-999','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/emergency-services-in-ireland-112-and-999/',0,'guide','',0),(22341,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the NCT?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>NCT (National Car Test)</strong> is the compulsory roadworthiness inspection for vehicles in Ireland. It is run by the <strong>RSA (Road Safety Authority)</strong> and checks the safety and emissions of the vehicle.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs an NCT?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cars over <strong>4 years old</strong> need an NCT. The inspection is compulsory and the vehicle must pass to be driven legally.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/bookings/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Book or manage an NCT</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>When to do it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Cars aged 4-9 years: NCT every <strong>2 years</strong></li>\n<li>Cars aged 10+ years: NCT <strong>every year</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Brakes</li>\n<li>Tyres</li>\n<li>Lights</li>\n<li>Emissions</li>\n<li>Steering</li>\n<li>Suspension</li>\n<li>Bodywork</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The NCT fee is <strong>€55</strong> for cars (full inspection). A retest costs <strong>€28</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to do it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Book an NCT:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - National Car Test</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Driving without a valid NCT is <strong>illegal</strong> and can result in a fine</li>\n<li>A valid NCT is required to <strong>renew the motor tax</strong></li>\n<li>If the car fails you have a deadline to fix the faults and get a retest</li>\n<li>The NCT certificate is valid for 1 or 2 years, depending on the age of the car</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an NCT to buy a car?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is not compulsory to buy, but the car needs a valid NCT to be driven legally. Check that the car has an NCT before you buy.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does the NCT cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A full inspection costs €55. A retest costs €28.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - National Car Test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - Road Safety Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/buying-or-selling-a-vehicle/national-car-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - NCT</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','NCT in Ireland: Vehicle Inspection Guide','What the NCT is, which cars need it, how often and what it costs.','publish','closed','closed','','nct-in-ireland-vehicle-inspection-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/nct-in-ireland-vehicle-inspection-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22342,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Motor Tax?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Motor Tax</strong> is the annual road tax on vehicles in Ireland. Every vehicle registered in Ireland must have its motor tax paid to be driven legally.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has to pay?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All owners of vehicles registered in Ireland must pay motor tax every year. The amount depends on the type of vehicle, engine size and CO2 emissions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to pay</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>motortax.ie</strong> website</li>\n<li>Enter the vehicle registration number</li>\n<li>Check the vehicle details</li>\n<li>Choose the period (3, 6 or 12 months)</li>\n<li>Pay by credit/debit card</li>\n<li>Receive the receipt</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The motor tax amount depends on the vehicle. For cars it is based on CO2 emissions, so lower-emission cars pay less. Amounts range from roughly <strong>€180 to €2,400</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to pay</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Pay Motor Tax:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Driving without motor tax is <strong>illegal</strong> and can result in a fine</li>\n<li>You need a <strong>valid NCT</strong> to renew the motor tax</li>\n<li>Motor tax can be paid for <strong>3, 6 or 12 months</strong></li>\n<li>Paying for 12 months is cheaper than paying for 3 months</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an NCT to pay motor tax?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, for cars over 4 years old you need a valid NCT to renew the motor tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does motor tax cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The amount depends on the vehicle. For cars it is based on CO2 emissions. Check the exact amount on motortax.ie.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/motor-tax-and-insurance/motor-tax-rates/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Motor Tax Rates</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Motor Tax in Ireland: How to Pay','How to pay the Irish motor tax online, what it costs and what you need first.','publish','closed','closed','','motor-tax-in-ireland-how-to-pay','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/motor-tax-in-ireland-how-to-pay/',0,'guide','',0),(22343,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:41','2026-08-17 13:13:41','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Eircode?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Eircode</strong> is the Irish postcode. It is a unique 7-character code (e.g. D01 F5P2) assigned to every address in Ireland. It was introduced in 2015 and is essential for deliveries, emergencies and services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs an Eircode?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents and businesses in Ireland have an Eircode. You need it to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Receive mail and parcels</li>\n<li>Give your address on public services</li>\n<li>Emergencies (112/999)</li>\n<li>Register with services (banks, doctors, etc.)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to find your Eircode</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>eircode.ie</strong> website</li>\n<li>Type your address in the search box</li>\n<li>Find the matching Eircode</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find an Eircode</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to find it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Find an Eircode:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eircode</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Every address has a <strong>unique</strong> Eircode</li>\n<li>It is <strong>free</strong> to look up an Eircode</li>\n<li>Use your Eircode on <strong>all</strong> correspondence and forms</li>\n<li>In an emergency, giving your Eircode helps services find you quickly</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the Irish postcode: a unique 7-character code for each address in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I find my Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Go to eircode.ie and search for your address. The Eircode is displayed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eircode</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/consumer/phones-internet-tv-and-postal-services/postal-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Postal Services</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Eircode in Ireland: What It Is and How to Find It','What an Eircode is, who needs one and how to look it up for any Irish address.','publish','closed','closed','','eircode-in-ireland-what-it-is-and-how-to-find-it','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/eircode-in-ireland-what-it-is-and-how-to-find-it/',0,'guide','',0),(22344,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:42','2026-08-17 13:13:42','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is HAP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>HAP (Housing Assistance Payment)</strong> is an Irish government scheme that helps eligible people pay their rent. HAP is administered by the <strong>local authorities</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.hap.ie/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for HAP online</a> · <a href=\"https://www.hap.ie/localauthorities/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find your local authority for HAP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>HAP is for people who:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Are on the <strong>social housing waiting list</strong></li>\n<li>Have a <strong>low income</strong></li>\n<li>Are <strong>habitual residents</strong> of Ireland</li>\n<li>Do not have suitable housing</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How it works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>With HAP, the local authority pays the rent directly to the landlord. The tenant pays a contribution to the council (usually between 15% and 35% of income). The tenant finds the property on the private market.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Contact the <strong>local authority</strong> for your area</li>\n<li>Request an assessment for HAP</li>\n<li>If approved, find a property on the private market</li>\n<li>The council inspects the property and the landlord</li>\n<li>The council starts paying the rent to the landlord</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>HAP information:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/service/76f3e-housing-assistance-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - HAP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>HAP is for people on the <strong>social housing waiting list</strong></li>\n<li>You have to find the property <strong>yourself</strong> on the private market</li>\n<li>The local authority pays the rent <strong>directly to the landlord</strong></li>\n<li>You pay a <strong>contribution</strong> to the council based on your income</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians apply for HAP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are a habitual resident of Ireland and on the social housing waiting list. Having a valid immigration status is important.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much do I pay with HAP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You pay a contribution based on your income, usually between 15% and 35% of your weekly income.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/service/76f3e-housing-assistance-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - HAP</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/housing/renting-a-home/housing-assistance-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - HAP</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/help/local-authorities/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local Authorities</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','HAP in Ireland: Help Paying Your Rent','What the Housing Assistance Payment is, who qualifies and how to apply through your local authority.','publish','closed','closed','','hap-in-ireland-help-paying-your-rent','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/hap-in-ireland-help-paying-your-rent/',0,'guide','',0),(22345,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:42','2026-08-17 13:13:42','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the RTB?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>RTB (Residential Tenancies Board)</strong> is the official body that regulates residential renting in Ireland. It registers tenancy agreements, resolves disputes between tenants and landlords, and provides information on rights and responsibilities.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://rtb.ie/renting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check renting rights and rules - RTB</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to know about the RTB?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All tenants and landlords in Ireland. The RTB is essential to protect your rights as a tenant.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://rtb.ie/renting/rights-responsibilities/tenant-rights-responsibilities/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tenant rights at the RTB</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What the RTB does</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Registers</strong> tenancy agreements</li>\n<li><strong>Resolves disputes</strong> between tenants and landlords</li>\n<li><strong>Provides information</strong> on rights and responsibilities</li>\n<li><strong>Regulates rent increases</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Holds the deposit</strong> (in some cases)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Your rights as a tenant</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The landlord must <strong>register the tenancy</strong> with the RTB within 1 month</li>\n<li>The maximum deposit is <strong>1 month&#8217;s rent</strong></li>\n<li>The landlord must give <strong>notice</strong> to end the tenancy</li>\n<li>In <strong>Rent Pressure Zones</strong> the rent increase is limited</li>\n<li>The landlord must provide a <strong>receipt</strong> for the deposit</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register a dispute</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>RTB</strong> website</li>\n<li>Complete the dispute form</li>\n<li>Pay the fee (if applicable)</li>\n<li>The RTB reviews the case</li>\n<li>Take part in mediation or a hearing</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>RTB:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rtb.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">rtb.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check that your tenancy is <strong>registered with the RTB</strong></li>\n<li>Keep <strong>all payment receipts</strong></li>\n<li>If the landlord does not return the deposit you can <strong>take the case to the RTB</strong></li>\n<li>The RTB has <strong>decision-making power</strong> in disputes</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the RTB?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the official body that regulates residential renting in Ireland. It registers tenancies and resolves disputes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does my landlord have to register the tenancy?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, the landlord must register the tenancy with the RTB within 1 month of the tenancy starting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rtb.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RTB - Residential Tenancies Board</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/housing/renting-a-home/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Renting a Home</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','RTB in Ireland: Your Rights as a Tenant','What the Residential Tenancies Board does and how to register a dispute.','publish','closed','closed','','rtb-in-ireland-your-rights-as-a-tenant','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/rtb-in-ireland-your-rights-as-a-tenant/',0,'guide','',0),(22346,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:42','2026-08-17 13:13:42','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the CAO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>CAO (Central Applications Office)</strong> is the central body that processes admission applications for higher education courses (universities and institutes) in Ireland. It is the system through which you apply for undergraduate courses.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to use the CAO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Students who want to enter undergraduate courses at universities and institutes of technology in Ireland. The CAO is used by both Irish and international students.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/apply.php?altmenu=aentry\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make or access your CAO application</a> · <a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/apply.php?page=myapp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access My CAO Application</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How it works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Create an account on the <strong>CAO</strong> website</li>\n<li>Choose up to <strong>10 courses</strong> in order of preference</li>\n<li>Pay the application fee</li>\n<li>Send the required documents</li>\n<li>Wait for offers in August</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Key dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>1 February</strong>: normal application deadline</li>\n<li><strong>1 May</strong>: deadline to change preferences</li>\n<li><strong>August</strong>: offers are made</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The CAO application fee is <strong>€45</strong> (for 10 courses) or <strong>€30</strong> (for late applications).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply through the CAO:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CAO - Central Applications Office</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The CAO is used for <strong>undergraduate</strong> courses</li>\n<li>For postgraduate study you apply <strong>directly</strong> to the university</li>\n<li>International students may have <strong>additional</strong> requirements (English, grade equivalency)</li>\n<li>Offers are based on <strong>points</strong> (Leaving Certificate or equivalent)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians use the CAO?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, international students can apply through the CAO. You will need a grade equivalency and proof of English proficiency.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The normal deadline is 1 February for the following academic year. Apply as early as possible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CAO - Central Applications Office</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/education/third-level-education/applying-to-college/central-applications-office/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - CAO</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://hea.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Higher Education Authority</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','CAO in Ireland: How to Apply to University','What the CAO is, who applies through it, the key dates and the application fee.','publish','closed','closed','','cao-in-ireland-how-to-apply-to-university','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/cao-in-ireland-how-to-apply-to-university/',0,'guide','',0),(22347,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:42','2026-08-17 13:13:42','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are consumer rights in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Consumer rights in Ireland are protected by law and enforced by the <strong>CCPC (Competition and Consumer Protection Commission)</strong>. All consumers, including Brazilians, have guaranteed rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/consumers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check consumer rights and guidance - CCPC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has consumer rights?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All consumers in Ireland, regardless of nationality. If you buy goods or services in Ireland your rights are protected.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main rights</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Right of return</strong>: you can return faulty goods</li>\n<li><strong>Guarantee</strong>: goods must work as advertised</li>\n<li><strong>Clear information</strong>: prices and terms must be transparent</li>\n<li><strong>Protection against misleading practices</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right of cancellation</strong>: online purchases can be cancelled within 14 days</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Online shopping</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For online purchases you have the right to <strong>cancel</strong> within 14 days (the cooling-off period). The seller must refund you within 14 days of the cancellation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to get help</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have a problem with a product or service:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Contact the <strong>seller</strong> first</li>\n<li>If that does not solve it, contact the <strong>CCPC</strong></li>\n<li>For financial services, contact the <strong>FSPO (Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman)</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>CCPC:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ccpc.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Keep the <strong>receipts</strong> for all purchases</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>terms and conditions</strong> before buying</li>\n<li>Be wary of <strong>too-good-to-be-true</strong> offers</li>\n<li>The CCPC has <strong>free information</strong> about your rights</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I return a product bought in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if the product is faulty or does not match the description. For online purchases you have 14 days to cancel.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the CCPC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is Ireland&#8217;s consumer protection commission. It provides information and help with complaints.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CCPC - Competition and Consumer Protection Commission</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/consumer/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Consumer</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Consumer Rights in Ireland: Complete Guide','Returns, guarantees, online shopping and where to complain about a problem.','publish','closed','closed','','consumer-rights-in-ireland-complete-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/consumer-rights-in-ireland-complete-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22348,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:42','2026-08-17 13:13:42','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is data protection in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Data protection in Ireland is regulated by the <strong>Data Protection Commission (DPC)</strong>. Ireland follows the EU <strong>GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation)</strong>, which gives citizens strong rights over their personal data.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dataprotection.ie/en/individuals/exercising-your-rights\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Exercise your data protection rights - DPC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is protected?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents of Ireland have data protection rights under the GDPR. This includes Brazilians living in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Your rights under the GDPR</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Right of access</strong>: see what data a company holds about you</li>\n<li><strong>Right of rectification</strong>: correct inaccurate data</li>\n<li><strong>Right to erasure</strong>: ask for your data to be deleted</li>\n<li><strong>Right to portability</strong>: transfer your data to another company</li>\n<li><strong>Right to object</strong>: object to the processing of your data</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to exercise your rights</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Contact the <strong>company</strong> that holds your data</li>\n<li>Make the request in writing</li>\n<li>The company must respond within <strong>1 month</strong></li>\n<li>If it is not resolved, contact the <strong>DPC</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>DPC:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dataprotection.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">dataprotection.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The GDPR applies to <strong>all</strong> companies processing your data in the EU</li>\n<li>Companies must obtain your <strong>consent</strong> to process your data</li>\n<li>You can request <strong>copies</strong> of your data at any time</li>\n<li>The DPC can <strong>fine</strong> companies that breach the GDPR</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the GDPR?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the EU General Data Protection Regulation. It gives citizens strong rights over their personal data.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I make a data protection complaint?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Contact the company first. If it is not resolved, contact the Data Protection Commission (DPC).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dataprotection.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Data Protection Commission</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/government-in-ireland/data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Data Protection</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Data Protection in Ireland: Your Rights (GDPR)','Your rights under the GDPR in Ireland and how to exercise them.','publish','closed','closed','','data-protection-in-ireland-your-rights-gdpr','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/data-protection-in-ireland-your-rights-gdpr/',0,'guide','',0),(22349,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:42','2026-08-17 13:13:42','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is An Garda Síochána?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>An Garda Síochána</strong> (pronounced &#8220;Garda Siokana&#8221;) is the national police service of Ireland. It is responsible for public safety, crime prevention and law enforcement.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Garda Síochána</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to know about the Garda?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents and visitors in Ireland. It is important to know how to contact the Garda in an emergency and how to deal with the police.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Emergency numbers</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong>: emergencies (police, ambulance, fire brigade)</li>\n<li><strong>Garda Confidential Line</strong>: 1800 666 111 (for anonymous reports)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to deal with the Garda</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>In an emergency, call <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong></li>\n<li>For non-urgent matters, go to your local <strong>Garda station</strong></li>\n<li>If stopped by the Garda, <strong>cooperate</strong> and present your documents</li>\n<li>You are entitled to an <strong>interpreter</strong> if you do not speak English</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/contact-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official Garda contacts</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Immigration registration</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Outside Dublin, immigration registration (IRP) is done at the <strong>Garda National Immigration Bureau (GNIB)</strong>. In Dublin it is done at the <strong>Immigration Office</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>An Garda Síochána:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">garda.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The Garda is <strong>approachable</strong> and generally helpful with information</li>\n<li>You can ask for a <strong>reference number</strong> when reporting a crime</li>\n<li>If you are a victim of crime the Garda can provide <strong>support</strong></li>\n<li>Never offer a <strong>bribe</strong> to the Garda &#8211; it is a serious crime</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Garda?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the national police service of Ireland. The full name is Garda Síochána, which means &#8220;Guardians of the Peace&#8221; in Irish.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Which number do I call in an emergency?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Call 112 or 999. Both are free and work 24 hours.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Garda Síochána</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/justice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Justice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','An Garda Síochána in Ireland: Police and Emergencies','How to contact An Garda Síochána, emergency numbers and what to expect.','publish','closed','closed','','garda-in-ireland-police-and-emergencies','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/garda-in-ireland-police-and-emergencies/',0,'guide','',0),(22350,283039560,'2026-08-17 13:13:42','2026-08-17 13:13:42','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Revenue MyAccount?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Revenue MyAccount</strong> is the online portal of the <strong>Revenue (Office of the Revenue Commissioners)</strong> for individual taxpayers. It is where you manage your tax, check your tax credits, claim refunds and get tax information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/online-services/services/myaccount/register-for-myaccount.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign in or register for Revenue myAccount</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ros.ie/myaccount-web/sign_in.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access Revenue myAccount</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to use it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers in Ireland. If you are employed (PAYE), Revenue MyAccount is essential to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check your <strong>tax credits</strong></li>\n<li>Claim a tax <strong>refund</strong></li>\n<li>Register <strong>new jobs</strong></li>\n<li>Update your <strong>personal details</strong></li>\n<li>Access your <strong>income statements</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to create an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>Revenue</strong> website</li>\n<li>Click on &#8220;MyAccount&#8221;</li>\n<li>Register with your <strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n<li>Verify your identity</li>\n<li>Create your password</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you can do</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check and update <strong>tax credits</strong></li>\n<li>Claim a tax <strong>refund</strong></li>\n<li>Register <strong>new jobs</strong></li>\n<li>Declare <strong>additional income</strong></li>\n<li>Access <strong>income and tax certificates</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Access Revenue MyAccount:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - MyAccount</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Revenue MyAccount is <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>You need a <strong>PPS Number</strong> to create an account</li>\n<li>Check your tax credits <strong>regularly</strong></li>\n<li>If you paid too much tax, claim a <strong>refund</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number for Revenue?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The PPS Number is your tax identification in Ireland and is needed to create a Revenue account.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I claim a tax refund?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Go to Revenue MyAccount, check your tax credits and claim the refund. Revenue processes it and deposits it into your bank account.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/money-and-tax/tax/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Tax</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Revenue MyAccount in Ireland: How to Use It','How to create a Revenue MyAccount and what you can do with it.','publish','closed','closed','','revenue-myaccount-in-ireland-how-to-use-it','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/revenue-myaccount-in-ireland-how-to-use-it/',0,'guide','',0),(22351,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:50','2026-09-25 13:35:50','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Child Benefit?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Child Benefit</strong> is a monthly payment from the Irish government to families with children under 16 (or under 18 if they are in full-time education or have a disability).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/topics/parents-children-family/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for Child Benefit on MyWelfare</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information about Child Benefit</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is entitled?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Habitual residents</strong> in Ireland (living here and intending to stay for at least 1 year)</li>\n<li>Families with children <strong>under 16</strong></li>\n<li>Children <strong>under 18</strong> if they are in full-time education or have a disability</li>\n<li>The applicant must have a <strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much is it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Child Benefit is <strong>€140 per month</strong> for each child. Payment is made on the <strong>first working day of each month</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Sign in to <strong>MyWelfare</strong> with your MyGovID</li>\n<li>Complete the Child Benefit application form</li>\n<li>Enter the child&#8217;s details (birth certificate)</li>\n<li>Submit the application</li>\n<li>Track the status on MyWelfare</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for Child Benefit:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Child Benefit is paid <strong>monthly</strong> on the first working day of the month</li>\n<li>Payment is made to <strong>one parent</strong> (usually the mother)</li>\n<li>If you have children in Brazil, you may qualify for Child Benefit if they live with you in Ireland</li>\n<li>The payment is <strong>not affected</strong> by family income</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a visa to receive Child Benefit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You need to be a habitual resident of Ireland. Having a valid immigration status (such as an Employment Permit) helps prove residence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does my child have to live in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Child Benefit is paid for children who reside in Ireland with you.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/service/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Child Benefit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/social-welfare/families-and-children/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Child Benefit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Child Benefit in Ireland: Complete Guide','What Child Benefit is, who qualifies, how much it pays and how to apply through MyWelfare.','inherit','closed','closed','','22331-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:50','2026-09-25 13:35:50','',22331,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22351',0,'revision','',0),(22352,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:50','2026-09-25 13:35:50','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Social Welfare?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Social Welfare</strong> is the Irish social security system, run by the <strong>Department of Social Protection</strong>. It offers a range of payments for workers, families and people in vulnerable situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/en/explore-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Explore and start Social Welfare claims on MyWelfare</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/collections/social-welfare/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official collection of social payments and services</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can access it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Social Welfare payments are available to <strong>habitual residents</strong> of Ireland. Some payments require PRSI contributions, while others are means-tested (allowances).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main payments</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit</strong>: unemployment insurance for those who have paid PRSI</li>\n<li><strong>Jobseeker&#8217;s Allowance</strong>: means-tested unemployment assistance</li>\n<li><strong>Illness Benefit</strong>: payment if you cannot work for health reasons</li>\n<li><strong>Maternity Benefit</strong>: paid maternity leave</li>\n<li><strong>Paternity Benefit</strong>: paid paternity leave</li>\n<li><strong>Child Benefit</strong>: monthly payment for families with children</li>\n<li><strong>State Pension</strong>: the state pension</li>\n<li><strong>Working Family Payment</strong>: income top-up for working families</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Sign in to <strong>MyWelfare</strong> with your MyGovID</li>\n<li>Find the payment you need</li>\n<li>Complete the online form</li>\n<li>Send the required documents</li>\n<li>Track the status on MyWelfare</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for payments:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For PRSI-based payments (such as Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit) you must have <strong>contributed</strong> for a minimum period</li>\n<li>For means-tested payments (such as Jobseeker&#8217;s Allowance) you must pass a <strong>means test</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>PPS Number</strong> is required to apply for any payment</li>\n<li>You must be a <strong>habitual resident</strong> of Ireland</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians claim Social Welfare?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are a habitual resident of Ireland and meet the requirements for each payment. Having an Employment Permit or another valid immigration status is important.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is MyGovID?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>MyGovID is the Irish government&#8217;s authentication system. You need it to access MyWelfare and other online government services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mywelfare.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyWelfare</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Social Protection</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/social-welfare/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Social Welfare</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Social Welfare in Ireland: Benefits Guide','The main Irish social welfare payments, who qualifies and how to apply through MyWelfare.','inherit','closed','closed','','22332-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:50','2026-09-25 13:35:50','',22332,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22352',0,'revision','',0),(22353,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:50','2026-09-25 13:35:50','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What do you need to open a company in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Opening a company in Ireland is a relatively simple process. Registration is done with the <strong>CRO (Companies Registration Office)</strong>, and you need to register with <strong>Revenue</strong> for tax purposes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can open a company?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Anyone aged 18 or over can open a company in Ireland, regardless of nationality. Brazilians with a valid immigration status can open companies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Types of company</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Sole Trader</strong>: you trade in your own name. Simpler, but you are personally liable for the debts.</li>\n<li><strong>Limited Company</strong>: a company separate from you. More protection, but more paperwork.</li>\n<li><strong>Partnership</strong>: two or more partners trading together.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to open one</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Choose the type of company</li>\n<li>Register the company with the <strong>CRO</strong> (for a Limited Company)</li>\n<li>Register with <strong>Revenue</strong> for tax purposes</li>\n<li>Get a <strong>PPS Number</strong> (if you do not have one yet)</li>\n<li>Open a <strong>business bank account</strong></li>\n<li>Appoint an <strong>accountant</strong> (recommended)</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Registering a Limited Company with the CRO costs <strong>€50</strong> (online registration). Registering as a Sole Trader with Revenue is <strong>free</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to register</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Register a company:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CRO - Companies Registration Office</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://cro.ie/services-and-help/core/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register a company on CORE (CRO)</a> · <a href=\"https://cro.ie/Registration/Company/Registration-Methods/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register a company - official CRO page</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>As a <strong>Sole Trader</strong> you are personally liable for the company&#8217;s debts</li>\n<li>As a <strong>Limited Company</strong> the company is a separate entity &#8211; your personal assets are protected</li>\n<li>You must file tax returns every year (Form 11 for a Sole Trader, CT1 for a Limited Company)</li>\n<li>Appoint an <strong>accountant</strong> to handle the tax obligations</li>\n<li><strong>Revenue</strong> can fine anyone who files tax incorrectly</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians open a company in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, as long as they have a valid immigration status. Brazilians on Stamp 1 (work) or Stamp 4 (residence) can open companies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an entrepreneur visa?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It depends on your immigration status. If you already have Stamp 4 you can open a company. If you are on Stamp 1 you may need a specific permission. Check with the Department of Justice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CRO - Companies Registration Office</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/employment/types-of-employment/self-employment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Self-Employment</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Opening a Company in Ireland: Complete Guide','Types of company in Ireland, how to register with the CRO and Revenue, and what it costs.','inherit','closed','closed','','22333-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:50','2026-09-25 13:35:50','',22333,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22353',0,'revision','',0),(22354,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Irish immigration system?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish immigration system is run by the <strong>Department of Justice</strong> and the <strong>Irish Immigration Service (ISD)</strong>. Brazilians <strong>do not need a visa</strong> to enter Ireland as tourists (up to 90 days), but they do need a permission to work or to reside for longer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official immigration portal</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs a visa?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians <strong>do not need a visa</strong> to enter Ireland as tourists for up to 90 days. However, to <strong>work</strong>, <strong>study</strong> or <strong>reside</strong> for more than 90 days you need the right permission.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Types of permission (Stamps)</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Stamp 1</strong>: work permission (Employment Permit)</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 2</strong>: student</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 2A</strong>: student not entitled to work</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 3</strong>: visitor/family member (cannot work)</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 4</strong>: residence (can work without a separate permit)</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 5</strong>: residence without a time limit</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 6</strong>: Irish citizen with dual citizenship</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to get work permission</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To work in Ireland you generally need an <strong>Employment Permit</strong>. The process is:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Find a job with an Irish employer</li>\n<li>The employer applies for the <strong>Employment Permit</strong> to the Department of Enterprise</li>\n<li>With the permit approved you can enter Ireland</li>\n<li>Register at the <strong>Immigration Office</strong> and receive your <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the IRP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong> is the document that proves your legal residence in Ireland. It is a card with your details and your immigration status (Stamp). You must renew it periodically.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register with immigration</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Book a visit to the <strong>Immigration Office</strong> (in Dublin) or the <strong>Garda National Immigration Bureau (GNIB)</strong> (outside Dublin)</li>\n<li>Bring your passport, Employment Permit, proof of address and photo</li>\n<li>Pay the registration fee</li>\n<li>Receive your IRP</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The immigration registration (IRP) fee is <strong>€300</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Immigration information:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>You must register with immigration <strong>within 90 days</strong> of arriving in Ireland (if you are staying more than 90 days)</li>\n<li>The IRP must be <strong>renewed annually</strong></li>\n<li>Do not work without the right permission &#8211; this can lead to <strong>deportation</strong></li>\n<li>If you lose your job, notify the Department of Justice immediately</li>\n<li><strong>Stamp 4</strong> allows you to work without a separate permit</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do Brazilians need a visa for Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not for tourism (up to 90 days). To work or study you need the right permission (an Employment Permit or a Student visa).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is Stamp 4?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Stamp 4 is a residence permission that allows you to work without a separate Employment Permit. It is granted in several situations, such as after 2 years on Stamp 1 or for family members of Irish citizens.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does the IRP cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The IRP registration fee is €300 per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/moving-country/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Moving to Ireland</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Visas and Immigration in Ireland: Complete Guide','Which permissions (Stamps) exist in Ireland, who needs a visa and how to register with immigration.','inherit','closed','closed','','22334-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22334,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22354',0,'revision','',0),(22355,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the IRP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>IRP (Irish Residence Permit)</strong> is the document that proves your legal residence in Ireland. It is a card with your personal details, photo and immigration status (Stamp). You must renew it <strong>annually</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to renew it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All non-EEA residents in Ireland with a residence permission need to renew their IRP. Brazilians on Stamp 1, Stamp 2, Stamp 3 or Stamp 4 need to renew.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid <strong>passport</strong></li>\n<li>Your <strong>current IRP</strong> (if you have it)</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of address</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li><strong>Proof of status</strong>: Employment Permit, school letter, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Photo</strong> to the required standard</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to renew</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>Irish Immigration Service</strong> website</li>\n<li>Complete the online renewal form</li>\n<li>Send the required documents</li>\n<li>Pay the €300 fee</li>\n<li>Track the application status</li>\n<li>Receive your new IRP by post</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The IRP renewal fee is <strong>€300</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to renew</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Renew your IRP:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registration-renewal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - Registration Renewal</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Renew your IRP <strong>before</strong> it expires</li>\n<li>If your IRP has expired you may be <strong>illegally</strong> in Ireland</li>\n<li>The IRP is <strong>personal and non-transferable</strong></li>\n<li>Keep your IRP in a safe place &#8211; it is your residence document</li>\n<li>If you lose your job, notify the Department of Justice immediately</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take to renew the IRP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Processing can take from a few weeks to a few months, depending on application volumes. Apply in advance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What happens if my IRP expires?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You may be illegally in Ireland. Request the renewal as soon as possible and explain your situation to the Department of Justice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-renew-your-current-permission/renewing-your-registration-permission-if-you-live-in-the-republic-of-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Renew your IRP online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registration-renewal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - Registration Renewal</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration - Registration</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','IRP Renewal in Ireland: Complete Guide','Who has to renew the Irish Residence Permit, what you need and how to renew it online.','inherit','closed','closed','','22335-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22335,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22355',0,'revision','',0),(22356,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is MyGovID?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>MyGovID</strong> is the Irish government&#8217;s authentication system. It works like a &#8220;single sign-on&#8221; that lets you access several public services online with one account. It is essential for <strong>MyWelfare</strong>, <strong>Revenue</strong> and other government services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mygovid.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access MyGovID</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs MyGovID?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Anyone who needs to access public services online in Ireland, including to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Apply for social welfare payments (MyWelfare)</li>\n<li>Access Revenue (tax)</li>\n<li>Apply for a PPS Number</li>\n<li>Access health services</li>\n<li>Renew documents</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>MyGovID levels</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Basic</strong>: basic account with an email and password. It gives access to some services.</li>\n<li><strong>Verified</strong>: verified account with an identity document. It gives access to most services.</li>\n<li><strong>Mobile</strong>: additional verification with the mobile app for more sensitive services.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to create an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>MyGovID</strong> website</li>\n<li>Click &#8220;Create Account&#8221;</li>\n<li>Enter your email and create a password</li>\n<li>Verify your email</li>\n<li>To verify your identity you will need your <strong>PPS Number</strong> and an identity document</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to create it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Create a MyGovID account:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.mygovid.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyGovID</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>MyGovID is <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>You need a <strong>PPS Number</strong> to verify your account</li>\n<li>Keep your password in a safe place &#8211; it is the key to all your public services</li>\n<li>Never share your password with third parties</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number to create MyGovID?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. To verify your account and access most services you need your PPS Number.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is MyGovID free?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, creating and using MyGovID is free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mygovid.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MyGovID</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/government-in-ireland/how-government-works/egovernment/mygovid/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - MyGovID</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','MyGovID in Ireland: How to Create and Use It','What MyGovID is, which levels exist and how to create a verified account.','inherit','closed','closed','','22336-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22336,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22356',0,'revision','',0),(22357,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are employment rights in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Employment rights in Ireland are protected by law and administered by the <strong>Workplace Relations Commission (WRC)</strong>. All workers, including Brazilians with work permission, have guaranteed rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has employment rights?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers in Ireland have employment rights regardless of nationality. This includes the minimum wage, annual leave, working hours and protection against discrimination.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Minimum wage</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Irish national minimum wage is <strong>€13.50 per hour</strong> (from January 2026). Reduced rates apply to under-20s.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Annual leave</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>All workers are entitled to <strong>4 weeks of paid annual leave</strong></li>\n<li>Leave accrues in proportion to time worked</li>\n<li>The employer must pay out unused leave at the end of the contract</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Working hours</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The maximum working week is <strong>48 hours</strong> (on average)</li>\n<li>You are entitled to <strong>11 hours of rest</strong> between shifts</li>\n<li>You are entitled to <strong>1 rest day</strong> per week</li>\n<li>Overtime must be paid or time off in lieu</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Contract of employment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Your employer must give you a <strong>written contract of employment</strong> within 5 days of starting work. The contract must include pay, hours, leave and the notice period.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Notice period</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The notice period depends on length of service:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>1-2 years: 1 week</li>\n<li>2-5 years: 2 weeks</li>\n<li>5-10 years: 4 weeks</li>\n<li>10+ years: 8 weeks</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to get help</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have a problem at work, contact the <strong>Workplace Relations Commission (WRC)</strong>:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>WRC:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">workplacerelations.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The minimum wage applies to <strong>all</strong> workers, including non-nationals</li>\n<li>You are entitled to detailed <strong>payslips</strong></li>\n<li>It is <strong>illegal</strong> to discriminate on the basis of nationality, race, gender, etc.</li>\n<li>If you were unfairly dismissed you can take the case to the WRC</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the minimum wage in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The national minimum wage is €13.50 per hour (from January 2026). Check the current rate on the government website.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much annual leave am I entitled to?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers are entitled to 4 weeks of paid annual leave per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the WRC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The WRC (Workplace Relations Commission) is the official body dealing with workplace disputes, inspections and information on employment rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make a complaint to the WRC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Workplace Relations Commission</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/employment/employment-rights-and-conditions/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Employment Rights</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/publication/3a4d5-national-minimum-wage/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - National Minimum Wage</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Employment Rights in Ireland: Complete Guide','Minimum wage, annual leave, working hours, written contracts and where to get help.','inherit','closed','closed','','22337-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22337,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22357',0,'revision','',0),(22358,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is public transport in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Public transport in Ireland is administered by the <strong>National Transport Authority (NTA)</strong> and operated by several companies. The system includes buses, trains (DART, Luas, Intercity) and regional services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who uses public transport?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Public transport is used by residents and visitors all over Ireland. In Dublin the system is the most complete, with buses, Luas (light rail) and DART (coastal train).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check transport fares and services - TFI</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main services</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Dublin Bus</strong>: buses in Dublin</li>\n<li><strong>Luas</strong>: light rail in Dublin</li>\n<li><strong>DART</strong>: coastal train in Dublin</li>\n<li><strong>Irish Rail</strong>: intercity trains</li>\n<li><strong>Bus Éireann</strong>: intercity and rural buses</li>\n<li><strong>Local Link</strong>: rural buses</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to pay</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Leap Card</strong>: rechargeable transport card with reduced fares</li>\n<li><strong>TFI Go</strong>: mobile payment app</li>\n<li>Cash payment (higher fares)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Leap Card?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Leap Card</strong> is Ireland&#8217;s public transport card. You can buy and top it up in stations, participating shops and online. With a Leap Card the fares are cheaper than paying cash.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to buy a Leap Card</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Buy a Leap Card:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.leapcard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Leap Card</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information about the Leap Card</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>A Leap Card costs <strong>€5</strong> (one-off card fee)</li>\n<li>You can top it up online, at stations or in participating shops</li>\n<li>Students may get <strong>discounts</strong> with the Student Leap Card</li>\n<li><strong>TFI</strong> (Transport for Ireland) is the official transport information portal</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Leap Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is Ireland&#8217;s rechargeable public transport card. It offers reduced fares on buses, trains and the Luas.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a Leap Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is not compulsory, but fares are cheaper with a Leap Card than paying cash.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Transport for Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.nationaltransport.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">National Transport Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.leapcard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Leap Card</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Public Transport in Ireland: Complete Guide','Buses, trains, Luas and DART in Ireland, and how to pay with the Leap Card.','inherit','closed','closed','','22338-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22338,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22358',0,'revision','',0),(22359,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is SUSI?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>SUSI (Student Universal Support Ireland)</strong> is the official body that administers student grants for further-education students in Ireland. The grants help pay fees and living costs.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/start-an-application/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Start a SUSI application</a> · <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for or check a SUSI grant</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To apply for SUSI you need:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>To be a <strong>habitual resident</strong> of Ireland (living here for at least 3 of the last 5 years)</li>\n<li>To be enrolled in an <strong>eligible course</strong> (usually at third level)</li>\n<li>To pass the <strong>income assessment</strong></li>\n<li>To have a <strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Types of grant</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Maintenance Grant</strong>: for living costs (rent, food)</li>\n<li><strong>Fee Grant</strong>: to pay tuition fees</li>\n<li><strong>Field Trip Grant</strong>: for study trips</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>SUSI</strong> website</li>\n<li>Create an account</li>\n<li>Complete the online form</li>\n<li>Send the required documents (proof of income, residence, etc.)</li>\n<li>Track the application status</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply for a SUSI grant:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SUSI</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The application period is usually <strong>April to November</strong> for the following academic year</li>\n<li>The income test considers parental income (if you are dependent) or your own income</li>\n<li>The income limits vary according to family size</li>\n<li>You must be a <strong>habitual resident</strong> &#8211; newly arrived Brazilians may not be eligible immediately</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians receive a SUSI grant?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are a habitual resident of Ireland (usually 3 of the last 5 years) and meet the income and course requirements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The main window is April to November for the following academic year. Apply as early as possible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/how-to-apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for a SUSI grant</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.susi.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SUSI - Student Universal Support Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/education/third-level-education/fees-and-supports-for-third-level-education/student-grant-scheme/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Student Grant Scheme</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','SUSI in Ireland: How to Apply for a Grant','Who can apply for a SUSI student grant in Ireland, the types of grant and how to apply.','inherit','closed','closed','','22339-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22339,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22359',0,'revision','',0),(22360,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are emergency services in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In Ireland, emergency services are reached on <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong>. Both are free and work 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to know?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents and visitors in Ireland should know how to reach emergency services. For a medical emergency, fire, crime or accident, call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Emergency numbers</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>112</strong>: European emergency number (works across the EU)</li>\n<li><strong>999</strong>: Irish emergency number</li>\n<li><strong>112/999</strong>: Garda (police), ambulance, fire brigade, sea rescue</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to call</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Call <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong></li>\n<li>Say which service you need (police, ambulance, fire brigade)</li>\n<li>Give your location (address or Eircode)</li>\n<li>Describe the emergency</li>\n<li>Do not hang up until the operator tells you to</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/contact-us/useful-contact-numbers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Garda emergency numbers and guidance</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE guidance on urgent care</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Eircode?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Eircode</strong> is the Irish postcode (7 characters, e.g. D01 F5P2). Every address in Ireland has a unique Eircode. In an emergency, giving your Eircode helps services find you quickly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Calls to 112/999 are <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>You can call from any phone, including a mobile with no credit</li>\n<li>The operator may speak <strong>English</strong> &#8211; have the basics ready</li>\n<li>If you do not speak English, say the name of your language and ask for an interpreter</li>\n<li>For non-urgent medical problems, call your <strong>GP</strong> or the <strong>HSE</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Which number should I call in an emergency?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Call 112 or 999. Both work and are free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the Irish postcode. Every address has a unique 7-character Eircode. You can look up the Eircode for an address on eircode.ie.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.hse.ie/eng/services/list/4/healthservices/emergency/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Emergency Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/health/health-services/emergency-health-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Emergency Health Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eircode</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Emergency Services in Ireland: 112 and 999','How to reach emergency services in Ireland, what to say and why your Eircode matters.','inherit','closed','closed','','22340-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22340,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22360',0,'revision','',0),(22361,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the NCT?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>NCT (National Car Test)</strong> is the compulsory roadworthiness inspection for vehicles in Ireland. It is run by the <strong>RSA (Road Safety Authority)</strong> and checks the safety and emissions of the vehicle.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs an NCT?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cars over <strong>4 years old</strong> need an NCT. The inspection is compulsory and the vehicle must pass to be driven legally.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/bookings/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Book or manage an NCT</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>When to do it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Cars aged 4-9 years: NCT every <strong>2 years</strong></li>\n<li>Cars aged 10+ years: NCT <strong>every year</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Brakes</li>\n<li>Tyres</li>\n<li>Lights</li>\n<li>Emissions</li>\n<li>Steering</li>\n<li>Suspension</li>\n<li>Bodywork</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The NCT fee is <strong>€55</strong> for cars (full inspection). A retest costs <strong>€28</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to do it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Book an NCT:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - National Car Test</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Driving without a valid NCT is <strong>illegal</strong> and can result in a fine</li>\n<li>A valid NCT is required to <strong>renew the motor tax</strong></li>\n<li>If the car fails you have a deadline to fix the faults and get a retest</li>\n<li>The NCT certificate is valid for 1 or 2 years, depending on the age of the car</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an NCT to buy a car?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is not compulsory to buy, but the car needs a valid NCT to be driven legally. Check that the car has an NCT before you buy.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does the NCT cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A full inspection costs €55. A retest costs €28.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ncts.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCT - National Car Test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - Road Safety Authority</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/buying-or-selling-a-vehicle/national-car-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - NCT</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','NCT in Ireland: Vehicle Inspection Guide','What the NCT is, which cars need it, how often and what it costs.','inherit','closed','closed','','22341-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22341,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22361',0,'revision','',0),(22362,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Motor Tax?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Motor Tax</strong> is the annual road tax on vehicles in Ireland. Every vehicle registered in Ireland must have its motor tax paid to be driven legally.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has to pay?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All owners of vehicles registered in Ireland must pay motor tax every year. The amount depends on the type of vehicle, engine size and CO2 emissions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to pay</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>motortax.ie</strong> website</li>\n<li>Enter the vehicle registration number</li>\n<li>Check the vehicle details</li>\n<li>Choose the period (3, 6 or 12 months)</li>\n<li>Pay by credit/debit card</li>\n<li>Receive the receipt</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The motor tax amount depends on the vehicle. For cars it is based on CO2 emissions, so lower-emission cars pay less. Amounts range from roughly <strong>€180 to €2,400</strong> per year.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to pay</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Pay Motor Tax:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Driving without motor tax is <strong>illegal</strong> and can result in a fine</li>\n<li>You need a <strong>valid NCT</strong> to renew the motor tax</li>\n<li>Motor tax can be paid for <strong>3, 6 or 12 months</strong></li>\n<li>Paying for 12 months is cheaper than paying for 3 months</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an NCT to pay motor tax?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, for cars over 4 years old you need a valid NCT to renew the motor tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does motor tax cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The amount depends on the vehicle. For cars it is based on CO2 emissions. Check the exact amount on motortax.ie.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.motortax.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Motor Tax Online</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/travel-and-recreation/motoring/motor-tax-and-insurance/motor-tax-rates/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Motor Tax Rates</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Motor Tax in Ireland: How to Pay','How to pay the Irish motor tax online, what it costs and what you need first.','inherit','closed','closed','','22342-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22342,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22362',0,'revision','',0),(22363,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the Eircode?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Eircode</strong> is the Irish postcode. It is a unique 7-character code (e.g. D01 F5P2) assigned to every address in Ireland. It was introduced in 2015 and is essential for deliveries, emergencies and services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs an Eircode?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents and businesses in Ireland have an Eircode. You need it to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Receive mail and parcels</li>\n<li>Give your address on public services</li>\n<li>Emergencies (112/999)</li>\n<li>Register with services (banks, doctors, etc.)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to find your Eircode</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>eircode.ie</strong> website</li>\n<li>Type your address in the search box</li>\n<li>Find the matching Eircode</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find an Eircode</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to find it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Find an Eircode:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eircode</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Every address has a <strong>unique</strong> Eircode</li>\n<li>It is <strong>free</strong> to look up an Eircode</li>\n<li>Use your Eircode on <strong>all</strong> correspondence and forms</li>\n<li>In an emergency, giving your Eircode helps services find you quickly</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the Irish postcode: a unique 7-character code for each address in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I find my Eircode?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Go to eircode.ie and search for your address. The Eircode is displayed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.eircode.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eircode</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/consumer/phones-internet-tv-and-postal-services/postal-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Postal Services</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Eircode in Ireland: What It Is and How to Find It','What an Eircode is, who needs one and how to look it up for any Irish address.','inherit','closed','closed','','22343-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:51','2026-09-25 13:35:51','',22343,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22363',0,'revision','',0),(22364,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is HAP?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>HAP (Housing Assistance Payment)</strong> is an Irish government scheme that helps eligible people pay their rent. HAP is administered by the <strong>local authorities</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.hap.ie/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for HAP online</a> · <a href=\"https://www.hap.ie/localauthorities/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find your local authority for HAP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who can apply?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>HAP is for people who:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Are on the <strong>social housing waiting list</strong></li>\n<li>Have a <strong>low income</strong></li>\n<li>Are <strong>habitual residents</strong> of Ireland</li>\n<li>Do not have suitable housing</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How it works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>With HAP, the local authority pays the rent directly to the landlord. The tenant pays a contribution to the council (usually between 15% and 35% of income). The tenant finds the property on the private market.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Contact the <strong>local authority</strong> for your area</li>\n<li>Request an assessment for HAP</li>\n<li>If approved, find a property on the private market</li>\n<li>The council inspects the property and the landlord</li>\n<li>The council starts paying the rent to the landlord</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>HAP information:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/service/76f3e-housing-assistance-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - HAP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>HAP is for people on the <strong>social housing waiting list</strong></li>\n<li>You have to find the property <strong>yourself</strong> on the private market</li>\n<li>The local authority pays the rent <strong>directly to the landlord</strong></li>\n<li>You pay a <strong>contribution</strong> to the council based on your income</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians apply for HAP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if you are a habitual resident of Ireland and on the social housing waiting list. Having a valid immigration status is important.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much do I pay with HAP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You pay a contribution based on your income, usually between 15% and 35% of your weekly income.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/service/76f3e-housing-assistance-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - HAP</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/housing/renting-a-home/housing-assistance-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - HAP</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/help/local-authorities/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Local Authorities</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','HAP in Ireland: Help Paying Your Rent','What the Housing Assistance Payment is, who qualifies and how to apply through your local authority.','inherit','closed','closed','','22344-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','',22344,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22364',0,'revision','',0),(22365,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the RTB?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>RTB (Residential Tenancies Board)</strong> is the official body that regulates residential renting in Ireland. It registers tenancy agreements, resolves disputes between tenants and landlords, and provides information on rights and responsibilities.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://rtb.ie/renting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check renting rights and rules - RTB</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to know about the RTB?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All tenants and landlords in Ireland. The RTB is essential to protect your rights as a tenant.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://rtb.ie/renting/rights-responsibilities/tenant-rights-responsibilities/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tenant rights at the RTB</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What the RTB does</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Registers</strong> tenancy agreements</li>\n<li><strong>Resolves disputes</strong> between tenants and landlords</li>\n<li><strong>Provides information</strong> on rights and responsibilities</li>\n<li><strong>Regulates rent increases</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Holds the deposit</strong> (in some cases)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Your rights as a tenant</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The landlord must <strong>register the tenancy</strong> with the RTB within 1 month</li>\n<li>The maximum deposit is <strong>1 month&#8217;s rent</strong></li>\n<li>The landlord must give <strong>notice</strong> to end the tenancy</li>\n<li>In <strong>Rent Pressure Zones</strong> the rent increase is limited</li>\n<li>The landlord must provide a <strong>receipt</strong> for the deposit</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to register a dispute</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>RTB</strong> website</li>\n<li>Complete the dispute form</li>\n<li>Pay the fee (if applicable)</li>\n<li>The RTB reviews the case</li>\n<li>Take part in mediation or a hearing</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>RTB:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.rtb.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">rtb.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check that your tenancy is <strong>registered with the RTB</strong></li>\n<li>Keep <strong>all payment receipts</strong></li>\n<li>If the landlord does not return the deposit you can <strong>take the case to the RTB</strong></li>\n<li>The RTB has <strong>decision-making power</strong> in disputes</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the RTB?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the official body that regulates residential renting in Ireland. It registers tenancies and resolves disputes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does my landlord have to register the tenancy?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, the landlord must register the tenancy with the RTB within 1 month of the tenancy starting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rtb.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RTB - Residential Tenancies Board</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/housing/renting-a-home/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Renting a Home</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','RTB in Ireland: Your Rights as a Tenant','What the Residential Tenancies Board does and how to register a dispute.','inherit','closed','closed','','22345-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','',22345,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22365',0,'revision','',0),(22366,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is the CAO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>CAO (Central Applications Office)</strong> is the central body that processes admission applications for higher education courses (universities and institutes) in Ireland. It is the system through which you apply for undergraduate courses.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to use the CAO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Students who want to enter undergraduate courses at universities and institutes of technology in Ireland. The CAO is used by both Irish and international students.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/apply.php?altmenu=aentry\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make or access your CAO application</a> · <a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/apply.php?page=myapp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access My CAO Application</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How it works</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Create an account on the <strong>CAO</strong> website</li>\n<li>Choose up to <strong>10 courses</strong> in order of preference</li>\n<li>Pay the application fee</li>\n<li>Send the required documents</li>\n<li>Wait for offers in August</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Key dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>1 February</strong>: normal application deadline</li>\n<li><strong>1 May</strong>: deadline to change preferences</li>\n<li><strong>August</strong>: offers are made</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How much does it cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The CAO application fee is <strong>€45</strong> (for 10 courses) or <strong>€30</strong> (for late applications).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to apply</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Apply through the CAO:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CAO - Central Applications Office</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The CAO is used for <strong>undergraduate</strong> courses</li>\n<li>For postgraduate study you apply <strong>directly</strong> to the university</li>\n<li>International students may have <strong>additional</strong> requirements (English, grade equivalency)</li>\n<li>Offers are based on <strong>points</strong> (Leaving Certificate or equivalent)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can Brazilians use the CAO?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, international students can apply through the CAO. You will need a grade equivalency and proof of English proficiency.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The normal deadline is 1 February for the following academic year. Apply as early as possible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cao.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CAO - Central Applications Office</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/education/third-level-education/applying-to-college/central-applications-office/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - CAO</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://hea.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Higher Education Authority</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','CAO in Ireland: How to Apply to University','What the CAO is, who applies through it, the key dates and the application fee.','inherit','closed','closed','','22346-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','',22346,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22366',0,'revision','',0),(22367,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are consumer rights in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Consumer rights in Ireland are protected by law and enforced by the <strong>CCPC (Competition and Consumer Protection Commission)</strong>. All consumers, including Brazilians, have guaranteed rights.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/consumers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check consumer rights and guidance - CCPC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who has consumer rights?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All consumers in Ireland, regardless of nationality. If you buy goods or services in Ireland your rights are protected.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Main rights</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Right of return</strong>: you can return faulty goods</li>\n<li><strong>Guarantee</strong>: goods must work as advertised</li>\n<li><strong>Clear information</strong>: prices and terms must be transparent</li>\n<li><strong>Protection against misleading practices</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right of cancellation</strong>: online purchases can be cancelled within 14 days</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Online shopping</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For online purchases you have the right to <strong>cancel</strong> within 14 days (the cooling-off period). The seller must refund you within 14 days of the cancellation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to get help</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have a problem with a product or service:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Contact the <strong>seller</strong> first</li>\n<li>If that does not solve it, contact the <strong>CCPC</strong></li>\n<li>For financial services, contact the <strong>FSPO (Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman)</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>CCPC:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ccpc.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Keep the <strong>receipts</strong> for all purchases</li>\n<li>Read the <strong>terms and conditions</strong> before buying</li>\n<li>Be wary of <strong>too-good-to-be-true</strong> offers</li>\n<li>The CCPC has <strong>free information</strong> about your rights</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I return a product bought in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, if the product is faulty or does not match the description. For online purchases you have 14 days to cancel.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the CCPC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is Ireland&#8217;s consumer protection commission. It provides information and help with complaints.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ccpc.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CCPC - Competition and Consumer Protection Commission</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - Financial Services and Pensions Ombudsman</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/consumer/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Consumer</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Consumer Rights in Ireland: Complete Guide','Returns, guarantees, online shopping and where to complain about a problem.','inherit','closed','closed','','22347-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','',22347,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22367',0,'revision','',0),(22368,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is data protection in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Data protection in Ireland is regulated by the <strong>Data Protection Commission (DPC)</strong>. Ireland follows the EU <strong>GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation)</strong>, which gives citizens strong rights over their personal data.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dataprotection.ie/en/individuals/exercising-your-rights\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Exercise your data protection rights - DPC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is protected?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents of Ireland have data protection rights under the GDPR. This includes Brazilians living in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Your rights under the GDPR</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>Right of access</strong>: see what data a company holds about you</li>\n<li><strong>Right of rectification</strong>: correct inaccurate data</li>\n<li><strong>Right to erasure</strong>: ask for your data to be deleted</li>\n<li><strong>Right to portability</strong>: transfer your data to another company</li>\n<li><strong>Right to object</strong>: object to the processing of your data</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to exercise your rights</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Contact the <strong>company</strong> that holds your data</li>\n<li>Make the request in writing</li>\n<li>The company must respond within <strong>1 month</strong></li>\n<li>If it is not resolved, contact the <strong>DPC</strong></li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>DPC:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dataprotection.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">dataprotection.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The GDPR applies to <strong>all</strong> companies processing your data in the EU</li>\n<li>Companies must obtain your <strong>consent</strong> to process your data</li>\n<li>You can request <strong>copies</strong> of your data at any time</li>\n<li>The DPC can <strong>fine</strong> companies that breach the GDPR</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the GDPR?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the EU General Data Protection Regulation. It gives citizens strong rights over their personal data.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I make a data protection complaint?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Contact the company first. If it is not resolved, contact the Data Protection Commission (DPC).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dataprotection.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Data Protection Commission</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/government-in-ireland/data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Data Protection</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Data Protection in Ireland: Your Rights (GDPR)','Your rights under the GDPR in Ireland and how to exercise them.','inherit','closed','closed','','22348-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','',22348,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22368',0,'revision','',0),(22369,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is An Garda Síochána?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>An Garda Síochána</strong> (pronounced &#8220;Garda Siokana&#8221;) is the national police service of Ireland. It is responsible for public safety, crime prevention and law enforcement.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Garda Síochána</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to know about the Garda?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All residents and visitors in Ireland. It is important to know how to contact the Garda in an emergency and how to deal with the police.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Emergency numbers</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong>: emergencies (police, ambulance, fire brigade)</li>\n<li><strong>Garda Confidential Line</strong>: 1800 666 111 (for anonymous reports)</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to deal with the Garda</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>In an emergency, call <strong>112</strong> or <strong>999</strong></li>\n<li>For non-urgent matters, go to your local <strong>Garda station</strong></li>\n<li>If stopped by the Garda, <strong>cooperate</strong> and present your documents</li>\n<li>You are entitled to an <strong>interpreter</strong> if you do not speak English</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/contact-us/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official Garda contacts</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Immigration registration</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Outside Dublin, immigration registration (IRP) is done at the <strong>Garda National Immigration Bureau (GNIB)</strong>. In Dublin it is done at the <strong>Immigration Office</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>An Garda Síochána:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">garda.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>The Garda is <strong>approachable</strong> and generally helpful with information</li>\n<li>You can ask for a <strong>reference number</strong> when reporting a crime</li>\n<li>If you are a victim of crime the Garda can provide <strong>support</strong></li>\n<li>Never offer a <strong>bribe</strong> to the Garda &#8211; it is a serious crime</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the Garda?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the national police service of Ireland. The full name is Garda Síochána, which means &#8220;Guardians of the Peace&#8221; in Irish.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Which number do I call in an emergency?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Call 112 or 999. Both are free and work 24 hours.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Garda Síochána</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/justice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Justice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','An Garda Síochána in Ireland: Police and Emergencies','How to contact An Garda Síochána, emergency numbers and what to expect.','inherit','closed','closed','','22349-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','',22349,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22369',0,'revision','',0),(22370,0,'2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is Revenue MyAccount?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Revenue MyAccount</strong> is the online portal of the <strong>Revenue (Office of the Revenue Commissioners)</strong> for individual taxpayers. It is where you manage your tax, check your tax credits, claim refunds and get tax information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/online-services/services/myaccount/register-for-myaccount.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign in or register for Revenue myAccount</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ros.ie/myaccount-web/sign_in.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access Revenue myAccount</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who needs to use it?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All workers in Ireland. If you are employed (PAYE), Revenue MyAccount is essential to:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check your <strong>tax credits</strong></li>\n<li>Claim a tax <strong>refund</strong></li>\n<li>Register <strong>new jobs</strong></li>\n<li>Update your <strong>personal details</strong></li>\n<li>Access your <strong>income statements</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How to create an account</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Go to the <strong>Revenue</strong> website</li>\n<li>Click on &#8220;MyAccount&#8221;</li>\n<li>Register with your <strong>PPS Number</strong></li>\n<li>Verify your identity</li>\n<li>Create your password</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What you can do</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check and update <strong>tax credits</strong></li>\n<li>Claim a tax <strong>refund</strong></li>\n<li>Register <strong>new jobs</strong></li>\n<li>Declare <strong>additional income</strong></li>\n<li>Access <strong>income and tax certificates</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to access it</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Access Revenue MyAccount:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - MyAccount</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Good to know</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Revenue MyAccount is <strong>free</strong></li>\n<li>You need a <strong>PPS Number</strong> to create an account</li>\n<li>Check your tax credits <strong>regularly</strong></li>\n<li>If you paid too much tax, claim a <strong>refund</strong></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPS Number for Revenue?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The PPS Number is your tax identification in Ireland and is needed to create a Revenue account.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I claim a tax refund?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Go to Revenue MyAccount, check your tax credits and claim the refund. Revenue processes it and deposits it into your bank account.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide is for information only. Rules and procedures can change. Always check the official source before making a decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Last checked: 3 September 2026</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Office of the Revenue Commissioners</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/money-and-tax/tax/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Tax</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Revenue MyAccount in Ireland: How to Use It','How to create a Revenue MyAccount and what you can do with it.','inherit','closed','closed','','22350-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:35:52','2026-09-25 13:35:52','',22350,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22370',0,'revision','',0),(22371,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how the first IRP registration works for Brazilians and other non-EU/EEA citizens, which documents to prepare, what it costs and where to follow the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/primeira-inscricao-irp-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are Brazilian and have come to Ireland to work, study, live or join family for more than 90 days, registering your immigration permission for the first time is one of the most important steps of your arrival. The current process is carried out by the Immigration Service Delivery (ISD).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information for first-time registration</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians and other citizens from outside the EU/EEA and Switzerland who need to register a residence permission in the Republic of Ireland for the first time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check whether you need to register: the ISD states that people from outside the EU/EEA and Switzerland who come to work, study, live or join family for more than 90 days must register their permission.</li>\n<li>Create or access your personal account in the ISD Customer Service Portal and use it to book your first appointment.</li>\n<li>Prepare the documents specific to your Stamp. The official page lists, among others, a valid passport and proof of your current address; additional documents vary by permission.</li>\n<li>Attend the Registration Office at Burgh Quay, Dublin. Your documents are reviewed, a photo and fingerprints are taken and the permission is registered.</li>\n<li>Pay the fee where applicable. The standard fee is €300 for many categories, but exemptions exist.</li>\n<li>After registration, track the issue of your IRP and keep your passport, address and other details updated with the ISD when needed.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid passport, including the biometric page.</li>\n<li>Proof of your current address in Ireland.</li>\n<li>Documents proving the basis of your permission, such as an enrolment letter, employment contract or family documents, where applicable.</li>\n<li>Your appointment details and any additional documents requested by the ISD.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Booking the first appointment is free. The standard registration fee is €300 for many categories; exemptions exist. The official fee table was updated on 17 July 2026.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do not leave the registration until the end of the period in which you are allowed to remain legally in the State. Availability of appointments must be checked on the official portal.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Paying third parties for an appointment: the ISD warns that valid appointments are made through the Customer Service Portal.</li>\n<li>Assuming every Stamp has the same documentation or fee.</li>\n<li>Travelling without checking the validity of the IRP and the return rules.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - first-time registration</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/required-documents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - documents and fees</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/customer-service-portal-a-guide-to-using-the-online-self-service-portal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Customer Service Portal</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - first-time registration</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/required-documents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - documents and fees</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/customer-service-portal-a-guide-to-using-the-online-self-service-portal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Customer Service Portal</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to pay to book the appointment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Booking the first appointment has no fee; the registration fee, where applicable, is a separate step.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is the fee always €300?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. €300 is the standard fee for many categories, but exemptions exist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I pay someone to get me an appointment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The ISD states that valid appointments are made through the Customer Service Portal and warns about scams.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Are the IRP and the immigration permission the same thing?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not exactly. The permission sets the conditions of residence; the IRP is the card that proves the registration.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/customer-service-portal-a-guide-to-using-the-online-self-service-portal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Book your first IRP registration through the ISD service portal</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information on first-time IRP registration</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>General information; it does not replace individual legal or immigration advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','First IRP Registration in Ireland: Step-by-Step for Brazilians','How the first registration of your Irish immigration permission works, which documents to prepare and what it costs.','publish','closed','closed','','first-irp-registration-in-ireland-step-by-step-for-brazilians','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/first-irp-registration-in-ireland-step-by-step-for-brazilians/',0,'guide','',0),(22372,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand when the IRP lets you return to Ireland without a re-entry visa, what to do if the card is lost or expired, and what to check before travelling.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/viajar-fora-irlanda-com-irp-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For Brazilians living in Ireland, travelling raises two questions: can I enter the destination, and can I come back to Ireland? The IRP answers only part of it. The destination&#8217;s own rules must be checked separately.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians and other non-EU/EEA citizens who already hold a registered residence permission in Ireland and plan to travel internationally.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check the validity of your IRP before travelling. For a visa-national who holds a valid IRP, the ISD states that no re-entry visa is needed to return to Ireland.</li>\n<li>Check the entry rules of the destination country: an Irish IRP does not guarantee entry to another country.</li>\n<li>Check your passport, your name and any other documents used for the trip.</li>\n<li>If the IRP is lost, stolen, has not arrived yet or has an important error, check the ISD emergency procedure before travelling.</li>\n<li>Do not count on being able to request a re-entry visa after you leave: the ISD states that the emergency procedure must be dealt with before travelling.</li>\n<li>Keep digital copies of your documents and confirm the airline and destination requirements.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid passport.</li>\n<li>Valid IRP, where applicable.</li>\n<li>Proof of your permission and of the travel, if needed.</li>\n<li>Documents required by the destination country.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A normal return with a valid IRP does not require a re-entry visa where the ISD rule applies. Emergency procedures or specific visas may have their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do this check before you buy or confirm the trip, especially if your IRP is close to expiring.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing the right to return to Ireland with the right to enter the destination.</li>\n<li>Travelling with an expired IRP or without the card when it is required.</li>\n<li>Assuming that a renewal request automatically replaces the physical card for travel.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/frequently-asked-questions-for-registration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Registration FAQ</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/frequently-asked-questions-for-registration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Registration FAQ</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a visa to come back to Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>With a valid IRP, the ISD states that a re-entry visa is not needed to return to Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the IRP let me enter any country?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. An Irish IRP does not guarantee entry to another country: you must meet that country&#8217;s own entry rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What if my IRP card was stolen while I am abroad?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Contact the ISD before travelling to find out the emergency procedure that applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace immigration or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Travelling Outside Ireland with an IRP: What Brazilians Need to Know','When you can return to Ireland without a re-entry visa, and what to do if your IRP is lost or expired.','publish','closed','closed','','travelling-outside-ireland-with-irp-what-brazilians-need-to-know','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/travelling-outside-ireland-with-irp-what-brazilians-need-to-know/',0,'guide','',0),(22373,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand when Brazilians need an Employment Permit to work in Ireland, the differences between the Critical Skills and General Employment Permit, and the main steps.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/employment-permit-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A job offer does not automatically mean a Brazilian citizen can start working. For non-EEA nationals the general rule is that an Employment Permit is needed, unless an exemption applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians and other workers from outside the EEA who are looking for a job, changing employer, or assessing a work offer in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confirm your immigration status and whether you are exempt from the Employment Permit requirement.</li>\n<li>Check the right type: the current system has nine types; the Critical Skills and General Employment Permits are important examples.</li>\n<li>Check the occupation, remuneration and other current criteria: the lists can change.</li>\n<li>Collect the offer and the contract. The DETE states that a contract signed by both parties must accompany each application.</li>\n<li>Use Employment Permits Online to create the account, submit the application and follow the process.</li>\n<li>When the Labour Market Needs Test applies, confirm the advertising rules before submitting.</li>\n<li>Plan ahead: the DETE states that the application must be received at least 12 weeks before the proposed start date.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Signed offer and contract.</li>\n<li>Employer and worker details.</li>\n<li>Role, remuneration, occupation and work location.</li>\n<li>The checklist and documents specific to the permit type.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fees depend on the type and duration of the Employment Permit. Check the specific type on the DETE website before submitting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>General rule published by the DETE: apply at least 12 weeks before the start date. Where applicable, the Labour Market Needs Test involves additional advertising requirements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing an Employment Permit with a Residence Permission: the DETE is clear that they are not the same.</li>\n<li>Starting work before confirming the authorisation you need.</li>\n<li>Using old lists of eligible occupations.</li>\n<li>Not checking the DETE&#8217;s current processing times.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Employment Permits</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/permit-types/permit-types.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - permit types</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/employment-permit-eligibility/highly-skilled-eligible-occupations-list/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Critical Skills Occupations List</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://employmentpermits.enterprise.gov.ie/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Employment Permits Online</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Employment Permits</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/permit-types/permit-types.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - permit types</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/employment-permit-eligibility/highly-skilled-eligible-occupations-list/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Critical Skills Occupations List</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://employmentpermits.enterprise.gov.ie/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Employment Permits Online</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do Brazilians always need an Employment Permit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not in every case. The general rule applies to non-EEA nationals, but there are exemptions and immigration permissions that authorise work.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Are Critical Skills and General the same?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. They are different categories, with their own criteria and consequences.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does an Employment Permit give residence automatically?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The DETE expressly states that the permit is not a Residence Permission.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://employmentpermits.enterprise.gov.ie/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access the Employment Permits Online portal</a> · <a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check Employment Permit types and criteria</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace immigration or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Employment Permit in Ireland: When a Brazilian Needs One','When Brazilians need an Employment Permit, the difference between Critical Skills and General, and the main steps.','publish','closed','closed','','employment-permit-in-ireland-when-a-brazilian-needs-one','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/employment-permit-in-ireland-when-a-brazilian-needs-one/',0,'guide','',0),(22374,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Just started your first job in Ireland? See how to register the job with Revenue, use myAccount, avoid Emergency Tax and check your Tax Credit Certificate.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/primeiro-emprego-irlanda-emergency-tax/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Your first payslip can carry more tax than expected when Revenue cannot yet give your employer the correct Revenue Payroll Notification (RPN). This is called Emergency Tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians in their first job in Ireland, especially those who have just obtained their PPSN and have not yet registered their first job with Revenue.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Have your PPSN.</li>\n<li>Create or access Revenue myAccount.</li>\n<li>In PAYE Services, use Add Job or Pension Details to register the first job.</li>\n<li>Have your PPSN, the employer&#8217;s Tax Registration Number and the start date to hand.</li>\n<li>Give your PPSN to the employer and check that the job appears in myAccount.</li>\n<li>Check the Tax Credit Certificate.</li>\n<li>If Emergency Tax applied, follow Revenue&#8217;s instructions; once the correct RPN is available, amounts overpaid can be corrected through payroll under the applicable rules.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>PPSN.</li>\n<li>Employer details and Tax Registration Number.</li>\n<li>Start date.</li>\n<li>An active myAccount.</li>\n<li>Tax Credit Certificate, when available.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no fee to register a job in myAccount.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Register as soon as possible, ideally before the first payment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Assuming the employer always registers the first job.</li>\n<li>Not giving the employer your PPSN.</li>\n<li>Not checking the TCC and the jobs listed in myAccount.</li>\n<li>Confusing Emergency Tax with a fine.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Starting your first job</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/what-you-should-do.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - what to do</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Emergency Tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/getting-off-emergency-tax.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - getting off Emergency Tax</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Starting your first job</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/what-you-should-do.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - what to do</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Emergency Tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/getting-off-emergency-tax.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - getting off Emergency Tax</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to register every job in myAccount?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For the first job in the State, Revenue advises the worker to register it. For later jobs the employer normally reports the employment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What happens if I do not register?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The employer may not be able to obtain the required RPN and may apply Emergency Tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When does the TCC appear?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Revenue states that after registration the TCC can be available within up to two working days.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is Emergency Tax refunded?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Amounts overpaid can be corrected once the correct RPN is available, under Revenue&#8217;s rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register your first job in Revenue myAccount</a> · <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/getting-off-emergency-tax.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guidance on getting off Emergency Tax</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace tax or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','First Job in Ireland: How to Avoid Emergency Tax','Register your first job with Revenue, use myAccount and check your Tax Credit Certificate.','publish','closed','closed','','first-job-in-ireland-how-to-avoid-emergency-tax','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/first-job-in-ireland-how-to-avoid-emergency-tax/',0,'guide','',0),(22375,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how to set up sole trader tax registration in Ireland, when to register for Income Tax, how ROS is used and what to keep in mind.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/sole-trader-autonomo-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For freelancers and professionals who work for themselves, the tax route is usually that of the sole trader and self-assessment. This is different from incorporating a limited company.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians in Ireland who intend to provide services on their own account or start a sole activity.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confirm that your working relationship really is self-employed.</li>\n<li>Have a PPSN: Revenue states that it is needed before the sole trader tax registration.</li>\n<li>Register for Income Tax according to your situation, using myAccount or the channel indicated by Revenue.</li>\n<li>After registration, use ROS where required for returns and payments.</li>\n<li>If you trade under a name different from your own, check the business name registration at the CRO.</li>\n<li>Keep records of income, expenses and documents from the start.</li>\n<li>Check additional obligations such as VAT or Employer PAYE where they apply.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>PPSN.</li>\n<li>Start date and activity details.</li>\n<li>Expected turnover information.</li>\n<li>Records of income and expenses.</li>\n<li>Additional details for VAT/PAYE, if applicable.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Revenue does not present the sole trader tax registration as a set-up fee; other registrations or licences may have their own costs.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Returns and payments follow the tax calendar. Self-assessment has annual Pay and File dates: check the year in question.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing a sole trader with a limited company.</li>\n<li>Starting to invoice without checking the tax registration you need.</li>\n<li>Not keeping adequate documents and records.</li>\n<li>Using thresholds and dates from a previous year.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Registering for tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/how-to-register-for-tax-as-a-sole-trader.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - sole trader</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/self-assessment-and-self-employment/guide-to-self-assessment/register-it-self-assessment.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - self-assessment</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Companies Registration Office</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Registering for tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/how-to-register-for-tax-as-a-sole-trader.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - sole trader</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/self-assessment-and-self-employment/guide-to-self-assessment/register-it-self-assessment.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - self-assessment</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Companies Registration Office</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPSN?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Revenue states that a PPSN is needed before the sole trader tax registration.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to use ROS?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>After registration, Revenue states that ROS is used for business-related returns and payments where applicable.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Are there income limits for self-assessment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Revenue sets criteria and limits for non-PAYE income: check the current figures for the year in question.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is a sole trader the same as a company?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. A sole trader is an individual activity; a company is a separate legal entity.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/online-services/services/ros/ros-help/getting-started-on-ros/registering-for-ros/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign in to ROS</a> · <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/how-to-register-for-tax-as-a-sole-trader.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register for Income Tax as a sole trader with Revenue</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace tax or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Work as a Sole Trader in Ireland','Sole trader tax registration, self-assessment and how ROS is used in Ireland.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-work-as-a-sole-trader-in-ireland','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-work-as-a-sole-trader-in-ireland/',0,'guide','',0),(22376,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand where to seek medical care in Ireland: GP, GP out-of-hours, Injury Unit and Emergency Department, including costs and when to call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/hse-irlanda-gp-out-of-hours-injury-unit-emergency-department/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ireland&#8217;s urgent care system can be different from Brazil&#8217;s. Not every situation needs an Emergency Department. The HSE advises choosing the service according to the severity and type of problem.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians living in Ireland who need to know where to seek care when their GP is closed, or when an injury or emergency happens.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For non-urgent problems, see your GP during normal opening hours.</li>\n<li>If the GP is closed and you need urgent care, use the GP out-of-hours service; the HSE states that it normally works by phone with triage.</li>\n<li>For recent non-life-threatening injuries that are unlikely to need admission, check whether there is an Injury Unit suitable for your age and region.</li>\n<li>Go to an Emergency Department for serious or potentially life-threatening situations.</li>\n<li>If there is a risk to life or a need for urgent treatment, call 112 or 999.</li>\n<li>Before you leave, check the HSE finder for the unit, opening hours, the ages it treats and any disruptions.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find urgent or emergency care - HSE</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find a GP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Medical Card or GP Visit Card, if you have one.</li>\n<li>Information about your medication and relevant conditions.</li>\n<li>Identity document, when requested.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE currently states €75 for an Injury Unit and €100 for an Emergency Department, with exemptions in situations such as holding a Medical Card or being referred. Confirm the amounts before publication.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no deadline in an emergency. In a serious situation do not wait: call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Going to an Emergency Department for any symptom.</li>\n<li>Using an Injury Unit for problems it does not treat.</li>\n<li>Not checking the unit&#8217;s age range and opening hours.</li>\n<li>Assuming the cost is always the same.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Urgent and emergency care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - find care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-go-to-a-gp-out-of-hours/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - GP out-of-hours</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-visit-an-injury-unit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Injury Unit</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Urgent and emergency care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - find care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-go-to-a-gp-out-of-hours/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - GP out-of-hours</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-visit-an-injury-unit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Injury Unit</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I use an Injury Unit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For recent, non-life-threatening injuries such as some fractures, sprains, cuts and minor burns.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I go to an Emergency Department?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For serious or potentially life-threatening problems; if life is at risk, call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is GP out-of-hours walk-in?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that it normally works by phone with triage.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does an Injury Unit cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The amount currently stated by the HSE is €75, with some exemptions. Confirm before publishing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Health information does not replace a medical assessment. In an emergency, follow the HSE and call 112/999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','HSE in Ireland: When to See a GP, GP Out-of-Hours, Injury Unit or Emergency Department','Where to seek medical care in Ireland, what it costs and when to call 112 or 999.','publish','closed','closed','','hse-in-ireland-gp-out-of-hours-injury-unit-or-emergency-department','','','2026-09-25 14:46:48','2026-09-25 13:46:48','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/hse-in-ireland-gp-out-of-hours-injury-unit-or-emergency-department/',0,'guide','',0),(22377,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Find out who can apply for an EHIC in Ireland, which documents the HSE asks for, where it is valid and why it is not travel insurance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/ehic-irlanda-brasileiros-residentes/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The EHIC is useful for Brazilians living in Ireland who travel around Europe. It can give access to necessary public healthcare during temporary stays, subject to the rules of the country visited.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians living in Ireland who plan to travel to EU, EEA or Swiss countries.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confirm your eligibility: the HSE states that people living in Ireland can apply if they intend to live here for at least one year, subject to the applicable criteria.</li>\n<li>Have proof of residence and proof of your PPSN ready, where they are required.</li>\n<li>Apply online &#8211; the option the HSE describes as the fastest &#8211; or use the official postal/email channels.</li>\n<li>Each family member needs their own card where applicable.</li>\n<li>If you are travelling in less than 10 days, look at the urgent option and the Provisional Replacement Certificate.</li>\n<li>Take the card with you and do not treat the EHIC as travel insurance.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Proof of residence in Ireland.</li>\n<li>Proof of your PPSN.</li>\n<li>Personal details of the applicants and dependants.</li>\n<li>Any additional documents required by the HSE.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE provides the EHIC application service; confirm the current conditions at the time you apply.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the trip is coming up, use the online option and check the urgent procedure described by the HSE.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Thinking the EHIC covers private treatment.</li>\n<li>Thinking the EHIC covers repatriation.</li>\n<li>Using the EHIC for planned treatment.</li>\n<li>Not checking the destination&#8217;s co-payment rules.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - apply for an EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pprd2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/visitors-to-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - using the EHIC</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - apply for an EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pprd2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/visitors-to-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - using the EHIC</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can a Brazilian get an Irish EHIC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilian nationality is not the only criterion. Eligibility depends on your residence situation and the HSE rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Where is the EHIC valid?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that it is accepted in EU, EEA and Swiss countries, under the local rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the EHIC cover a private hospital?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The HSE states that it relates to necessary public care.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the EHIC replace travel insurance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The HSE expressly states that it does not replace travel insurance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for an EHIC online</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official EHIC information</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Health information does not replace a medical assessment or travel advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','EHIC in Ireland: How Resident Brazilians Can Get the European Health Insurance Card','Who can apply for an EHIC in Ireland, what the HSE asks for and where it is valid.','publish','closed','closed','','ehic-in-ireland-for-resident-brazilians','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/ehic-in-ireland-for-resident-brazilians/',0,'guide','',0),(22378,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how to use NARIC Ireland/QQI to compare a Brazilian qualification with the National Framework of Qualifications (NFQ) and what the limits of that comparison are.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reconhecer-diploma-brasileiro-na-irlanda-naric-qqi/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>People who arrive with a Brazilian degree, postgraduate degree or other qualification often need to explain the level of their diploma to employers or institutions. NARIC Ireland, operated by the QQI, provides academic guidance through comparability statements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI guidance on recognising foreign qualifications</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians with diplomas or academic qualifications obtained in Brazil who need to present their education in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Go to the NARIC Ireland Foreign Qualifications Database.</li>\n<li>Select Brazil and look for the type and title of the qualification.</li>\n<li>If it is in the database, download the Comparability Statement.</li>\n<li>Read the comparable level in the Irish NFQ and the notes in the statement.</li>\n<li>If it is not in the database, use the QQI Qualifications Recognition Advice service.</li>\n<li>Present the statement to the employer or institution when it is requested.</li>\n<li>For a regulated profession, also check the professional body: the NARIC statement is not a professional licence.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Official name of the qualification.</li>\n<li>The institution that awarded the diploma.</li>\n<li>Academic documents and translations, when required by the receiving body.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The NARIC database is provided free of charge by the QQI. Translations, certifications and professional processes may have their own costs.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no universal deadline: apply in advance if the statement is needed for an application.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Treating a comparability statement as a professional licence.</li>\n<li>Assuming every diploma will automatically be in the database.</li>\n<li>Using an old statement without checking the current version.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the regulator for a regulated profession.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI - recognition of foreign qualifications</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qsearch.qqi.ie/WebPart/Search?searchtype=recognitions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NARIC - Foreign Qualifications Database</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qhelp.qqi.ie/learners/qualifications-recognition-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI QHelp - Recognition Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI - recognition of foreign qualifications</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qsearch.qqi.ie/WebPart/Search?searchtype=recognitions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NARIC - Foreign Qualifications Database</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qhelp.qqi.ie/learners/qualifications-recognition-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI QHelp - Recognition Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does NARIC recognise my diploma for any job?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The QQI explains that the information is academic guidance and does not in itself give access to a job, a regulated profession or a course.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What if my diploma is not in the database?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can request general guidance from the QQI.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to translate my diploma?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It depends on the institution, employer or body that requests the document.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is a comparability statement mandatory?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily: it is an official tool that may be requested or useful depending on the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://qsearch.qqi.ie/WebPart/Search?searchtype=recognitions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the NARIC/QQI comparability list</a> · <a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI/NARIC - recognition of qualifications</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace academic, professional or immigration advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Get a Brazilian Diploma Recognised in Ireland: NARIC and QQI Guide','How to compare a Brazilian qualification with the Irish NFQ and what the limits are.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-recognise-a-brazilian-diploma-in-ireland-naric-and-qqi-guide','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-recognise-a-brazilian-diploma-in-ireland-naric-and-qqi-guide/',0,'guide','',0),(22379,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Learn how the TFI Leap Card works, where to use it, how to top it up, what fare caps exist and what to do if you lose the card.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/leap-card-irlanda-como-usar/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For people who use buses, the Luas, DART and other services, the TFI Leap Card is a practical way to pay for journeys and can offer lower fares than cash in many situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians living, studying or working in Ireland who use public transport regularly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Choose the type of Leap Card that suits your situation.</li>\n<li>Buy the card through an official channel and, where possible, register it.</li>\n<li>Add Travel Credit or the appropriate product.</li>\n<li>Use the card according to the service&#8217;s tap on/tap off rules.</li>\n<li>Check fares, zones and fare capping on the current TFI page.</li>\n<li>Register the card to make replacement or refunds easier where allowed.</li>\n<li>Use the app/online account to check your balance and transactions.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For a standard adult card there is normally no complex documentation.</li>\n<li>Discounted cards have specific eligibility and evidence requirements.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fares depend on the passenger, zone and service. TFI keeps the current fare tables: avoid publishing figures without checking the page on the day you publish.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no general deadline; validity rules depend on the product.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Not registering the card.</li>\n<li>Assuming every line has the same fare or capping.</li>\n<li>Confusing Adult, Young Adult and Student cards.</li>\n<li>Using old fares from third parties.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Leap Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/new-fare-zones/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Fare Zones</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/bus-fares/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Bus Fares</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Leap Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/new-fare-zones/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Fare Zones</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/bus-fares/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Bus Fares</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Where can I use the Leap Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>TFI states that it works on many services in the network, including buses, the Luas, DART and some rail services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is it worth registering?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. TFI recommends registering the card because unregistered cards cannot be replaced or refunded if lost.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is there a daily cap?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, in certain zones and services. TFI applies fare capping.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do all cards have the same discounts?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. There are different types, such as Adult, Young Adult/Student and Child, with their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.leapcard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check and order a Leap Card</a> · <a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check TFI fares and zones</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace official TFI or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Leap Card in Ireland: How to Use the Transport Card and Pay Less','How the TFI Leap Card works, where to use it, how to top it up and what to do if you lose it.','publish','closed','closed','','leap-card-in-ireland-how-to-use-it-and-pay-less','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/leap-card-in-ireland-how-to-use-it-and-pay-less/',0,'guide','',0),(22380,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how Civil Legal Aid from the Legal Aid Board works, who can apply, how the means test works and what contributions may apply.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/assistencia-juridica-legal-aid-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Legal problems are harder when you are still learning the Irish system. The Legal Aid Board provides civil legal advice and representation services for people who meet the financial criteria and the merits test.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians in Ireland with a civil legal matter who want to check access to the Legal Aid Board.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check that the matter is within the scope of Civil Legal Aid. Criminal matters are not covered by the Civil Legal Aid Act.</li>\n<li>Check your possible financial eligibility. The Board currently states disposable income below €18,000, and also takes assets and circumstances into account.</li>\n<li>Use the financial eligibility indicator as a first guide, without treating it as a guarantee.</li>\n<li>Prepare proof of income, expenses and assets.</li>\n<li>Submit the application online or go to a Law Centre.</li>\n<li>Wait for the financial and merits assessment; there may be a waiting list.</li>\n<li>Check the contribution that applies and any exemptions.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for Civil Legal Aid</a> · <a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/financial-eligibility-and-merits-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check financial eligibility</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Address and PPSN.</li>\n<li>Proof of income and benefits.</li>\n<li>Proof of expenses.</li>\n<li>Information about assets and debts.</li>\n<li>Documents relating to the legal problem.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Board currently states a minimum contribution of €30 for advice, plus contributions for representation depending on the situation. Exemptions and special rules exist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Court deadlines keep running while you seek assistance. Do not let a deadline expire while waiting for a place.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing the Legal Aid Board with free help for any issue.</li>\n<li>Not sending financial evidence.</li>\n<li>Letting a court deadline pass.</li>\n<li>Assuming the calculator guarantees approval.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Apply Now</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/financial-eligibility-and-merits-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - eligibility</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/contact-us/find-a-law-centre/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Law Centres</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/waiting-times/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - waiting times</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Apply Now</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/financial-eligibility-and-merits-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - eligibility</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/contact-us/find-a-law-centre/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Law Centres</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/waiting-times/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - waiting times</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can any Brazilian apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Nationality by itself neither guarantees nor prevents it. The type of case, your financial situation and the merits test are what matter.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the income limit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Board currently states disposable income below €18,000, plus asset criteria and the specifics of the case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is it always free?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. In many cases there is a contribution; exemptions exist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It varies by Law Centre and there may be a waiting list; the Board publishes statistics.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is not legal advice. In an urgent situation seek qualified assistance immediately.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Legal Aid in Ireland: How to Apply for Civil Legal Aid','How Civil Legal Aid works, who can apply, the means test and possible contributions.','publish','closed','closed','','legal-aid-in-ireland-how-to-apply-for-civil-legal-aid','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/legal-aid-in-ireland-how-to-apply-for-civil-legal-aid/',0,'guide','',0),(22381,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand how to register a birth in Ireland and how civil marriage works, including notice, documents, fees and the extra steps for foreign nationals.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/casamento-registro-nascimento-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Having children and getting married in Ireland involve civil registration procedures that can be different from the Brazilian ones. The General Register Office and the civil registration services hold the official information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who have children in Ireland, plan to marry in the country, or need civil registration certificates.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For a birth, register the child within the official deadline: gov.ie states a maximum of three months after the birth.</li>\n<li>Use the Civil Registration Service and check the online option available.</li>\n<li>Request the birth certificate when you need it for school, a passport or other processes.</li>\n<li>For a marriage, give at least three months notice to the civil registration service.</li>\n<li>Book the marriage notification appointment and both partners must attend.</li>\n<li>Prepare passports, birth certificates, proof of address and PPS Numbers, plus special documents where applicable.</li>\n<li>If one partner is a foreign national, check the interview, proof of immigration status, translation and interpreter requirements where they apply.</li>\n<li>After the ceremony, follow the registration procedure and request the marriage certificate if you need it.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For a birth: the information and documents requested by the Civil Registration Service.</li>\n<li>For a marriage: passport, birth certificate, proof of address, PPSN and specific documents.</li>\n<li>Translations where documents are not in English or Irish.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The marriage notification fee is a non-refundable €200. The marriage registration itself is free, but certificates have fees. Confirm the current amounts.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Birth: up to three months. Marriage: at least three months notice before the ceremony.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Leaving the birth registration too late.</li>\n<li>Booking a marriage without allowing for the three months notice.</li>\n<li>Bringing copies without the originals where they are required.</li>\n<li>Forgetting translations or the extra documentation for foreign nationals.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/register-a-birth-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Register a birth</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-married-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get married in Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/collections/all-services-available-from-the-general-register-office/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - General Register Office services</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/register-a-birth-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Register a birth</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-married-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get married in Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/collections/all-services-available-from-the-general-register-office/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - General Register Office services</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long do I have to register a birth?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>gov.ie currently states a maximum of three months after the birth.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How far in advance do I notify a marriage?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The general rule is three months notice, given in person to the civil registration service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do foreign nationals need an interview?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>gov.ie states that an interview is required when one of the partners is a foreign national, among other situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does the marriage notice cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The notification fee is currently €200 and non-refundable; registration and certificates have their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/register-a-birth-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register a birth online</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-married-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information on getting married in Ireland</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>General information; it does not replace official civil registration guidance or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Marriage and Birth Registration in Ireland: A Guide for Brazilian Families','How to register a birth and how civil marriage works, including notice and fees.','publish','closed','closed','','marriage-and-birth-registration-in-ireland-guide-for-brazilian-families','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/marriage-and-birth-registration-in-ireland-guide-for-brazilian-families/',0,'guide','',0),(22382,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Learn how to check that a financial firm is authorised in Ireland and how to complain about a bank, insurer, credit provider or other financial service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reclamar-banco-seguro-servico-financeiro-irlanda/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Problems with a bank, insurer, credit, investment or payments can be hard in another language. The Central Bank of Ireland keeps the register of authorised firms, and the FSPO offers an independent route for eligible complaints.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who use banks, insurance, credit, investments, payments or other financial services in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Before you sign up, look the firm up in the Central Bank Register of Authorised Firms.</li>\n<li>Confirm the legal name, because a firm may trade under a different name.</li>\n<li>If there is a problem, make a formal complaint directly to the firm: firms must have a complaints procedure.</li>\n<li>Keep the contract, statements, emails, letters, receipts and call logs.</li>\n<li>If the reply does not resolve it, check whether the case can go to the FSPO.</li>\n<li>Use the FSPO form and attach the relevant documents and the final response letter where applicable.</li>\n<li>If you suspect an unauthorised firm, report it to the Central Bank of Ireland.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Contract/policy.</li>\n<li>Statements and receipts.</li>\n<li>Communications with the firm.</li>\n<li>Final Response Letter where applicable.</li>\n<li>Account or policy details.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The FSPO states that its service is free. The Central Bank register is also public and free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The FSPO states that the firm can take up to 40 working days in its internal process. There are time limits for referring a case to the Ombudsman: check them before you wait.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Sending money without checking authorisation.</li>\n<li>Complaining without keeping evidence.</li>\n<li>Skipping the formal complaint to the provider.</li>\n<li>Confusing an individual complaint with a systemic regulatory report.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/contact-us/complaints-against-a-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - complaints</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://registers.centralbank.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - registers</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/consumer-hub/explainers/why-is-it-important-to-deal-with-an-authorised-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - authorised firms</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-your-provider/financial-services-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complain to your provider</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/complaint-form.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complaint form</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/contact-us/complaints-against-a-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - complaints</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://registers.centralbank.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - registers</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/consumer-hub/explainers/why-is-it-important-to-deal-with-an-authorised-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - authorised firms</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-your-provider/financial-services-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complain to your provider</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/complaint-form.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complaint form</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I check whether a firm is authorised?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Search the Register of Authorised Firms and confirm the legal name.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I complain to the Central Bank to get my money back?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Generally not. For individual complaints the normal route is the firm and then, where applicable, the FSPO.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the FSPO charge?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The FSPO states that the service is free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to keep evidence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The FSPO recommends documents such as contracts, statements, emails and letters.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-your-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Complain to your financial provider first</a> · <a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-the-fspo/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make a complaint to the FSPO</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace financial or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Complain About a Bank, Insurer or Financial Service in Ireland','Check that a firm is authorised and complain about a bank, insurer or financial service.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-complain-about-a-bank-insurer-or-financial-service-in-ireland','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-complain-about-a-bank-insurer-or-financial-service-in-ireland/',0,'guide','',0),(22383,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how to complain about a government department, local authority or public service in Ireland and when the Office of the Ombudsman can examine the complaint.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reclamar-servico-publico-irlanda-ombudsman/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If a Brazilian has a problem with an Irish public service, there is a formal route. The Office of the Ombudsman can investigate certain complaints against government departments, local authorities and the HSE.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who had a problem with a government department, local authority or the HSE and could not resolve it through the normal channel.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Identify the body that made the decision or provided the service.</li>\n<li>First complain directly to the body, explaining what happened and what you expect to be corrected.</li>\n<li>Keep references, emails, letters, names, dates and documents.</li>\n<li>If the reply does not resolve it, check whether the Ombudsman can handle that type of complaint.</li>\n<li>Submit the complaint within the applicable time limit. The Ombudsman states a general rule of 12 months, save for special circumstances.</li>\n<li>Do not use the Ombudsman for matters outside its remit, such as many complaints against private companies, banks or An Garda Síochána.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Description of the facts.</li>\n<li>The body&#8217;s response.</li>\n<li>References and case numbers.</li>\n<li>Related documents, letters and emails.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No fee is stated for making a complaint to the Ombudsman.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Published general rule: 12 months, save for very special circumstances.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Going straight to the Ombudsman without trying to resolve it with the body.</li>\n<li>Waiting more than 12 months without checking whether you can complain.</li>\n<li>Sending a complaint without a timeline and evidence.</li>\n<li>Using the Ombudsman for an FSPO or WRC matter.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/b7cef-how-to-complain-to-a-public-service-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - how to complain</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/000df-making-a-complaint/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - making a complaint</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/b7cef-how-to-complain-to-a-public-service-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - how to complain</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/000df-making-a-complaint/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - making a complaint</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I go straight to the Ombudsman?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In general, try to resolve it with the public service first.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the Ombudsman deal with private companies?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not in general: the body itself lists several categories that are outside its remit.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is there a deadline?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The published general rule is 12 months, save for very special circumstances.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is the HSE included?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The Ombudsman states that it investigates certain complaints about HSE services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/000df-making-a-complaint/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make a complaint to the Ombudsman</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Complain About a Public Service in Ireland: When to Use the Ombudsman','Complain about a government department, local authority or public service in Ireland.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-complain-about-a-public-service-in-ireland-when-to-use-the-ombudsman','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-complain-about-a-public-service-in-ireland-when-to-use-the-ombudsman/',0,'guide','',0),(22384,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> A practical checklist for Brazilians resident in Ireland before travelling: passport, IRP, destination, return and emergency documents.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/checklist-viagem-internacional-irlanda-brasileiros-irp/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide turns the official travel and return rules into a simple check before departure. It complements the guide on the IRP and travel, with a focus on planning.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians resident in Ireland who are travelling to Brazil, Europe or another country and want to reduce the risk of documentation problems.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check the validity of your passport.</li>\n<li>Check the validity of your IRP and that the details are correct.</li>\n<li>Check the entry rules of the destination on the relevant official source.</li>\n<li>Check whether the airline requires any additional documentation.</li>\n<li>Save digital copies of your passport, IRP, insurance and bookings.</li>\n<li>If the IRP is lost or you have no valid card, contact the ISD before you leave.</li>\n<li>After you return, check whether your address, passport or other details changed and need to be reported to the ISD.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Passport.</li>\n<li>IRP.</li>\n<li>Travel insurance, if purchased.</li>\n<li>Destination entry documents.</li>\n<li>Travel confirmations and emergency contacts.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no cost to do the checklist. Visa, insurance and other document costs depend on the destination.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do the check at least a few days before you travel and again on the eve of departure.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Leaving the check until the airport.</li>\n<li>Assuming the IRP replaces a destination visa.</li>\n<li>Travelling without copies of your documents.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DFA - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DFA - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the IRP replace a passport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The IRP and the passport have different functions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the IRP guarantee entry to the destination?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Check with the destination country.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What do I do if I lose the IRP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Check the ISD emergency procedure before travelling.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need travel insurance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The EHIC does not replace travel insurance: for travel, consider cover that matches the destination and your situation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules for travel and re-entry with an IRP</a> · <a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs travel advice</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Informative checklist; always confirm the official rules for your destination and situation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','First International Trip with an IRP: Documents Checklist for Brazilians','A practical checklist before travelling: passport, IRP, destination, return and emergency documents.','publish','closed','closed','','first-international-trip-with-irp-documents-checklist-for-brazilians','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/first-international-trip-with-irp-documents-checklist-for-brazilians/',0,'guide','',0),(22385,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand how to file a workplace complaint or dispute with the Workplace Relations Commission (WRC) and what to keep before starting the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reclamacao-trabalhista-wrc-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Even with a general guide on employment rights, many Brazilians need practical guidance on what to do once the problem has happened. The WRC provides an eComplaint Portal for referring certain complaints and disputes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who work or worked in Ireland and need to understand the official route for a workplace complaint.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Identify which right or obligation may have been breached and check the WRC information.</li>\n<li>Collect the contract, payslips, timesheets, messages, emails, internal policies and other documents.</li>\n<li>Where possible, try to resolve the issue with the employer and record what was requested and answered.</li>\n<li>Use the WRC eComplaint Portal when the matter falls within the WRC&#8217;s remit.</li>\n<li>Complete the complaint with facts, dates and relevant documents, without exaggerating or leaving out information.</li>\n<li>After submitting, follow the WRC communications. The case may proceed to inspection, conciliation or adjudication depending on the nature of the dispute.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Contract of employment.</li>\n<li>Payslips.</li>\n<li>Timesheets.</li>\n<li>Emails and messages.</li>\n<li>Documents or responses from the employer.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The WRC provides the eComplaint Portal: check the official page to see whether there is any specific cost for your procedure.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Complaint time limits vary with the legislation and the type of case. Do not wait until close to the limit; confirm the applicable deadline directly with the WRC.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Not keeping evidence.</li>\n<li>Missing the applicable deadline.</li>\n<li>Sending a generic complaint with no dates or facts.</li>\n<li>Confusing the WRC with the Labour Court or another body.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - Make a Complaint / Refer a Dispute</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - main website</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - Make a Complaint / Refer a Dispute</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - main website</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to complain to the employer first?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not every procedure requires exactly the same step, but trying to resolve it internally can help and should be checked according to the type of complaint.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I submit the complaint online?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The WRC provides an eComplaint Portal for the complaints and disputes it covers.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Which evidence should I keep?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The contract, payslips, timesheets, emails, messages and the employer&#8217;s responses are useful examples.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long do I have?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The time limit depends on the legislation and the type of complaint. Check with the WRC for your case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://ecomplaint.workplacerelations.ie/en-IE/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">File a complaint in the WRC eComplaint Portal</a> · <a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guidance on making a complaint</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to File and Track a Workplace Complaint with the WRC','How to file a complaint or dispute with the Workplace Relations Commission and what to keep.','publish','closed','closed','','how-to-file-and-track-a-workplace-complaint-with-the-wrc','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/how-to-file-and-track-a-workplace-complaint-with-the-wrc/',0,'guide','',0),(22386,283039560,'2026-08-28 00:00:00','2026-08-28 00:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Raising a child on your own is already a big challenge. For anyone living in Ireland as an immigrant, understanding which benefits, payments and supports may be available can be even more complicated.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ireland has different schemes for families with children, but <strong>there is no single &#8220;single mother&#8221; or &#8220;single father&#8221; benefit that automatically entitles everyone to all the supports</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are the main benefits for lone parents?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>One-Parent Family Payment (OFP)</li>\n<li>Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment (JST)</li>\n<li>Child Benefit</li>\n<li>Working Family Payment (WFP)</li>\n<li>Single Person Child Carer Credit (SPCCC)</li>\n<li>Domiciliary Care Allowance, where the child meets the criteria</li>\n<li>Carer&#8217;s Allowance or Carer&#8217;s Benefit in certain situations</li>\n<li>housing and income supports, depending on your financial situation</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/en/explore-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Explore and start other payments on MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>1. One-Parent Family Payment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>One-Parent Family Payment (OFP)</strong> is one of the main payments for people raising children without the support of a partner. It is generally a payment subject to a <strong>means test</strong>, where certain income and resources are assessed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The rules depend on your individual situation and include requirements linked to the child&#8217;s age, income and family situation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/one-parent-family-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the One-Parent Family Payment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I work and still receive the OFP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The fact that you work does not necessarily mean the payment is automatically cancelled. The OFP is subject to an income assessment under the scheme rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What about child maintenance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The rules on child maintenance have changed. Do not use old information found on blogs or videos without checking the current rule directly with the Department of Social Protection.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>2. Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment (JST)</strong> was created to help certain lone parents who stop receiving the One-Parent Family Payment because their youngest child reaches the OFP age limit. The JST has its own eligibility rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/jobseekers-transitional-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>3. Child Benefit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Child Benefit</strong> is different from the OFP. It is a payment linked to the child and can be received by families who meet the scheme criteria. A lone parent should check Child Benefit even if they are not entitled to the OFP.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/topics/parents-children-family/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for Child Benefit</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check Child Benefit</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>4. Working Family Payment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you work but your family income is relatively low, the <strong>Working Family Payment (WFP)</strong> is worth looking at. The scheme is for workers with children whose family income is below the limit that applies to their family size.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A common mistake is to think: &#8220;I work, so I am not entitled to any benefit.&#8221; That is not necessarily true.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/en/topics/parents-children-family/working-family-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for the Working Family Payment</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/WFP\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check and apply for the Working Family Payment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>5. Single Person Child Carer Credit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Single Person Child Carer Credit (SPCCC)</strong> is a tax credit administered by Revenue. For 2026 the credit is <strong>€1,900 per year</strong>. Anyone who qualifies may also have an additional band of income taxed at 20%, under Revenue&#8217;s rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/personal-tax-credits-reliefs-and-exemptions/children/single-person-child-carer-credit/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check and claim the SPCCC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Who can apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You must meet Revenue&#8217;s conditions to be considered eligible and have a qualifying child. There are specific rules for separated and widowed people and for those who share the care of the child.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are PAYE you can claim through Revenue myAccount, under <em>Manage your tax for the current year</em>, <em>PAYE Services</em>, <em>Claim tax credits</em> and <em>You and your family</em>. If you are self-employed, follow Revenue&#8217;s instructions for ROS.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>6. Domiciliary Care Allowance</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If your child has a disability and needs significantly more care and attention than a child of the same age would normally need, another important payment may apply: <strong>Domiciliary Care Allowance (DCA)</strong>. This payment is not exclusive to lone parents and has its own criteria.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>7. Carer&#8217;s Allowance and Carer&#8217;s Benefit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have to stop working or significantly reduce your hours to care for someone who needs full-time care, there may be carer-related payments. They are not exclusive to lone parents and have their own requirements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>8. Support to return to work or start a business</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Back to Work Enterprise Allowance (BTWEA)</strong> may be relevant for some people who receive certain social payments and want to start a business. There are eligibility requirements, benefit duration rules and prior approval of the business.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>9. Housing supports</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Depending on your income and family situation, it may be worth checking the Housing Assistance Payment (HAP), social housing and other housing-related supports. There is no general rule saying that all lone parents get automatic priority in any housing scheme.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>10. If I am unemployed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are a lone parent who has lost their job, the options may depend on the child&#8217;s age, PRSI record, income and other circumstances. Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment, Jobseeker&#8217;s Allowance, Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit or other payments may be available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What should a Brazilian have ready?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>PPS Number</li>\n<li>identity document</li>\n<li>proof of address</li>\n<li>bank details</li>\n<li>the child&#8217;s documents and PPS Number</li>\n<li>salary and payslips</li>\n<li>information about other income</li>\n<li>information about custody, separation or divorce, where relevant</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Checklist: am I a lone parent in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Do I have a PPS Number?</li>\n<li>Does my child have a PPS Number?</li>\n<li>Am I receiving Child Benefit?</li>\n<li>Can I apply for the One-Parent Family Payment?</li>\n<li>Should I check the Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment?</li>\n<li>Could my income qualify me for the Working Family Payment?</li>\n<li>Can I claim the Single Person Child Carer Credit?</li>\n<li>Does my child have needs that may qualify for the Domiciliary Care Allowance?</li>\n<li>Do I need to check Carer&#8217;s Allowance or Carer&#8217;s Benefit?</li>\n<li>Am I entitled to any housing support?</li>\n<li>Is my family and tax situation up to date?</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is there a specific single mother benefit in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The One-Parent Family Payment is one of the main payments for people raising children without the support of a partner, but it is not the only support available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can a single father also receive it?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The schemes are not exclusively for women. Eligibility depends on the rules of each payment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I work and receive benefits?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In some cases, yes. How income is treated varies by payment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the SPCCC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the Single Person Child Carer Credit, a tax credit administered by Revenue. In 2026 the credit is €1,900.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What if I share custody - can I receive the SPCCC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There may be a possibility of a secondary claimant, but there are specific rules about the days the child lives with each parent and about transferring the credit.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to report a change in my family situation?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Changes such as marriage, reconciliation or living together can affect certain payments and tax credits. Check and report to the responsible body when needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Social Protection</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/birth-family-relationships/parenting-alone/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Parenting alone</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/personal-tax-credits-reliefs-and-exemptions/children/single-person-child-carer-credit/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Single Person Child Carer Credit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/personal-tax-credits-reliefs-and-exemptions/tax-relief-charts/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Tax rates, bands and reliefs</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 28 August 2026.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Social welfare payments and tax credits can change with the Budget, new laws, changes in rates, income limits, residence rules and eligibility criteria. Always confirm the information directly with the Department of Social Protection, Revenue or Citizens Information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><em>Notice: This guide is for information only. Social welfare payments, taxes and eligibility criteria depend on individual circumstances. This information does not constitute personalised legal, tax or financial advice.</em></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Benefits for Lone Parents in Ireland: Payments, Tax and Other Supports','Which payments, tax credits and supports may exist for lone parents in Ireland.','publish','closed','closed','','benefits-for-lone-parents-in-ireland-payments-tax-and-other-supports','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/benefits-for-lone-parents-in-ireland-payments-tax-and-other-supports/',0,'guide','',0),(22387,283039560,'2026-08-29 00:00:00','2026-08-29 00:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are living with domestic violence in Ireland, <strong>you do not have to face it alone</strong>. Public services, specialist organisations, legal support, police protection and court measures are available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Domestic violence is not only physical assault. It can also involve threats, control, sexual violence, emotional abuse, financial control, digital abuse and coercive behaviour.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>If you are in danger now</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you or someone else is in <strong>immediate danger</strong>, call <strong>999 or 112</strong> and ask An Garda Síochána for help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can also go to your nearest Garda Station. If you do not speak English well, that should not stop you from asking for help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is domestic violence?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>physical assault;</li>\n<li>threats or intimidation;</li>\n<li>sexual violence or coercion;</li>\n<li>psychological or emotional control;</li>\n<li>financial control;</li>\n<li>control through technology or social media;</li>\n<li>stalking or unwanted contact;</li>\n<li>coercive or controlling behaviour;</li>\n<li>threats against children or family members;</li>\n<li>destruction of property.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Courts Service explains that domestic violence can be carried out by current or former partners, spouses, civil partners, people with whom you have had an intimate relationship, the other parent of your children and certain family members.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/apply-for-a-domestic-violence-court-order\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for a domestic violence protection order</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to find help in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Women&#8217;s Aid</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Women&#8217;s Aid runs a free, confidential national helpline for women experiencing domestic violence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>1800 341 900 &#8212; 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Men&#8217;s Aid</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Men can also be victims of domestic violence. The HSE lists Men&#8217;s Aid among the national support services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>01 554 3811</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>National Male Advice Line</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also lists the National Male Advice Line as a support service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>1800 816 588</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>HSE</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE holds information on domestic, sexual and gender-based violence services and lists national and local services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Do I need to report to the Garda immediately?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If there is immediate danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>. In other situations you can go to your local Garda Station to explain what happened, ask for guidance or make a report.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can also contact specialist services before deciding whether to report. The important thing is to find a safe way to get guidance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/crime/domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Report to / get help from the Garda</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Make a safety plan</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If it is safe to do so, consider preparing a plan for a possible emergency.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>choose someone you trust;</li>\n<li>identify a safe place where you can stay;</li>\n<li>keep important documents accessible;</li>\n<li>have in mind how to ask for help in an emergency;</li>\n<li>if it is safe, prepare a bag with documents, keys, money, clothes and anything the children need;</li>\n<li>be careful when researching help if your phone or computer is being monitored.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Do not do anything that increases your risk.</strong> If preparing documents or a bag could put you in danger, put your safety first.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Can I get a court order against the perpetrator?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Ireland has different types of Domestic Violence Orders:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Protection Order;</li>\n<li>Safety Order;</li>\n<li>Barring Order;</li>\n<li>Interim Barring Order;</li>\n<li>Emergency Barring Order.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Protection Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is a short-term protective measure. It can order the person to stop perpetrating violence or making threats. If the perpetrator lives with you, a Protection Order does not automatically order them to leave the home.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Safety Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is a longer-term protective order. It can prevent certain acts of violence, threats, stalking or contact. If the perpetrator lives with you, a Safety Order does not order them to leave the house. A Safety Order granted by the District Court can last up to <strong>5 years</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Barring Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A Barring Order can order the perpetrator to leave or not enter the home, and can impose other restrictions relating to violence, threats, stalking or contact. A Barring Order granted by the District Court can last up to <strong>3 years</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Interim Barring Order and Emergency Barring Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>These are emergency protective measures. Whether each one can be applied for depends on the relationship between the people and on the rights to the home. The Courts Service explains the specific criteria for each order.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How do I apply for a Domestic Violence Order?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Complete the Application for a Domestic Violence Order.</li>\n<li>Submit the application at the appropriate Court Office, normally the District Court.</li>\n<li>The Court Office will arrange the hearing before a judge.</li>\n<li>The Courts Service or the Gardaí will notify the respondent in line with the procedure.</li>\n<li>You may attend the full hearing so that the judge can decide on long-term protection.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have dependent children you want included in the protection, they must be included in the application.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Courts Service currently provides the official Application for a Domestic Violence Order form and advises that the correct version is used before filing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/crime/domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Garda - Domestic Abuse</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/domestic-sexual-gender-based-violence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Domestic, Sexual and Gender-Based Violence</a> · <a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/hubs/domestic-violence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Domestic Violence</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/my-situation-has-changed-since-i-arrived-in-ireland/immigration-guidelines-for-victims-of-domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service - Victims of Domestic Abuse</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What if the perpetrator breaches the order?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Breaching a Domestic Violence Order is a <strong>crime</strong>. If it happens, contact the Gardaí immediately. If there is immediate danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What if I cannot afford a lawyer?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Legal Aid Board</strong> provides legal advice and mediation services subject to the applicable criteria. The Government also advises victims of domestic violence to contact the Legal Aid Board for legal assistance information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>I am Brazilian. What if my immigration status is linked to my partner?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If your Irish immigration permission is linked to the partner perpetrating the violence, <strong>do not assume you have to stay in the relationship to keep your immigration status</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Department of Justice has a procedure for victims and survivors of domestic violence whose permission is linked to the perpetrator to apply for an <strong>independent immigration permission</strong>, where the applicable criteria are met.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Government has also announced a waiver of the immigration registration fee for people who receive this independent permission on domestic violence grounds.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Everyone&#8217;s immigration situation is different. If you hold another type of Stamp, are in an international protection process, hold European citizenship, have children or other specific circumstances, seek proper advice before making an important immigration decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What if I have children?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If children are involved, include their safety in your plan. Depending on the court order and the situation, dependent children can be included in the protection.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If a child is in immediate danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>. You may also need advice from Tusla, the Child and Family Agency, where there are concerns about a child&#8217;s safety or welfare.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Financial and digital abuse</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Domestic violence can include control over money, accounts, transport or other resources. It can also involve the use of technology to control, stalk or intimidate someone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you suspect your phone or computer is being monitored, be careful when researching help or installing apps. Where possible, use a secure device or ask someone you trust for help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What to do in the next 24 hours?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>If there is immediate danger, call 999 or 112.</li>\n<li>If there is no immediate danger, reach out to someone you trust.</li>\n<li>Contact a specialist service.</li>\n<li>Consider making a safety plan.</li>\n<li>Seek advice about your children, housing and immigration situation.</li>\n<li>Consider speaking to the Garda.</li>\n<li>Seek legal advice if you need a court order.</li>\n<li>Do not confront the perpetrator if that could increase the risk.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important phone numbers</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Contact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Emergency / immediate danger</td><td><strong>999 or 112</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Women&#8217;s Aid</td><td><strong>1800 341 900 &#8212; 24h</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Men&#8217;s Aid</td><td><strong>01 554 3811</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>National Male Advice Line</td><td><strong>1800 816 588</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Legal Aid Board &#8212; domestic violence emergency in Dublin</td><td><strong>01 675 5566</strong></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful official links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful official links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/crime/domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Garda Síochána - Domestic Abuse</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/policy-information/domestic-violence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice - Domestic Violence</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/domestic-sexual-gender-based-violence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Domestic, Sexual and Gender-Based Violence Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/hubs/domestic-violence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Domestic Violence</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/apply-for-a-domestic-violence-court-order\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Apply for a Domestic Violence Court Order</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/court-forms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Court Forms</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/my-situation-has-changed-since-i-arrived-in-ireland/immigration-guidelines-for-victims-of-domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service - Victims of Domestic Abuse</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/court-forms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the Courts Service forms</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I call the Garda even if I have no evidence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can go to the Garda and explain what is happening. The Garda can assess the situation, investigate possible crimes and advise on protective measures and services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to leave home before seeking help?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. You can seek advice and make a safety plan before deciding, as long as it is safe to do so.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I get a court order even if I am not divorced?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Domestic Violence Orders depend on the relationship between the people and on the applicable legal criteria. Being divorced is not a general requirement for every form of protection.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>My partner is responsible for my visa. Do I have to stay with them?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. There are specific procedures for certain victims and survivors whose immigration permission is linked to the perpetrator to apply for an independent permission.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>I am a man. Can I seek help?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. There are specific male support services, including Men&#8217;s Aid and the National Male Advice Line.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>I do not speak English. Can I ask for help?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. When you contact the Garda, say that you need help with the language. The Garda has arrangements to make communication easier for people whose first language is not English.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>One last important thing</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>The violence is not your fault.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You are not responsible for another person&#8217;s abusive behaviour. Nor do you have to make every decision all at once.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are in danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>. If you are not in immediate danger, contact a specialist service and talk to someone you trust.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 29 August 2026.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Phone numbers, services, forms, immigration procedures, fees and criteria can change. Always confirm the details directly on the official sources before acting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><em>Notice: This guide is for information only and does not constitute personalised legal, immigration or safety advice. In a situation of immediate danger, call 999 or 112.</em></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Domestic Violence in Ireland: Where to Find Help, How to Report and Your Rights','Where to seek help, how to contact the Garda, protective orders and immigration rights.','publish','closed','closed','','domestic-violence-in-ireland-where-to-find-help-report-and-your-rights','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/domestic-violence-in-ireland-where-to-find-help-report-and-your-rights/',0,'guide','',0),(22388,283039560,'2026-08-31 20:54:00','2026-08-31 19:54:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Winter in Ireland can be challenging. The days get shorter, there is less natural light, temperatures drop and many people spend more time indoors. For anyone living far from family and home country, this period can also bring longing, loneliness and isolation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For some people, the seasonal changes can significantly affect their mental health. This guide explains how to recognise the signs of seasonal depression, when to seek help and where to find support in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🚨 If you are at immediate risk</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you or someone else is at <strong>immediate risk</strong>, do not wait for an online answer. Call <strong>112 or 999</strong> or go to a hospital <strong>Emergency Department</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also advises seeing a GP or an out-of-hours GP service when needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🌧️ Can winter affect our mental health?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Some people experience a type of depression linked to the changes of the seasons called <strong>Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</strong>, known in Portuguese as seasonal depression or winter depression.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>According to the HSE, symptoms usually start in autumn or winter and tend to improve during spring. For some people they are mild; for others they can significantly interfere with everyday life.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Possible signs</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>persistent sadness or low mood;</li>\n<li>loss of interest or pleasure in activities;</li>\n<li>irritability;</li>\n<li>tiredness and lack of energy;</li>\n<li>anxiety or stress;</li>\n<li>feelings of guilt, worthlessness or hopelessness;</li>\n<li>frequent crying;</li>\n<li>low self-esteem;</li>\n<li>social isolation;</li>\n<li>changes in sleep;</li>\n<li>loss of interest in usual activities;</li>\n<li>thoughts about death or suicide.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Having some of these symptoms does not automatically mean a person has SAD or depression. Persistent or significant changes in mood and behaviour deserve attention.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If symptoms of depression are present most of the day, every day, for more than two weeks, the HSE recommends talking to a GP.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🇧🇷 And for those living far from home?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For many immigrants, winter brings feelings that go beyond the weather: missing family, friends, food, sunshine and the feeling of being at home.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is possible to love the life you have built in Ireland and still miss the life you left behind. Someone can be working, smiling and looking after everyone and still not be emotionally well.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Not all suffering is visible.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💭 &#8220;I&#8217;m fine.&#8221;</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Sometimes the person who says &#8220;I&#8217;m fine&#8221; is just trying to get through the day. One simple question can make a difference: <strong>&#8220;How are you, really?&#8221;</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>And, most importantly: be ready to hear the answer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🫂 How to help someone who is not well?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>❤️ Ask how they are.</li>\n<li>❤️ Listen without judgement.</li>\n<li>❤️ Show them they are not alone.</li>\n<li>❤️ Avoid phrases that minimise their suffering.</li>\n<li>❤️ Encourage them to seek professional help.</li>\n<li>❤️ Stay in contact, especially if they are isolating themselves.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You do not need to be a psychologist to show concern. A conversation can be the first step for someone to accept seeking help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also advises that support services can be contacted when we are worried about someone else, not only when we are going through a personal crisis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🚨 And when someone talks about suicide?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Take it seriously.</strong> If someone says they do not want to live any more, that they cannot take it any more, or shows suicidal thoughts, do not ignore it and do not simply try to convince them to &#8220;think positively&#8221;.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Listen.</li>\n<li>Stay close, when it is safe to do so.</li>\n<li>Encourage them to seek help.</li>\n<li>Use the services available.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE advises that when someone is thinking about suicide it is important to tell someone and to seek support. The person does not have to face those feelings alone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💛 Where to get help in Ireland</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Contact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>🚨 Emergency</td><td><strong>112 or 999</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>💛 Samaritans</td><td><strong>116 123 &#8212; 24/7</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>💛 Pieta</td><td><strong>1800 247 247 &#8212; 24/7</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>💬 Text About It</td><td><strong>HELLO to 50808 &#8212; 24/7</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>🩺 HSE Mental Health Information Line</td><td><strong>1800 111 888</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>👨‍⚕️ Medical care</td><td><strong>GP / GP out of hours / Emergency Department</strong></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>📞 Samaritans</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A confidential, non-judgemental emotional support service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>116 123</strong> &#8212; available 24 hours a day, every day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Email: <a href=\"mailto:jo@samaritans.ie\">jo@samaritans.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.samaritans.org/samaritans-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Samaritans Ireland - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.samaritans.org/samaritans-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">24/7 emotional support - Samaritans Ireland</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>💛 Pieta</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pieta supports people who are experiencing suicidal thoughts or self-harm, and people affected by someone else&#8217;s suicide.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Crisis Helpline: 1800 247 247 &#8212; 24 hours.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Text support: send HELP to 51444.</strong> The message may be charged according to your plan.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pieta also offers therapy for people affected by suicidal thoughts, self-harm or bereavement by suicide. The service can be reached on <strong>0818 111 126</strong>, subject to the criteria and services available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.pieta.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pieta - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.pieta.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Crisis support - Pieta</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>💬 Text About It</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For anyone who is not comfortable talking on the phone, there is a text-based alternative.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Send HELLO to 50808.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is a free text conversation service, available 24 hours a day, offering emotional support and help in crisis situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://textaboutit.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Text About It - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://textaboutit.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Text About It</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🩺 Talk to your GP</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you notice that your mood has changed, that you are constantly without energy, have lost interest in things, are isolating yourself or feel you are not coping, talk to your <strong>GP (General Practitioner)</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You do not have to wait until a crisis to seek help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE provides information on mental health services and can point you to where to find support.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>HSE Mental Health Information Line</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>1800 111 888</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is an information line about mental health services. It does not replace an emergency service or clinical advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/your-mental-health-information-line/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Your Mental Health Information Line</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/seasonal-affective-disorder/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE information on Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/suicide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Help for thoughts about suicide</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/get-urgent-help/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Get urgent help for a mental health crisis</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/your-mental-health-information-line/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Your Mental Health Information Line</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🌍 Do you not speak perfect English?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do not let the language barrier stop you from seeking help. When contacting a health or emergency service, explain that you need help communicating and ask about the available options.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🌱 Looking after your mental health is also prevention</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Prevention starts before a crisis: when we ask how someone is, we notice changes in behaviour and stop treating emotional suffering as weakness.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Seeking a GP, a psychologist or a support service does not mean someone has failed. Asking for help can be an important step in looking after yourself.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💛 You do not have to get through the winter alone</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the days are hard, reach out to someone. Talk. Ask for help. Listen to whoever needs it. Stay close to the people who make you feel looked after.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Winter passes.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You do not have to solve everything today. Sometimes it is enough to take the next step.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Talk. Listen. Hold space. Seek help.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💛 A message from Conexão BR Ireland</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We are a community of Brazilians living in Ireland. And we believe that connection also means looking after one another.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If this article made you think of someone, <strong>send it to them</strong>. Maybe they need exactly this information today.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>You are not alone. 🇧🇷🇮🇪💛</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Sources and official information</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/seasonal-affective-disorder/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/suicide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Help for thoughts about suicide</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/get-urgent-help/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Get urgent help for a mental health crisis</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/your-mental-health-information-line/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Your Mental Health Information Line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.samaritans.org/samaritans-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Samaritans Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.pieta.ie/how-we-can-help/helpline/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pieta - Crisis Helpline</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://textaboutit.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Text About It</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 31 August 2026.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Phone numbers, services, opening hours and ways of accessing them can change. Always confirm the contacts directly on the official pages before acting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><em>Notice: This guide is for information only and does not replace a professional assessment, diagnosis or treatment. In an emergency or a situation of immediate risk, call 112 or 999 or go to a hospital.</em></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','🌧️ Winter in Ireland and Mental Health: Seasonal Depression, Loneliness and Where to Find Help','Recognise the signs of seasonal depression, when to seek help and which services are available in Ireland.','publish','closed','closed','','winter-in-ireland-and-mental-health-seasonal-depression-loneliness-and-where-to-find-help','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/winter-in-ireland-and-mental-health-seasonal-depression-loneliness-and-where-to-find-help/',0,'guide','',0),(22389,283039560,'2026-08-19 00:00:00','2026-08-19 00:00:00','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism in Ireland: diagnosis, the HSE and where to find support</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For Brazilians living in Ireland who are autistic &#8212; or who have a child who may be autistic &#8212; it is important to understand how the Irish health system works. The HSE provides information and different kinds of autism-related support, but the route depends on the person&#8217;s age, their needs and the services available in the area where they live.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow\"><p><strong>Important:</strong> this guide is for information and does not replace a medical, psychological or individual professional assessment.</p></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/assessment-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE information on autism assessment and support</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/where-to-get-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find HSE autism-related support</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Autism</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is this guide for?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>autistic adults;</li>\n<li>parents or carers of autistic children;</li>\n<li>adults who suspect they may be autistic;</li>\n<li>families moving to Ireland;</li>\n<li>Brazilians who already have a diagnosis from Brazil and want to understand which supports they can seek in Ireland.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How does autism assessment work in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The route varies by age. For children, the HSE describes different assessment and support pathways through health and disability services. For adults, the current HSE information is different: the HSE states that it does not provide adult autism diagnostic assessment, and advises anyone seeking an assessment to consider a private assessment with a psychologist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Check the official HSE page on <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/assessment-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">autism assessment</a> before starting the process, because services, referrals and availability can change.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>If the person is a child</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you believe your child may be autistic, the HSE recommends speaking to a health or education professional. Depending on the situation, the route may involve the GP, the Public Health Nurse, the Primary Care Team, the Children&#8217;s Disability Network Team (CDNT) or the school.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>book an appointment with the GP, or speak to the health professional who already looks after the child;</li>\n<li>explain which behaviours, communication needs, learning difficulties or sensory issues led you to seek help;</li>\n<li>ask which local service is the right one;</li>\n<li>keep relevant reports and documentation ready to show professionals.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>If the child already has a diagnosis in Brazil</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A diagnosis carried out in Brazil can help explain the child&#8217;s history to the professionals who will support them in Ireland. Bring diagnostic reports, school reports, therapy information and other relevant documents.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>diagnostic report;</li>\n<li>reports from psychologists, therapists or doctors;</li>\n<li>school reports;</li>\n<li>information about therapies carried out;</li>\n<li>a list of medication, where applicable;</li>\n<li>a description of communication and sensory needs.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the documents are in Portuguese, check in advance with the service receiving them whether a translation will be required. Do not assume a certified translation is mandatory: the requirement depends on the service and the purpose of the documents.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Adults who suspect they are autistic</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE currently states that it does not provide adult autism diagnostic assessment. The official advice is to seek a private assessment. The HSE also points to professional organisations such as the Psychological Society of Ireland (PSI) as a reference for finding professionals.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Before booking, confirm directly with the professional what type of assessment is offered, the cost, the timeframe and which documents will be needed. <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/assessment-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - adult autism assessment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Is it possible to get support without a diagnosis?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that a person does not necessarily have to wait for a formal diagnosis to access certain services. A health professional can advise on the most suitable service for the needs presented.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This can be especially relevant for families waiting for an assessment. See <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/where-to-get-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - where to get support</a> and talk to the GP or another professional who supports the person.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What kinds of support may be available?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Support depends on individual needs. The HSE describes different services and professionals who may be involved, including psychologists, psychiatrists, occupational therapists, speech and language therapists, disability services and mental health services where needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also points to organisations such as AsIAm and the Irish Society for Autism as sources of information and support. <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/where-to-get-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - where to get support</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Benefits and social supports</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Depending on the person&#8217;s and the family&#8217;s situation, different social welfare payments may exist. The HSE mentions, among others, Carer&#8217;s Allowance, Carer&#8217;s Benefit, Carer&#8217;s Support Grant, Domiciliary Care Allowance and Disability Allowance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Being autistic does not automatically mean you are entitled to a payment. Each scheme has its own criteria. To check eligibility, contact the Department of Social Protection and Citizens Information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism and education</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For children, talk to the school about individual communication, learning, routine and sensory needs. For third-level education, the HSE also points to DARE as a possible support route for eligible students.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Transitioning from children&#8217;s services to adult services</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The transition should be planned well in advance. The HSE recommends starting to prepare the move to adult services at around 14 or 15 years old where possible. More recent HSE information also indicates that, in some disability services, the transition can begin about 18 months before the young person leaves school.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>See <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/changing-from-child-to-adult-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - changing from child to adult care</a> and <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/babies-children/disabilities/services/moving-from-child-to-adult-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - moving from child to adult services</a>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Organisations that can help</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Autism</a> &#8212; official health information.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://asiam.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AsIAm</a> &#8212; an Irish organisation dedicated to autism.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://autism.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Society for Autism</a> &#8212; autism-related information and resources.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information</a> &#8212; information on public services and benefits.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Social Protection</a> &#8212; social welfare payments and social protection.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>I have an autism diagnosis from Brazil. Do I need another diagnosis in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is not possible to say that every Brazilian diagnosis will have to be repeated. The need depends on the service and the purpose. Bring your reports and confirm directly with the Irish service which documents are needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can adults be assessed by the HSE?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE currently states that it does not provide adult autism diagnostic assessment, and advises seeking a private assessment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>My child is waiting for an assessment. Can they get any support?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that certain services can be accessed without waiting for a formal diagnosis, depending on needs. Talk to the GP or another professional to identify the right route.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does a diagnosis automatically give entitlement to a social welfare payment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Each payment has its own criteria and must be assessed individually.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 19 August 2026.</strong> We recommend checking the official pages before making decisions, as service availability, criteria, procedures and contacts can change.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Notice:</strong> this guide is for information only. It does not constitute personalised medical, legal, financial or professional advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Autism in Ireland: Diagnosis, HSE and Where to Find Support','How autism assessment works in Ireland for children and adults, and what support exists.','publish','closed','closed','','autism-in-ireland-diagnosis-hse-and-where-to-find-support','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/autism-in-ireland-diagnosis-hse-and-where-to-find-support/',0,'guide','',0),(22390,283039560,'2026-08-19 00:00:00','2026-08-19 00:00:00','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism and air travel in Ireland: a guide for Dublin and Cork Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Flying can be particularly challenging for some autistic people because of queues, noise, lighting, changes of routine, security, waiting, communication and unfamiliar environments.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport and Cork Airport offer specific resources for passengers with autism and other non-visible disabilities, and some airlines also have preparation programmes and materials.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow\"><p><strong>Important:</strong> airport and airline services, requirements and procedures can change. Always confirm the information directly with the airport and the airline before each trip.</p></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Accessibility/autism at Dublin Airport</a> · <a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Autism support at Cork Airport</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is this guide for?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>autistic adults;</li>\n<li>parents of autistic children;</li>\n<li>Brazilians travelling between Ireland and Brazil;</li>\n<li>families who have never flown with an autistic person;</li>\n<li>passengers with other non-visible disabilities;</li>\n<li>people who may need more time or a different way of communicating during the journey.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>First of all: tell the airline</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>One of the most important recommendations is to contact the airline before the trip. <a href=\"https://www.aerlingus.com/support/special-assistance/needs-relating-to-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Aer Lingus</a> has a specific area for needs relating to autism and provides an assistance request form.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the flight is not operated by Aer Lingus, do not assume the same services will be available. Check directly with the airline operating the flight.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Prepare the person before the trip</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Making the process predictable can help. Cork Airport recommends preparing the person well in advance using visual resources, photographs and explanations of each step of the journey.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>leaving home;</li>\n<li>arriving at the airport;</li>\n<li>checking in;</li>\n<li>handing over the bag;</li>\n<li>going through security;</li>\n<li>finding the gate;</li>\n<li>waiting;</li>\n<li>boarding;</li>\n<li>sitting on the plane;</li>\n<li>taking off;</li>\n<li>arriving at the destination;</li>\n<li>leaving the airport.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Visit the airport before the trip</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If possible, getting to know the airport before the day of the flight can help. <a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport</a> recommends a prior visit to familiarise the person with the environment, including the movement and the noise.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Use visual resources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Photographs and symbols can help explain what will happen. Cork Airport recommends visual guides for situations such as check-in, security, queues, the gate, boarding and waiting. Aer Lingus also offers visual guides for the different stages of the journey.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important Flyer &#8212; Dublin Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport</a> has a programme called <strong>Important Flyer</strong> for passengers with autism or a Sensory Processing Disorder. The identifier can be a lanyard or a wristband, and it discreetly signals to staff that the passenger may need more time, clear communication, a calmer environment or additional understanding.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How to apply</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>complete the Important Flyer form;</li>\n<li>attach a one-page letter from a GP or an AsIAm card confirming an autism or SPD diagnosis;</li>\n<li>make a separate application for each person who needs the identifier;</li>\n<li>provide the Eircode.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport states that Important Flyer delivery is available to postal addresses in Ireland, including Northern Ireland, and that there is currently no airport collection option.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important Flyer and Fast Track</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport states that Fast Track must be booked in advance. Up to four Fast Track passes can be free when the programme conditions are met. The Important Flyer holder must be travelling; the airport states that up to three companions may travel with the holder using this benefit on the same day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Do not confuse:</strong> having an autism card is not the same as taking part in the Important Flyer programme. You have to follow the airport&#8217;s procedure and make the booking where it applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Sunflower at Dublin Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport</a> takes part in the <strong>Hidden Disabilities Sunflower</strong> scheme. The airport states that its staff are trained to recognise the symbol and to offer additional support, understanding or more time when needed. The airport advises passengers to use the PRM Assist App to request Sunflower use.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://hdsunflower.com/uk/insights/post/airports-around-the-world\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hidden Disabilities Sunflower</a> keeps an international list of participating airports, but the kind of support varies by airport.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Autism &amp; non-visible disabilities</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Sensory room at Dublin Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/sensory-room\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Sensory Room</a> has Sensory Rooms in both terminals for passengers who may become overwhelmed by the airport environment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>the sessions are free;</li>\n<li>each session lasts 60 minutes;</li>\n<li>there is no walk-in service;</li>\n<li>the rooms are after security;</li>\n<li>there is one room in Terminal 1, near the Gates 200;</li>\n<li>there is one room in Terminal 2, near the Gates 400.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The room must be booked in advance. If you have already booked assistance, an OCS agent can help during security and accompany the passenger to the room and then to the gate.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Cork Airport: Sunflower Lanyard</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport - Autism / ASD</a> offers support for passengers with non-visible disabilities. The airport states that passengers with autism can use a <strong>Sunflower Lanyard</strong>, available free of charge at the OCS desk in the terminal.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport - Autism/ASD support</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Cork Airport: Sensory Pod</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cork Airport has a sensory pod for passengers with non-visible disabilities. The space is free, must be booked, and it is recommended to request it in advance; the airport recommends around 48 hours where possible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Assistance to move through the airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the person needs help to move through the airport, request assistance in advance. At Cork Airport the service can help from arrival at the airport to boarding. The airport recommends booking through the airline or the responsible agent, because asking for assistance only on arrival can mean waiting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Identification and assistance are not necessarily the same thing.</strong> A Sunflower lanyard does not replace an assistance booking.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.aerlingus.com/support/special-assistance/needs-relating-to-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Request special assistance with Aer Lingus</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism ID cards</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There are different identification cards available in Ireland and they are not equivalent.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>AsIAm Autism ID Card</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://asiam.ie/what-we-do/asiam-autism-id-card\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AsIAm</a> offers an Autism ID Card. The organisation currently states a cost of €27.50, inclusion of a lanyard and holder, processing of up to eight weeks, validity of three years for children and five years for adults, plus formal proof of diagnosis and a photograph.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>AsIAm itself explains that the card serves as proof of diagnosis and can be shown to service providers, but it does not automatically guarantee discounts, preferential treatment or benefits. Any support depends on the service used.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>ASD Ireland ID Card</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.asdireland.ie/resources/idcard/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ASD Ireland</a> also has its own card. The organisation currently states a cost of €20 and validity of two years, and requires clinical documentation proving the diagnosis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This card is an ASD Ireland programme of its own and should not be confused with the AsIAm card or the Dublin Airport Important Flyer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What to put in the bag?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>headphones;</li>\n<li>ear defenders;</li>\n<li>a toy or regulating object;</li>\n<li>a tablet or entertainment device;</li>\n<li>a charger or power bank, where allowed;</li>\n<li>suitable food;</li>\n<li>any medication needed;</li>\n<li>documents;</li>\n<li>identification cards;</li>\n<li>visual resources;</li>\n<li>contact details.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>And medication?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the person takes medication or has specific dietary needs, check the rules of the airline, the airport and the destination country well in advance. Cork Airport states that, in certain situations, a letter from a GP can help explain needs related to medication or diet during the trip.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This does not replace airport security rules or immigration/customs rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>And if there is a meltdown or sensory overload?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>move to a quieter place;</li>\n<li>reduce stimuli;</li>\n<li>use headphones or another familiar resource;</li>\n<li>use simple communication;</li>\n<li>offer a break;</li>\n<li>avoid too many questions;</li>\n<li>follow the visual plan;</li>\n<li>tell the airport team if assistance is needed.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no universal strategy for every autistic person. Cork Airport emphasises that each person understands and experiences social situations differently and that strategies must be adapted to the individual.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Checklist before leaving home</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>2&#8211;3 weeks before</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>confirm the flight;</li>\n<li>confirm the assistance services;</li>\n<li>contact the airline;</li>\n<li>check the options available at the airport;</li>\n<li>prepare visual resources;</li>\n<li>practise the travel sequence;</li>\n<li>check documents;</li>\n<li>prepare medication and medical information;</li>\n<li>book the Sensory Room, where available;</li>\n<li>request the Important Flyer, if applicable;</li>\n<li>arrange the Sunflower or another identifier.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>A few days before</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>confirm times;</li>\n<li>check the baggage rules again;</li>\n<li>charge devices;</li>\n<li>prepare the bag;</li>\n<li>print important documents;</li>\n<li>review the visual plan.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>On the day of travel</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>arrive with enough time;</li>\n<li>tell the team if you need assistance;</li>\n<li>keep comfort resources accessible;</li>\n<li>follow the visual sequence;</li>\n<li>allow time for breaks;</li>\n<li>have a plan for delays.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Note: services can change</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Accessibility programmes, availability of rooms, assistance procedures and Fast Track conditions can change. Always confirm directly with the airport, the airline or the responsible organisation before the trip.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Autism &amp; Non-Visible Disabilities</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/sensory-room\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Sensory Room</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport - Travelling With Autism</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.aerlingus.com/support/special-assistance/needs-relating-to-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Aer Lingus - Needs Relating to Autism</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://asiam.ie/what-we-do/asiam-autism-id-card\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AsIAm - Autism ID Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.asdireland.ie/resources/idcard/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ASD Ireland - ID Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://hdsunflower.com/uk/insights/post/airports-around-the-world\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hidden Disabilities Sunflower - Airports Around the World</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an autism card to travel?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. The cards are a form of identification or proof, but the assistance procedures depend on the airport and the airline.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the AsIAm Autism ID Card guarantee Fast Track?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The AsIAm card and the Dublin Airport Important Flyer are different things. The free Fast Track described by Dublin Airport is tied to the Important Flyer programme and requires advance booking.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I use the Sunflower at the airport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport takes part in the Hidden Disabilities Sunflower network and states that its staff are trained to recognise the symbol. Cork Airport also provides Sunflower lanyards.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is the Dublin Airport sensory room free?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The airport states that the sessions are free, last 60 minutes and must be booked in advance. There are no walk-ins.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I just arrive at the airport and ask for assistance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is better not to rely on that. Cork Airport recommends booking assistance in advance because there may be a wait if the request is only made on arrival.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the airline need to know I am travelling with an autistic person?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is highly recommended to tell the airline in advance and ask which assistance services are available. Aer Lingus has a specific procedure for needs relating to autism.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the Sunflower work in other countries?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Hidden Disabilities Sunflower network is international and includes many airports, but the kind of support offered varies. Always check the specific airport before travelling.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 19 August 2026.</strong> Card prices, booking rules, room availability, assistance procedures and Fast Track conditions can change. Always confirm directly with the airport, the airline or the responsible organisation before the trip.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Notice:</strong> this guide is for information and does not constitute personalised medical, legal or travel advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Autism and Air Travel in Ireland: Dublin and Cork Airport','Important Flyer, Sunflower, sensory rooms and what to do if you lose the card.','publish','closed','closed','','autism-and-air-travel-in-ireland-dublin-and-cork-airport','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/autism-and-air-travel-in-ireland-dublin-and-cork-airport/',0,'guide','',0),(22391,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:46','2026-09-25 13:36:46','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how the first IRP registration works for Brazilians and other non-EU/EEA citizens, which documents to prepare, what it costs and where to follow the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/primeira-inscricao-irp-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are Brazilian and have come to Ireland to work, study, live or join family for more than 90 days, registering your immigration permission for the first time is one of the most important steps of your arrival. The current process is carried out by the Immigration Service Delivery (ISD).</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information for first-time registration</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians and other citizens from outside the EU/EEA and Switzerland who need to register a residence permission in the Republic of Ireland for the first time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check whether you need to register: the ISD states that people from outside the EU/EEA and Switzerland who come to work, study, live or join family for more than 90 days must register their permission.</li>\n<li>Create or access your personal account in the ISD Customer Service Portal and use it to book your first appointment.</li>\n<li>Prepare the documents specific to your Stamp. The official page lists, among others, a valid passport and proof of your current address; additional documents vary by permission.</li>\n<li>Attend the Registration Office at Burgh Quay, Dublin. Your documents are reviewed, a photo and fingerprints are taken and the permission is registered.</li>\n<li>Pay the fee where applicable. The standard fee is €300 for many categories, but exemptions exist.</li>\n<li>After registration, track the issue of your IRP and keep your passport, address and other details updated with the ISD when needed.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid passport, including the biometric page.</li>\n<li>Proof of your current address in Ireland.</li>\n<li>Documents proving the basis of your permission, such as an enrolment letter, employment contract or family documents, where applicable.</li>\n<li>Your appointment details and any additional documents requested by the ISD.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Booking the first appointment is free. The standard registration fee is €300 for many categories; exemptions exist. The official fee table was updated on 17 July 2026.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do not leave the registration until the end of the period in which you are allowed to remain legally in the State. Availability of appointments must be checked on the official portal.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Paying third parties for an appointment: the ISD warns that valid appointments are made through the Customer Service Portal.</li>\n<li>Assuming every Stamp has the same documentation or fee.</li>\n<li>Travelling without checking the validity of the IRP and the return rules.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - first-time registration</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/required-documents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - documents and fees</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/customer-service-portal-a-guide-to-using-the-online-self-service-portal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Customer Service Portal</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - first-time registration</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/required-documents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - documents and fees</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/customer-service-portal-a-guide-to-using-the-online-self-service-portal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Customer Service Portal</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to pay to book the appointment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Booking the first appointment has no fee; the registration fee, where applicable, is a separate step.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is the fee always €300?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. €300 is the standard fee for many categories, but exemptions exist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I pay someone to get me an appointment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The ISD states that valid appointments are made through the Customer Service Portal and warns about scams.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Are the IRP and the immigration permission the same thing?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not exactly. The permission sets the conditions of residence; the IRP is the card that proves the registration.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/customer-service-portal-a-guide-to-using-the-online-self-service-portal/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Book your first IRP registration through the ISD service portal</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/how-to-register-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/information-on-registering-your-immigration-permission-for-the-first-time/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information on first-time IRP registration</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>General information; it does not replace individual legal or immigration advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','First IRP Registration in Ireland: Step-by-Step for Brazilians','How the first registration of your Irish immigration permission works, which documents to prepare and what it costs.','inherit','closed','closed','','22371-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:46','2026-09-25 13:36:46','',22371,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22391',0,'revision','',0),(22392,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand when the IRP lets you return to Ireland without a re-entry visa, what to do if the card is lost or expired, and what to check before travelling.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/viajar-fora-irlanda-com-irp-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For Brazilians living in Ireland, travelling raises two questions: can I enter the destination, and can I come back to Ireland? The IRP answers only part of it. The destination&#8217;s own rules must be checked separately.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians and other non-EU/EEA citizens who already hold a registered residence permission in Ireland and plan to travel internationally.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check the validity of your IRP before travelling. For a visa-national who holds a valid IRP, the ISD states that no re-entry visa is needed to return to Ireland.</li>\n<li>Check the entry rules of the destination country: an Irish IRP does not guarantee entry to another country.</li>\n<li>Check your passport, your name and any other documents used for the trip.</li>\n<li>If the IRP is lost, stolen, has not arrived yet or has an important error, check the ISD emergency procedure before travelling.</li>\n<li>Do not count on being able to request a re-entry visa after you leave: the ISD states that the emergency procedure must be dealt with before travelling.</li>\n<li>Keep digital copies of your documents and confirm the airline and destination requirements.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Valid passport.</li>\n<li>Valid IRP, where applicable.</li>\n<li>Proof of your permission and of the travel, if needed.</li>\n<li>Documents required by the destination country.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A normal return with a valid IRP does not require a re-entry visa where the ISD rule applies. Emergency procedures or specific visas may have their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do this check before you buy or confirm the trip, especially if your IRP is close to expiring.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing the right to return to Ireland with the right to enter the destination.</li>\n<li>Travelling with an expired IRP or without the card when it is required.</li>\n<li>Assuming that a renewal request automatically replaces the physical card for travel.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/frequently-asked-questions-for-registration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Registration FAQ</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/frequently-asked-questions-for-registration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Registration FAQ</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a visa to come back to Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>With a valid IRP, the ISD states that a re-entry visa is not needed to return to Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the IRP let me enter any country?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. An Irish IRP does not guarantee entry to another country: you must meet that country&#8217;s own entry rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What if my IRP card was stolen while I am abroad?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Contact the ISD before travelling to find out the emergency procedure that applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace immigration or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Travelling Outside Ireland with an IRP: What Brazilians Need to Know','When you can return to Ireland without a re-entry visa, and what to do if your IRP is lost or expired.','inherit','closed','closed','','22372-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22372,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22392',0,'revision','',0),(22393,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand when Brazilians need an Employment Permit to work in Ireland, the differences between the Critical Skills and General Employment Permit, and the main steps.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/employment-permit-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A job offer does not automatically mean a Brazilian citizen can start working. For non-EEA nationals the general rule is that an Employment Permit is needed, unless an exemption applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians and other workers from outside the EEA who are looking for a job, changing employer, or assessing a work offer in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confirm your immigration status and whether you are exempt from the Employment Permit requirement.</li>\n<li>Check the right type: the current system has nine types; the Critical Skills and General Employment Permits are important examples.</li>\n<li>Check the occupation, remuneration and other current criteria: the lists can change.</li>\n<li>Collect the offer and the contract. The DETE states that a contract signed by both parties must accompany each application.</li>\n<li>Use Employment Permits Online to create the account, submit the application and follow the process.</li>\n<li>When the Labour Market Needs Test applies, confirm the advertising rules before submitting.</li>\n<li>Plan ahead: the DETE states that the application must be received at least 12 weeks before the proposed start date.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Signed offer and contract.</li>\n<li>Employer and worker details.</li>\n<li>Role, remuneration, occupation and work location.</li>\n<li>The checklist and documents specific to the permit type.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fees depend on the type and duration of the Employment Permit. Check the specific type on the DETE website before submitting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>General rule published by the DETE: apply at least 12 weeks before the start date. Where applicable, the Labour Market Needs Test involves additional advertising requirements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing an Employment Permit with a Residence Permission: the DETE is clear that they are not the same.</li>\n<li>Starting work before confirming the authorisation you need.</li>\n<li>Using old lists of eligible occupations.</li>\n<li>Not checking the DETE&#8217;s current processing times.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Employment Permits</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/permit-types/permit-types.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - permit types</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/employment-permit-eligibility/highly-skilled-eligible-occupations-list/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Critical Skills Occupations List</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://employmentpermits.enterprise.gov.ie/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Employment Permits Online</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Employment Permits</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/permit-types/permit-types.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - permit types</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/employment-permit-eligibility/highly-skilled-eligible-occupations-list/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DETE - Critical Skills Occupations List</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://employmentpermits.enterprise.gov.ie/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Employment Permits Online</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do Brazilians always need an Employment Permit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not in every case. The general rule applies to non-EEA nationals, but there are exemptions and immigration permissions that authorise work.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Are Critical Skills and General the same?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. They are different categories, with their own criteria and consequences.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does an Employment Permit give residence automatically?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The DETE expressly states that the permit is not a Residence Permission.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://employmentpermits.enterprise.gov.ie/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Access the Employment Permits Online portal</a> · <a href=\"https://enterprise.gov.ie/en/what-we-do/workplace-and-skills/employment-permits/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check Employment Permit types and criteria</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace immigration or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Employment Permit in Ireland: When a Brazilian Needs One','When Brazilians need an Employment Permit, the difference between Critical Skills and General, and the main steps.','inherit','closed','closed','','22373-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22373,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22393',0,'revision','',0),(22394,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Just started your first job in Ireland? See how to register the job with Revenue, use myAccount, avoid Emergency Tax and check your Tax Credit Certificate.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/primeiro-emprego-irlanda-emergency-tax/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Your first payslip can carry more tax than expected when Revenue cannot yet give your employer the correct Revenue Payroll Notification (RPN). This is called Emergency Tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians in their first job in Ireland, especially those who have just obtained their PPSN and have not yet registered their first job with Revenue.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Have your PPSN.</li>\n<li>Create or access Revenue myAccount.</li>\n<li>In PAYE Services, use Add Job or Pension Details to register the first job.</li>\n<li>Have your PPSN, the employer&#8217;s Tax Registration Number and the start date to hand.</li>\n<li>Give your PPSN to the employer and check that the job appears in myAccount.</li>\n<li>Check the Tax Credit Certificate.</li>\n<li>If Emergency Tax applied, follow Revenue&#8217;s instructions; once the correct RPN is available, amounts overpaid can be corrected through payroll under the applicable rules.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>PPSN.</li>\n<li>Employer details and Tax Registration Number.</li>\n<li>Start date.</li>\n<li>An active myAccount.</li>\n<li>Tax Credit Certificate, when available.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no fee to register a job in myAccount.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Register as soon as possible, ideally before the first payment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Assuming the employer always registers the first job.</li>\n<li>Not giving the employer your PPSN.</li>\n<li>Not checking the TCC and the jobs listed in myAccount.</li>\n<li>Confusing Emergency Tax with a fine.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Starting your first job</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/what-you-should-do.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - what to do</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Emergency Tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/getting-off-emergency-tax.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - getting off Emergency Tax</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Starting your first job</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/what-you-should-do.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - what to do</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Emergency Tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/getting-off-emergency-tax.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - getting off Emergency Tax</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to register every job in myAccount?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For the first job in the State, Revenue advises the worker to register it. For later jobs the employer normally reports the employment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What happens if I do not register?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The employer may not be able to obtain the required RPN and may apply Emergency Tax.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When does the TCC appear?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Revenue states that after registration the TCC can be available within up to two working days.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is Emergency Tax refunded?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Amounts overpaid can be corrected once the correct RPN is available, under Revenue&#8217;s rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/starting-your-first-job/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register your first job in Revenue myAccount</a> · <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/jobs-and-pensions/emergency-tax/getting-off-emergency-tax.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guidance on getting off Emergency Tax</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace tax or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','First Job in Ireland: How to Avoid Emergency Tax','Register your first job with Revenue, use myAccount and check your Tax Credit Certificate.','inherit','closed','closed','','22374-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22374,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22394',0,'revision','',0),(22395,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how to set up sole trader tax registration in Ireland, when to register for Income Tax, how ROS is used and what to keep in mind.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/sole-trader-autonomo-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For freelancers and professionals who work for themselves, the tax route is usually that of the sole trader and self-assessment. This is different from incorporating a limited company.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians in Ireland who intend to provide services on their own account or start a sole activity.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confirm that your working relationship really is self-employed.</li>\n<li>Have a PPSN: Revenue states that it is needed before the sole trader tax registration.</li>\n<li>Register for Income Tax according to your situation, using myAccount or the channel indicated by Revenue.</li>\n<li>After registration, use ROS where required for returns and payments.</li>\n<li>If you trade under a name different from your own, check the business name registration at the CRO.</li>\n<li>Keep records of income, expenses and documents from the start.</li>\n<li>Check additional obligations such as VAT or Employer PAYE where they apply.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>PPSN.</li>\n<li>Start date and activity details.</li>\n<li>Expected turnover information.</li>\n<li>Records of income and expenses.</li>\n<li>Additional details for VAT/PAYE, if applicable.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Revenue does not present the sole trader tax registration as a set-up fee; other registrations or licences may have their own costs.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Returns and payments follow the tax calendar. Self-assessment has annual Pay and File dates: check the year in question.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing a sole trader with a limited company.</li>\n<li>Starting to invoice without checking the tax registration you need.</li>\n<li>Not keeping adequate documents and records.</li>\n<li>Using thresholds and dates from a previous year.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Registering for tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/how-to-register-for-tax-as-a-sole-trader.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - sole trader</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/self-assessment-and-self-employment/guide-to-self-assessment/register-it-self-assessment.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - self-assessment</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Companies Registration Office</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Registering for tax</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/how-to-register-for-tax-as-a-sole-trader.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - sole trader</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/self-assessment-and-self-employment/guide-to-self-assessment/register-it-self-assessment.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - self-assessment</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.cro.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Companies Registration Office</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need a PPSN?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Revenue states that a PPSN is needed before the sole trader tax registration.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to use ROS?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>After registration, Revenue states that ROS is used for business-related returns and payments where applicable.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Are there income limits for self-assessment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Revenue sets criteria and limits for non-PAYE income: check the current figures for the year in question.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is a sole trader the same as a company?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. A sole trader is an individual activity; a company is a separate legal entity.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/online-services/services/ros/ros-help/getting-started-on-ros/registering-for-ros/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign in to ROS</a> · <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/starting-a-business/registering-for-tax/how-to-register-for-tax-as-a-sole-trader.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register for Income Tax as a sole trader with Revenue</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace tax or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Work as a Sole Trader in Ireland','Sole trader tax registration, self-assessment and how ROS is used in Ireland.','inherit','closed','closed','','22375-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22375,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22395',0,'revision','',0),(22396,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand where to seek medical care in Ireland: GP, GP out-of-hours, Injury Unit and Emergency Department, including costs and when to call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/hse-irlanda-gp-out-of-hours-injury-unit-emergency-department/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ireland&#8217;s urgent care system can be different from Brazil&#8217;s. Not every situation needs an Emergency Department. The HSE advises choosing the service according to the severity and type of problem.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians living in Ireland who need to know where to seek care when their GP is closed, or when an injury or emergency happens.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For non-urgent problems, see your GP during normal opening hours.</li>\n<li>If the GP is closed and you need urgent care, use the GP out-of-hours service; the HSE states that it normally works by phone with triage.</li>\n<li>For recent non-life-threatening injuries that are unlikely to need admission, check whether there is an Injury Unit suitable for your age and region.</li>\n<li>Go to an Emergency Department for serious or potentially life-threatening situations.</li>\n<li>If there is a risk to life or a need for urgent treatment, call 112 or 999.</li>\n<li>Before you leave, check the HSE finder for the unit, opening hours, the ages it treats and any disruptions.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find urgent or emergency care - HSE</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-a-gp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find a GP</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Medical Card or GP Visit Card, if you have one.</li>\n<li>Information about your medication and relevant conditions.</li>\n<li>Identity document, when requested.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE currently states €75 for an Injury Unit and €100 for an Emergency Department, with exemptions in situations such as holding a Medical Card or being referred. Confirm the amounts before publication.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no deadline in an emergency. In a serious situation do not wait: call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Going to an Emergency Department for any symptom.</li>\n<li>Using an Injury Unit for problems it does not treat.</li>\n<li>Not checking the unit&#8217;s age range and opening hours.</li>\n<li>Assuming the cost is always the same.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Urgent and emergency care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - find care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-go-to-a-gp-out-of-hours/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - GP out-of-hours</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-visit-an-injury-unit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Injury Unit</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Urgent and emergency care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/find-urgent-emergency-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - find care</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-go-to-a-gp-out-of-hours/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - GP out-of-hours</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/emergencies/when-to-visit-an-injury-unit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Injury Unit</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I use an Injury Unit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For recent, non-life-threatening injuries such as some fractures, sprains, cuts and minor burns.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>When should I go to an Emergency Department?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For serious or potentially life-threatening problems; if life is at risk, call 112 or 999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is GP out-of-hours walk-in?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that it normally works by phone with triage.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does an Injury Unit cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The amount currently stated by the HSE is €75, with some exemptions. Confirm before publishing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Health information does not replace a medical assessment. In an emergency, follow the HSE and call 112/999.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','HSE in Ireland: When to See a GP, GP Out-of-Hours, Injury Unit or Emergency Department','Where to seek medical care in Ireland, what it costs and when to call 112 or 999.','inherit','closed','closed','','22376-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22376,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22396',0,'revision','',0),(22397,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Find out who can apply for an EHIC in Ireland, which documents the HSE asks for, where it is valid and why it is not travel insurance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/ehic-irlanda-brasileiros-residentes/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The EHIC is useful for Brazilians living in Ireland who travel around Europe. It can give access to necessary public healthcare during temporary stays, subject to the rules of the country visited.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians living in Ireland who plan to travel to EU, EEA or Swiss countries.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confirm your eligibility: the HSE states that people living in Ireland can apply if they intend to live here for at least one year, subject to the applicable criteria.</li>\n<li>Have proof of residence and proof of your PPSN ready, where they are required.</li>\n<li>Apply online &#8211; the option the HSE describes as the fastest &#8211; or use the official postal/email channels.</li>\n<li>Each family member needs their own card where applicable.</li>\n<li>If you are travelling in less than 10 days, look at the urgent option and the Provisional Replacement Certificate.</li>\n<li>Take the card with you and do not treat the EHIC as travel insurance.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Proof of residence in Ireland.</li>\n<li>Proof of your PPSN.</li>\n<li>Personal details of the applicants and dependants.</li>\n<li>Any additional documents required by the HSE.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE provides the EHIC application service; confirm the current conditions at the time you apply.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the trip is coming up, use the online option and check the urgent procedure described by the HSE.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Thinking the EHIC covers private treatment.</li>\n<li>Thinking the EHIC covers repatriation.</li>\n<li>Using the EHIC for planned treatment.</li>\n<li>Not checking the destination&#8217;s co-payment rules.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - apply for an EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pprd2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/visitors-to-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - using the EHIC</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - apply for an EHIC</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pprd2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/visitors-to-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - using the EHIC</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can a Brazilian get an Irish EHIC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilian nationality is not the only criterion. Eligibility depends on your residence situation and the HSE rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Where is the EHIC valid?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that it is accepted in EU, EEA and Swiss countries, under the local rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the EHIC cover a private hospital?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The HSE states that it relates to necessary public care.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the EHIC replace travel insurance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The HSE expressly states that it does not replace travel insurance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/apply/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for an EHIC online</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/schemes-allowances/ehic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official EHIC information</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Health information does not replace a medical assessment or travel advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','EHIC in Ireland: How Resident Brazilians Can Get the European Health Insurance Card','Who can apply for an EHIC in Ireland, what the HSE asks for and where it is valid.','inherit','closed','closed','','22377-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22377,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22397',0,'revision','',0),(22398,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how to use NARIC Ireland/QQI to compare a Brazilian qualification with the National Framework of Qualifications (NFQ) and what the limits of that comparison are.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reconhecer-diploma-brasileiro-na-irlanda-naric-qqi/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>People who arrive with a Brazilian degree, postgraduate degree or other qualification often need to explain the level of their diploma to employers or institutions. NARIC Ireland, operated by the QQI, provides academic guidance through comparability statements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI guidance on recognising foreign qualifications</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians with diplomas or academic qualifications obtained in Brazil who need to present their education in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Go to the NARIC Ireland Foreign Qualifications Database.</li>\n<li>Select Brazil and look for the type and title of the qualification.</li>\n<li>If it is in the database, download the Comparability Statement.</li>\n<li>Read the comparable level in the Irish NFQ and the notes in the statement.</li>\n<li>If it is not in the database, use the QQI Qualifications Recognition Advice service.</li>\n<li>Present the statement to the employer or institution when it is requested.</li>\n<li>For a regulated profession, also check the professional body: the NARIC statement is not a professional licence.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Official name of the qualification.</li>\n<li>The institution that awarded the diploma.</li>\n<li>Academic documents and translations, when required by the receiving body.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The NARIC database is provided free of charge by the QQI. Translations, certifications and professional processes may have their own costs.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no universal deadline: apply in advance if the statement is needed for an application.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Treating a comparability statement as a professional licence.</li>\n<li>Assuming every diploma will automatically be in the database.</li>\n<li>Using an old statement without checking the current version.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the regulator for a regulated profession.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI - recognition of foreign qualifications</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qsearch.qqi.ie/WebPart/Search?searchtype=recognitions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NARIC - Foreign Qualifications Database</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qhelp.qqi.ie/learners/qualifications-recognition-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI QHelp - Recognition Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI - recognition of foreign qualifications</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qsearch.qqi.ie/WebPart/Search?searchtype=recognitions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NARIC - Foreign Qualifications Database</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://qhelp.qqi.ie/learners/qualifications-recognition-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI QHelp - Recognition Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does NARIC recognise my diploma for any job?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The QQI explains that the information is academic guidance and does not in itself give access to a job, a regulated profession or a course.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What if my diploma is not in the database?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can request general guidance from the QQI.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to translate my diploma?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It depends on the institution, employer or body that requests the document.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is a comparability statement mandatory?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily: it is an official tool that may be requested or useful depending on the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://qsearch.qqi.ie/WebPart/Search?searchtype=recognitions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the NARIC/QQI comparability list</a> · <a href=\"https://www.qqi.ie/recognition-of-foreign-qualifications\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">QQI/NARIC - recognition of qualifications</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace academic, professional or immigration advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Get a Brazilian Diploma Recognised in Ireland: NARIC and QQI Guide','How to compare a Brazilian qualification with the Irish NFQ and what the limits are.','inherit','closed','closed','','22378-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22378,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22398',0,'revision','',0),(22399,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Learn how the TFI Leap Card works, where to use it, how to top it up, what fare caps exist and what to do if you lose the card.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/leap-card-irlanda-como-usar/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For people who use buses, the Luas, DART and other services, the TFI Leap Card is a practical way to pay for journeys and can offer lower fares than cash in many situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians living, studying or working in Ireland who use public transport regularly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Choose the type of Leap Card that suits your situation.</li>\n<li>Buy the card through an official channel and, where possible, register it.</li>\n<li>Add Travel Credit or the appropriate product.</li>\n<li>Use the card according to the service&#8217;s tap on/tap off rules.</li>\n<li>Check fares, zones and fare capping on the current TFI page.</li>\n<li>Register the card to make replacement or refunds easier where allowed.</li>\n<li>Use the app/online account to check your balance and transactions.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For a standard adult card there is normally no complex documentation.</li>\n<li>Discounted cards have specific eligibility and evidence requirements.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fares depend on the passenger, zone and service. TFI keeps the current fare tables: avoid publishing figures without checking the page on the day you publish.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no general deadline; validity rules depend on the product.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Not registering the card.</li>\n<li>Assuming every line has the same fare or capping.</li>\n<li>Confusing Adult, Young Adult and Student cards.</li>\n<li>Using old fares from third parties.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Leap Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/new-fare-zones/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Fare Zones</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/bus-fares/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Bus Fares</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Leap Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/new-fare-zones/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Fare Zones</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/bus-fares/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TFI - Bus Fares</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Where can I use the Leap Card?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>TFI states that it works on many services in the network, including buses, the Luas, DART and some rail services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is it worth registering?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. TFI recommends registering the card because unregistered cards cannot be replaced or refunded if lost.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is there a daily cap?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes, in certain zones and services. TFI applies fare capping.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do all cards have the same discounts?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. There are different types, such as Adult, Young Adult/Student and Child, with their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.leapcard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check and order a Leap Card</a> · <a href=\"https://www.transportforireland.ie/fares/leap-card/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check TFI fares and zones</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace official TFI or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Leap Card in Ireland: How to Use the Transport Card and Pay Less','How the TFI Leap Card works, where to use it, how to top it up and what to do if you lose it.','inherit','closed','closed','','22379-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:47','2026-09-25 13:36:47','',22379,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22399',0,'revision','',0),(22400,283039560,'2026-08-18 12:00:00','2026-08-18 11:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand the route for Brazilians who cannot exchange their foreign licence: theory test, learner permit, EDT and driving test.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/learner-permit-theory-test-irlanda-cnh-brasileira/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Exchanging a foreign licence depends on exchange agreements. When the licence cannot be exchanged, the driver may have to follow the Irish process to obtain the licence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians with a foreign driving licence who need to understand the route to an Irish licence when no exchange applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>First check with the NDLS whether your licence is eligible for exchange.</li>\n<li>If you need the learner driver route, pass the Driver Theory Test for the correct category.</li>\n<li>Apply for the first learner permit through the NDLS, noting identity, address, ordinary residence, PPSN and reports where applicable.</li>\n<li>If you hold a full licence from a country with no exchange agreement, check whether you are entitled to the Reduced EDT Programme.</li>\n<li>For the first learner permit, the RSA states it must be held for at least six months and the required training completed before the driving test.</li>\n<li>Book the driving test once all the requirements are met.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Passport or an accepted document.</li>\n<li>PPSN.</li>\n<li>Proof of address where required.</li>\n<li>Proof of ordinary residence where applicable.</li>\n<li>Theory test certificate.</li>\n<li>Eye or medical report, if required.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The theory test, learner permit, lessons and driving test each have their own costs. Check RSA/NDLS for the current amounts.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The RSA states that the learner permit must be applied for within two years of passing the theory test; after that the certificate expires.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Driving unaccompanied with a learner permit.</li>\n<li>Booking the theory test on an unofficial site.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the six months for the first learner permit.</li>\n<li>Assuming a Brazilian licence is automatically exchangeable.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - learner permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/apply-for-your-first-permit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - first permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/theory-test/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - theory test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - learner permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/apply-for-your-first-permit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - first permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/theory-test/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - theory test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I exchange my Brazilian licence directly?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It depends on the exchange rules in force. Check with the NDLS before starting the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to take the theory test?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For the first learner permit, the RSA states that you must pass the theory test for the category.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long do I hold a learner permit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For the first learner permit, the RSA states six months, in addition to the required training.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I drive on my own?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The learner permit rules require an accompanying driver according to the category and the applicable conditions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://theorytest.ie/book-your-theory-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Book the Driver Theory Test</a> · <a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/apply-for-your-first-permit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for your first Learner Permit</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/learner-driver/learner-permit.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS - Learner Permit</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace RSA/NDLS guidance or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Brazilian Driving Licence in Ireland: When You Need the Theory Test and Learner Permit','The route for Brazilians who cannot exchange their licence: theory test, learner permit, EDT and driving test.','publish','closed','closed','','brazilian-driving-licence-in-ireland-theory-test-and-learner-permit','','','2026-09-25 14:46:49','2026-09-25 13:46:49','',0,'http://localhost:8080/guias/brazilian-driving-licence-in-ireland-theory-test-and-learner-permit/',0,'guide','',0),(22401,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how Civil Legal Aid from the Legal Aid Board works, who can apply, how the means test works and what contributions may apply.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/assistencia-juridica-legal-aid-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Legal problems are harder when you are still learning the Irish system. The Legal Aid Board provides civil legal advice and representation services for people who meet the financial criteria and the merits test.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians in Ireland with a civil legal matter who want to check access to the Legal Aid Board.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check that the matter is within the scope of Civil Legal Aid. Criminal matters are not covered by the Civil Legal Aid Act.</li>\n<li>Check your possible financial eligibility. The Board currently states disposable income below €18,000, and also takes assets and circumstances into account.</li>\n<li>Use the financial eligibility indicator as a first guide, without treating it as a guarantee.</li>\n<li>Prepare proof of income, expenses and assets.</li>\n<li>Submit the application online or go to a Law Centre.</li>\n<li>Wait for the financial and merits assessment; there may be a waiting list.</li>\n<li>Check the contribution that applies and any exemptions.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for Civil Legal Aid</a> · <a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/financial-eligibility-and-merits-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check financial eligibility</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Address and PPSN.</li>\n<li>Proof of income and benefits.</li>\n<li>Proof of expenses.</li>\n<li>Information about assets and debts.</li>\n<li>Documents relating to the legal problem.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Board currently states a minimum contribution of €30 for advice, plus contributions for representation depending on the situation. Exemptions and special rules exist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Court deadlines keep running while you seek assistance. Do not let a deadline expire while waiting for a place.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Confusing the Legal Aid Board with free help for any issue.</li>\n<li>Not sending financial evidence.</li>\n<li>Letting a court deadline pass.</li>\n<li>Assuming the calculator guarantees approval.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Apply Now</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/financial-eligibility-and-merits-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - eligibility</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/contact-us/find-a-law-centre/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Law Centres</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/waiting-times/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - waiting times</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Apply Now</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/financial-eligibility-and-merits-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - eligibility</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/contact-us/find-a-law-centre/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - Law Centres</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/en/our-legal-aid-service/apply-now/waiting-times/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board - waiting times</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can any Brazilian apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Nationality by itself neither guarantees nor prevents it. The type of case, your financial situation and the merits test are what matter.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the income limit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Board currently states disposable income below €18,000, plus asset criteria and the specifics of the case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is it always free?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. In many cases there is a contribution; exemptions exist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long does it take?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It varies by Law Centre and there may be a waiting list; the Board publishes statistics.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is not legal advice. In an urgent situation seek qualified assistance immediately.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Legal Aid in Ireland: How to Apply for Civil Legal Aid','How Civil Legal Aid works, who can apply, the means test and possible contributions.','inherit','closed','closed','','22380-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','',22380,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22401',0,'revision','',0),(22402,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand how to register a birth in Ireland and how civil marriage works, including notice, documents, fees and the extra steps for foreign nationals.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/casamento-registro-nascimento-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Having children and getting married in Ireland involve civil registration procedures that can be different from the Brazilian ones. The General Register Office and the civil registration services hold the official information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who have children in Ireland, plan to marry in the country, or need civil registration certificates.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For a birth, register the child within the official deadline: gov.ie states a maximum of three months after the birth.</li>\n<li>Use the Civil Registration Service and check the online option available.</li>\n<li>Request the birth certificate when you need it for school, a passport or other processes.</li>\n<li>For a marriage, give at least three months notice to the civil registration service.</li>\n<li>Book the marriage notification appointment and both partners must attend.</li>\n<li>Prepare passports, birth certificates, proof of address and PPS Numbers, plus special documents where applicable.</li>\n<li>If one partner is a foreign national, check the interview, proof of immigration status, translation and interpreter requirements where they apply.</li>\n<li>After the ceremony, follow the registration procedure and request the marriage certificate if you need it.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>For a birth: the information and documents requested by the Civil Registration Service.</li>\n<li>For a marriage: passport, birth certificate, proof of address, PPSN and specific documents.</li>\n<li>Translations where documents are not in English or Irish.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The marriage notification fee is a non-refundable €200. The marriage registration itself is free, but certificates have fees. Confirm the current amounts.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Birth: up to three months. Marriage: at least three months notice before the ceremony.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Leaving the birth registration too late.</li>\n<li>Booking a marriage without allowing for the three months notice.</li>\n<li>Bringing copies without the originals where they are required.</li>\n<li>Forgetting translations or the extra documentation for foreign nationals.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/register-a-birth-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Register a birth</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-married-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get married in Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/collections/all-services-available-from-the-general-register-office/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - General Register Office services</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/register-a-birth-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Register a birth</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-married-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - Get married in Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/collections/all-services-available-from-the-general-register-office/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">gov.ie - General Register Office services</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long do I have to register a birth?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>gov.ie currently states a maximum of three months after the birth.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How far in advance do I notify a marriage?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The general rule is three months notice, given in person to the civil registration service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do foreign nationals need an interview?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>gov.ie states that an interview is required when one of the partners is a foreign national, among other situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How much does the marriage notice cost?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The notification fee is currently €200 and non-refundable; registration and certificates have their own rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/register-a-birth-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Register a birth online</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/get-married-in-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official information on getting married in Ireland</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>General information; it does not replace official civil registration guidance or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Marriage and Birth Registration in Ireland: A Guide for Brazilian Families','How to register a birth and how civil marriage works, including notice and fees.','inherit','closed','closed','','22381-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','',22381,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22402',0,'revision','',0),(22403,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Learn how to check that a financial firm is authorised in Ireland and how to complain about a bank, insurer, credit provider or other financial service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reclamar-banco-seguro-servico-financeiro-irlanda/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Problems with a bank, insurer, credit, investment or payments can be hard in another language. The Central Bank of Ireland keeps the register of authorised firms, and the FSPO offers an independent route for eligible complaints.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who use banks, insurance, credit, investments, payments or other financial services in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Before you sign up, look the firm up in the Central Bank Register of Authorised Firms.</li>\n<li>Confirm the legal name, because a firm may trade under a different name.</li>\n<li>If there is a problem, make a formal complaint directly to the firm: firms must have a complaints procedure.</li>\n<li>Keep the contract, statements, emails, letters, receipts and call logs.</li>\n<li>If the reply does not resolve it, check whether the case can go to the FSPO.</li>\n<li>Use the FSPO form and attach the relevant documents and the final response letter where applicable.</li>\n<li>If you suspect an unauthorised firm, report it to the Central Bank of Ireland.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Contract/policy.</li>\n<li>Statements and receipts.</li>\n<li>Communications with the firm.</li>\n<li>Final Response Letter where applicable.</li>\n<li>Account or policy details.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The FSPO states that its service is free. The Central Bank register is also public and free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The FSPO states that the firm can take up to 40 working days in its internal process. There are time limits for referring a case to the Ombudsman: check them before you wait.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Sending money without checking authorisation.</li>\n<li>Complaining without keeping evidence.</li>\n<li>Skipping the formal complaint to the provider.</li>\n<li>Confusing an individual complaint with a systemic regulatory report.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/contact-us/complaints-against-a-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - complaints</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://registers.centralbank.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - registers</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/consumer-hub/explainers/why-is-it-important-to-deal-with-an-authorised-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - authorised firms</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-your-provider/financial-services-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complain to your provider</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/complaint-form.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complaint form</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/contact-us/complaints-against-a-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - complaints</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://registers.centralbank.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - registers</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.centralbank.ie/consumer-hub/explainers/why-is-it-important-to-deal-with-an-authorised-financial-service-provider\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Central Bank - authorised firms</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-your-provider/financial-services-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complain to your provider</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/complaint-form.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FSPO - complaint form</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I check whether a firm is authorised?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Search the Register of Authorised Firms and confirm the legal name.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I complain to the Central Bank to get my money back?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Generally not. For individual complaints the normal route is the firm and then, where applicable, the FSPO.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the FSPO charge?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The FSPO states that the service is free.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to keep evidence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The FSPO recommends documents such as contracts, statements, emails and letters.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-your-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Complain to your financial provider first</a> · <a href=\"https://www.fspo.ie/make-a-complaint/how-to-make-a-complaint-to-the-fspo/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make a complaint to the FSPO</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace financial or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Complain About a Bank, Insurer or Financial Service in Ireland','Check that a firm is authorised and complain about a bank, insurer or financial service.','inherit','closed','closed','','22382-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','',22382,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22403',0,'revision','',0),(22404,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> See how to complain about a government department, local authority or public service in Ireland and when the Office of the Ombudsman can examine the complaint.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reclamar-servico-publico-irlanda-ombudsman/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If a Brazilian has a problem with an Irish public service, there is a formal route. The Office of the Ombudsman can investigate certain complaints against government departments, local authorities and the HSE.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who had a problem with a government department, local authority or the HSE and could not resolve it through the normal channel.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Identify the body that made the decision or provided the service.</li>\n<li>First complain directly to the body, explaining what happened and what you expect to be corrected.</li>\n<li>Keep references, emails, letters, names, dates and documents.</li>\n<li>If the reply does not resolve it, check whether the Ombudsman can handle that type of complaint.</li>\n<li>Submit the complaint within the applicable time limit. The Ombudsman states a general rule of 12 months, save for special circumstances.</li>\n<li>Do not use the Ombudsman for matters outside its remit, such as many complaints against private companies, banks or An Garda Síochána.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Description of the facts.</li>\n<li>The body&#8217;s response.</li>\n<li>References and case numbers.</li>\n<li>Related documents, letters and emails.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No fee is stated for making a complaint to the Ombudsman.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Published general rule: 12 months, save for very special circumstances.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Going straight to the Ombudsman without trying to resolve it with the body.</li>\n<li>Waiting more than 12 months without checking whether you can complain.</li>\n<li>Sending a complaint without a timeline and evidence.</li>\n<li>Using the Ombudsman for an FSPO or WRC matter.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/b7cef-how-to-complain-to-a-public-service-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - how to complain</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/000df-making-a-complaint/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - making a complaint</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/b7cef-how-to-complain-to-a-public-service-provider/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - how to complain</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/000df-making-a-complaint/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ombudsman - making a complaint</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I go straight to the Ombudsman?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In general, try to resolve it with the public service first.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the Ombudsman deal with private companies?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not in general: the body itself lists several categories that are outside its remit.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is there a deadline?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The published general rule is 12 months, save for very special circumstances.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is the HSE included?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The Ombudsman states that it investigates certain complaints about HSE services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.ombudsman.ie/en/publication/000df-making-a-complaint/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Make a complaint to the Ombudsman</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to Complain About a Public Service in Ireland: When to Use the Ombudsman','Complain about a government department, local authority or public service in Ireland.','inherit','closed','closed','','22383-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','',22383,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22404',0,'revision','',0),(22405,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> A practical checklist for Brazilians resident in Ireland before travelling: passport, IRP, destination, return and emergency documents.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/checklist-viagem-internacional-irlanda-brasileiros-irp/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This guide turns the official travel and return rules into a simple check before departure. It complements the guide on the IRP and travel, with a focus on planning.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians resident in Ireland who are travelling to Brazil, Europe or another country and want to reduce the risk of documentation problems.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Check the validity of your passport.</li>\n<li>Check the validity of your IRP and that the details are correct.</li>\n<li>Check the entry rules of the destination on the relevant official source.</li>\n<li>Check whether the airline requires any additional documentation.</li>\n<li>Save digital copies of your passport, IRP, insurance and bookings.</li>\n<li>If the IRP is lost or you have no valid card, contact the ISD before you leave.</li>\n<li>After you return, check whether your address, passport or other details changed and need to be reported to the ISD.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Passport.</li>\n<li>IRP.</li>\n<li>Travel insurance, if purchased.</li>\n<li>Destination entry documents.</li>\n<li>Travel confirmations and emergency contacts.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no cost to do the checklist. Visa, insurance and other document costs depend on the destination.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do the check at least a few days before you travel and again on the eve of departure.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Leaving the check until the airport.</li>\n<li>Assuming the IRP replaces a destination visa.</li>\n<li>Travelling without copies of your documents.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DFA - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ISD - Travel and Re-Entry Visas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DFA - Travel Advice</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the IRP replace a passport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The IRP and the passport have different functions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the IRP guarantee entry to the destination?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Check with the destination country.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What do I do if I lose the IRP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Check the ISD emergency procedure before travelling.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need travel insurance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The EHIC does not replace travel insurance: for travel, consider cover that matches the destination and your situation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/registering-your-immigration-permission/travel-and-re-entry-visas/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official rules for travel and re-entry with an IRP</a> · <a href=\"https://www.dfa.ie/travel/travel-advice/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Foreign Affairs travel advice</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Informative checklist; always confirm the official rules for your destination and situation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','First International Trip with an IRP: Documents Checklist for Brazilians','A practical checklist before travelling: passport, IRP, destination, return and emergency documents.','inherit','closed','closed','','22384-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:48','2026-09-25 13:36:48','',22384,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22405',0,'revision','',0),(22406,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand how to file a workplace complaint or dispute with the Workplace Relations Commission (WRC) and what to keep before starting the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/reclamacao-trabalhista-wrc-irlanda-brasileiros/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Even with a general guide on employment rights, many Brazilians need practical guidance on what to do once the problem has happened. The WRC provides an eComplaint Portal for referring certain complaints and disputes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians who work or worked in Ireland and need to understand the official route for a workplace complaint.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Identify which right or obligation may have been breached and check the WRC information.</li>\n<li>Collect the contract, payslips, timesheets, messages, emails, internal policies and other documents.</li>\n<li>Where possible, try to resolve the issue with the employer and record what was requested and answered.</li>\n<li>Use the WRC eComplaint Portal when the matter falls within the WRC&#8217;s remit.</li>\n<li>Complete the complaint with facts, dates and relevant documents, without exaggerating or leaving out information.</li>\n<li>After submitting, follow the WRC communications. The case may proceed to inspection, conciliation or adjudication depending on the nature of the dispute.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Contract of employment.</li>\n<li>Payslips.</li>\n<li>Timesheets.</li>\n<li>Emails and messages.</li>\n<li>Documents or responses from the employer.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The WRC provides the eComplaint Portal: check the official page to see whether there is any specific cost for your procedure.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Complaint time limits vary with the legislation and the type of case. Do not wait until close to the limit; confirm the applicable deadline directly with the WRC.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Not keeping evidence.</li>\n<li>Missing the applicable deadline.</li>\n<li>Sending a generic complaint with no dates or facts.</li>\n<li>Confusing the WRC with the Labour Court or another body.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - Make a Complaint / Refer a Dispute</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - main website</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - Make a Complaint / Refer a Dispute</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WRC - main website</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to complain to the employer first?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not every procedure requires exactly the same step, but trying to resolve it internally can help and should be checked according to the type of complaint.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I submit the complaint online?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The WRC provides an eComplaint Portal for the complaints and disputes it covers.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Which evidence should I keep?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The contract, payslips, timesheets, emails, messages and the employer&#8217;s responses are useful examples.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long do I have?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The time limit depends on the legislation and the type of complaint. Check with the WRC for your case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://ecomplaint.workplacerelations.ie/en-IE/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">File a complaint in the WRC eComplaint Portal</a> · <a href=\"https://www.workplacerelations.ie/en/complaints_disputes/refer_a_dispute_make_a_complaint/how_to_make_a_complaint_refer_a_dispute.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official guidance on making a complaint</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','How to File and Track a Workplace Complaint with the WRC','How to file a complaint or dispute with the Workplace Relations Commission and what to keep.','inherit','closed','closed','','22385-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','',22385,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22406',0,'revision','',0),(22407,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Raising a child on your own is already a big challenge. For anyone living in Ireland as an immigrant, understanding which benefits, payments and supports may be available can be even more complicated.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ireland has different schemes for families with children, but <strong>there is no single &#8220;single mother&#8221; or &#8220;single father&#8221; benefit that automatically entitles everyone to all the supports</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What are the main benefits for lone parents?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>One-Parent Family Payment (OFP)</li>\n<li>Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment (JST)</li>\n<li>Child Benefit</li>\n<li>Working Family Payment (WFP)</li>\n<li>Single Person Child Carer Credit (SPCCC)</li>\n<li>Domiciliary Care Allowance, where the child meets the criteria</li>\n<li>Carer&#8217;s Allowance or Carer&#8217;s Benefit in certain situations</li>\n<li>housing and income supports, depending on your financial situation</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/en/explore-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Explore and start other payments on MyWelfare</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>1. One-Parent Family Payment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>One-Parent Family Payment (OFP)</strong> is one of the main payments for people raising children without the support of a partner. It is generally a payment subject to a <strong>means test</strong>, where certain income and resources are assessed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The rules depend on your individual situation and include requirements linked to the child&#8217;s age, income and family situation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/one-parent-family-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the One-Parent Family Payment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I work and still receive the OFP?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The fact that you work does not necessarily mean the payment is automatically cancelled. The OFP is subject to an income assessment under the scheme rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What about child maintenance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The rules on child maintenance have changed. Do not use old information found on blogs or videos without checking the current rule directly with the Department of Social Protection.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>2. Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment (JST)</strong> was created to help certain lone parents who stop receiving the One-Parent Family Payment because their youngest child reaches the OFP age limit. The JST has its own eligibility rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/jobseekers-transitional-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>3. Child Benefit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Child Benefit</strong> is different from the OFP. It is a payment linked to the child and can be received by families who meet the scheme criteria. A lone parent should check Child Benefit even if they are not entitled to the OFP.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/topics/parents-children-family/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for Child Benefit</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/services/child-benefit/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check Child Benefit</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>4. Working Family Payment</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you work but your family income is relatively low, the <strong>Working Family Payment (WFP)</strong> is worth looking at. The scheme is for workers with children whose family income is below the limit that applies to their family size.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A common mistake is to think: &#8220;I work, so I am not entitled to any benefit.&#8221; That is not necessarily true.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://services.mywelfare.ie/en/topics/parents-children-family/working-family-payment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for the Working Family Payment</a> · <a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/WFP\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check and apply for the Working Family Payment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>5. Single Person Child Carer Credit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Single Person Child Carer Credit (SPCCC)</strong> is a tax credit administered by Revenue. For 2026 the credit is <strong>€1,900 per year</strong>. Anyone who qualifies may also have an additional band of income taxed at 20%, under Revenue&#8217;s rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/personal-tax-credits-reliefs-and-exemptions/children/single-person-child-carer-credit/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check and claim the SPCCC</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Who can apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You must meet Revenue&#8217;s conditions to be considered eligible and have a qualifying child. There are specific rules for separated and widowed people and for those who share the care of the child.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How do I apply?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are PAYE you can claim through Revenue myAccount, under <em>Manage your tax for the current year</em>, <em>PAYE Services</em>, <em>Claim tax credits</em> and <em>You and your family</em>. If you are self-employed, follow Revenue&#8217;s instructions for ROS.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>6. Domiciliary Care Allowance</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If your child has a disability and needs significantly more care and attention than a child of the same age would normally need, another important payment may apply: <strong>Domiciliary Care Allowance (DCA)</strong>. This payment is not exclusive to lone parents and has its own criteria.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>7. Carer&#8217;s Allowance and Carer&#8217;s Benefit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have to stop working or significantly reduce your hours to care for someone who needs full-time care, there may be carer-related payments. They are not exclusive to lone parents and have their own requirements.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>8. Support to return to work or start a business</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Back to Work Enterprise Allowance (BTWEA)</strong> may be relevant for some people who receive certain social payments and want to start a business. There are eligibility requirements, benefit duration rules and prior approval of the business.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>9. Housing supports</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Depending on your income and family situation, it may be worth checking the Housing Assistance Payment (HAP), social housing and other housing-related supports. There is no general rule saying that all lone parents get automatic priority in any housing scheme.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>10. If I am unemployed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are a lone parent who has lost their job, the options may depend on the child&#8217;s age, PRSI record, income and other circumstances. Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment, Jobseeker&#8217;s Allowance, Jobseeker&#8217;s Benefit or other payments may be available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What should a Brazilian have ready?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>PPS Number</li>\n<li>identity document</li>\n<li>proof of address</li>\n<li>bank details</li>\n<li>the child&#8217;s documents and PPS Number</li>\n<li>salary and payslips</li>\n<li>information about other income</li>\n<li>information about custody, separation or divorce, where relevant</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Checklist: am I a lone parent in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Do I have a PPS Number?</li>\n<li>Does my child have a PPS Number?</li>\n<li>Am I receiving Child Benefit?</li>\n<li>Can I apply for the One-Parent Family Payment?</li>\n<li>Should I check the Jobseeker&#8217;s Transitional Payment?</li>\n<li>Could my income qualify me for the Working Family Payment?</li>\n<li>Can I claim the Single Person Child Carer Credit?</li>\n<li>Does my child have needs that may qualify for the Domiciliary Care Allowance?</li>\n<li>Do I need to check Carer&#8217;s Allowance or Carer&#8217;s Benefit?</li>\n<li>Am I entitled to any housing support?</li>\n<li>Is my family and tax situation up to date?</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is there a specific single mother benefit in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The One-Parent Family Payment is one of the main payments for people raising children without the support of a partner, but it is not the only support available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can a single father also receive it?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The schemes are not exclusively for women. Eligibility depends on the rules of each payment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I work and receive benefits?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>In some cases, yes. How income is treated varies by payment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What is the SPCCC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the Single Person Child Carer Credit, a tax credit administered by Revenue. In 2026 the credit is €1,900.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>What if I share custody - can I receive the SPCCC?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There may be a possibility of a secondary claimant, but there are specific rules about the days the child lives with each parent and about transferring the credit.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to report a change in my family situation?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Changes such as marriage, reconciliation or living together can affect certain payments and tax credits. Check and report to the responsible body when needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Social Protection</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/en/birth-family-relationships/parenting-alone/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information - Parenting alone</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/personal-tax-credits-reliefs-and-exemptions/children/single-person-child-carer-credit/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Single Person Child Carer Credit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/en/personal-tax-credits-reliefs-and-exemptions/tax-relief-charts/index.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue - Tax rates, bands and reliefs</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.revenue.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Revenue</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 28 August 2026.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Social welfare payments and tax credits can change with the Budget, new laws, changes in rates, income limits, residence rules and eligibility criteria. Always confirm the information directly with the Department of Social Protection, Revenue or Citizens Information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><em>Notice: This guide is for information only. Social welfare payments, taxes and eligibility criteria depend on individual circumstances. This information does not constitute personalised legal, tax or financial advice.</em></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Benefits for Lone Parents in Ireland: Payments, Tax and Other Supports','Which payments, tax credits and supports may exist for lone parents in Ireland.','inherit','closed','closed','','22386-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','',22386,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22407',0,'revision','',0),(22408,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are living with domestic violence in Ireland, <strong>you do not have to face it alone</strong>. Public services, specialist organisations, legal support, police protection and court measures are available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Domestic violence is not only physical assault. It can also involve threats, control, sexual violence, emotional abuse, financial control, digital abuse and coercive behaviour.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>If you are in danger now</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you or someone else is in <strong>immediate danger</strong>, call <strong>999 or 112</strong> and ask An Garda Síochána for help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can also go to your nearest Garda Station. If you do not speak English well, that should not stop you from asking for help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What is domestic violence?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>physical assault;</li>\n<li>threats or intimidation;</li>\n<li>sexual violence or coercion;</li>\n<li>psychological or emotional control;</li>\n<li>financial control;</li>\n<li>control through technology or social media;</li>\n<li>stalking or unwanted contact;</li>\n<li>coercive or controlling behaviour;</li>\n<li>threats against children or family members;</li>\n<li>destruction of property.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Courts Service explains that domestic violence can be carried out by current or former partners, spouses, civil partners, people with whom you have had an intimate relationship, the other parent of your children and certain family members.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/apply-for-a-domestic-violence-court-order\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for a domestic violence protection order</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Where to find help in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Women&#8217;s Aid</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Women&#8217;s Aid runs a free, confidential national helpline for women experiencing domestic violence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>1800 341 900 &#8212; 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Men&#8217;s Aid</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Men can also be victims of domestic violence. The HSE lists Men&#8217;s Aid among the national support services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>01 554 3811</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>National Male Advice Line</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also lists the National Male Advice Line as a support service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>1800 816 588</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>HSE</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE holds information on domestic, sexual and gender-based violence services and lists national and local services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Do I need to report to the Garda immediately?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If there is immediate danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>. In other situations you can go to your local Garda Station to explain what happened, ask for guidance or make a report.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can also contact specialist services before deciding whether to report. The important thing is to find a safe way to get guidance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/crime/domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Report to / get help from the Garda</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Make a safety plan</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If it is safe to do so, consider preparing a plan for a possible emergency.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>choose someone you trust;</li>\n<li>identify a safe place where you can stay;</li>\n<li>keep important documents accessible;</li>\n<li>have in mind how to ask for help in an emergency;</li>\n<li>if it is safe, prepare a bag with documents, keys, money, clothes and anything the children need;</li>\n<li>be careful when researching help if your phone or computer is being monitored.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Do not do anything that increases your risk.</strong> If preparing documents or a bag could put you in danger, put your safety first.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Can I get a court order against the perpetrator?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Ireland has different types of Domestic Violence Orders:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Protection Order;</li>\n<li>Safety Order;</li>\n<li>Barring Order;</li>\n<li>Interim Barring Order;</li>\n<li>Emergency Barring Order.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Protection Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is a short-term protective measure. It can order the person to stop perpetrating violence or making threats. If the perpetrator lives with you, a Protection Order does not automatically order them to leave the home.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Safety Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is a longer-term protective order. It can prevent certain acts of violence, threats, stalking or contact. If the perpetrator lives with you, a Safety Order does not order them to leave the house. A Safety Order granted by the District Court can last up to <strong>5 years</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Barring Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A Barring Order can order the perpetrator to leave or not enter the home, and can impose other restrictions relating to violence, threats, stalking or contact. A Barring Order granted by the District Court can last up to <strong>3 years</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Interim Barring Order and Emergency Barring Order</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>These are emergency protective measures. Whether each one can be applied for depends on the relationship between the people and on the rights to the home. The Courts Service explains the specific criteria for each order.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How do I apply for a Domestic Violence Order?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>Complete the Application for a Domestic Violence Order.</li>\n<li>Submit the application at the appropriate Court Office, normally the District Court.</li>\n<li>The Court Office will arrange the hearing before a judge.</li>\n<li>The Courts Service or the Gardaí will notify the respondent in line with the procedure.</li>\n<li>You may attend the full hearing so that the judge can decide on long-term protection.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have dependent children you want included in the protection, they must be included in the application.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Courts Service currently provides the official Application for a Domestic Violence Order form and advises that the correct version is used before filing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/crime/domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Garda - Domestic Abuse</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/domestic-sexual-gender-based-violence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Domestic, Sexual and Gender-Based Violence</a> · <a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/hubs/domestic-violence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Domestic Violence</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/my-situation-has-changed-since-i-arrived-in-ireland/immigration-guidelines-for-victims-of-domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service - Victims of Domestic Abuse</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What if the perpetrator breaches the order?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Breaching a Domestic Violence Order is a <strong>crime</strong>. If it happens, contact the Gardaí immediately. If there is immediate danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What if I cannot afford a lawyer?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The <strong>Legal Aid Board</strong> provides legal advice and mediation services subject to the applicable criteria. The Government also advises victims of domestic violence to contact the Legal Aid Board for legal assistance information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>I am Brazilian. What if my immigration status is linked to my partner?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If your Irish immigration permission is linked to the partner perpetrating the violence, <strong>do not assume you have to stay in the relationship to keep your immigration status</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Department of Justice has a procedure for victims and survivors of domestic violence whose permission is linked to the perpetrator to apply for an <strong>independent immigration permission</strong>, where the applicable criteria are met.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Government has also announced a waiver of the immigration registration fee for people who receive this independent permission on domestic violence grounds.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Everyone&#8217;s immigration situation is different. If you hold another type of Stamp, are in an international protection process, hold European citizenship, have children or other specific circumstances, seek proper advice before making an important immigration decision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What if I have children?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If children are involved, include their safety in your plan. Depending on the court order and the situation, dependent children can be included in the protection.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If a child is in immediate danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>. You may also need advice from Tusla, the Child and Family Agency, where there are concerns about a child&#8217;s safety or welfare.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Financial and digital abuse</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Domestic violence can include control over money, accounts, transport or other resources. It can also involve the use of technology to control, stalk or intimidate someone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you suspect your phone or computer is being monitored, be careful when researching help or installing apps. Where possible, use a secure device or ask someone you trust for help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What to do in the next 24 hours?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>If there is immediate danger, call 999 or 112.</li>\n<li>If there is no immediate danger, reach out to someone you trust.</li>\n<li>Contact a specialist service.</li>\n<li>Consider making a safety plan.</li>\n<li>Seek advice about your children, housing and immigration situation.</li>\n<li>Consider speaking to the Garda.</li>\n<li>Seek legal advice if you need a court order.</li>\n<li>Do not confront the perpetrator if that could increase the risk.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important phone numbers</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Contact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Emergency / immediate danger</td><td><strong>999 or 112</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Women&#8217;s Aid</td><td><strong>1800 341 900 &#8212; 24h</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Men&#8217;s Aid</td><td><strong>01 554 3811</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>National Male Advice Line</td><td><strong>1800 816 588</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Legal Aid Board &#8212; domestic violence emergency in Dublin</td><td><strong>01 675 5566</strong></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful official links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful official links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.garda.ie/en/crime/domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Garda Síochána - Domestic Abuse</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-justice-home-affairs-and-migration/policy-information/domestic-violence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Justice - Domestic Violence</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/services/domestic-sexual-gender-based-violence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Domestic, Sexual and Gender-Based Violence Services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/hubs/domestic-violence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Domestic Violence</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/apply-for-a-domestic-violence-court-order\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Apply for a Domestic Violence Court Order</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/court-forms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Courts Service - Court Forms</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.irishimmigration.ie/my-situation-has-changed-since-i-arrived-in-ireland/immigration-guidelines-for-victims-of-domestic-abuse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Immigration Service - Victims of Domestic Abuse</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.legalaidboard.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal Aid Board</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.courts.ie/guides/court-forms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Check the Courts Service forms</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I call the Garda even if I have no evidence?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You can go to the Garda and explain what is happening. The Garda can assess the situation, investigate possible crimes and advise on protective measures and services.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I have to leave home before seeking help?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. You can seek advice and make a safety plan before deciding, as long as it is safe to do so.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I get a court order even if I am not divorced?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Domestic Violence Orders depend on the relationship between the people and on the applicable legal criteria. Being divorced is not a general requirement for every form of protection.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>My partner is responsible for my visa. Do I have to stay with them?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. There are specific procedures for certain victims and survivors whose immigration permission is linked to the perpetrator to apply for an independent permission.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>I am a man. Can I seek help?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. There are specific male support services, including Men&#8217;s Aid and the National Male Advice Line.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>I do not speak English. Can I ask for help?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. When you contact the Garda, say that you need help with the language. The Garda has arrangements to make communication easier for people whose first language is not English.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>One last important thing</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>The violence is not your fault.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You are not responsible for another person&#8217;s abusive behaviour. Nor do you have to make every decision all at once.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are in danger, call <strong>999 or 112</strong>. If you are not in immediate danger, contact a specialist service and talk to someone you trust.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 29 August 2026.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Phone numbers, services, forms, immigration procedures, fees and criteria can change. Always confirm the details directly on the official sources before acting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><em>Notice: This guide is for information only and does not constitute personalised legal, immigration or safety advice. In a situation of immediate danger, call 999 or 112.</em></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Domestic Violence in Ireland: Where to Find Help, How to Report and Your Rights','Where to seek help, how to contact the Garda, protective orders and immigration rights.','inherit','closed','closed','','22387-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','',22387,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22408',0,'revision','',0),(22409,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Winter in Ireland can be challenging. The days get shorter, there is less natural light, temperatures drop and many people spend more time indoors. For anyone living far from family and home country, this period can also bring longing, loneliness and isolation.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For some people, the seasonal changes can significantly affect their mental health. This guide explains how to recognise the signs of seasonal depression, when to seek help and where to find support in Ireland.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🚨 If you are at immediate risk</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you or someone else is at <strong>immediate risk</strong>, do not wait for an online answer. Call <strong>112 or 999</strong> or go to a hospital <strong>Emergency Department</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also advises seeing a GP or an out-of-hours GP service when needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🌧️ Can winter affect our mental health?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. Some people experience a type of depression linked to the changes of the seasons called <strong>Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</strong>, known in Portuguese as seasonal depression or winter depression.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>According to the HSE, symptoms usually start in autumn or winter and tend to improve during spring. For some people they are mild; for others they can significantly interfere with everyday life.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Possible signs</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>persistent sadness or low mood;</li>\n<li>loss of interest or pleasure in activities;</li>\n<li>irritability;</li>\n<li>tiredness and lack of energy;</li>\n<li>anxiety or stress;</li>\n<li>feelings of guilt, worthlessness or hopelessness;</li>\n<li>frequent crying;</li>\n<li>low self-esteem;</li>\n<li>social isolation;</li>\n<li>changes in sleep;</li>\n<li>loss of interest in usual activities;</li>\n<li>thoughts about death or suicide.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Having some of these symptoms does not automatically mean a person has SAD or depression. Persistent or significant changes in mood and behaviour deserve attention.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If symptoms of depression are present most of the day, every day, for more than two weeks, the HSE recommends talking to a GP.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🇧🇷 And for those living far from home?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For many immigrants, winter brings feelings that go beyond the weather: missing family, friends, food, sunshine and the feeling of being at home.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is possible to love the life you have built in Ireland and still miss the life you left behind. Someone can be working, smiling and looking after everyone and still not be emotionally well.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Not all suffering is visible.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💭 &#8220;I&#8217;m fine.&#8221;</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Sometimes the person who says &#8220;I&#8217;m fine&#8221; is just trying to get through the day. One simple question can make a difference: <strong>&#8220;How are you, really?&#8221;</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>And, most importantly: be ready to hear the answer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🫂 How to help someone who is not well?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>❤️ Ask how they are.</li>\n<li>❤️ Listen without judgement.</li>\n<li>❤️ Show them they are not alone.</li>\n<li>❤️ Avoid phrases that minimise their suffering.</li>\n<li>❤️ Encourage them to seek professional help.</li>\n<li>❤️ Stay in contact, especially if they are isolating themselves.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You do not need to be a psychologist to show concern. A conversation can be the first step for someone to accept seeking help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also advises that support services can be contacted when we are worried about someone else, not only when we are going through a personal crisis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🚨 And when someone talks about suicide?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Take it seriously.</strong> If someone says they do not want to live any more, that they cannot take it any more, or shows suicidal thoughts, do not ignore it and do not simply try to convince them to &#8220;think positively&#8221;.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Listen.</li>\n<li>Stay close, when it is safe to do so.</li>\n<li>Encourage them to seek help.</li>\n<li>Use the services available.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE advises that when someone is thinking about suicide it is important to tell someone and to seek support. The person does not have to face those feelings alone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💛 Where to get help in Ireland</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Contact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>🚨 Emergency</td><td><strong>112 or 999</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>💛 Samaritans</td><td><strong>116 123 &#8212; 24/7</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>💛 Pieta</td><td><strong>1800 247 247 &#8212; 24/7</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>💬 Text About It</td><td><strong>HELLO to 50808 &#8212; 24/7</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>🩺 HSE Mental Health Information Line</td><td><strong>1800 111 888</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>👨‍⚕️ Medical care</td><td><strong>GP / GP out of hours / Emergency Department</strong></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>📞 Samaritans</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A confidential, non-judgemental emotional support service.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>116 123</strong> &#8212; available 24 hours a day, every day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Email: <a href=\"mailto:jo@samaritans.ie\">jo@samaritans.ie</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.samaritans.org/samaritans-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Samaritans Ireland - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.samaritans.org/samaritans-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">24/7 emotional support - Samaritans Ireland</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>💛 Pieta</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pieta supports people who are experiencing suicidal thoughts or self-harm, and people affected by someone else&#8217;s suicide.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Crisis Helpline: 1800 247 247 &#8212; 24 hours.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Text support: send HELP to 51444.</strong> The message may be charged according to your plan.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pieta also offers therapy for people affected by suicidal thoughts, self-harm or bereavement by suicide. The service can be reached on <strong>0818 111 126</strong>, subject to the criteria and services available.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.pieta.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pieta - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.pieta.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Crisis support - Pieta</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>💬 Text About It</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For anyone who is not comfortable talking on the phone, there is a text-based alternative.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Send HELLO to 50808.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is a free text conversation service, available 24 hours a day, offering emotional support and help in crisis situations.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://textaboutit.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Text About It - official website</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://textaboutit.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Text About It</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🩺 Talk to your GP</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you notice that your mood has changed, that you are constantly without energy, have lost interest in things, are isolating yourself or feel you are not coping, talk to your <strong>GP (General Practitioner)</strong>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You do not have to wait until a crisis to seek help.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE provides information on mental health services and can point you to where to find support.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>HSE Mental Health Information Line</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>1800 111 888</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is an information line about mental health services. It does not replace an emergency service or clinical advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/your-mental-health-information-line/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Your Mental Health Information Line</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/seasonal-affective-disorder/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE information on Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/suicide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Help for thoughts about suicide</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/get-urgent-help/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Get urgent help for a mental health crisis</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/your-mental-health-information-line/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Your Mental Health Information Line</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🌍 Do you not speak perfect English?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Do not let the language barrier stop you from seeking help. When contacting a health or emergency service, explain that you need help communicating and ask about the available options.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>🌱 Looking after your mental health is also prevention</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Prevention starts before a crisis: when we ask how someone is, we notice changes in behaviour and stop treating emotional suffering as weakness.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Seeking a GP, a psychologist or a support service does not mean someone has failed. Asking for help can be an important step in looking after yourself.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💛 You do not have to get through the winter alone</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the days are hard, reach out to someone. Talk. Ask for help. Listen to whoever needs it. Stay close to the people who make you feel looked after.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Winter passes.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You do not have to solve everything today. Sometimes it is enough to take the next step.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Talk. Listen. Hold space. Seek help.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>💛 A message from Conexão BR Ireland</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We are a community of Brazilians living in Ireland. And we believe that connection also means looking after one another.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If this article made you think of someone, <strong>send it to them</strong>. Maybe they need exactly this information today.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>You are not alone. 🇧🇷🇮🇪💛</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Sources and official information</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/seasonal-affective-disorder/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/suicide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Help for thoughts about suicide</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/get-urgent-help/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Get urgent help for a mental health crisis</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/mental-health/services-support/your-mental-health-information-line/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Your Mental Health Information Line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.samaritans.org/samaritans-ireland/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Samaritans Ireland</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.pieta.ie/how-we-can-help/helpline/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pieta - Crisis Helpline</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://textaboutit.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Text About It</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 31 August 2026.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Phone numbers, services, opening hours and ways of accessing them can change. Always confirm the contacts directly on the official pages before acting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><em>Notice: This guide is for information only and does not replace a professional assessment, diagnosis or treatment. In an emergency or a situation of immediate risk, call 112 or 999 or go to a hospital.</em></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','🌧️ Winter in Ireland and Mental Health: Seasonal Depression, Loneliness and Where to Find Help','Recognise the signs of seasonal depression, when to seek help and which services are available in Ireland.','inherit','closed','closed','','22388-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','',22388,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22409',0,'revision','',0),(22410,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism in Ireland: diagnosis, the HSE and where to find support</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For Brazilians living in Ireland who are autistic &#8212; or who have a child who may be autistic &#8212; it is important to understand how the Irish health system works. The HSE provides information and different kinds of autism-related support, but the route depends on the person&#8217;s age, their needs and the services available in the area where they live.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow\"><p><strong>Important:</strong> this guide is for information and does not replace a medical, psychological or individual professional assessment.</p></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/assessment-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE information on autism assessment and support</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/where-to-get-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Find HSE autism-related support</a> · <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Autism</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is this guide for?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>autistic adults;</li>\n<li>parents or carers of autistic children;</li>\n<li>adults who suspect they may be autistic;</li>\n<li>families moving to Ireland;</li>\n<li>Brazilians who already have a diagnosis from Brazil and want to understand which supports they can seek in Ireland.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>How does autism assessment work in Ireland?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The route varies by age. For children, the HSE describes different assessment and support pathways through health and disability services. For adults, the current HSE information is different: the HSE states that it does not provide adult autism diagnostic assessment, and advises anyone seeking an assessment to consider a private assessment with a psychologist.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Check the official HSE page on <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/assessment-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">autism assessment</a> before starting the process, because services, referrals and availability can change.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>If the person is a child</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you believe your child may be autistic, the HSE recommends speaking to a health or education professional. Depending on the situation, the route may involve the GP, the Public Health Nurse, the Primary Care Team, the Children&#8217;s Disability Network Team (CDNT) or the school.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>book an appointment with the GP, or speak to the health professional who already looks after the child;</li>\n<li>explain which behaviours, communication needs, learning difficulties or sensory issues led you to seek help;</li>\n<li>ask which local service is the right one;</li>\n<li>keep relevant reports and documentation ready to show professionals.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>If the child already has a diagnosis in Brazil</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A diagnosis carried out in Brazil can help explain the child&#8217;s history to the professionals who will support them in Ireland. Bring diagnostic reports, school reports, therapy information and other relevant documents.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>diagnostic report;</li>\n<li>reports from psychologists, therapists or doctors;</li>\n<li>school reports;</li>\n<li>information about therapies carried out;</li>\n<li>a list of medication, where applicable;</li>\n<li>a description of communication and sensory needs.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the documents are in Portuguese, check in advance with the service receiving them whether a translation will be required. Do not assume a certified translation is mandatory: the requirement depends on the service and the purpose of the documents.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Adults who suspect they are autistic</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE currently states that it does not provide adult autism diagnostic assessment. The official advice is to seek a private assessment. The HSE also points to professional organisations such as the Psychological Society of Ireland (PSI) as a reference for finding professionals.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Before booking, confirm directly with the professional what type of assessment is offered, the cost, the timeframe and which documents will be needed. <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/assessment-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - adult autism assessment</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Is it possible to get support without a diagnosis?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that a person does not necessarily have to wait for a formal diagnosis to access certain services. A health professional can advise on the most suitable service for the needs presented.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This can be especially relevant for families waiting for an assessment. See <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/where-to-get-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - where to get support</a> and talk to the GP or another professional who supports the person.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What kinds of support may be available?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Support depends on individual needs. The HSE describes different services and professionals who may be involved, including psychologists, psychiatrists, occupational therapists, speech and language therapists, disability services and mental health services where needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE also points to organisations such as AsIAm and the Irish Society for Autism as sources of information and support. <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/where-to-get-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - where to get support</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Benefits and social supports</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Depending on the person&#8217;s and the family&#8217;s situation, different social welfare payments may exist. The HSE mentions, among others, Carer&#8217;s Allowance, Carer&#8217;s Benefit, Carer&#8217;s Support Grant, Domiciliary Care Allowance and Disability Allowance.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Being autistic does not automatically mean you are entitled to a payment. Each scheme has its own criteria. To check eligibility, contact the Department of Social Protection and Citizens Information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism and education</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For children, talk to the school about individual communication, learning, routine and sensory needs. For third-level education, the HSE also points to DARE as a possible support route for eligible students.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Transitioning from children&#8217;s services to adult services</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The transition should be planned well in advance. The HSE recommends starting to prepare the move to adult services at around 14 or 15 years old where possible. More recent HSE information also indicates that, in some disability services, the transition can begin about 18 months before the young person leaves school.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>See <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/assessment-and-support/changing-from-child-to-adult-care/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - changing from child to adult care</a> and <a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/babies-children/disabilities/services/moving-from-child-to-adult-services/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - moving from child to adult services</a>.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Organisations that can help</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www2.hse.ie/conditions/autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HSE - Autism</a> &#8212; official health information.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://asiam.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AsIAm</a> &#8212; an Irish organisation dedicated to autism.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://autism.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Irish Society for Autism</a> &#8212; autism-related information and resources.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.citizensinformation.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Citizens Information</a> &#8212; information on public services and benefits.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-social-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Social Protection</a> &#8212; social welfare payments and social protection.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>I have an autism diagnosis from Brazil. Do I need another diagnosis in Ireland?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is not possible to say that every Brazilian diagnosis will have to be repeated. The need depends on the service and the purpose. Bring your reports and confirm directly with the Irish service which documents are needed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can adults be assessed by the HSE?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE currently states that it does not provide adult autism diagnostic assessment, and advises seeking a private assessment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>My child is waiting for an assessment. Can they get any support?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The HSE states that certain services can be accessed without waiting for a formal diagnosis, depending on needs. Talk to the GP or another professional to identify the right route.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does a diagnosis automatically give entitlement to a social welfare payment?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. Each payment has its own criteria and must be assessed individually.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 19 August 2026.</strong> We recommend checking the official pages before making decisions, as service availability, criteria, procedures and contacts can change.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Notice:</strong> this guide is for information only. It does not constitute personalised medical, legal, financial or professional advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Autism in Ireland: Diagnosis, HSE and Where to Find Support','How autism assessment works in Ireland for children and adults, and what support exists.','inherit','closed','closed','','22389-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','',22389,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22410',0,'revision','',0);
INSERT INTO `wp_posts` VALUES (22411,0,'2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism and air travel in Ireland: a guide for Dublin and Cork Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Flying can be particularly challenging for some autistic people because of queues, noise, lighting, changes of routine, security, waiting, communication and unfamiliar environments.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport and Cork Airport offer specific resources for passengers with autism and other non-visible disabilities, and some airlines also have preparation programmes and materials.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow\"><p><strong>Important:</strong> airport and airline services, requirements and procedures can change. Always confirm the information directly with the airport and the airline before each trip.</p></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Accessibility/autism at Dublin Airport</a> · <a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Autism support at Cork Airport</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who is this guide for?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>autistic adults;</li>\n<li>parents of autistic children;</li>\n<li>Brazilians travelling between Ireland and Brazil;</li>\n<li>families who have never flown with an autistic person;</li>\n<li>passengers with other non-visible disabilities;</li>\n<li>people who may need more time or a different way of communicating during the journey.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>First of all: tell the airline</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>One of the most important recommendations is to contact the airline before the trip. <a href=\"https://www.aerlingus.com/support/special-assistance/needs-relating-to-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Aer Lingus</a> has a specific area for needs relating to autism and provides an assistance request form.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the flight is not operated by Aer Lingus, do not assume the same services will be available. Check directly with the airline operating the flight.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Prepare the person before the trip</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Making the process predictable can help. Cork Airport recommends preparing the person well in advance using visual resources, photographs and explanations of each step of the journey.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>leaving home;</li>\n<li>arriving at the airport;</li>\n<li>checking in;</li>\n<li>handing over the bag;</li>\n<li>going through security;</li>\n<li>finding the gate;</li>\n<li>waiting;</li>\n<li>boarding;</li>\n<li>sitting on the plane;</li>\n<li>taking off;</li>\n<li>arriving at the destination;</li>\n<li>leaving the airport.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Visit the airport before the trip</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If possible, getting to know the airport before the day of the flight can help. <a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport</a> recommends a prior visit to familiarise the person with the environment, including the movement and the noise.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Use visual resources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Photographs and symbols can help explain what will happen. Cork Airport recommends visual guides for situations such as check-in, security, queues, the gate, boarding and waiting. Aer Lingus also offers visual guides for the different stages of the journey.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important Flyer &#8212; Dublin Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport</a> has a programme called <strong>Important Flyer</strong> for passengers with autism or a Sensory Processing Disorder. The identifier can be a lanyard or a wristband, and it discreetly signals to staff that the passenger may need more time, clear communication, a calmer environment or additional understanding.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How to apply</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>complete the Important Flyer form;</li>\n<li>attach a one-page letter from a GP or an AsIAm card confirming an autism or SPD diagnosis;</li>\n<li>make a separate application for each person who needs the identifier;</li>\n<li>provide the Eircode.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport states that Important Flyer delivery is available to postal addresses in Ireland, including Northern Ireland, and that there is currently no airport collection option.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important Flyer and Fast Track</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport states that Fast Track must be booked in advance. Up to four Fast Track passes can be free when the programme conditions are met. The Important Flyer holder must be travelling; the airport states that up to three companions may travel with the holder using this benefit on the same day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Do not confuse:</strong> having an autism card is not the same as taking part in the Important Flyer programme. You have to follow the airport&#8217;s procedure and make the booking where it applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Sunflower at Dublin Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport</a> takes part in the <strong>Hidden Disabilities Sunflower</strong> scheme. The airport states that its staff are trained to recognise the symbol and to offer additional support, understanding or more time when needed. The airport advises passengers to use the PRM Assist App to request Sunflower use.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://hdsunflower.com/uk/insights/post/airports-around-the-world\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hidden Disabilities Sunflower</a> keeps an international list of participating airports, but the kind of support varies by airport.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Autism &amp; non-visible disabilities</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Sensory room at Dublin Airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/sensory-room\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Sensory Room</a> has Sensory Rooms in both terminals for passengers who may become overwhelmed by the airport environment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>the sessions are free;</li>\n<li>each session lasts 60 minutes;</li>\n<li>there is no walk-in service;</li>\n<li>the rooms are after security;</li>\n<li>there is one room in Terminal 1, near the Gates 200;</li>\n<li>there is one room in Terminal 2, near the Gates 400.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The room must be booked in advance. If you have already booked assistance, an OCS agent can help during security and accompany the passenger to the room and then to the gate.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Cork Airport: Sunflower Lanyard</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport - Autism / ASD</a> offers support for passengers with non-visible disabilities. The airport states that passengers with autism can use a <strong>Sunflower Lanyard</strong>, available free of charge at the OCS desk in the terminal.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport - Autism/ASD support</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Cork Airport: Sensory Pod</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cork Airport has a sensory pod for passengers with non-visible disabilities. The space is free, must be booked, and it is recommended to request it in advance; the airport recommends around 48 hours where possible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Assistance to move through the airport</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the person needs help to move through the airport, request assistance in advance. At Cork Airport the service can help from arrival at the airport to boarding. The airport recommends booking through the airline or the responsible agent, because asking for assistance only on arrival can mean waiting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Identification and assistance are not necessarily the same thing.</strong> A Sunflower lanyard does not replace an assistance booking.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.aerlingus.com/support/special-assistance/needs-relating-to-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Request special assistance with Aer Lingus</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Autism ID cards</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There are different identification cards available in Ireland and they are not equivalent.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>AsIAm Autism ID Card</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://asiam.ie/what-we-do/asiam-autism-id-card\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AsIAm</a> offers an Autism ID Card. The organisation currently states a cost of €27.50, inclusion of a lanyard and holder, processing of up to eight weeks, validity of three years for children and five years for adults, plus formal proof of diagnosis and a photograph.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>AsIAm itself explains that the card serves as proof of diagnosis and can be shown to service providers, but it does not automatically guarantee discounts, preferential treatment or benefits. Any support depends on the service used.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>ASD Ireland ID Card</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://www.asdireland.ie/resources/idcard/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ASD Ireland</a> also has its own card. The organisation currently states a cost of €20 and validity of two years, and requires clinical documentation proving the diagnosis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This card is an ASD Ireland programme of its own and should not be confused with the AsIAm card or the Dublin Airport Important Flyer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>What to put in the bag?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>headphones;</li>\n<li>ear defenders;</li>\n<li>a toy or regulating object;</li>\n<li>a tablet or entertainment device;</li>\n<li>a charger or power bank, where allowed;</li>\n<li>suitable food;</li>\n<li>any medication needed;</li>\n<li>documents;</li>\n<li>identification cards;</li>\n<li>visual resources;</li>\n<li>contact details.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>And medication?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the person takes medication or has specific dietary needs, check the rules of the airline, the airport and the destination country well in advance. Cork Airport states that, in certain situations, a letter from a GP can help explain needs related to medication or diet during the trip.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This does not replace airport security rules or immigration/customs rules.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>And if there is a meltdown or sensory overload?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ol>\n<li>move to a quieter place;</li>\n<li>reduce stimuli;</li>\n<li>use headphones or another familiar resource;</li>\n<li>use simple communication;</li>\n<li>offer a break;</li>\n<li>avoid too many questions;</li>\n<li>follow the visual plan;</li>\n<li>tell the airport team if assistance is needed.</li>\n</ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is no universal strategy for every autistic person. Cork Airport emphasises that each person understands and experiences social situations differently and that strategies must be adapted to the individual.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Checklist before leaving home</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>2&#8211;3 weeks before</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>confirm the flight;</li>\n<li>confirm the assistance services;</li>\n<li>contact the airline;</li>\n<li>check the options available at the airport;</li>\n<li>prepare visual resources;</li>\n<li>practise the travel sequence;</li>\n<li>check documents;</li>\n<li>prepare medication and medical information;</li>\n<li>book the Sensory Room, where available;</li>\n<li>request the Important Flyer, if applicable;</li>\n<li>arrange the Sunflower or another identifier.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>A few days before</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>confirm times;</li>\n<li>check the baggage rules again;</li>\n<li>charge devices;</li>\n<li>prepare the bag;</li>\n<li>print important documents;</li>\n<li>review the visual plan.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>On the day of travel</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>arrive with enough time;</li>\n<li>tell the team if you need assistance;</li>\n<li>keep comfort resources accessible;</li>\n<li>follow the visual sequence;</li>\n<li>allow time for breaks;</li>\n<li>have a plan for delays.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Note: services can change</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Accessibility programmes, availability of rooms, assistance procedures and Fast Track conditions can change. Always confirm directly with the airport, the airline or the responsible organisation before the trip.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/autism-non-visible-disabilities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Autism &amp; Non-Visible Disabilities</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dublinairport.com/accessibility/sensory-room\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dublin Airport - Sensory Room</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.corkairport.com/at-the-airport/help-support/autism-asd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cork Airport - Travelling With Autism</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.aerlingus.com/support/special-assistance/needs-relating-to-autism/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Aer Lingus - Needs Relating to Autism</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://asiam.ie/what-we-do/asiam-autism-id-card\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AsIAm - Autism ID Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.asdireland.ie/resources/idcard/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ASD Ireland - ID Card</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://hdsunflower.com/uk/insights/post/airports-around-the-world\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hidden Disabilities Sunflower - Airports Around the World</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need an autism card to travel?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not necessarily. The cards are a form of identification or proof, but the assistance procedures depend on the airport and the airline.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the AsIAm Autism ID Card guarantee Fast Track?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The AsIAm card and the Dublin Airport Important Flyer are different things. The free Fast Track described by Dublin Airport is tied to the Important Flyer programme and requires advance booking.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I use the Sunflower at the airport?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dublin Airport takes part in the Hidden Disabilities Sunflower network and states that its staff are trained to recognise the symbol. Cork Airport also provides Sunflower lanyards.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Is the Dublin Airport sensory room free?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes. The airport states that the sessions are free, last 60 minutes and must be booked in advance. There are no walk-ins.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I just arrive at the airport and ask for assistance?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is better not to rely on that. Cork Airport recommends booking assistance in advance because there may be a wait if the request is only made on arrival.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the airline need to know I am travelling with an autistic person?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is highly recommended to tell the airline in advance and ask which assistance services are available. Aer Lingus has a specific procedure for needs relating to autism.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Does the Sunflower work in other countries?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Hidden Disabilities Sunflower network is international and includes many airports, but the kind of support offered varies. Always check the specific airport before travelling.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Information checked: 19 August 2026.</strong> Card prices, booking rules, room availability, assistance procedures and Fast Track conditions can change. Always confirm directly with the airport, the airline or the responsible organisation before the trip.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Notice:</strong> this guide is for information and does not constitute personalised medical, legal or travel advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Autism and Air Travel in Ireland: Dublin and Cork Airport','Important Flyer, Sunflower, sensory rooms and what to do if you lose the card.','inherit','closed','closed','','22390-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:36:49','2026-09-25 13:36:49','',22390,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22411',0,'revision','',0),(22413,0,'2026-09-25 14:46:19','2026-09-25 13:46:19','<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Meta description:</strong> Understand the route for Brazilians who cannot exchange their foreign licence: theory test, learner permit, EDT and driving test.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Suggested URL:</strong> /guias/learner-permit-theory-test-irlanda-cnh-brasileira/</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Introduction</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Exchanging a foreign licence depends on exchange agreements. When the licence cannot be exchanged, the driver may have to follow the Irish process to obtain the licence.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Who this guide is for</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brazilians with a foreign driving licence who need to understand the route to an Irish licence when no exchange applies.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>First check with the NDLS whether your licence is eligible for exchange.</li>\n<li>If you need the learner driver route, pass the Driver Theory Test for the correct category.</li>\n<li>Apply for the first learner permit through the NDLS, noting identity, address, ordinary residence, PPSN and reports where applicable.</li>\n<li>If you hold a full licence from a country with no exchange agreement, check whether you are entitled to the Reduced EDT Programme.</li>\n<li>For the first learner permit, the RSA states it must be held for at least six months and the required training completed before the driving test.</li>\n<li>Book the driving test once all the requirements are met.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Documents and information needed</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Passport or an accepted document.</li>\n<li>PPSN.</li>\n<li>Proof of address where required.</li>\n<li>Proof of ordinary residence where applicable.</li>\n<li>Theory test certificate.</li>\n<li>Eye or medical report, if required.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Costs and fees</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The theory test, learner permit, lessons and driving test each have their own costs. Check RSA/NDLS for the current amounts.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Timelines and important dates</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The RSA states that the learner permit must be applied for within two years of passing the theory test; after that the certificate expires.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Common mistakes and points to note</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li>Driving unaccompanied with a learner permit.</li>\n<li>Booking the theory test on an unofficial site.</li>\n<li>Ignoring the six months for the first learner permit.</li>\n<li>Assuming a Brazilian licence is automatically exchangeable.</li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Official sources</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - learner permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/apply-for-your-first-permit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - first permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/theory-test/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - theory test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Useful links</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:list --><ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - learner permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/apply-for-your-first-permit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - first permit</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/theory-test/what-it-is\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RSA - theory test</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS</a></li>\n</ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I exchange my Brazilian licence directly?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It depends on the exchange rules in force. Check with the NDLS before starting the process.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Do I need to take the theory test?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For the first learner permit, the RSA states that you must pass the theory test for the category.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>How long do I hold a learner permit?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For the first learner permit, the RSA states six months, in addition to the required training.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h3>Can I drive on my own?</h3><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>No. The learner permit rules require an accompanying driver according to the category and the applicable conditions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Official links:</strong> <a href=\"https://theorytest.ie/book-your-theory-test/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Book the Driver Theory Test</a> · <a href=\"https://www.rsa.ie/services/learner-drivers/learner-permit/apply-for-your-first-permit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apply for your first Learner Permit</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ndls.ie/learner-driver/learner-permit.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NDLS - Learner Permit</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Last checked</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Information checked on 18 August 2026. Reconfirm fees, deadlines, forms, occupation lists, opening hours and procedures before publication and before advising readers on a specific case.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2>Important notice</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For information only; it does not replace RSA/NDLS guidance or legal advice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\n\n','Brazilian Driving Licence in Ireland: When You Need the Theory Test and Learner Permit','The route for Brazilians who cannot exchange their licence: theory test, learner permit, EDT and driving test.','inherit','closed','closed','','22400-revision-v1','','','2026-09-25 14:46:19','2026-09-25 13:46:19','',22400,'http://localhost:8080/?p=22413',0,'revision','',0),(22415,283039560,'2026-09-25 00:00:00','2026-09-25 00:00:00','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Para nós, brasileiros que moramos na Irlanda, a chegada do outono pode ser uma mudança e tanto.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Os dias começam a ficar mais curtos, temos menos horas de luz, as temperaturas caem e, aos poucos, nossa rotina também muda. Podemos perceber mudanças na nossa energia, no humor, no apetite e até naquela vontade de sair de casa e se movimentar.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">O outono muda nossa rotina</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>E a nossa alimentação também acompanha essa mudança.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Talvez aquela salada que parecia deliciosa no verão já não seja tão convidativa. O smoothie gelado perde espaço e começamos a procurar comidas mais quentinhas e reconfortantes.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p><strong>E está tudo bem. 💚</strong></p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Uma alimentação saudável não precisa ser igual o ano inteiro. Podemos adaptar nossas escolhas às estações, respeitando nossas preferências e, ao mesmo tempo, mantendo uma alimentação equilibrada e nutritiva.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Adaptando a alimentação ao outono</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\r\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">🍲 Não está com vontade de comer salada?</h3>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Você não precisa se obrigar. Sopas, ensopados, legumes assados, refogados ou grelhados são ótimas maneiras de continuar incluindo vegetais nas refeições.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\r\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">🍎 E as frutas?</h3>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Elas também podem ganhar uma versão mais aconchegante. Experimente maçã ou pera assada com canela, frutas cozidas ou inclua frutas em preparações como panquecas de banana, overnight oats quentinho ou um bolo caseiro de maçã.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\r\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">💧 Percebeu que está bebendo menos água?</h3>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Isso pode acontecer com a chegada do frio. Deixe sua garrafinha por perto e aproveite para incluir chás e infusões sem açúcar ao longo do dia. Eles também podem contribuir para a sua hidratação.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} -->\r\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">🥣 Aproveite os pratos quentinhos para aumentar a variedade</h3>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Sopas e os famosos <em>stews</em> daqui da Irlanda podem ser uma ótima oportunidade para incluir diferentes vegetais, feijão, lentilha, grão-de-bico e outras leguminosas. Comida quentinha e reconfortante também pode ser nutritiva.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Adaptar, não lutar contra a estação</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>E talvez essa seja uma das coisas mais importantes para lembrar nessa época: não precisamos lutar contra a estação.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Podemos adaptar nossa alimentação, nossa rotina e nossos hábitos ao que esse momento do ano pede, sem abandonar o cuidado com a nossa saúde.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Guia de Sobrevivência para o Outono na Irlanda 🍂</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Por isso, preparei um Guia de Sobrevivência para o Outono na Irlanda 🍂, com dicas práticas para ajudar você a passar pelos meses mais frios cuidando da alimentação e do bem-estar. Vou deixar o guia aqui para vocês. 💚</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Acompanhamento individualizado</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>E se você sente que precisa de um acompanhamento mais individualizado, seja para emagrecimento e controle de peso, colesterol elevado, pré-diabetes, melhorar sua relação com a comida ou simplesmente construir hábitos mais saudáveis que realmente funcionem na sua rotina aqui na Irlanda, me mande uma mensagem.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Será um prazer te acompanhar. 🌿</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:separator --><hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\" /><!-- /wp:separator -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sobre Marcela Ferreira</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p><strong>Nutri Marcela Ferreira</strong><br />Consultas online e presenciais em Kilcock<br />Instagram: <strong>@marcela.holisticnutrition</strong></p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>📲 Entre em contato comigo pelo WhatsApp para informações e agendamentos.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','O outono chegou à Irlanda… e o nosso corpo também sente essa mudança','Para brasileiros que vivem na Irlanda, o outono traz dias mais curtos e mudanças na rotina. Veja ideias simples para adaptar a alimentação, manter a hidratação e cuidar do bem-estar nos meses mais frios.','publish','closed','closed','','outono-irlanda-alimentacao-bem-estar','','','2026-09-25 17:33:52','2026-09-25 16:33:52','',0,'https://conexaobr.ie/?guide=outono-irlanda-alimentacao-bem-estar',0,'guide','',0),(22416,283039560,'2026-09-25 17:33:52','2026-09-25 16:33:52','<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Para nós, brasileiros que moramos na Irlanda, a chegada do outono pode ser uma mudança e tanto.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Os dias começam a ficar mais curtos, temos menos horas de luz, as temperaturas caem e, aos poucos, nossa rotina também muda. Podemos perceber mudanças na nossa energia, no humor, no apetite e até naquela vontade de sair de casa e se movimentar.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">O outono muda nossa rotina</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>E a nossa alimentação também acompanha essa mudança.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Talvez aquela salada que parecia deliciosa no verão já não seja tão convidativa. O smoothie gelado perde espaço e começamos a procurar comidas mais quentinhas e reconfortantes.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p><strong>E está tudo bem. 💚</strong></p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\r\n<p>Uma alimentação saudável não precisa ser igual o ano inteiro. Podemos adaptar nossas escolhas às estações, respeitando nossas preferências e, ao mesmo tempo, mantendo uma alimentação equilibrada e nutritiva.</p>\r\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2} -->\r\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Adaptando a alimentação ao outono</h2>\r\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\r\n\r\n<!-- wp:heading {\"level\":