://localhost:8080/2026/03/20/health-and-wellbeing-support-in-ireland/',0,'post','',0),(28660,0,'2026-09-26 15:25:36','2026-09-26 14:25:36','<p></p>\n','Health and Wellbeing Support in Ireland','','inherit','closed','closed','','28659-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 15:25:36','2026-09-26 14:25:36','',28659,'http://localhost:8080/?p=28660',0,'revision','',0),(28661,283039559,'2026-03-17 20:43:02','2026-03-17 20:43:02','<p>Text: Patricia Vidal</p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>\n','A Health Tip for Travellers','Text: Patricia Vidal. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/','publish','open','open','','a-health-tip-for-travellers','','','2026-09-26 15:25:37','2026-09-26 14:25:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/17/a-health-tip-for-travellers/',0,'post','',0),(28662,0,'2026-09-26 15:25:37','2026-09-26 14:25:37','<p>Text: Patricia Vidal</p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>\n','A Health Tip for Travellers','Text: Patricia Vidal. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/','inherit','closed','closed','','28661-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 15:25:37','2026-09-26 14:25:37','',28661,'http://localhost:8080/?p=28662',0,'revision','',0),(28663,283039559,'2026-01-03 14:20:50','2026-01-03 14:20:50','<p></p><p> One of the most complete sites for our region <strong>is</strong> <u>Laois Tourism</u>, where you will find attractions, dining recommendations and events across the county.</p><p></p><p></p>\n','Tourism and Leisure in County Laois, Ireland','One of the most complete sites for our region is Laois Tourism, where you will find attractions, dining recommendations and events across the county.','publish','open','open','','tourism-and-leisure-in-county-laois-ireland','','','2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/03/tourism-and-leisure-in-county-laois-ireland/',0,'post','',0),(28664,0,'2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','<p></p><p> One of the most complete sites for our region <strong>is</strong> <u>Laois Tourism</u>, where you will find attractions, dining recommendations and events across the county.</p><p></p><p></p>\n','Tourism and Leisure in County Laois, Ireland','One of the most complete sites for our region is Laois Tourism, where you will find attractions, dining recommendations and events across the county.','inherit','closed','closed','','28663-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','',28663,'http://localhost:8080/?p=28664',0,'revision','',0),(28665,283039559,'2026-01-03 14:23:37','2026-01-03 14:23:37','<p><u>https://heyzine.com/flip-book/CartilhaDM.html#page/1</u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>\n','Information for Women in Ireland','A practical guide for women in Ireland, published as a flip book.','publish','open','open','','information-for-women-in-ireland','','','2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/03/information-for-women-in-ireland/',0,'post','',0),(28666,0,'2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','<p><u>https://heyzine.com/flip-book/CartilhaDM.html#page/1</u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>\n','Information for Women in Ireland','A practical guide for women in Ireland, published as a flip book.','inherit','closed','closed','','28665-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','',28665,'http://localhost:8080/?p=28666',0,'revision','',0),(28667,283039559,'2026-02-15 21:36:40','2026-02-15 21:36:40','<h2>By: Chef Anderson Balico, Food Content Creator &amp; Digital Marketing</h2><p></p><p></p><p></p><p><strong>Ingredients</strong></p><p><strong>400 g of strips of beef</strong></p><p><strong>2 tablespoons of Korean BBQ ÍON Organic Seasoning</strong></p><p><strong>2 tablespoons of soy sauce (shoyu)</strong></p><p><strong>1 tablespoon of honey</strong></p><p><strong>1 tablespoon of ÍON sunflower oil</strong></p><p><strong>1 red pepper, sliced</strong></p><p><strong>1 onion, sliced</strong></p><p><strong>1 spring onion, finely chopped</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Method:</strong></p><p><strong>Coat the beef with the soy sauce, honey and Korean BBQ seasoning. Leave to marinate for 15 minutes.</strong></p><p><strong>Heat the sunflower oil in a very hot frying pan or wok and stir-fry the beef for about 5 minutes, until golden.</strong></p><p><strong>Add the onion and red pepper and cook for a further 3 to 4 minutes, until tender.</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Finish with the spring onion and serve with rice or pasta.</strong></p><p></p><p><u><strong>@chefandersonbalico</strong></u></p>\n','Korean BBQ Style Stir-Fried Beef','By: Chef Anderson Balico, Food Content Creator &amp; Digital Marketing.','publish','open','open','','korean-bbq-style-stir-fried-beef','','','2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/02/15/korean-bbq-style-stir-fried-beef/',0,'post','',0),(28668,0,'2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','<h2>By: Chef Anderson Balico, Food Content Creator &amp; Digital Marketing</h2><p></p><p></p><p></p><p><strong>Ingredients</strong></p><p><strong>400 g of strips of beef</strong></p><p><strong>2 tablespoons of Korean BBQ ÍON Organic Seasoning</strong></p><p><strong>2 tablespoons of soy sauce (shoyu)</strong></p><p><strong>1 tablespoon of honey</strong></p><p><strong>1 tablespoon of ÍON sunflower oil</strong></p><p><strong>1 red pepper, sliced</strong></p><p><strong>1 onion, sliced</strong></p><p><strong>1 spring onion, finely chopped</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Method:</strong></p><p><strong>Coat the beef with the soy sauce, honey and Korean BBQ seasoning. Leave to marinate for 15 minutes.</strong></p><p><strong>Heat the sunflower oil in a very hot frying pan or wok and stir-fry the beef for about 5 minutes, until golden.</strong></p><p><strong>Add the onion and red pepper and cook for a further 3 to 4 minutes, until tender.</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Finish with the spring onion and serve with rice or pasta.</strong></p><p></p><p><u><strong>@chefandersonbalico</strong></u></p>\n','Korean BBQ Style Stir-Fried Beef','By: Chef Anderson Balico, Food Content Creator &amp; Digital Marketing.','inherit','closed','closed','','28667-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 15:25:38','2026-09-26 14:25:38','',28667,'http://localhost:8080/?p=28668',0,'revision','',0),(29152,283039559,'2025-12-29 22:47:44','2025-12-29 22:47:44','<p><u><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>The Sun and the Moon play a fascinating role in our lives, influencing both our consciousness and our emotions.</p><p>The Sun, with its majestic presence, is our central star, providing the vital energy that keeps us alive. It symbolises consciousness, self-assertion and the capacity to express our own light into the world. When we think of the Sun, we can imagine the strength and confidence it inspires in us, like a powerful and just lion.</p><p>The Moon, on the other hand, is the queen of emotions and emotional intelligence. She reflects the light of the Sun and is intimately connected to our most sensitive side. The Moon influences not only the tides and nature, but also our feelings, intuitions and memories. Each lunar phase affects us in its own way, from the introspection of the new moon to the intensity of the full moon.</p><p>During the winter in northern Europe, when the days are short and the nights long, keeping a mental and emotional balance can be challenging. Here are a few practical tips to help you face this season:</p><ul><li><p>Get out in natural light whenever you can: make the most of the sunny moments to help regulate your body clock and improve your mood.</p></li><li><p>Exercise regularly: exercise does not only warm the body, it also releases endorphins, which are essential for mental wellbeing.</p></li><li><p>Keep a routine: fixed times for waking, eating and sleeping help maintain emotional stability.</p></li><li><p>Cultivate moments of self-care: meditation, relaxing reading or a warm bath can help calm the mind and nurture the emotions.</p></li><li><p>Stay connected with other people: even when the weather invites you to withdraw, keeping in touch with friends and family is crucial for emotional support.</p></li></ul><p>It is worth noticing that many cultures around the world have different interpretations of the influence of the Sun and the Moon on our lives. Some see the Sun as a symbol of authority and leadership, while others associate it with a spiritual guide. In the same way, the Moon, in its constant change, is seen as a magical and seductive force inviting us to reflect and to connect with our inner self.</p><p>Would you like to know more about how these celestial forces can influence your daily life? Leave a comment and I will bring more information in the next article.</p><p></p><p></p><p><u><strong>Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p>\n','Sun and Moon: Balancing Consciousness and Emotions Through the Northern European Winter','The Sun and the Moon play a fascinating role in our lives, influencing both our consciousness and our emotions. Practical tips for facing the northern European winter.','publish','open','open','','sun-and-moon-balancing-consciousness-and-emotions-in-the-northern-european-winter','','','2026-09-26 16:26:41','2026-09-26 15:26:41','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2025/12/29/sun-and-moon-balancing-consciousness-and-emotions-in-the-northern-european-winter/',0,'post','',0),(29153,0,'2026-09-26 16:26:41','2026-09-26 15:26:41','<p><u><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>The Sun and the Moon play a fascinating role in our lives, influencing both our consciousness and our emotions.</p><p>The Sun, with its majestic presence, is our central star, providing the vital energy that keeps us alive. It symbolises consciousness, self-assertion and the capacity to express our own light into the world. When we think of the Sun, we can imagine the strength and confidence it inspires in us, like a powerful and just lion.</p><p>The Moon, on the other hand, is the queen of emotions and emotional intelligence. She reflects the light of the Sun and is intimately connected to our most sensitive side. The Moon influences not only the tides and nature, but also our feelings, intuitions and memories. Each lunar phase affects us in its own way, from the introspection of the new moon to the intensity of the full moon.</p><p>During the winter in northern Europe, when the days are short and the nights long, keeping a mental and emotional balance can be challenging. Here are a few practical tips to help you face this season:</p><ul><li><p>Get out in natural light whenever you can: make the most of the sunny moments to help regulate your body clock and improve your mood.</p></li><li><p>Exercise regularly: exercise does not only warm the body, it also releases endorphins, which are essential for mental wellbeing.</p></li><li><p>Keep a routine: fixed times for waking, eating and sleeping help maintain emotional stability.</p></li><li><p>Cultivate moments of self-care: meditation, relaxing reading or a warm bath can help calm the mind and nurture the emotions.</p></li><li><p>Stay connected with other people: even when the weather invites you to withdraw, keeping in touch with friends and family is crucial for emotional support.</p></li></ul><p>It is worth noticing that many cultures around the world have different interpretations of the influence of the Sun and the Moon on our lives. Some see the Sun as a symbol of authority and leadership, while others associate it with a spiritual guide. In the same way, the Moon, in its constant change, is seen as a magical and seductive force inviting us to reflect and to connect with our inner self.</p><p>Would you like to know more about how these celestial forces can influence your daily life? Leave a comment and I will bring more information in the next article.</p><p></p><p></p><p><u><strong>Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p>\n','Sun and Moon: Balancing Consciousness and Emotions Through the Northern European Winter','The Sun and the Moon play a fascinating role in our lives, influencing both our consciousness and our emotions. Practical tips for facing the northern European winter.','inherit','closed','closed','','29152-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:26:41','2026-09-26 15:26:41','',29152,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29153',0,'revision','',0),(29154,283039559,'2026-01-03 12:28:14','2026-01-03 12:28:14','<p><u><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p><p></p><p></p><p>There is a season inside every soul in which everything grows quieter.</p><p>A gentle inner winter, in which the Ego loosens its grip and everything in excess begins to fall away.</p><p>Winter is not a punishment — it is preparation.</p><p></p><p>It strips us gently, like the trees letting go of their leaves, to reveal what is</p><p>true beneath the noise: the procrastination, the fear, the resistance, the unconscious</p><p>search for validation.</p><p>Everything that limits our capacity to give, to grow and to serve.</p><p>This is the sacred process of deconstructing the Ego — the cleaning of the inner soil.</p><p>When we surrender, even in silence, we launch our intention to the Universe.</p><p>Not out of the Ego’s need to receive, but out of the soul’s readiness to grow.</p><p>True spirituality begins here: in honesty, in service, in the willingness to let old identities dissolve.</p><p>Winter teaches us our limits and our desires, what no longer belongs, and what still</p><p>pulses to stay.</p><p>And then, without effort, Spring arrives.</p><p>Spring is born when the Ego softens, when the inner ground thaws just enough for new seeds to put down roots.</p><p>All at once clarity becomes action, love becomes presence, and the act of giving becomes the natural language of the heart.</p><p>There is no blossoming without its Winter.</p><p>You cannot sow into frozen ground.</p><p>You have to allow yourself to be taken apart first.</p><p>This is the rhythm of healing:</p><p>Winter cleanses.</p><p>Spring is reborn.</p><p>And the soul, patient and luminous, crosses the bridge between the two.</p><p></p><p><u><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p>\n','The Winter of the Ego — A Poetic Rebirth','There is a season inside every soul in which everything grows quieter: a gentle inner winter where the Ego loosens its grip and everything in excess begins to fall away.','publish','open','open','','the-winter-of-the-ego-a-poetic-rebirth','','','2026-09-26 16:26:42','2026-09-26 15:26:42','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/03/the-winter-of-the-ego-a-poetic-rebirth/',0,'post','',0),(29155,0,'2026-09-26 16:26:42','2026-09-26 15:26:42','<p><u><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p><p></p><p></p><p>There is a season inside every soul in which everything grows quieter.</p><p>A gentle inner winter, in which the Ego loosens its grip and everything in excess begins to fall away.</p><p>Winter is not a punishment — it is preparation.</p><p></p><p>It strips us gently, like the trees letting go of their leaves, to reveal what is</p><p>true beneath the noise: the procrastination, the fear, the resistance, the unconscious</p><p>search for validation.</p><p>Everything that limits our capacity to give, to grow and to serve.</p><p>This is the sacred process of deconstructing the Ego — the cleaning of the inner soil.</p><p>When we surrender, even in silence, we launch our intention to the Universe.</p><p>Not out of the Ego’s need to receive, but out of the soul’s readiness to grow.</p><p>True spirituality begins here: in honesty, in service, in the willingness to let old identities dissolve.</p><p>Winter teaches us our limits and our desires, what no longer belongs, and what still</p><p>pulses to stay.</p><p>And then, without effort, Spring arrives.</p><p>Spring is born when the Ego softens, when the inner ground thaws just enough for new seeds to put down roots.</p><p>All at once clarity becomes action, love becomes presence, and the act of giving becomes the natural language of the heart.</p><p>There is no blossoming without its Winter.</p><p>You cannot sow into frozen ground.</p><p>You have to allow yourself to be taken apart first.</p><p>This is the rhythm of healing:</p><p>Winter cleanses.</p><p>Spring is reborn.</p><p>And the soul, patient and luminous, crosses the bridge between the two.</p><p></p><p><u><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p>\n','The Winter of the Ego — A Poetic Rebirth','There is a season inside every soul in which everything grows quieter: a gentle inner winter where the Ego loosens its grip and everything in excess begins to fall away.','inherit','closed','closed','','29154-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:26:42','2026-09-26 15:26:42','',29154,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29155',0,'revision','',0),(29156,283039559,'2026-01-03 12:57:26','2026-01-03 12:57:26','<p><u><em><strong>Text: Ana Luiza A Duarte</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Integrative Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>December arrives as a warm embrace in the middle of the cold.</p><p>While the streets light up and the world seems to adorn itself with promises, there are those who feel a sweet longing — for home, for family, for the language, for the smell of fresh coffee coming from the kitchen. It is the heart trying to find its place even far from where it began. It is natural to miss the sun that accompanied us all year long. To miss the light that warmed the skin, the full table, the familiar voices. But perhaps it is now, in the middle of this foreign winter, that we can rediscover another kind of warmth — the kind that is born from meeting, from sharing, from presence.</p><p>Missing home can be a bridge. It can take us to the other, to the new community that welcomes us, to the affinities that bring us together. An invitation to open the doors of the house and of the heart, to build bonds that warm us when the sun is slow to appear.</p><p>And while the year says goodbye, something in us is also reorganising itself. The silence between one celebration and the next invites us to look inwards: what do we want to carry forward? What do we leave to bloom again in 2025?</p><p>There is always a spark that holds on, even on the shortest days.</p><p>Perhaps the secret is to remember that the light we seek so much on the outside also lives</p><p>inside us.</p><p>That the summer warmth left behind can be rekindled in the smile of a new friend,</p><p>in a gesture of care, in a moment of gratitude.</p><p>So this December, light your own sun.</p><p>Gather. Share. Celebrate — even from a distance.</p><p>Because when we meet, home happens again — wherever we are.</p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p><u>Ana Luiza A Duarte</u></p><p><u>Integrative Therapist</u></p>\n','The Sun That Lives in Us','December arrives as a warm embrace in the middle of the cold. Missing home can be a bridge, not just a weight.','publish','open','open','','the-sun-that-lives-in-us','','','2026-09-26 16:26:42','2026-09-26 15:26:42','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/03/the-sun-that-lives-in-us/',0,'post','',0),(29157,0,'2026-09-26 16:26:42','2026-09-26 15:26:42','<p><u><em><strong>Text: Ana Luiza A Duarte</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Integrative Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>December arrives as a warm embrace in the middle of the cold.</p><p>While the streets light up and the world seems to adorn itself with promises, there are those who feel a sweet longing — for home, for family, for the language, for the smell of fresh coffee coming from the kitchen. It is the heart trying to find its place even far from where it began. It is natural to miss the sun that accompanied us all year long. To miss the light that warmed the skin, the full table, the familiar voices. But perhaps it is now, in the middle of this foreign winter, that we can rediscover another kind of warmth — the kind that is born from meeting, from sharing, from presence.</p><p>Missing home can be a bridge. It can take us to the other, to the new community that welcomes us, to the affinities that bring us together. An invitation to open the doors of the house and of the heart, to build bonds that warm us when the sun is slow to appear.</p><p>And while the year says goodbye, something in us is also reorganising itself. The silence between one celebration and the next invites us to look inwards: what do we want to carry forward? What do we leave to bloom again in 2025?</p><p>There is always a spark that holds on, even on the shortest days.</p><p>Perhaps the secret is to remember that the light we seek so much on the outside also lives</p><p>inside us.</p><p>That the summer warmth left behind can be rekindled in the smile of a new friend,</p><p>in a gesture of care, in a moment of gratitude.</p><p>So this December, light your own sun.</p><p>Gather. Share. Celebrate — even from a distance.</p><p>Because when we meet, home happens again — wherever we are.</p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p><u>Ana Luiza A Duarte</u></p><p><u>Integrative Therapist</u></p>\n','The Sun That Lives in Us','December arrives as a warm embrace in the middle of the cold. Missing home can be a bridge, not just a weight.','inherit','closed','closed','','29156-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:26:42','2026-09-26 15:26:42','',29156,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29157',0,'revision','',0),(29158,283039559,'2026-01-03 14:21:24','2026-01-03 14:21:24','<p><u><em>Text: Ana Luiza A Duarte</em></u></p><p><u><em>Integrative Therapist</em></u></p><p></p><p>Winter arrives with its dense air, its short days and a certain unavoidable withdrawal. And while the world seems to fall asleep, something in us begins to awaken. It is time to listen to what the body is asking for, what the heart whispers, what the soul wants to keep.</p><p>Cold, sometimes, is a form of care: it slows us down, preserves, protects what is yet to bloom. And since it is time to preserve and prepare, perhaps winter is also an invitation to choose.</p><p>Before sowing the seeds, you have to know <strong>what you want to see grow</strong>. Which dreams deserve to germinate when the sun warms the earth again? Which ideas, feelings or plans do you</p><p>want to nurture now, in the silence, so that they sprout with strength when the season turns?</p><p>Because inner warmth is not limited to the body — it can take the form of <strong>ideas that catch light</strong>, of <strong>intentions that warm</strong>, of <strong>plans that take shape</strong> even in the cold.</p><p>They are small embers that keep alive the hope of a new blossoming.</p><p>While everything outside seems quiet, inside us something reorganises itself. What looks like a pause is, in fact, preparation. Rest is the fertile ground for everything that is to come.</p><p>There is a kind of warmth that does not come from fire — it is born slowly, in silence, when the cold invites us to turn inwards.</p><p>It is a warmth that springs from the heart and needs to be nurtured with care. The mind, fed by prosperous thoughts, by inspiring ideas and by gestures that awaken creativity, helps keep that flame lit. This warmth heals us, wakes us, and allows us to give <strong>new ideas their first flight</strong> — small sparks that can bring us closer to ourselves and to the people around us.</p><p>Perhaps it is exactly this withdrawal that lets us <strong>reach our creative source.</strong></p><p>To create, we have to become sensitive to what surrounds us, notice the possibilities around us, choose what inspires us and turn it into something unique — an action, an expression, an idea carrying passion and determination. When the noise outside diminishes, we manage to hear those subtle voices within us, the ones asking for space, form and colour.</p><p>Winter, with its calm, creates the perfect ground for imagining and recreating what we want to live.</p><p>So, light your little fires: a book, a song, a hot soup, a good memory. Allow yourself that warmth born from within, spreading slowly.</p><p><em>Winter is a reminder that true warmth is not only in the sun — it is in what we choose to nurture when the world grows cold.</em></p><p></p><p></p><p><u><strong>Ana Luiza A Duarte</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Integrative Therapist</strong></u></p>\n','The Warmth That Is Born of Winter','Winter arrives with its dense air, its short days and an unavoidable inwardness — and while the world seems to fall asleep, something in us begins to wake.','publish','open','open','','the-warmth-born-of-winter','','','2026-09-26 16:26:43','2026-09-26 15:26:43','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/03/the-warmth-born-of-winter/',0,'post','',0),(29159,0,'2026-09-26 16:26:43','2026-09-26 15:26:43','<p><u><em>Text: Ana Luiza A Duarte</em></u></p><p><u><em>Integrative Therapist</em></u></p><p></p><p>Winter arrives with its dense air, its short days and a certain unavoidable withdrawal. And while the world seems to fall asleep, something in us begins to awaken. It is time to listen to what the body is asking for, what the heart whispers, what the soul wants to keep.</p><p>Cold, sometimes, is a form of care: it slows us down, preserves, protects what is yet to bloom. And since it is time to preserve and prepare, perhaps winter is also an invitation to choose.</p><p>Before sowing the seeds, you have to know <strong>what you want to see grow</strong>. Which dreams deserve to germinate when the sun warms the earth again? Which ideas, feelings or plans do you</p><p>want to nurture now, in the silence, so that they sprout with strength when the season turns?</p><p>Because inner warmth is not limited to the body — it can take the form of <strong>ideas that catch light</strong>, of <strong>intentions that warm</strong>, of <strong>plans that take shape</strong> even in the cold.</p><p>They are small embers that keep alive the hope of a new blossoming.</p><p>While everything outside seems quiet, inside us something reorganises itself. What looks like a pause is, in fact, preparation. Rest is the fertile ground for everything that is to come.</p><p>There is a kind of warmth that does not come from fire — it is born slowly, in silence, when the cold invites us to turn inwards.</p><p>It is a warmth that springs from the heart and needs to be nurtured with care. The mind, fed by prosperous thoughts, by inspiring ideas and by gestures that awaken creativity, helps keep that flame lit. This warmth heals us, wakes us, and allows us to give <strong>new ideas their first flight</strong> — small sparks that can bring us closer to ourselves and to the people around us.</p><p>Perhaps it is exactly this withdrawal that lets us <strong>reach our creative source.</strong></p><p>To create, we have to become sensitive to what surrounds us, notice the possibilities around us, choose what inspires us and turn it into something unique — an action, an expression, an idea carrying passion and determination. When the noise outside diminishes, we manage to hear those subtle voices within us, the ones asking for space, form and colour.</p><p>Winter, with its calm, creates the perfect ground for imagining and recreating what we want to live.</p><p>So, light your little fires: a book, a song, a hot soup, a good memory. Allow yourself that warmth born from within, spreading slowly.</p><p><em>Winter is a reminder that true warmth is not only in the sun — it is in what we choose to nurture when the world grows cold.</em></p><p></p><p></p><p><u><strong>Ana Luiza A Duarte</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Integrative Therapist</strong></u></p>\n','The Warmth That Is Born of Winter','Winter arrives with its dense air, its short days and an unavoidable inwardness — and while the world seems to fall asleep, something in us begins to wake.','inherit','closed','closed','','29158-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:26:43','2026-09-26 15:26:43','',29158,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29159',0,'revision','',0),(29160,283039559,'2026-01-03 14:25:22','2026-01-03 14:25:22','<p>The role of food, rest and grounding in the autumn transition.</p><p></p><p><u><em>Text: Simone Holz</em></u></p><p><u><em>Holistic Nutritional Therapist</em></u></p><p></p><p></p><p><em>Autumn</em> is the season in which the mornings begin to turn colder, the sun says goodbye earlier and nature silently shifts its rhythm. The trees let go of what no longer serves them, and that same movement invites us to introspect, to turn inwards, to nourish the roots and to prepare body, mind and soul for the stillness of the winter to come.</p><p>Just as the earth reorganises itself, our body enters this same process of adaptation. In the first weeks of autumn it is common to feel heavier, with muscles and joints more sensitive, a tiredness that arrives without warning, or a deeper sleep that asks for more time at rest. All of this is natural: the</p><p>organism is detoxifying the excess heat, sugar and stimulation of summer — the ice creams, the long days, the accelerated pace.</p><p>Autumn is nature’s gentle reminder that it is time to slow down, clear what has accumulated and strengthen our foundations. It is the time to “put down roots” and let go of what has already done its job,</p><p>be it toxins, emotions or habits that no longer sustain our vitality.</p><p>Through autumn our body begins to ask for more nourishing, denser and warmer foods, and it is not by chance. This is the moment when we need to strengthen our immune</p><p>system and build energy reserves for winter.</p><p>This is the phase of nourishing and fortifying. Nature already offers us exactly what we need: earth foods,</p><p>root vegetables — potato, carrot, beetroot, turnip, parsnip. These are naturally richer in complex carbohydrates and minerals, providing steady energy and supporting inner warmth. It is as if the earth itself were saying: “the time to root has come.”</p><p><strong>In Irish culture</strong>, <strong>this ancestral wisdom is reflected in the stews, coddle and typical soups of the season — warm, comforting dishes that combine root vegetables,</strong></p><p><strong>legumes and herbs: a simple and delicious way to warm the body and strengthen immunity.</strong></p><p>Beyond that, this is the moment to look with care at the nutrients that sustain our natural defences. Vitamin C, present in citrus fruits, kiwi and peppers,</p><p>helps protect the cells; zinc, found in pumpkin seeds and nuts, is essential for immune function; and selenium, present in eggs and Brazil</p><p>nuts, plays a fundamental role in thyroid hormone regulation and antioxidant protection. Vitamin D, often reduced by lower</p><p>sun exposure, can be reinforced through foods such as oily fish (salmon, sardines) and eggs, or with professional guidance on supplementation. Small adjustments to what you eat make a big difference to the body’s ability to face the cold with balance and vitality.</p><p>Another essential point is caring for the gut microbiota, the basis of our immune system. Including fermented foods such as sauerkraut, kefir, kombucha or natural yoghurt helps keep the gut healthy and the body more resistant to infection. Good fats such as olive oil, avocado, seeds and nuts nourish the cells, support hormonal balance and favour the production of serotonin and melatonin — neurotransmitters linked to mood and sleep, which tend to fluctuate at this time of year. This nutritional transition, at once simple and wise, stabilises blood sugar, calms the nervous system and prepares the body for winter’s retreat with strength and serenity.</p><p>Autumn is an invitation to pause, a space between doing and being, between the movement of summer and the withdrawal of winter. It is the moment to quieten a little of the outside noise and listen to the body, which wisely shows what it needs: more rest, more warmth, more presence. Preparing for winter is not only about reinforcing</p><p>immunity, but about cultivating an inner state of balance and welcome.</p><p><strong>Every nourishing meal, every well-slept night, every conscious breath is an act of love and respect for the body that sustains you. When you allow yourself to slow down and nourish your roots now, you create the fertile ground for crossing the cold months with energy, clarity and serenity.</strong></p><p></p><p><em>This autumn, listen to your body. Slow the pace down, warm your soul and trust the natural process of the seasons.</em></p><p><em>When you align with the cycle of nature, you discover that winter is not the end — it is the rest needed for the blossoming that is to come.</em></p><p></p><p></p><p><u><em><strong>Simone Holz</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Holistic Nutritional Therapist</strong></em></u></p>\n','Preparing the Body for Winter','The role of food, rest and grounding in the autumn transition. Written by Simone Holz, Holistic Nutritional Therapist.','publish','open','open','','preparing-the-body-for-winter','','','2026-09-26 16:26:43','2026-09-26 15:26:43','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/03/preparing-the-body-for-winter/',0,'post','',0),(29161,0,'2026-09-26 16:26:43','2026-09-26 15:26:43','<p>The role of food, rest and grounding in the autumn transition.</p><p></p><p><u><em>Text: Simone Holz</em></u></p><p><u><em>Holistic Nutritional Therapist</em></u></p><p></p><p></p><p><em>Autumn</em> is the season in which the mornings begin to turn colder, the sun says goodbye earlier and nature silently shifts its rhythm. The trees let go of what no longer serves them, and that same movement invites us to introspect, to turn inwards, to nourish the roots and to prepare body, mind and soul for the stillness of the winter to come.</p><p>Just as the earth reorganises itself, our body enters this same process of adaptation. In the first weeks of autumn it is common to feel heavier, with muscles and joints more sensitive, a tiredness that arrives without warning, or a deeper sleep that asks for more time at rest. All of this is natural: the</p><p>organism is detoxifying the excess heat, sugar and stimulation of summer — the ice creams, the long days, the accelerated pace.</p><p>Autumn is nature’s gentle reminder that it is time to slow down, clear what has accumulated and strengthen our foundations. It is the time to “put down roots” and let go of what has already done its job,</p><p>be it toxins, emotions or habits that no longer sustain our vitality.</p><p>Through autumn our body begins to ask for more nourishing, denser and warmer foods, and it is not by chance. This is the moment when we need to strengthen our immune</p><p>system and build energy reserves for winter.</p><p>This is the phase of nourishing and fortifying. Nature already offers us exactly what we need: earth foods,</p><p>root vegetables — potato, carrot, beetroot, turnip, parsnip. These are naturally richer in complex carbohydrates and minerals, providing steady energy and supporting inner warmth. It is as if the earth itself were saying: “the time to root has come.”</p><p><strong>In Irish culture</strong>, <strong>this ancestral wisdom is reflected in the stews, coddle and typical soups of the season — warm, comforting dishes that combine root vegetables,</strong></p><p><strong>legumes and herbs: a simple and delicious way to warm the body and strengthen immunity.</strong></p><p>Beyond that, this is the moment to look with care at the nutrients that sustain our natural defences. Vitamin C, present in citrus fruits, kiwi and peppers,</p><p>helps protect the cells; zinc, found in pumpkin seeds and nuts, is essential for immune function; and selenium, present in eggs and Brazil</p><p>nuts, plays a fundamental role in thyroid hormone regulation and antioxidant protection. Vitamin D, often reduced by lower</p><p>sun exposure, can be reinforced through foods such as oily fish (salmon, sardines) and eggs, or with professional guidance on supplementation. Small adjustments to what you eat make a big difference to the body’s ability to face the cold with balance and vitality.</p><p>Another essential point is caring for the gut microbiota, the basis of our immune system. Including fermented foods such as sauerkraut, kefir, kombucha or natural yoghurt helps keep the gut healthy and the body more resistant to infection. Good fats such as olive oil, avocado, seeds and nuts nourish the cells, support hormonal balance and favour the production of serotonin and melatonin — neurotransmitters linked to mood and sleep, which tend to fluctuate at this time of year. This nutritional transition, at once simple and wise, stabilises blood sugar, calms the nervous system and prepares the body for winter’s retreat with strength and serenity.</p><p>Autumn is an invitation to pause, a space between doing and being, between the movement of summer and the withdrawal of winter. It is the moment to quieten a little of the outside noise and listen to the body, which wisely shows what it needs: more rest, more warmth, more presence. Preparing for winter is not only about reinforcing</p><p>immunity, but about cultivating an inner state of balance and welcome.</p><p><strong>Every nourishing meal, every well-slept night, every conscious breath is an act of love and respect for the body that sustains you. When you allow yourself to slow down and nourish your roots now, you create the fertile ground for crossing the cold months with energy, clarity and serenity.</strong></p><p></p><p><em>This autumn, listen to your body. Slow the pace down, warm your soul and trust the natural process of the seasons.</em></p><p><em>When you align with the cycle of nature, you discover that winter is not the end — it is the rest needed for the blossoming that is to come.</em></p><p></p><p></p><p><u><em><strong>Simone Holz</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Holistic Nutritional Therapist</strong></em></u></p>\n','Preparing the Body for Winter','The role of food, rest and grounding in the autumn transition. Written by Simone Holz, Holistic Nutritional Therapist.','inherit','closed','closed','','29160-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:26:43','2026-09-26 15:26:43','',29160,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29161',0,'revision','',0),(29162,283039559,'2026-01-08 22:24:47','2026-01-08 22:24:47','<p><em><strong>Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer</strong></em></p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/</u></p><p></p><p>As a woman over 30, I understand the difficulty of fitting a training routine into everyday life. I also know how essential it is, because from that age on we begin to lose muscle mass at an accelerated rate, which directly affects bone density. Strength training is the best tool for counteracting that process, and that is why I insist so much on building routines my clients can genuinely maintain in the long term — for life.</p><p></p><p></p><p>As a Personal Trainer, my mission is to help each client find the best exercise routine, aligned with their reality and their lifestyle, so that training is continuous and sustainable rather than momentary. After all, physical exercise is a lifelong commitment, not merely a means of achieving the “summer body”.</p><p></p><p></p><p>My name is Ana Paula and I have been a Personal Trainer for 5 years. I am passionate about movement and about helping people — beginners especially — take that first step into an exercise routine, promoting a better relationship with exercise and with food as well.</p><p>Over those five years I have also taken professional courses in Nutrition and Mat Pilates, because I believe constant updating is fundamental. To genuinely contribute to improving physical health — which is directly linked to mental health — it is essential to keep learning continuously and, above all, to listen to each client, respecting their story and their experiences.</p><p></p><p><em><strong>Movement is medicine!</strong></em></p><p><strong>To age with health, autonomy and quality of life, we need to stay strong.</strong></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>\n','Movement Is Medicine!','Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/ — building training routines you can actually keep, for life.','publish','open','open','','movement-is-medicine','','','2026-09-26 16:26:44','2026-09-26 15:26:44','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/08/movement-is-medicine/',0,'post','',0),(29163,0,'2026-09-26 16:26:44','2026-09-26 15:26:44','<p><em><strong>Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer</strong></em></p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/</u></p><p></p><p>As a woman over 30, I understand the difficulty of fitting a training routine into everyday life. I also know how essential it is, because from that age on we begin to lose muscle mass at an accelerated rate, which directly affects bone density. Strength training is the best tool for counteracting that process, and that is why I insist so much on building routines my clients can genuinely maintain in the long term — for life.</p><p></p><p></p><p>As a Personal Trainer, my mission is to help each client find the best exercise routine, aligned with their reality and their lifestyle, so that training is continuous and sustainable rather than momentary. After all, physical exercise is a lifelong commitment, not merely a means of achieving the “summer body”.</p><p></p><p></p><p>My name is Ana Paula and I have been a Personal Trainer for 5 years. I am passionate about movement and about helping people — beginners especially — take that first step into an exercise routine, promoting a better relationship with exercise and with food as well.</p><p>Over those five years I have also taken professional courses in Nutrition and Mat Pilates, because I believe constant updating is fundamental. To genuinely contribute to improving physical health — which is directly linked to mental health — it is essential to keep learning continuously and, above all, to listen to each client, respecting their story and their experiences.</p><p></p><p><em><strong>Movement is medicine!</strong></em></p><p><strong>To age with health, autonomy and quality of life, we need to stay strong.</strong></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p>\n','Movement Is Medicine!','Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/ — building training routines you can actually keep, for life.','inherit','closed','closed','','29162-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:26:44','2026-09-26 15:26:44','',29162,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29163',0,'revision','',0),(29166,283039559,'2026-01-19 17:06:30','2026-01-19 17:06:30','<p></p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>Once upon a time — not in the sky, but on paper — human beings decided where the year should begin.</p><p>The Gregorian calendar was created to organise society, not consciousness. Dated 1582, it was instituted by Pope Gregory. It aligns contracts, taxes, harvests, work, debts and systems of governance. Time became something that could be counted,</p><p>regulated, inspected and controlled. The reason was: for civilisation to work at scale, a common agreement was needed — and so 1 January was chosen as the starting line: clean, numeric, administrative.</p><p>This calendar governs the material agreement of life: bills, deadlines, fiscal years, the school calendar, productivity targets. It belongs to the constructed dimension of reality, where continuity, control and predictability are essential. It does not answer to nature — it organises people.</p><p>And that is not a flaw. It is its function. And nothing beyond that.</p><p>But, in parallel with that created rhythm, there is another year — one that does not begin by decision, but by alignment.</p><p>The solar return, in energetic and astrological terms, happens when the Sun enters Aries. That moment marks the true beginning of the solar cycle. It is when the light starts to overcome the darkness, when the Earth tilts again towards growth, when life receives the signal to move forward. This is not symbolic; it is seasonal, biological, energetic.</p><p>Aries inaugurates movement. The seeds awaken. The blood warms. The systems come online. Long before calendars existed, life on Earth was already responding to this change. That is why many ancient cultures recognised spring — and not midwinter — as the true New Year.</p><p>The solar return governs energy, not administration.</p><p>It rules vitality, instinct, initiative, courage and birth.</p><p>It belongs to nature, to astrology, to the time lived in the body.</p><p>So we live inside two simultaneous cycles, two dimensions of existence, two</p><p>timelines.</p><p>One begins in January and asks:</p><p>How are you going to manage your life?</p><p>How will you pay, plan, organise, keep going?</p><p>The other begins when the Sun enters Aries and asks:</p><p>How are you going to live it?</p><p>What do you want to be born, to be initiated, to incarnate?</p><p></p><p>Winter — January, February and much of March in the northern hemisphere — exists between these two thresholds. By the Gregorian calendar it looks like a beginning. By the solar calendar it is still an ending.</p><p>That is why so many people feel discomfort in early January.</p><p>The system says “move forward”.</p><p>The body answers “not yet”.</p><p>Winter is the last chapter of the energetic year. It is the phase of fertile decomposition. The nervous system slows down. The psyche reviews. The body conserves. Expecting full performance in this period is not discipline — it is misalignment.</p><p>Caring for yourself while respecting both systems is learning to speak two languages.</p><p>● Mind: use January to organise, not to launch. Plan without demanding immediate execution. Structure without pressure.</p><p>● Body: honour the low energy as functional, not as failure. Sleep more. Eat more densely. Move gently.</p><p>● Emotions: allow the introspection. Winter brings truths to the surface so they do not sabotage spring. Emotions are information, not obstacles.</p><p></p><p>So, when the Sun enters Aries, something changes naturally. Action stops being forced.</p><p>Decisions gain warmth. The direction becomes clear.</p><p>The Gregorian calendar keeps society running.</p><p>The solar return keeps you alive.</p><p>When both are honoured, life stops looking like resistance — and becomes rhythm instead.</p><p></p>\n','Two New Years: One Measured by Man, the Other Felt by the Earth','The Gregorian calendar organises society; the solar return rules energy. Living inside both cycles means learning to speak two languages.','publish','open','open','','two-new-years-one-measured-by-man-the-other-felt-by-the-earth','','','2026-09-26 16:27:28','2026-09-26 15:27:28','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/19/two-new-years-one-measured-by-man-the-other-felt-by-the-earth/',0,'post','',0),(29167,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:28','2026-09-26 15:27:28','<p></p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>Once upon a time — not in the sky, but on paper — human beings decided where the year should begin.</p><p>The Gregorian calendar was created to organise society, not consciousness. Dated 1582, it was instituted by Pope Gregory. It aligns contracts, taxes, harvests, work, debts and systems of governance. Time became something that could be counted,</p><p>regulated, inspected and controlled. The reason was: for civilisation to work at scale, a common agreement was needed — and so 1 January was chosen as the starting line: clean, numeric, administrative.</p><p>This calendar governs the material agreement of life: bills, deadlines, fiscal years, the school calendar, productivity targets. It belongs to the constructed dimension of reality, where continuity, control and predictability are essential. It does not answer to nature — it organises people.</p><p>And that is not a flaw. It is its function. And nothing beyond that.</p><p>But, in parallel with that created rhythm, there is another year — one that does not begin by decision, but by alignment.</p><p>The solar return, in energetic and astrological terms, happens when the Sun enters Aries. That moment marks the true beginning of the solar cycle. It is when the light starts to overcome the darkness, when the Earth tilts again towards growth, when life receives the signal to move forward. This is not symbolic; it is seasonal, biological, energetic.</p><p>Aries inaugurates movement. The seeds awaken. The blood warms. The systems come online. Long before calendars existed, life on Earth was already responding to this change. That is why many ancient cultures recognised spring — and not midwinter — as the true New Year.</p><p>The solar return governs energy, not administration.</p><p>It rules vitality, instinct, initiative, courage and birth.</p><p>It belongs to nature, to astrology, to the time lived in the body.</p><p>So we live inside two simultaneous cycles, two dimensions of existence, two</p><p>timelines.</p><p>One begins in January and asks:</p><p>How are you going to manage your life?</p><p>How will you pay, plan, organise, keep going?</p><p>The other begins when the Sun enters Aries and asks:</p><p>How are you going to live it?</p><p>What do you want to be born, to be initiated, to incarnate?</p><p></p><p>Winter — January, February and much of March in the northern hemisphere — exists between these two thresholds. By the Gregorian calendar it looks like a beginning. By the solar calendar it is still an ending.</p><p>That is why so many people feel discomfort in early January.</p><p>The system says “move forward”.</p><p>The body answers “not yet”.</p><p>Winter is the last chapter of the energetic year. It is the phase of fertile decomposition. The nervous system slows down. The psyche reviews. The body conserves. Expecting full performance in this period is not discipline — it is misalignment.</p><p>Caring for yourself while respecting both systems is learning to speak two languages.</p><p>● Mind: use January to organise, not to launch. Plan without demanding immediate execution. Structure without pressure.</p><p>● Body: honour the low energy as functional, not as failure. Sleep more. Eat more densely. Move gently.</p><p>● Emotions: allow the introspection. Winter brings truths to the surface so they do not sabotage spring. Emotions are information, not obstacles.</p><p></p><p>So, when the Sun enters Aries, something changes naturally. Action stops being forced.</p><p>Decisions gain warmth. The direction becomes clear.</p><p>The Gregorian calendar keeps society running.</p><p>The solar return keeps you alive.</p><p>When both are honoured, life stops looking like resistance — and becomes rhythm instead.</p><p></p>\n','Two New Years: One Measured by Man, the Other Felt by the Earth','The Gregorian calendar organises society; the solar return rules energy. Living inside both cycles means learning to speak two languages.','inherit','closed','closed','','29166-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:28','2026-09-26 15:27:28','',29166,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29167',0,'revision','',0),(29168,283039559,'2026-01-19 17:24:09','2026-01-19 17:24:09','<p></p><p><strong>The end of the year always invites us to </strong><u><strong>reflect.</strong></u></p><p>The Christmas lights do not only light up the streets of our cities, but also that which we so often let go out along the entrepreneurial journey: the flame of courage.</p><p>Being an entrepreneur is, above all, an act of faith. Faith in an idea, faith in a purpose, faith in your own ability to get up after every stumble. Because stumbles will come, and they are not a sign of weakness.</p><p></p><p>They are part of the route. In fact, nobody builds something extraordinary by walking in a straight line. The great business stories are born precisely in the detours, in the route adjustments, in the ability to stop, breathe deeply and decide to try again. And here is a truth that few will tell you. Money is not enough.</p><p></p><p>An academic qualification is not enough. Having done every course, gone through every mentorship and studied hard is not enough. Those elements help a great deal, but they do not sustain a business on their own. The backbone of entrepreneurship, yesterday, today and always, is courage! Courage to start. Courage to take the calculated risks and the uncalculated ones. Courage to recalculate the route a thousand times, if necessary.</p><p></p><p>Courage to keep going even when nobody understands what you see. While some believe that entrepreneurship is about strategy, others think it is about capital. But we know: entrepreneurship is about heart. About putting your soul into every attempt and understanding that growing is not a destination, it is a continuous movement.</p><p></p><p>This Christmas, allow yourself to celebrate not only the results, but above all the journey. Celebrate the attempts, the falls, the restarts. Celebrate every time you dared to believe, even when everything seemed uncertain.</p><p></p><p>And may the coming year bring new routes, new ideas, new possibilities and the courage to travel them. Happy Christmas!</p><p></p><p>May this date renew your strength, your faith and your determination to keep creating, daring and building.</p><p></p>\n','Courage! The Star That Lights the Entrepreneur’s Path — Christmas Special','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. Being an entrepreneur is, above all, an act of faith.','publish','open','open','','courage-the-star-that-lights-the-entrepreneurs-path-christmas-special','','','2026-09-26 16:27:29','2026-09-26 15:27:29','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/01/19/courage-the-star-that-lights-the-entrepreneurs-path-christmas-special/',0,'post','',0),(29169,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:29','2026-09-26 15:27:29','<p></p><p><strong>The end of the year always invites us to </strong><u><strong>reflect.</strong></u></p><p>The Christmas lights do not only light up the streets of our cities, but also that which we so often let go out along the entrepreneurial journey: the flame of courage.</p><p>Being an entrepreneur is, above all, an act of faith. Faith in an idea, faith in a purpose, faith in your own ability to get up after every stumble. Because stumbles will come, and they are not a sign of weakness.</p><p></p><p>They are part of the route. In fact, nobody builds something extraordinary by walking in a straight line. The great business stories are born precisely in the detours, in the route adjustments, in the ability to stop, breathe deeply and decide to try again. And here is a truth that few will tell you. Money is not enough.</p><p></p><p>An academic qualification is not enough. Having done every course, gone through every mentorship and studied hard is not enough. Those elements help a great deal, but they do not sustain a business on their own. The backbone of entrepreneurship, yesterday, today and always, is courage! Courage to start. Courage to take the calculated risks and the uncalculated ones. Courage to recalculate the route a thousand times, if necessary.</p><p></p><p>Courage to keep going even when nobody understands what you see. While some believe that entrepreneurship is about strategy, others think it is about capital. But we know: entrepreneurship is about heart. About putting your soul into every attempt and understanding that growing is not a destination, it is a continuous movement.</p><p></p><p>This Christmas, allow yourself to celebrate not only the results, but above all the journey. Celebrate the attempts, the falls, the restarts. Celebrate every time you dared to believe, even when everything seemed uncertain.</p><p></p><p>And may the coming year bring new routes, new ideas, new possibilities and the courage to travel them. Happy Christmas!</p><p></p><p>May this date renew your strength, your faith and your determination to keep creating, daring and building.</p><p></p>\n','Courage! The Star That Lights the Entrepreneur’s Path — Christmas Special','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. Being an entrepreneur is, above all, an act of faith.','inherit','closed','closed','','29168-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:29','2026-09-26 15:27:29','',29168,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29169',0,'revision','',0),(29170,283039559,'2026-02-07 18:27:57','2026-02-07 18:27:57','<p></p><p></p><p></p><p><em>Text: Lili Fraga</em></p><p><em>Therapist and Parenting Educator</em></p><p></p><p><h4>Central theme: self-care as an adaptation tool in immigration</h4></p><p>Emotional difficulties, overload, missing family and the feeling of always being in survival mode. How do you create moments of pause, connection with yourself and simple practices to protect your mental health even with little time or few resources?</p><p></p><p><h2><strong>✨ Caring for Yourself in a Foreign Land</strong></h2></p><p></p><p><strong>A look at the emotional wellbeing of those who start again outside Brazil</strong></p><p>Living far from your country of origin can be challenging. On top of the demands of everyday life, the immigrant faces deep emotional layers — often marked by a sense of not belonging. Some discover an incredible resilience and recognise their inner strength. Others decide to return to their country. And some take on the challenge of finding a new place in the world. What we know is that we Brazilians — with our warm blood, easy laughter and exposed affections — carry far more than other people’s eyes can see.</p><p></p><p></p><p>The truth is that there is no single path to happiness and wellbeing. Being well far from home invites us on a journey of <strong>self-knowledge</strong>, in which we learn to respect our limits, to accept that we will not always be understood and that we may not be able to please everyone. Sometimes we need to create our own space in order to feel at ease with the discomfort.</p><p></p><p>And that is not only poetry. Studies show that self-care practices (such as building routines, maintaining social ties or spirituality) are fundamental to the mental health of immigrants and refugees, with psychological and social strategies proving the most effective in preventing anxiety, sadness and chronic discouragement (Mehjabeen et al., 2023). In addition, public health researchers in Ireland warn about the importance of access to culturally sensitive services that respect our language, our history and our time of adaptation (<u>McMahon et al., 2021</u>).</p><p><strong>Looking after our own wellbeing is necessary.</strong> Creating relationships that allow us to be who we really are is crucial to crossing this path of beginnings, where emotional bridges may look fragile but can be built with affection and courage.</p><p>And that is why I invite you to look at <em>one moment at a time</em>, to make your choices based on your reality and to live at peace with yourself. You will notice that happiness can indeed be part of your nomadic life — not as a fixed destination, but as a state of presence.</p><p><strong>If you need help, do not hesitate to ask for it.</strong> Far from home, we can build a new home — as long as we are aware of the pillars that support it.</p><p></p><p>🗓️ <strong>Next month we will talk about: “Mental Health and Immigration – What Nobody Tells You”.</strong> We will make space to talk about anxiety, insomnia, guilt, and what to do when it feels like you are losing your strength.</p><p></p><p>I will be waiting for you there. With care, Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator <u>www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</u></p><p></p>\n','1. Caring for Yourself in a Foreign Land','Text: Lili Fraga, Therapist and Parenting Educator. Central theme: self-care as an adaptation tool in immigration.','publish','open','open','','1-caring-for-yourself-in-a-foreign-land','','','2026-09-26 16:27:30','2026-09-26 15:27:30','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/02/07/1-caring-for-yourself-in-a-foreign-land/',0,'post','',0),(29171,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:30','2026-09-26 15:27:30','<p></p><p></p><p></p><p><em>Text: Lili Fraga</em></p><p><em>Therapist and Parenting Educator</em></p><p></p><p><h4>Central theme: self-care as an adaptation tool in immigration</h4></p><p>Emotional difficulties, overload, missing family and the feeling of always being in survival mode. How do you create moments of pause, connection with yourself and simple practices to protect your mental health even with little time or few resources?</p><p></p><p><h2><strong>✨ Caring for Yourself in a Foreign Land</strong></h2></p><p></p><p><strong>A look at the emotional wellbeing of those who start again outside Brazil</strong></p><p>Living far from your country of origin can be challenging. On top of the demands of everyday life, the immigrant faces deep emotional layers — often marked by a sense of not belonging. Some discover an incredible resilience and recognise their inner strength. Others decide to return to their country. And some take on the challenge of finding a new place in the world. What we know is that we Brazilians — with our warm blood, easy laughter and exposed affections — carry far more than other people’s eyes can see.</p><p></p><p></p><p>The truth is that there is no single path to happiness and wellbeing. Being well far from home invites us on a journey of <strong>self-knowledge</strong>, in which we learn to respect our limits, to accept that we will not always be understood and that we may not be able to please everyone. Sometimes we need to create our own space in order to feel at ease with the discomfort.</p><p></p><p>And that is not only poetry. Studies show that self-care practices (such as building routines, maintaining social ties or spirituality) are fundamental to the mental health of immigrants and refugees, with psychological and social strategies proving the most effective in preventing anxiety, sadness and chronic discouragement (Mehjabeen et al., 2023). In addition, public health researchers in Ireland warn about the importance of access to culturally sensitive services that respect our language, our history and our time of adaptation (<u>McMahon et al., 2021</u>).</p><p><strong>Looking after our own wellbeing is necessary.</strong> Creating relationships that allow us to be who we really are is crucial to crossing this path of beginnings, where emotional bridges may look fragile but can be built with affection and courage.</p><p>And that is why I invite you to look at <em>one moment at a time</em>, to make your choices based on your reality and to live at peace with yourself. You will notice that happiness can indeed be part of your nomadic life — not as a fixed destination, but as a state of presence.</p><p><strong>If you need help, do not hesitate to ask for it.</strong> Far from home, we can build a new home — as long as we are aware of the pillars that support it.</p><p></p><p>🗓️ <strong>Next month we will talk about: “Mental Health and Immigration – What Nobody Tells You”.</strong> We will make space to talk about anxiety, insomnia, guilt, and what to do when it feels like you are losing your strength.</p><p></p><p>I will be waiting for you there. With care, Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator <u>www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</u></p><p></p>\n','1. Caring for Yourself in a Foreign Land','Text: Lili Fraga, Therapist and Parenting Educator. Central theme: self-care as an adaptation tool in immigration.','inherit','closed','closed','','29170-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:30','2026-09-26 15:27:30','',29170,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29171',0,'revision','',0),(29172,283039559,'2026-02-07 18:32:23','2026-02-07 18:32:23','<p></p><p></p><p><em>Text: Lili Fraga — </em></p><p><em>Therapist and Parenting Educator</em> </p><p></p><p></p><p><h4>Central theme: anxiety, loneliness and depression among immigrants</h4></p><p>Talking about the emotional impact of starting over in another country, the pressure to make it work, and how to seek help (in Portuguese or English) in Ireland. Including warning signs and pathways to support.</p><p><h2><strong>🧠 Mental Health and Immigration: What Nobody Tells You</strong></h2></p><p><strong>Anxiety, loneliness and depression far from home</strong></p><p>Have you ever heard that phrase — “Just come!” — when someone talks about living abroad, doing an exchange or moving country? Well… it sounds like a light invitation, full of promises — freedom, opportunities, money, quality of life. But what almost nobody tells you is that along with the suitcases come the doubts, the pressures, the longing and, often, the emotional silence.</p><p>Sometimes the desire to change is so intense that we leave without investigating our <strong>inner resources</strong> in any depth. There is no time (or courage) to ask ourselves: <strong>“Am I ready for this new beginning?”</strong> Because yes, living abroad is a transformative experience — but it demands an <strong>enormous emotional maturity</strong>.</p><p>The worries are not limited to housing or work. They invade sleep, mood and vital energy. Starting over, reinventing yourself and, above all, <strong>finding yourself</strong> are the cornerstones that keep you from losing yourself along the way.</p><p>Living abroad demands <strong>emotional planning</strong> and deep listening:</p><p>● What are my limits?</p><p>● How do I deal with frustration?</p><p>● Am I willing to live the new without erasing who I am?</p><p>And that is where the central point of this conversation comes in: <strong>mental health in immigration</strong>.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>🔍 The psychological impact of starting over</strong></h3></p><p>Studies point to immigrants having <strong>higher rates of anxiety, depression and post-traumatic stress</strong> than local populations. This happens for several reasons: social isolation, language difficulties, culture shock, financial insecurity and lack of a support network (FERREIRA, Alisson Vinícius Silva; BORGES, Lucienne Martins, 2022;<u> </u>WHO, 2018).</p><p>In Ireland, a study with Brazilians revealed that many live with significant levels of stress, especially women and mothers who feel overloaded by being the pillar of the family while facing cultural and language barriers (<u>Ribeiro et al., 2020</u>).</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>⚠️ Warning signs</strong></h3></p><p>It is not always easy to notice that something is not right. But watch out for some signs:</p><p>● Difficulty sleeping or waking up rested</p><p>● Constant irritability or bouts of crying with no apparent reason</p><p>● Loss of motivation, loss of pleasure in simple things</p><p>● Self-deprecating thoughts or a feeling of inadequacy</p><p>● A feeling of loneliness even when surrounded by people</p><p>● Intense anxiety in the face of ordinary tasks (going to the supermarket, talking to someone, leaving the house)</p><p>These symptoms are not weakness. They are requests for help.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>🧭 Pathways to support</strong></h3></p><p>If you recognised yourself in any of these situations, know that you are not alone — and you do not have to go through it unsupported. Here in Ireland there are resources available, in both Portuguese and English:</p><p><h4><strong>🌿 Support in Portuguese:</strong></h4></p><p>● <strong>Jigsaw (Wellbeing for Young Adults)</strong> – although in English, it offers support for young adults and usually has interpreters available.</p><p><u>https://jigsaw.ie/talk-online/live-chat/?gad_source=1&amp;gad_campaignid=10646051693&amp;gbraid=0AAAAADgoh_Lkn3GQPGrOmaEOHaAC2Hl7P&amp;gclid=Cj0KCQjw5c_FBhDJARIsAIcmHK-LcSCQsv64GoMKCmZ-BnUqx6yxZ--9UbybKLXHbkkEg7lk_guxdUQaAvmKEALw_wcB</u></p><p>● <strong>Dublin Community Support Groups</strong> – there are informal groups of Brazilians and therapists who work online.</p><p><u>https://grow.ie/category/recovery-stories/?gad_source=1&amp;gad_campaignid=13611074611&amp;gbraid=0AAAAADmGKBwtsy7WFEb3_AayfRM2ZjC-3&amp;gclid=Cj0KCQjw5c_FBhDJARIsAIcmHK8KnYFNNnYedcAMITdkJXl6XuRxYITttshrEEljeJHZrbuRBdjC2MgaAsR8EALw_wcB</u></p><p><h4><strong>🌱 Support in English:</strong></h4></p><p>● <strong>Mental Health Ireland</strong> <u>https://www.mentalhealthireland.ie/</u> </p><p>● <strong>Samaritans</strong> (free 24h call: 116 123)</p><p>● <strong>MyMind.ie</strong> – offers psychotherapy sessions at affordable prices (often with therapists who speak Portuguese) <u>https://mymind.org/</u></p><p><h3><strong>💛 Wrapping up...</strong></h3></p><p>Dealing with mental health in immigration is an act of courage. It is stepping out of denial and saying: <strong>“I matter. My feelings are valid. And I deserve support.”</strong></p><p>You do not have to be well all the time. But you can learn to be well <strong>with yourself</strong>, even on the hard days.</p><p>🗓️ <strong>Next month</strong>, we will talk about the power of <strong>support networks</strong> in the immigrant’s life:</p><p> <strong>“Building a Support Network Far from Home”</strong>. Because nobody has to walk this path alone — and when we walk it together, everything becomes lighter.</p><p>With care, Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator <u>www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</u></p><p></p><p></p>\n','2. Mental Health and Immigration: What Nobody Tells You','Text: Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator. Central theme: anxiety, loneliness and depression among immigrants.','publish','open','open','','2-mental-health-and-immigration-what-nobody-tells-you','','','2026-09-26 16:27:30','2026-09-26 15:27:30','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/02/07/2-mental-health-and-immigration-what-nobody-tells-you/',0,'post','',0),(29173,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:30','2026-09-26 15:27:30','<p></p><p></p><p><em>Text: Lili Fraga — </em></p><p><em>Therapist and Parenting Educator</em> </p><p></p><p></p><p><h4>Central theme: anxiety, loneliness and depression among immigrants</h4></p><p>Talking about the emotional impact of starting over in another country, the pressure to make it work, and how to seek help (in Portuguese or English) in Ireland. Including warning signs and pathways to support.</p><p><h2><strong>🧠 Mental Health and Immigration: What Nobody Tells You</strong></h2></p><p><strong>Anxiety, loneliness and depression far from home</strong></p><p>Have you ever heard that phrase — “Just come!” — when someone talks about living abroad, doing an exchange or moving country? Well… it sounds like a light invitation, full of promises — freedom, opportunities, money, quality of life. But what almost nobody tells you is that along with the suitcases come the doubts, the pressures, the longing and, often, the emotional silence.</p><p>Sometimes the desire to change is so intense that we leave without investigating our <strong>inner resources</strong> in any depth. There is no time (or courage) to ask ourselves: <strong>“Am I ready for this new beginning?”</strong> Because yes, living abroad is a transformative experience — but it demands an <strong>enormous emotional maturity</strong>.</p><p>The worries are not limited to housing or work. They invade sleep, mood and vital energy. Starting over, reinventing yourself and, above all, <strong>finding yourself</strong> are the cornerstones that keep you from losing yourself along the way.</p><p>Living abroad demands <strong>emotional planning</strong> and deep listening:</p><p>● What are my limits?</p><p>● How do I deal with frustration?</p><p>● Am I willing to live the new without erasing who I am?</p><p>And that is where the central point of this conversation comes in: <strong>mental health in immigration</strong>.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>🔍 The psychological impact of starting over</strong></h3></p><p>Studies point to immigrants having <strong>higher rates of anxiety, depression and post-traumatic stress</strong> than local populations. This happens for several reasons: social isolation, language difficulties, culture shock, financial insecurity and lack of a support network (FERREIRA, Alisson Vinícius Silva; BORGES, Lucienne Martins, 2022;<u> </u>WHO, 2018).</p><p>In Ireland, a study with Brazilians revealed that many live with significant levels of stress, especially women and mothers who feel overloaded by being the pillar of the family while facing cultural and language barriers (<u>Ribeiro et al., 2020</u>).</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>⚠️ Warning signs</strong></h3></p><p>It is not always easy to notice that something is not right. But watch out for some signs:</p><p>● Difficulty sleeping or waking up rested</p><p>● Constant irritability or bouts of crying with no apparent reason</p><p>● Loss of motivation, loss of pleasure in simple things</p><p>● Self-deprecating thoughts or a feeling of inadequacy</p><p>● A feeling of loneliness even when surrounded by people</p><p>● Intense anxiety in the face of ordinary tasks (going to the supermarket, talking to someone, leaving the house)</p><p>These symptoms are not weakness. They are requests for help.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>🧭 Pathways to support</strong></h3></p><p>If you recognised yourself in any of these situations, know that you are not alone — and you do not have to go through it unsupported. Here in Ireland there are resources available, in both Portuguese and English:</p><p><h4><strong>🌿 Support in Portuguese:</strong></h4></p><p>● <strong>Jigsaw (Wellbeing for Young Adults)</strong> – although in English, it offers support for young adults and usually has interpreters available.</p><p><u>https://jigsaw.ie/talk-online/live-chat/?gad_source=1&amp;gad_campaignid=10646051693&amp;gbraid=0AAAAADgoh_Lkn3GQPGrOmaEOHaAC2Hl7P&amp;gclid=Cj0KCQjw5c_FBhDJARIsAIcmHK-LcSCQsv64GoMKCmZ-BnUqx6yxZ--9UbybKLXHbkkEg7lk_guxdUQaAvmKEALw_wcB</u></p><p>● <strong>Dublin Community Support Groups</strong> – there are informal groups of Brazilians and therapists who work online.</p><p><u>https://grow.ie/category/recovery-stories/?gad_source=1&amp;gad_campaignid=13611074611&amp;gbraid=0AAAAADmGKBwtsy7WFEb3_AayfRM2ZjC-3&amp;gclid=Cj0KCQjw5c_FBhDJARIsAIcmHK8KnYFNNnYedcAMITdkJXl6XuRxYITttshrEEljeJHZrbuRBdjC2MgaAsR8EALw_wcB</u></p><p><h4><strong>🌱 Support in English:</strong></h4></p><p>● <strong>Mental Health Ireland</strong> <u>https://www.mentalhealthireland.ie/</u> </p><p>● <strong>Samaritans</strong> (free 24h call: 116 123)</p><p>● <strong>MyMind.ie</strong> – offers psychotherapy sessions at affordable prices (often with therapists who speak Portuguese) <u>https://mymind.org/</u></p><p><h3><strong>💛 Wrapping up...</strong></h3></p><p>Dealing with mental health in immigration is an act of courage. It is stepping out of denial and saying: <strong>“I matter. My feelings are valid. And I deserve support.”</strong></p><p>You do not have to be well all the time. But you can learn to be well <strong>with yourself</strong>, even on the hard days.</p><p>🗓️ <strong>Next month</strong>, we will talk about the power of <strong>support networks</strong> in the immigrant’s life:</p><p> <strong>“Building a Support Network Far from Home”</strong>. Because nobody has to walk this path alone — and when we walk it together, everything becomes lighter.</p><p>With care, Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator <u>www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</u></p><p></p><p></p>\n','2. Mental Health and Immigration: What Nobody Tells You','Text: Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator. Central theme: anxiety, loneliness and depression among immigrants.','inherit','closed','closed','','29172-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:30','2026-09-26 15:27:30','',29172,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29173',0,'revision','',0),(29174,283039559,'2026-02-07 18:42:06','2026-02-07 18:42:06','<p></p><p></p><p><em>Lili Fraga — </em></p><p><em>Therapist and Parenting Educator </em></p><p></p><p><h4>Have you ever stopped to reflect on how your body and your emotions react to the arrival of autumn and winter here in Ireland?</h4></p><p>Of course there are those who love the crisp cold, the silence, being tucked up in their own corner — and that is perfectly fine if it is your case. But for many of us, especially those who come from tropical countries or countries with well-defined seasons, this time of year can be challenging.</p><p>The short days, the grey sky and the constant cold affect our mood, our disposition and even our self-esteem. This has a name: what is scientifically called <strong>Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</strong> — a type of depression related to the change of season, common in northern hemisphere countries (GUZMAN et al., 2007; SAHEER et al., 2013).</p><p>Furthermore, studies with immigrants show that the <strong>sense of isolation and low exposure to natural light</strong> amplify symptoms such as sadness, fatigue, irritability and a drop in immunity (SAHEER et al., 2013).</p><p>That is why, more than ever, this is a time to <strong>observe your own behaviour with tenderness and compassion</strong>. Recognising what affects you is the first step towards turning discomfort into care.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>🌱 And what if we met?</strong></h3></p><p>Know that <strong>you have a real opportunity to meet other people, laugh, talk and feel part of something bigger</strong>. The <strong>Portlaoise group of Brazilians is celebrating 5 years</strong> — and to mark that beautiful journey of mutual support and connection, <strong>there will be a special in-person gathering at the park</strong>. <em><strong>(Event postponed; a new date will be announced).</strong></em></p><p><strong>Contact with nature and with other people is a powerful way of caring for your mental health</strong>, especially at this time of year.</p><p>Studies show that <strong>frequent exposure to green areas is directly associated with reduced anxiety and depression</strong>, as well as promoting the release of wellbeing-related hormones (TANG et al., 2023). Another survey reveals that <strong>20 to 30 minutes in natural environments</strong> is already enough to reduce cortisol levels and increase the sense of social connection (WHO, 2021; GASCON et al., 2015).</p><p>Being outdoors, with people who share the same journey, is an invitation to welcome, to affection and to belonging.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>💡 Practical tips for looking after yourself in winter:</strong></h3></p><p>✅ <strong>Make the most of natural light</strong>, even if only for a few minutes a day.</p><p>✅ <strong>Move your body</strong> — walks, dancing or stretching already help a great deal. </p><p>✅ <strong>Eat with intention</strong> — banana, nuts, oats and cocoa stimulate serotonin.</p><p>✅ <strong>Talk to someone</strong> — join groups, call a friend, seek therapy.</p><p>✅ <strong>Plan small weekly pleasures</strong> — a coffee, a book, a warm bath.</p><p>✅ <strong>And if you can: come to the in-person meeting!</strong> Being with other people warms the heart.</p><p></p><p>✨ <strong>Do not let winter freeze you from the inside.</strong> Let us carry on together, creating human warmth, caring for our mental health and celebrating every step of this journey away from home — but each time closer to ourselves.</p><p></p><p>📍<strong>Shall we meet at the park?</strong> It will be lovely to share this moment with you.</p><p>With care, Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator <u>www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</u></p><p></p><p></p>\n','How Are You Feeling at This Time of Year?','Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator. Seasonal Affective Disorder, the role of nature, and a meeting for the Brazilian community in Portlaoise.','publish','open','open','','how-are-you-feeling-at-this-time-of-year','','','2026-09-26 16:27:31','2026-09-26 15:27:31','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/02/07/how-are-you-feeling-at-this-time-of-year/',0,'post','',0),(29175,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:31','2026-09-26 15:27:31','<p></p><p></p><p><em>Lili Fraga — </em></p><p><em>Therapist and Parenting Educator </em></p><p></p><p><h4>Have you ever stopped to reflect on how your body and your emotions react to the arrival of autumn and winter here in Ireland?</h4></p><p>Of course there are those who love the crisp cold, the silence, being tucked up in their own corner — and that is perfectly fine if it is your case. But for many of us, especially those who come from tropical countries or countries with well-defined seasons, this time of year can be challenging.</p><p>The short days, the grey sky and the constant cold affect our mood, our disposition and even our self-esteem. This has a name: what is scientifically called <strong>Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</strong> — a type of depression related to the change of season, common in northern hemisphere countries (GUZMAN et al., 2007; SAHEER et al., 2013).</p><p>Furthermore, studies with immigrants show that the <strong>sense of isolation and low exposure to natural light</strong> amplify symptoms such as sadness, fatigue, irritability and a drop in immunity (SAHEER et al., 2013).</p><p>That is why, more than ever, this is a time to <strong>observe your own behaviour with tenderness and compassion</strong>. Recognising what affects you is the first step towards turning discomfort into care.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>🌱 And what if we met?</strong></h3></p><p>Know that <strong>you have a real opportunity to meet other people, laugh, talk and feel part of something bigger</strong>. The <strong>Portlaoise group of Brazilians is celebrating 5 years</strong> — and to mark that beautiful journey of mutual support and connection, <strong>there will be a special in-person gathering at the park</strong>. <em><strong>(Event postponed; a new date will be announced).</strong></em></p><p><strong>Contact with nature and with other people is a powerful way of caring for your mental health</strong>, especially at this time of year.</p><p>Studies show that <strong>frequent exposure to green areas is directly associated with reduced anxiety and depression</strong>, as well as promoting the release of wellbeing-related hormones (TANG et al., 2023). Another survey reveals that <strong>20 to 30 minutes in natural environments</strong> is already enough to reduce cortisol levels and increase the sense of social connection (WHO, 2021; GASCON et al., 2015).</p><p>Being outdoors, with people who share the same journey, is an invitation to welcome, to affection and to belonging.</p><p></p><p><h3><strong>💡 Practical tips for looking after yourself in winter:</strong></h3></p><p>✅ <strong>Make the most of natural light</strong>, even if only for a few minutes a day.</p><p>✅ <strong>Move your body</strong> — walks, dancing or stretching already help a great deal. </p><p>✅ <strong>Eat with intention</strong> — banana, nuts, oats and cocoa stimulate serotonin.</p><p>✅ <strong>Talk to someone</strong> — join groups, call a friend, seek therapy.</p><p>✅ <strong>Plan small weekly pleasures</strong> — a coffee, a book, a warm bath.</p><p>✅ <strong>And if you can: come to the in-person meeting!</strong> Being with other people warms the heart.</p><p></p><p>✨ <strong>Do not let winter freeze you from the inside.</strong> Let us carry on together, creating human warmth, caring for our mental health and celebrating every step of this journey away from home — but each time closer to ourselves.</p><p></p><p>📍<strong>Shall we meet at the park?</strong> It will be lovely to share this moment with you.</p><p>With care, Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator <u>www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</u></p><p></p><p></p>\n','How Are You Feeling at This Time of Year?','Lili Fraga — Therapist and Parenting Educator. Seasonal Affective Disorder, the role of nature, and a meeting for the Brazilian community in Portlaoise.','inherit','closed','closed','','29174-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:31','2026-09-26 15:27:31','',29174,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29175',0,'revision','',0),(29176,283039559,'2026-02-07 18:48:11','2026-02-07 18:48:11','<p><em>Written by: Lili Fraga — Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development</em></p><p><em><strong>Connections that strengthen the immigrant’s wellbeing</strong></em></p><p></p><p></p><p>Living outside your country of origin is already, in itself, a plunge into the unknown. But for expat mothers, the challenges multiply. Motherhood away from home carries layers of longing, tiredness, fear and, often, <strong>the temporary loss of your own identity</strong>. Who you were before you left seems distant — and now, besides being a mother, you are also an immigrant, starting from zero in a place where you do not always speak the language or understand the social dynamics.</p><p>The absence of a support network — that grandmother, that friend, that neighbour — is felt. The routine becomes heavier, rest more rare, and self-esteem can begin to weaken. The good news? <strong>Even far from home, it is possible to build new networks.</strong> Perhaps different from the ones you knew, but just as powerful, genuine and welcoming.</p><p>Help does not always appear spontaneously, and that is fine. But know this: <strong>you can find or create spaces of support that hold you emotionally, help with the challenges of daily life and remind you that you are not alone.</strong></p><p></p><p><h3><strong>💡 Practical strategies for finding your network in Ireland</strong></h3></p><p>Here are some real and accessible ways to step out of isolation and start connecting with other people who share similar values:</p><p><h4><strong>👩‍👧‍👦 1. Parent &amp; toddler groups</strong></h4></p><p>Community spaces where mothers (or fathers) and their small children meet to play, socialise and exchange experiences. Many are free or cost a nominal amount. 🔎 Where to find them: <em>Family Resource Centres</em>, local churches, nurseries or community centres. Examples: <strong>A Mother’s Embrace</strong> (Waterford), <strong>Barnardos</strong>, <strong>TUSLA parent support groups</strong>.</p><p><h4><strong>🤱 2. Bilingual or cultural playgroups</strong></h4></p><p>Joining groups that respect and celebrate your culture (such as Brazilian or Latin American mothers’ groups) helps you feel seen and welcomed. 🔎 Look for groups on WhatsApp, Facebook (“Brazilians in Ireland”, “Mothers in Dublin”, etc.) or in community schools.</p><p><h4><strong>☕ 3. Immigrant cafés and themed in-person meetups</strong></h4></p><p>Moments designed to create connections: conversation circles, workshops, wellbeing and motherhood events. 🔎 Follow local projects such as Mum talks <u>https://www.instagram.com/mum_talks/</u></p><p><h4><strong>🧘 4. Activities that combine self-care and company</strong></h4></p><p>Yoga classes, walking groups, art workshops or guided meditation with other mothers. A lovely way of caring for yourself while socialising. 🔎 Search Eventbrite, Meetup or Instagram for local activities with keywords like “mothers”, “community wellness”, “creative circles”.</p><p>Some apps that can help you find the right group for you: - <u>meetup.com</u>- <u>peanut-app.io</u>- <u>letsmush.com</u>- <u>nextdoor.ie</u></p><p><h4><strong>📱 5. Create your own network!</strong></h4></p><p>If you did not find anything that suits you, <strong>create it!</strong> A coffee morning at home with other mothers, a virtual support group and a picnic in the park. Someone is always waiting for that invitation.</p><p><h3><strong>💛 Wrapping up...</strong></h3></p><p><strong>Connections heal. Belonging strengthens. Being seen transforms.</strong> Even far from your homeland, you can — little by little — rebuild your support network, surround yourself with people who welcome who you are today, and experience motherhood with more lightness and truth.</p><p>📌 And remember: seeking support is not selfishness. It is wisdom. You deserve to be well so that you can care — including for yourself.</p><p></p><p><em>Written by: Lili Fraga — Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development</em></p><p><em><strong>Connections that strengthen the immigrant’s wellbeing</strong></em></p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve/</u></p><p></p>\n','Building a Support Network Far from Home','Written by: Lili Fraga — Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development. Connections that strengthen the immigrant’s wellbeing.','publish','open','open','','building-a-support-network-far-from-home','','','2026-09-26 16:27:31','2026-09-26 15:27:31','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/02/07/building-a-support-network-far-from-home/',0,'post','',0),(29177,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:31','2026-09-26 15:27:31','<p><em>Written by: Lili Fraga — Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development</em></p><p><em><strong>Connections that strengthen the immigrant’s wellbeing</strong></em></p><p></p><p></p><p>Living outside your country of origin is already, in itself, a plunge into the unknown. But for expat mothers, the challenges multiply. Motherhood away from home carries layers of longing, tiredness, fear and, often, <strong>the temporary loss of your own identity</strong>. Who you were before you left seems distant — and now, besides being a mother, you are also an immigrant, starting from zero in a place where you do not always speak the language or understand the social dynamics.</p><p>The absence of a support network — that grandmother, that friend, that neighbour — is felt. The routine becomes heavier, rest more rare, and self-esteem can begin to weaken. The good news? <strong>Even far from home, it is possible to build new networks.</strong> Perhaps different from the ones you knew, but just as powerful, genuine and welcoming.</p><p>Help does not always appear spontaneously, and that is fine. But know this: <strong>you can find or create spaces of support that hold you emotionally, help with the challenges of daily life and remind you that you are not alone.</strong></p><p></p><p><h3><strong>💡 Practical strategies for finding your network in Ireland</strong></h3></p><p>Here are some real and accessible ways to step out of isolation and start connecting with other people who share similar values:</p><p><h4><strong>👩‍👧‍👦 1. Parent &amp; toddler groups</strong></h4></p><p>Community spaces where mothers (or fathers) and their small children meet to play, socialise and exchange experiences. Many are free or cost a nominal amount. 🔎 Where to find them: <em>Family Resource Centres</em>, local churches, nurseries or community centres. Examples: <strong>A Mother’s Embrace</strong> (Waterford), <strong>Barnardos</strong>, <strong>TUSLA parent support groups</strong>.</p><p><h4><strong>🤱 2. Bilingual or cultural playgroups</strong></h4></p><p>Joining groups that respect and celebrate your culture (such as Brazilian or Latin American mothers’ groups) helps you feel seen and welcomed. 🔎 Look for groups on WhatsApp, Facebook (“Brazilians in Ireland”, “Mothers in Dublin”, etc.) or in community schools.</p><p><h4><strong>☕ 3. Immigrant cafés and themed in-person meetups</strong></h4></p><p>Moments designed to create connections: conversation circles, workshops, wellbeing and motherhood events. 🔎 Follow local projects such as Mum talks <u>https://www.instagram.com/mum_talks/</u></p><p><h4><strong>🧘 4. Activities that combine self-care and company</strong></h4></p><p>Yoga classes, walking groups, art workshops or guided meditation with other mothers. A lovely way of caring for yourself while socialising. 🔎 Search Eventbrite, Meetup or Instagram for local activities with keywords like “mothers”, “community wellness”, “creative circles”.</p><p>Some apps that can help you find the right group for you: - <u>meetup.com</u>- <u>peanut-app.io</u>- <u>letsmush.com</u>- <u>nextdoor.ie</u></p><p><h4><strong>📱 5. Create your own network!</strong></h4></p><p>If you did not find anything that suits you, <strong>create it!</strong> A coffee morning at home with other mothers, a virtual support group and a picnic in the park. Someone is always waiting for that invitation.</p><p><h3><strong>💛 Wrapping up...</strong></h3></p><p><strong>Connections heal. Belonging strengthens. Being seen transforms.</strong> Even far from your homeland, you can — little by little — rebuild your support network, surround yourself with people who welcome who you are today, and experience motherhood with more lightness and truth.</p><p>📌 And remember: seeking support is not selfishness. It is wisdom. You deserve to be well so that you can care — including for yourself.</p><p></p><p><em>Written by: Lili Fraga — Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development</em></p><p><em><strong>Connections that strengthen the immigrant’s wellbeing</strong></em></p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve/</u></p><p></p>\n','Building a Support Network Far from Home','Written by: Lili Fraga — Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development. Connections that strengthen the immigrant’s wellbeing.','inherit','closed','closed','','29176-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:31','2026-09-26 15:27:31','',29176,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29177',0,'revision','',0),(29178,283039559,'2026-02-15 21:59:52','2026-02-15 21:59:52','<p><em>Text: Janine Meira Henriques</em></p><p><em>Holistic therapist, writer, playwright, artist, photographer, journalist and mother of four.</em></p><p><em>Qualifications: undergraduate degrees in journalism, performing arts and holistic therapy, including reiki, radionics table work, Access bars, astrology and tarot. Psychology studies in progress, completing in June 2026.</em></p><p><em>Postgraduate studies: Brazilian literature, human sexuality.</em></p><p></p><p> </p><p></p><p>In recent years, integrative practices that bring spirituality and personal care together have gained ground in conversations about quality of life. Among them, work with chakras and crystals stands out for combining ancestral symbolism, energetic sensitivity and an invitation to self-knowledge.</p><p> </p><p>The chakras — subtle energy centres distributed along the body — have traditionally been associated with different physical, emotional and spiritual aspects of the human being. When these centres are in balance, the vital flow tends to circulate more smoothly, favouring a sense of lightness, mental clarity and emotional stability. When there are blockages, feelings of anxiety, excessive tiredness or difficulty expressing emotions may appear.</p><p> </p><p>It is in this context that crystals enter as natural allies. Each stone has its own vibration, formed over thousands of years by pressure and by the elements of the earth. When placed near the body or used during moments of meditation, they can act as energetic catalysts, helping release tensions and re-establish the harmony of the chakras.</p><p> </p><p>For example, rose quartz is often associated with the heart chakra, stimulating compassion, empathy and self-love. Amethyst, linked to the third eye, is seen as a support for intuition and mental serenity. Citrine, related to the solar plexus, inspires confidence and vitality.</p><p> </p><p>More than a technique, aligning the chakras with crystals can be understood as a ritual of pause and presence. By dedicating a few minutes a day to breathing, feeling and observing, a person creates an inner space of listening, reconnecting with themselves and with their deepest needs.</p><p> </p><p>In an accelerated world, where doing often outweighs feeling, this practice invites us to slow down and remember that balance is also born of silence, of attention and of respect for one’s own energy. After all, caring for the chakras is, in essence, caring for the life that pulses inside each of us.</p><p></p><p>If you would like to know a little more and to book an alignment session, you can get in touch on WhatsApp 085 2338910.</p>\n','Aligning the Invisible to Harmonise Everyday Life','Text: Janine Meira Henriques. Holistic therapist, writer, playwright, artist, photographer, journalist and mother of four.','publish','open','open','','aligning-the-invisible-to-harmonise-everyday-life','','','2026-09-26 16:27:41','2026-09-26 15:27:41','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/02/15/aligning-the-invisible-to-harmonise-everyday-life/',0,'post','',0),(29179,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:41','2026-09-26 15:27:41','<p><em>Text: Janine Meira Henriques</em></p><p><em>Holistic therapist, writer, playwright, artist, photographer, journalist and mother of four.</em></p><p><em>Qualifications: undergraduate degrees in journalism, performing arts and holistic therapy, including reiki, radionics table work, Access bars, astrology and tarot. Psychology studies in progress, completing in June 2026.</em></p><p><em>Postgraduate studies: Brazilian literature, human sexuality.</em></p><p></p><p> </p><p></p><p>In recent years, integrative practices that bring spirituality and personal care together have gained ground in conversations about quality of life. Among them, work with chakras and crystals stands out for combining ancestral symbolism, energetic sensitivity and an invitation to self-knowledge.</p><p> </p><p>The chakras — subtle energy centres distributed along the body — have traditionally been associated with different physical, emotional and spiritual aspects of the human being. When these centres are in balance, the vital flow tends to circulate more smoothly, favouring a sense of lightness, mental clarity and emotional stability. When there are blockages, feelings of anxiety, excessive tiredness or difficulty expressing emotions may appear.</p><p> </p><p>It is in this context that crystals enter as natural allies. Each stone has its own vibration, formed over thousands of years by pressure and by the elements of the earth. When placed near the body or used during moments of meditation, they can act as energetic catalysts, helping release tensions and re-establish the harmony of the chakras.</p><p> </p><p>For example, rose quartz is often associated with the heart chakra, stimulating compassion, empathy and self-love. Amethyst, linked to the third eye, is seen as a support for intuition and mental serenity. Citrine, related to the solar plexus, inspires confidence and vitality.</p><p> </p><p>More than a technique, aligning the chakras with crystals can be understood as a ritual of pause and presence. By dedicating a few minutes a day to breathing, feeling and observing, a person creates an inner space of listening, reconnecting with themselves and with their deepest needs.</p><p> </p><p>In an accelerated world, where doing often outweighs feeling, this practice invites us to slow down and remember that balance is also born of silence, of attention and of respect for one’s own energy. After all, caring for the chakras is, in essence, caring for the life that pulses inside each of us.</p><p></p><p>If you would like to know a little more and to book an alignment session, you can get in touch on WhatsApp 085 2338910.</p>\n','Aligning the Invisible to Harmonise Everyday Life','Text: Janine Meira Henriques. Holistic therapist, writer, playwright, artist, photographer, journalist and mother of four.','inherit','closed','closed','','29178-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:41','2026-09-26 15:27:41','',29178,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29179',0,'revision','',0),(29180,283039559,'2026-02-17 21:32:58','2026-02-17 21:32:58','<p></p><p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p><p><em>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</em></p><p><em>Instagram: </em><u>https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>I was delighted to receive the invitation from dear Tati to write for this magazine for Brazilians in <em>Portlaoise</em>. It is an honour to be able to share reflections on a theme as essential as it is delicate: life purpose.</p><p>My name is Juliana, I am Brazilian, I am 43 years old and I have lived in Ireland for nine years. I am Lia’s mother, Thiago’s wife and the founder of <strong>Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong>, a school of feminine consciousness based on the Natural Laws of Cooperation of Life — laws that reconnect us to the natural cycle of existence, to ancestry and to the deep sense of belonging.</p><p>And it is precisely about meaning that I want to talk with you today.</p><p>The word “purpose” is one of the most repeated of our era. Many of us look for quick answers: <em>What is my purpose? How do I find it?</em></p><p>But perhaps the truest question is a different one:</p><p><strong>And what if purpose is not something you find, but something revealed when there is space?</strong></p><p>Purpose is not only a mental concept. It is an intimate, spiritual and emotional perception. Especially we, women, so often overloaded by external demands, try to understand life only with the head, when purpose can only be heard with the heart.</p><p>In my experience, purpose is not confused with who we are, nor is it limited to what we do professionally. Purpose is that which moves us from within. It is a sense of direction, like an internal compass pointing to where the soul wishes to walk.</p><p>When we are aligned with that sense, life gains more harmony. But on human journeys we lose our way many times. And that happens because we live disconnected from our essence and from the natural cycle of life.</p><p>I like to think of purpose the way we think about our children: we bring them into the world, care for them, educate them, but they do not belong to us. One day they gain the world. Purpose is like that too. It is not tied to us. It needs to gain life. It is a gift given away.</p><p>Much of the emotional illness of our era is born precisely from this disconnection: burnout, anxiety, chronic stress. It is not only an excess of tasks; it is the absence of meaning in what you give.</p><p>Reconnection, however, can begin simply. A few minutes of silence. A conscious breath. A prayer. An intimate piece of writing. A sincere request to return to one’s essence.</p><p>Because it is not only <em>what we do</em>, but <em>how we do it</em>.</p><p>Sometimes the route will need to be recalculated. In some cases, the purpose is inside the current path, but with a truer “how”. In others, it will be necessary to listen to an old calling that has been waiting a long time for courage.</p><p>And it is important to remember: purpose is not a prize for those who search, but a process for those who give themselves.</p><p>May this text be only a small opening. An invitation for you to breathe and to ask yourself:</p><p><strong>What in me wants to gain life now?</strong></p><p><strong>What meaning wishes to move me?</strong></p><p></p><p>With gratitude,</p><p></p><p><strong>Juliana</strong></p><p>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</p>\n','Purpose: The Meaning That Moves Us from Within','Text: Juliana Gomes, founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/','publish','open','open','','purpose-the-meaning-that-moves-us-from-within','','','2026-09-26 16:27:41','2026-09-26 15:27:41','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/02/17/purpose-the-meaning-that-moves-us-from-within/',0,'post','',0),(29181,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:41','2026-09-26 15:27:41','<p></p><p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p><p><em>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</em></p><p><em>Instagram: </em><u>https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>I was delighted to receive the invitation from dear Tati to write for this magazine for Brazilians in <em>Portlaoise</em>. It is an honour to be able to share reflections on a theme as essential as it is delicate: life purpose.</p><p>My name is Juliana, I am Brazilian, I am 43 years old and I have lived in Ireland for nine years. I am Lia’s mother, Thiago’s wife and the founder of <strong>Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong>, a school of feminine consciousness based on the Natural Laws of Cooperation of Life — laws that reconnect us to the natural cycle of existence, to ancestry and to the deep sense of belonging.</p><p>And it is precisely about meaning that I want to talk with you today.</p><p>The word “purpose” is one of the most repeated of our era. Many of us look for quick answers: <em>What is my purpose? How do I find it?</em></p><p>But perhaps the truest question is a different one:</p><p><strong>And what if purpose is not something you find, but something revealed when there is space?</strong></p><p>Purpose is not only a mental concept. It is an intimate, spiritual and emotional perception. Especially we, women, so often overloaded by external demands, try to understand life only with the head, when purpose can only be heard with the heart.</p><p>In my experience, purpose is not confused with who we are, nor is it limited to what we do professionally. Purpose is that which moves us from within. It is a sense of direction, like an internal compass pointing to where the soul wishes to walk.</p><p>When we are aligned with that sense, life gains more harmony. But on human journeys we lose our way many times. And that happens because we live disconnected from our essence and from the natural cycle of life.</p><p>I like to think of purpose the way we think about our children: we bring them into the world, care for them, educate them, but they do not belong to us. One day they gain the world. Purpose is like that too. It is not tied to us. It needs to gain life. It is a gift given away.</p><p>Much of the emotional illness of our era is born precisely from this disconnection: burnout, anxiety, chronic stress. It is not only an excess of tasks; it is the absence of meaning in what you give.</p><p>Reconnection, however, can begin simply. A few minutes of silence. A conscious breath. A prayer. An intimate piece of writing. A sincere request to return to one’s essence.</p><p>Because it is not only <em>what we do</em>, but <em>how we do it</em>.</p><p>Sometimes the route will need to be recalculated. In some cases, the purpose is inside the current path, but with a truer “how”. In others, it will be necessary to listen to an old calling that has been waiting a long time for courage.</p><p>And it is important to remember: purpose is not a prize for those who search, but a process for those who give themselves.</p><p>May this text be only a small opening. An invitation for you to breathe and to ask yourself:</p><p><strong>What in me wants to gain life now?</strong></p><p><strong>What meaning wishes to move me?</strong></p><p></p><p>With gratitude,</p><p></p><p><strong>Juliana</strong></p><p>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</p>\n','Purpose: The Meaning That Moves Us from Within','Text: Juliana Gomes, founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/','inherit','closed','closed','','29180-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:41','2026-09-26 15:27:41','',29180,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29181',0,'revision','',0),(29182,283039559,'2026-03-17 19:39:51','2026-03-17 19:39:51','<p>(February as a portal of transition)</p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>There is a moment in the year when life seems to breathe more slowly.</p><p>It is not the height of winter, when everything has already clearly withdrawn.</p><p>Nor is it yet spring, with its open promise of movement and expansion.</p><p>February is that sensitive interval — a silent corridor between what has ended and what is</p><p>still being born.</p><p></p><p>Energetically, it is a month that disturbs deep layers.</p><p>Old emotions may reappear, tiredness becomes more visible, and a strange mixture</p><p>of impatience with apathy arises. As if one part wanted to move forward... while</p><p>another still needed to rest.</p><p></p><p>Astrologically, February usually runs through <em><strong>Aquarius and Pisces</strong></em> — archetypes of</p><p>liberation and dissolution.</p><p></p><p>Aquarius opens windows. It questions patterns, breaks old mental structures and shows what</p><p>no longer fits the version of us that is emerging.</p><p>Pisces, right after, softens the defences. It brings sensitivity and reveals where there is still</p><p>unfelt pain, unprocessed grief, forgotten dreams.</p><p></p><p>That is why February can be emotionally intense even when, on the outside, nothing</p><p>“big” happens. Inside, a great deal reorganises itself.</p><p>This is not a month of maximum productivity. It is a month of <em>inner honesty.</em></p><p></p><p>In the mind, it invites us to review narratives:</p><p>— What thoughts no longer make sense for who I am today?</p><p>— What decisions do I keep postponing out of fear of changing?</p><p>In the emotions, the invitation is to feel without hurry — not to resolve everything, but to allow</p><p>old processes to complete.</p><p></p><p>In the physical body, the request is simple and therapeutic: more rest than performance, more</p><p>warmth than demand, deep breathing, gentle stretching, slow walks.</p><p>The nervous system is still in winter mode.</p><p></p><p>And winter is not a mistake of nature — it is the laboratory where the roots grow.</p><p>In the practical areas of life, February is a month of adjustment, not of expansion.</p><p>It is when we notice what really deserves energy when spring arrives — what</p><p>continues, what ends, what needs a new form.</p><p></p><p>Spring does not arrive suddenly. It begins as a whisper: one day with more light,</p><p>one deeper breath, one new idea that arises without effort.</p><p>Navigating February wisely is not forcing flowers before their time.</p><p></p><p>It is allowing yourself to:</p><p>● close cycles without dramatising</p><p>● rest without guilt</p><p>● review priorities with honesty</p><p>● release habits that no longer sustain the next version of yourself</p><p>● care for the body as fertile soil</p><p></p><p>If winter was the time to gather, February is the time to listen to what within you</p><p>wants to be born.</p><p>And when spring finally opens — inside and out — what was cared for in silence</p><p>becomes natural movement, not effort.</p><p></p><p>Perhaps the truest invitation of this month is not to do more, but to soften enough to</p><p>notice who you are becoming.</p><p>Because it is in this space between the end and the beginning that life gently decides what</p><p>your next form will be.</p><p></p><p>Aria Milioni</p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p>\n','Between the Silence of Winter and the First Breath of Spring','(February as a portal of transition) Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist.','publish','open','open','','between-the-silence-of-winter-and-the-first-breath-of-spring','','','2026-09-26 16:27:42','2026-09-26 15:27:42','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/17/between-the-silence-of-winter-and-the-first-breath-of-spring/',0,'post','',0),(29183,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:42','2026-09-26 15:27:42','<p>(February as a portal of transition)</p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>There is a moment in the year when life seems to breathe more slowly.</p><p>It is not the height of winter, when everything has already clearly withdrawn.</p><p>Nor is it yet spring, with its open promise of movement and expansion.</p><p>February is that sensitive interval — a silent corridor between what has ended and what is</p><p>still being born.</p><p></p><p>Energetically, it is a month that disturbs deep layers.</p><p>Old emotions may reappear, tiredness becomes more visible, and a strange mixture</p><p>of impatience with apathy arises. As if one part wanted to move forward... while</p><p>another still needed to rest.</p><p></p><p>Astrologically, February usually runs through <em><strong>Aquarius and Pisces</strong></em> — archetypes of</p><p>liberation and dissolution.</p><p></p><p>Aquarius opens windows. It questions patterns, breaks old mental structures and shows what</p><p>no longer fits the version of us that is emerging.</p><p>Pisces, right after, softens the defences. It brings sensitivity and reveals where there is still</p><p>unfelt pain, unprocessed grief, forgotten dreams.</p><p></p><p>That is why February can be emotionally intense even when, on the outside, nothing</p><p>“big” happens. Inside, a great deal reorganises itself.</p><p>This is not a month of maximum productivity. It is a month of <em>inner honesty.</em></p><p></p><p>In the mind, it invites us to review narratives:</p><p>— What thoughts no longer make sense for who I am today?</p><p>— What decisions do I keep postponing out of fear of changing?</p><p>In the emotions, the invitation is to feel without hurry — not to resolve everything, but to allow</p><p>old processes to complete.</p><p></p><p>In the physical body, the request is simple and therapeutic: more rest than performance, more</p><p>warmth than demand, deep breathing, gentle stretching, slow walks.</p><p>The nervous system is still in winter mode.</p><p></p><p>And winter is not a mistake of nature — it is the laboratory where the roots grow.</p><p>In the practical areas of life, February is a month of adjustment, not of expansion.</p><p>It is when we notice what really deserves energy when spring arrives — what</p><p>continues, what ends, what needs a new form.</p><p></p><p>Spring does not arrive suddenly. It begins as a whisper: one day with more light,</p><p>one deeper breath, one new idea that arises without effort.</p><p>Navigating February wisely is not forcing flowers before their time.</p><p></p><p>It is allowing yourself to:</p><p>● close cycles without dramatising</p><p>● rest without guilt</p><p>● review priorities with honesty</p><p>● release habits that no longer sustain the next version of yourself</p><p>● care for the body as fertile soil</p><p></p><p>If winter was the time to gather, February is the time to listen to what within you</p><p>wants to be born.</p><p>And when spring finally opens — inside and out — what was cared for in silence</p><p>becomes natural movement, not effort.</p><p></p><p>Perhaps the truest invitation of this month is not to do more, but to soften enough to</p><p>notice who you are becoming.</p><p>Because it is in this space between the end and the beginning that life gently decides what</p><p>your next form will be.</p><p></p><p>Aria Milioni</p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p>\n','Between the Silence of Winter and the First Breath of Spring','(February as a portal of transition) Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist.','inherit','closed','closed','','29182-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:42','2026-09-26 15:27:42','',29182,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29183',0,'revision','',0),(29184,283039559,'2026-03-17 19:48:03','2026-03-17 19:48:03','<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>There is a moment in the year when the energy changes direction — silently, almost imperceptibly. It is like when the body wakes before the alarm and something inside you starts circulating again.</p><p>Spring begins like this.</p><p>After winter’s withdrawal, life starts pushing outwards again. The light increases, the air changes, and a natural wish arises to reorganise, to move the body and to decide what was left pending.</p><p></p><p>Astrologically, this portal coincides with the <em><strong>Sun entering Aries — the start of the</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Astrological New Year.</strong></em></p><p>If January opens the social calendar, March opens the calendar of the soul.</p><p>Aries represents the first impulse of life — the seed breaking through the soil. It does not ask for</p><p>perfection, it asks for movement. That is why, in this phase, many people feel more energy,</p><p>positive restlessness and the need to start something new.</p><p></p><p>After the dive of Pisces, which cleansed and closed cycles, the question emerges:</p><p>“And now, what are you going to do with who you have become?”</p><p>Spring is not only about flowers — it is about direction.</p><p></p><p>For mental health, this is an important period for choosing where to place your energy. The</p><p>mind wants to act, but the alignment comes from consciousness, not from excess. Ask yourself: what</p><p>really deserves my attention now?</p><p></p><p>Emotionally, the season asks for a gentle courage — allowing yourself to take up more space and</p><p>to seek relationships more aligned with who you are today.</p><p>In the physical body, Aries’ energy needs movement so that it does not turn into tension. Walking</p><p>more, stretching, returning to physical activity and receiving natural light help the nervous system leave</p><p>winter mode.</p><p></p><p>In practical life, this is the ideal moment to start projects, reorganise routines and test</p><p>new directions without demanding immediate results. It is a phase of conscious</p><p>experimentation.</p><p></p><p>Aligning yourself with the Astrological New Year is not changing everything at once, but acting from</p><p>a truer place.</p><p>If winter asked “what needs to end?”, spring asks “what deserves to be born?”</p><p></p><p>Choosing a living intention — something that can be felt in the body — is already enough to</p><p>open the way.</p><p>The Astrological New Year is an organic restart. A whisper from nature saying:</p><p>“Now you can move forward.”</p><p></p><p>The invitation is to move, without losing the inner listening that winter taught — so that action</p><p>and consciousness walk together and this new cycle is lived with presence, direction and</p><p>vitality.</p><p></p><p>Aria Milioni</p><p></p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p>\n','When the Earth Awakens: Spring and the Astrological New Year','Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist. Astrologically this portal coincides with the Sun entering Aries — the start of the astrological New Year.','publish','open','open','','when-the-earth-awakens-spring-and-the-astrological-new-year','','','2026-09-26 16:27:42','2026-09-26 15:27:42','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/17/when-the-earth-awakens-spring-and-the-astrological-new-year/',0,'post','',0),(29185,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:42','2026-09-26 15:27:42','<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>There is a moment in the year when the energy changes direction — silently, almost imperceptibly. It is like when the body wakes before the alarm and something inside you starts circulating again.</p><p>Spring begins like this.</p><p>After winter’s withdrawal, life starts pushing outwards again. The light increases, the air changes, and a natural wish arises to reorganise, to move the body and to decide what was left pending.</p><p></p><p>Astrologically, this portal coincides with the <em><strong>Sun entering Aries — the start of the</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Astrological New Year.</strong></em></p><p>If January opens the social calendar, March opens the calendar of the soul.</p><p>Aries represents the first impulse of life — the seed breaking through the soil. It does not ask for</p><p>perfection, it asks for movement. That is why, in this phase, many people feel more energy,</p><p>positive restlessness and the need to start something new.</p><p></p><p>After the dive of Pisces, which cleansed and closed cycles, the question emerges:</p><p>“And now, what are you going to do with who you have become?”</p><p>Spring is not only about flowers — it is about direction.</p><p></p><p>For mental health, this is an important period for choosing where to place your energy. The</p><p>mind wants to act, but the alignment comes from consciousness, not from excess. Ask yourself: what</p><p>really deserves my attention now?</p><p></p><p>Emotionally, the season asks for a gentle courage — allowing yourself to take up more space and</p><p>to seek relationships more aligned with who you are today.</p><p>In the physical body, Aries’ energy needs movement so that it does not turn into tension. Walking</p><p>more, stretching, returning to physical activity and receiving natural light help the nervous system leave</p><p>winter mode.</p><p></p><p>In practical life, this is the ideal moment to start projects, reorganise routines and test</p><p>new directions without demanding immediate results. It is a phase of conscious</p><p>experimentation.</p><p></p><p>Aligning yourself with the Astrological New Year is not changing everything at once, but acting from</p><p>a truer place.</p><p>If winter asked “what needs to end?”, spring asks “what deserves to be born?”</p><p></p><p>Choosing a living intention — something that can be felt in the body — is already enough to</p><p>open the way.</p><p>The Astrological New Year is an organic restart. A whisper from nature saying:</p><p>“Now you can move forward.”</p><p></p><p>The invitation is to move, without losing the inner listening that winter taught — so that action</p><p>and consciousness walk together and this new cycle is lived with presence, direction and</p><p>vitality.</p><p></p><p>Aria Milioni</p><p></p><p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p>\n','When the Earth Awakens: Spring and the Astrological New Year','Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist. Astrologically this portal coincides with the Sun entering Aries — the start of the astrological New Year.','inherit','closed','closed','','29184-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:42','2026-09-26 15:27:42','',29184,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29185',0,'revision','',0),(29186,283039559,'2026-03-19 16:37:24','2026-03-19 16:37:24','<p><strong>Here is a list of places in Co. Laois, Ireland, where you can find English courses, other languages, vocational training, workforce preparation and support for entrepreneurs, with links and contacts to make your research easier 👇</strong></p><p></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📘 English Courses and Other Languages</strong></p><p><strong>🗣️ Schools and Language Centres</strong></p><p><strong>Our Courses Ltd – English courses and general training in Carlow (near Laois).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.ourcourses.com/</strong></u></p><p><strong>Solenn’s School of French and English – English and French lessons in Rathdowney.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://solennsschool.com//</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Carlow International English Language School – English school in Carlow (reachable from Laois).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.ciels.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Equinox Education Services Ltd – English courses with international support.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://equinoxlearnabroad.com/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📌 English Centre in Portlaoise / Laois</strong></p><p><strong>LOETB Information Hub and the Laois and Offaly Education and Training Board – OF Education and Training Board (LOETB) has several options, including English for Speakers of Other Languages (ESOL) and English courses for adults. Visit the courses section on their website to see availability and enrolment.</strong></p><p><strong>Laois County Council</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🎓 Vocational Training and General Education</strong></p><p><strong>💼 Training and Skills Development Centres</strong></p><p><strong>Pitman Training Laois – Professional training courses (administration, business, software, etc.).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://www.pitman-training.ie/laois</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Portarlington Further Education and Training Centre – Adult education and additional qualifications.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://portarlingtonfetc.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Portlaoise Further Education and Training Centre – Technical courses and general training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://www.loetb.ie/portlaoise-fetc</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Abbeyleix Further Education Centre – Adult education and local training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://abbeyleixfetc.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Further education center (loetb) mountmellick – Further education in Mountmellick.</strong></p><p><strong>National Learning Network Portlaoise – Support for entering the labour market and professional training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://rehab.ie/centres/national-learning-network-portlaoise/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Metac Training – A variety of technical training and courses.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://metac.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong>SafetyTraining.ie</strong><strong> – Safety certifications and courses (useful for work).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://safetytraining.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Kearney Training &amp; Consultancy (KTC Safety) Ltd – Technical and professional training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.ktcsafety.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>ProLash Ireland – Courses and professional development.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.prolashireland.com/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>On The Job Training – Practical training and learning in a real work context.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://onthejobtraining.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Laois Partnership Company is the local community development company for County Laois. It is funded by the Irish State and the European Union and works to improve the lives of people and communities in the region.</strong></p><p><u><strong>laoispartnership.ie</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>What it does:</strong></p><p><strong>🔹 Promotes community and social development – helping to make Laois a better place to live and work.</strong></p><p><strong>🔹 Implements programmes such as:</strong></p><p><strong>• LEADER Programme – supports rural projects, initiatives and new businesses with funding and mentoring.</strong></p><p><strong>• SICAP (Social Inclusion Community Activation Programme) – supports social inclusion and employment.</strong></p><p><strong>• Community Employment, Tús and Rural Social Schemes – connection to work and support for vulnerable groups.</strong></p><p><strong>• Integration programmes for newly arrived people (for example, English classes and integration support).</strong></p><p><strong>• Support for older people, families and local community initiatives.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>The organisation works with local groups, offers small grants, facilitates jobs and helps communities that feel left out.</strong></p>\n','Training in Laois','Places in Co. Laois, Ireland, where you can find English courses, other languages, vocational training, workforce preparation and support for entrepreneurs.','publish','open','open','','training-in-laois-ireland','','','2026-09-26 16:27:43','2026-09-26 15:27:43','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/19/training-in-laois-ireland/',0,'post','',0),(29187,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:43','2026-09-26 15:27:43','<p><strong>Here is a list of places in Co. Laois, Ireland, where you can find English courses, other languages, vocational training, workforce preparation and support for entrepreneurs, with links and contacts to make your research easier 👇</strong></p><p></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📘 English Courses and Other Languages</strong></p><p><strong>🗣️ Schools and Language Centres</strong></p><p><strong>Our Courses Ltd – English courses and general training in Carlow (near Laois).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.ourcourses.com/</strong></u></p><p><strong>Solenn’s School of French and English – English and French lessons in Rathdowney.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://solennsschool.com//</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Carlow International English Language School – English school in Carlow (reachable from Laois).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.ciels.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Equinox Education Services Ltd – English courses with international support.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://equinoxlearnabroad.com/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📌 English Centre in Portlaoise / Laois</strong></p><p><strong>LOETB Information Hub and the Laois and Offaly Education and Training Board – OF Education and Training Board (LOETB) has several options, including English for Speakers of Other Languages (ESOL) and English courses for adults. Visit the courses section on their website to see availability and enrolment.</strong></p><p><strong>Laois County Council</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🎓 Vocational Training and General Education</strong></p><p><strong>💼 Training and Skills Development Centres</strong></p><p><strong>Pitman Training Laois – Professional training courses (administration, business, software, etc.).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://www.pitman-training.ie/laois</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Portarlington Further Education and Training Centre – Adult education and additional qualifications.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://portarlingtonfetc.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Portlaoise Further Education and Training Centre – Technical courses and general training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://www.loetb.ie/portlaoise-fetc</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Abbeyleix Further Education Centre – Adult education and local training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://abbeyleixfetc.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Further education center (loetb) mountmellick – Further education in Mountmellick.</strong></p><p><strong>National Learning Network Portlaoise – Support for entering the labour market and professional training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://rehab.ie/centres/national-learning-network-portlaoise/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Metac Training – A variety of technical training and courses.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://metac.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong>SafetyTraining.ie</strong><strong> – Safety certifications and courses (useful for work).</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://safetytraining.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Kearney Training &amp; Consultancy (KTC Safety) Ltd – Technical and professional training.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.ktcsafety.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>ProLash Ireland – Courses and professional development.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>http://www.prolashireland.com/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>On The Job Training – Practical training and learning in a real work context.</strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Website: </strong><u><strong>https://onthejobtraining.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Laois Partnership Company is the local community development company for County Laois. It is funded by the Irish State and the European Union and works to improve the lives of people and communities in the region.</strong></p><p><u><strong>laoispartnership.ie</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>What it does:</strong></p><p><strong>🔹 Promotes community and social development – helping to make Laois a better place to live and work.</strong></p><p><strong>🔹 Implements programmes such as:</strong></p><p><strong>• LEADER Programme – supports rural projects, initiatives and new businesses with funding and mentoring.</strong></p><p><strong>• SICAP (Social Inclusion Community Activation Programme) – supports social inclusion and employment.</strong></p><p><strong>• Community Employment, Tús and Rural Social Schemes – connection to work and support for vulnerable groups.</strong></p><p><strong>• Integration programmes for newly arrived people (for example, English classes and integration support).</strong></p><p><strong>• Support for older people, families and local community initiatives.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>The organisation works with local groups, offers small grants, facilitates jobs and helps communities that feel left out.</strong></p>\n','Training in Laois','Places in Co. Laois, Ireland, where you can find English courses, other languages, vocational training, workforce preparation and support for entrepreneurs.','inherit','closed','closed','','29186-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:43','2026-09-26 15:27:43','',29186,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29187',0,'revision','',0),(29188,283039559,'2026-03-19 16:46:20','2026-03-19 16:46:20','<p></p><p>📊 Laois Partnership</p><p>https://laoispartnership.ie/</p><p>https://www.instagram.com/laoispartnershipcompany/</p><p></p><p>Laois Partnership Company supports businesses and entrepreneurs through:</p><p></p><p>✔ rural development programmes (such as LEADER) with grants and business training</p><p>✔ SICAP, with mentoring, help with the business plan and links to support networks</p><p>✔ training and upskilling in skills that are useful for business</p><p>✔ referral to other business support organisations in Laois and the region</p><p></p><p>💡 1. Programmes and Business Information</p><p>One of the ways Laois Partnership supports businesses is through rural and community development programmes that also help local businesses grow and innovate. For example, the LEADER Programme 2023‑2027 (co-funded by the EU and the Department of Rural Development and Gaeltacht) provides financial support, training and advice for:</p><p>businesses that are developing or expanding</p><p>start‑ups that want to create new products or services</p><p>businesses in sectors such as tourism, food/craft and creative industries, and entities that want to prepare feasibility studies or specific training for their growth</p><p></p><p>👉 These grants usually require matched funding or proof that you have already invested some capital.</p><p></p><p>📈 2. SICAP – Business and Startup Support</p><p>Among the programmes Laois Partnership manages is also SICAP (Social Inclusion Community Activation Programme).</p><p>This programme offers free support to people and groups who want to:</p><p>develop a business idea or plan</p><p>put a business plan together</p><p>get guidance, training and networking</p><p>information on grants such as the Back to Work Enterprise Allowance (help for those leaving unemployment to start a business)</p><p>support for setting up or developing social enterprises (businesses with a social impact).</p><p></p><p>👉 SICAP services are confidential, free and optional, and can be useful both for those thinking about starting a business and for those who want to expand an existing one.</p><p></p><p>🧠 3. Training and Capacity Building</p><p>Through the programmes it implements, Laois Partnership offers or refers you to:</p><p>training in basic business skills</p><p>workshops on management, growth and sustainability</p><p>opportunities for mentoring, networking and sharing good practice</p><p>connections with other local resources and support organisations (for example, Laois Local Enterprise Office)</p><p></p><p>Laois County Council</p><p>📍 Useful contacts</p><p>📞 Phone: 057‑8661900</p><p>✉️ Email: info@laoispartnership.ie</p><p>📍 Location: New Road, Portlaoise, Co. Laois, R32 VY22, Ireland</p><p>👉 Official contacts and form: <u>https://laoispartnership.ie/contact-us/</u></p><p></p><p>Laois Partnership</p><p>🧩 How this fits with other business support</p><p>Although Laois Partnership offers guidance, training, networking and access to grant programmes, many businesses also use complementary services, such as:</p><p>Local Enterprise Office Laois – specific support for micro and small businesses, mentoring, technical courses and business grants (for example for start‑ups and expansion);</p><p>Laois Chamber Alliance – business networking, events and business promotion in the county;</p><p>National funding or business coaching programmes offered by public and private agencies.</p><p>👉 Laois Partnership can often refer businesses or entrepreneurs to these and other resources, and help with planning or applying for external support.</p><p></p><p>💡 Business &amp; Enterprise Training – Laois County Council</p><p>The Local Enterprise Office Laois (through the local council) provides courses and workshops for those who want to start a business, improve management skills and develop their company. The courses are updated regularly and are listed on their website.</p><p></p><p>Laois County Council</p><p>➡️ Visit: <u>https://laois.ie/business-and-economy/business-and-enterprise-training/business-and-enterprise-training</u></p><p></p><p>💡 LOETB – Laois &amp; Offaly Education and Training Board also has vocational and professional courses that can help employability and business skills development (look on their website under “Further Education &amp; Training”).</p><p>Laois Offaly ETB</p><p></p><p>📈 Laois Chamber Alliance — supporting business and tourism</p><p>The Chamber is a direct support body for businesses, helping them grow, innovate and influence local policies that benefit the region’s economy.</p><p>Laois Chamber Alliance</p><p></p><p>It is the county’s main business and tourism organisation. It arose from the merger of the Laois Chamber of Commerce with Laois Tourism to create a stronger group representing businesses, commerce and tourist attractions.</p><p>Laois Chamber Alliance</p><p></p><p>👉 Its main mission is to make Laois an ideal place to live, work, visit, invest and do business — facilitating networking, events, support and business growth opportunities.</p><p></p><p>What it does:</p><p>🔹 Represents and supports more than 150 members from different sectors.</p><p>🔹 Promotes networking, training and mentoring for local entrepreneurs and businesses.</p><p>🔹 Organises business events, conferences and promotional opportunities.</p><p>🔹 Creates initiatives that promote tourism and showcase Laois nationally and internationally.</p><p>🔹 Runs events such as the Laois Business Awards, which celebrate its most successful businesses.</p><p><u>laois-nationalist.ie</u></p><p></p><p>📚 Other Options and Resources</p><p>📖 eLearning and Online Courses</p><p>Even Laois public libraries offer members free access to online courses in areas such as technology, administration and communication, through the Universal Class platform.</p><p>Laois County Council</p><p></p><p>✅ Practical tip</p><p>Many of these centres — LOETB in particular — let you filter courses by area (e.g. English, technology, business) on their own website, and many have free or subsidised options for residents in Ireland.</p><p>Laois Offaly ETB</p>\n','Courses and Support for Entrepreneurs','Laois Partnership, the Local Enterprise Office and the Laois Chamber Alliance: programmes, grants, training and mentoring for local businesses.','publish','open','open','','courses-and-support-for-entrepreneurs','','','2026-09-26 16:27:43','2026-09-26 15:27:43','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/19/courses-and-support-for-entrepreneurs/',0,'post','',0),(29189,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:43','2026-09-26 15:27:43','<p></p><p>📊 Laois Partnership</p><p>https://laoispartnership.ie/</p><p>https://www.instagram.com/laoispartnershipcompany/</p><p></p><p>Laois Partnership Company supports businesses and entrepreneurs through:</p><p></p><p>✔ rural development programmes (such as LEADER) with grants and business training</p><p>✔ SICAP, with mentoring, help with the business plan and links to support networks</p><p>✔ training and upskilling in skills that are useful for business</p><p>✔ referral to other business support organisations in Laois and the region</p><p></p><p>💡 1. Programmes and Business Information</p><p>One of the ways Laois Partnership supports businesses is through rural and community development programmes that also help local businesses grow and innovate. For example, the LEADER Programme 2023‑2027 (co-funded by the EU and the Department of Rural Development and Gaeltacht) provides financial support, training and advice for:</p><p>businesses that are developing or expanding</p><p>start‑ups that want to create new products or services</p><p>businesses in sectors such as tourism, food/craft and creative industries, and entities that want to prepare feasibility studies or specific training for their growth</p><p></p><p>👉 These grants usually require matched funding or proof that you have already invested some capital.</p><p></p><p>📈 2. SICAP – Business and Startup Support</p><p>Among the programmes Laois Partnership manages is also SICAP (Social Inclusion Community Activation Programme).</p><p>This programme offers free support to people and groups who want to:</p><p>develop a business idea or plan</p><p>put a business plan together</p><p>get guidance, training and networking</p><p>information on grants such as the Back to Work Enterprise Allowance (help for those leaving unemployment to start a business)</p><p>support for setting up or developing social enterprises (businesses with a social impact).</p><p></p><p>👉 SICAP services are confidential, free and optional, and can be useful both for those thinking about starting a business and for those who want to expand an existing one.</p><p></p><p>🧠 3. Training and Capacity Building</p><p>Through the programmes it implements, Laois Partnership offers or refers you to:</p><p>training in basic business skills</p><p>workshops on management, growth and sustainability</p><p>opportunities for mentoring, networking and sharing good practice</p><p>connections with other local resources and support organisations (for example, Laois Local Enterprise Office)</p><p></p><p>Laois County Council</p><p>📍 Useful contacts</p><p>📞 Phone: 057‑8661900</p><p>✉️ Email: info@laoispartnership.ie</p><p>📍 Location: New Road, Portlaoise, Co. Laois, R32 VY22, Ireland</p><p>👉 Official contacts and form: <u>https://laoispartnership.ie/contact-us/</u></p><p></p><p>Laois Partnership</p><p>🧩 How this fits with other business support</p><p>Although Laois Partnership offers guidance, training, networking and access to grant programmes, many businesses also use complementary services, such as:</p><p>Local Enterprise Office Laois – specific support for micro and small businesses, mentoring, technical courses and business grants (for example for start‑ups and expansion);</p><p>Laois Chamber Alliance – business networking, events and business promotion in the county;</p><p>National funding or business coaching programmes offered by public and private agencies.</p><p>👉 Laois Partnership can often refer businesses or entrepreneurs to these and other resources, and help with planning or applying for external support.</p><p></p><p>💡 Business &amp; Enterprise Training – Laois County Council</p><p>The Local Enterprise Office Laois (through the local council) provides courses and workshops for those who want to start a business, improve management skills and develop their company. The courses are updated regularly and are listed on their website.</p><p></p><p>Laois County Council</p><p>➡️ Visit: <u>https://laois.ie/business-and-economy/business-and-enterprise-training/business-and-enterprise-training</u></p><p></p><p>💡 LOETB – Laois &amp; Offaly Education and Training Board also has vocational and professional courses that can help employability and business skills development (look on their website under “Further Education &amp; Training”).</p><p>Laois Offaly ETB</p><p></p><p>📈 Laois Chamber Alliance — supporting business and tourism</p><p>The Chamber is a direct support body for businesses, helping them grow, innovate and influence local policies that benefit the region’s economy.</p><p>Laois Chamber Alliance</p><p></p><p>It is the county’s main business and tourism organisation. It arose from the merger of the Laois Chamber of Commerce with Laois Tourism to create a stronger group representing businesses, commerce and tourist attractions.</p><p>Laois Chamber Alliance</p><p></p><p>👉 Its main mission is to make Laois an ideal place to live, work, visit, invest and do business — facilitating networking, events, support and business growth opportunities.</p><p></p><p>What it does:</p><p>🔹 Represents and supports more than 150 members from different sectors.</p><p>🔹 Promotes networking, training and mentoring for local entrepreneurs and businesses.</p><p>🔹 Organises business events, conferences and promotional opportunities.</p><p>🔹 Creates initiatives that promote tourism and showcase Laois nationally and internationally.</p><p>🔹 Runs events such as the Laois Business Awards, which celebrate its most successful businesses.</p><p><u>laois-nationalist.ie</u></p><p></p><p>📚 Other Options and Resources</p><p>📖 eLearning and Online Courses</p><p>Even Laois public libraries offer members free access to online courses in areas such as technology, administration and communication, through the Universal Class platform.</p><p>Laois County Council</p><p></p><p>✅ Practical tip</p><p>Many of these centres — LOETB in particular — let you filter courses by area (e.g. English, technology, business) on their own website, and many have free or subsidised options for residents in Ireland.</p><p>Laois Offaly ETB</p>\n','Courses and Support for Entrepreneurs','Laois Partnership, the Local Enterprise Office and the Laois Chamber Alliance: programmes, grants, training and mentoring for local businesses.','inherit','closed','closed','','29188-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:43','2026-09-26 15:27:43','',29188,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29189',0,'revision','',0),(29191,283039559,'2026-03-19 16:52:20','2026-03-19 16:52:20','<p></p><p><em><strong>By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence.</strong></em></p><p></p><p>instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/</u></p><p></p><p><strong>Many small business owners work hard and sell well, but at the end of the month still feel that no money is ever left over. In most cases the problem is not a lack of sales, but a lack of financial organisation.</strong></p><p><strong>Caring for the company’s finances is not only about controlling expenses. It is about understanding where the money goes, how it comes in and how it can grow. Below are the essential points for every beginning entrepreneur:</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>1. Separate the company’s finances from your own.</strong></p><p><strong>Mixing personal money with the company’s is the most common and the most expensive mistake. When that happens:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>The real profit becomes hard to identify</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>The cash flow gets muddled</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>Important decisions end up based on a feeling, not on numbers</strong></em></p><p><strong>The solution: open a separate business bank account and pay yourself a fixed salary. That guarantees clarity and security.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>2. The right price is essential</strong></p><p><strong>It is not enough to charge “what the customer is willing to pay”. The price needs to cover:</strong></p><p><strong>Fixed costs (rent, bills, equipment)</strong></p><p><strong>Variable costs (raw materials, packaging, commissions)</strong></p><p><strong>Taxes</strong></p><p><strong>An adequate profit margin</strong></p><p><em><strong>Without this you may well be selling a lot and still losing money without noticing.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>3. Profit is a tool, not a prize</strong></p><p><strong>When there is profit left over, many people spend it all. Solid businesses use profit to:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Improve processes</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Invest in marketing</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Buy better inputs</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Build a financial reserve</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>Businesses that reinvest grow; businesses that consume everything stagnate.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>4. Have a financial reserve</strong></p><p><strong>Problems happen. A financial reserve avoids desperation when cash is tight. It helps you to:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Get through weaker months</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Avoid emergency debt</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Make decisions without pressure</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>It does not have to start big, but start now, even little by little.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>5. Always track the numbers</strong></p><p><strong>You do not need complex systems, but daily control is essential. Be clear about:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>How much came in</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>How much went out</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Future obligations</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Where losses are occurring</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>Whoever does not track the numbers works in the dark. Whoever does, decides with confidence.</strong></em></p><p><em><strong> </strong></em></p><p><strong>6. The biggest risk is losing money without noticing</strong></p><p><strong>Many entrepreneurs take in revenue but see no profit because they:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Price incorrectly</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Spend without control</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Do not track the numbers</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>If you feel that no money is ever left over, the problem is not how much you sell, but how you manage what comes in.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p><p><strong>Financial management is not bureaucracy, it is strategy. Entrepreneurs who understand their numbers:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Make better decisions</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Reduce losses</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Create consistent growth</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>If you feel you work very hard and the money is never left over, raise an URGENT flag: perhaps you are working for the sake of working, letting profit opportunities pass that could take your business to the next level.</strong></em></p><p></p><p><strong>Sonia Campos, Specialist in Business Intelligence, Consultant and University Lecturer, MSc in Information Science</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Instagram </strong><u><strong>@soninhafields</strong></u></p>\n','Financial Management: The Invisible Foundation of Every Business That Prospers','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. instagram: https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/','publish','open','open','','financial-management-the-invisible-foundation-of-every-prospering-business','','','2026-09-26 16:45:37','2026-09-26 15:45:37','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/19/financial-management-the-invisible-foundation-of-every-prospering-business/',0,'post','',0),(29192,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:53','2026-09-26 15:27:53','<p></p><p><em><strong>By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence.</strong></em></p><p></p><p>instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/</u></p><p></p><p><strong>Many small business owners work hard and sell well, but at the end of the month still feel that no money is ever left over. In most cases the problem is not a lack of sales, but a lack of financial organisation.</strong></p><p><strong>Caring for the company’s finances is not only about controlling expenses. It is about understanding where the money goes, how it comes in and how it can grow. Below are the essential points for every beginning entrepreneur:</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>1. Separate the company’s finances from your own.</strong></p><p><strong>Mixing personal money with the company’s is the most common and the most expensive mistake. When that happens:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>The real profit becomes hard to identify</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>The cash flow gets muddled</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>Important decisions end up based on a feeling, not on numbers</strong></em></p><p><strong>The solution: open a separate business bank account and pay yourself a fixed salary. That guarantees clarity and security.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>2. The right price is essential</strong></p><p><strong>It is not enough to charge “what the customer is willing to pay”. The price needs to cover:</strong></p><p><strong>Fixed costs (rent, bills, equipment)</strong></p><p><strong>Variable costs (raw materials, packaging, commissions)</strong></p><p><strong>Taxes</strong></p><p><strong>An adequate profit margin</strong></p><p><em><strong>Without this you may well be selling a lot and still losing money without noticing.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>3. Profit is a tool, not a prize</strong></p><p><strong>When there is profit left over, many people spend it all. Solid businesses use profit to:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Improve processes</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Invest in marketing</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Buy better inputs</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Build a financial reserve</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>Businesses that reinvest grow; businesses that consume everything stagnate.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>4. Have a financial reserve</strong></p><p><strong>Problems happen. A financial reserve avoids desperation when cash is tight. It helps you to:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Get through weaker months</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Avoid emergency debt</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Make decisions without pressure</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>It does not have to start big, but start now, even little by little.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>5. Always track the numbers</strong></p><p><strong>You do not need complex systems, but daily control is essential. Be clear about:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>How much came in</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>How much went out</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Future obligations</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Where losses are occurring</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>Whoever does not track the numbers works in the dark. Whoever does, decides with confidence.</strong></em></p><p><em><strong> </strong></em></p><p><strong>6. The biggest risk is losing money without noticing</strong></p><p><strong>Many entrepreneurs take in revenue but see no profit because they:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Price incorrectly</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Spend without control</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Do not track the numbers</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>If you feel that no money is ever left over, the problem is not how much you sell, but how you manage what comes in.</strong></em></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Conclusion:</strong></p><p><strong>Financial management is not bureaucracy, it is strategy. Entrepreneurs who understand their numbers:</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Make better decisions</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Reduce losses</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Create consistent growth</strong></p></li></ul><p><em><strong>If you feel you work very hard and the money is never left over, raise an URGENT flag: perhaps you are working for the sake of working, letting profit opportunities pass that could take your business to the next level.</strong></em></p><p></p><p><strong>Sonia Campos, Specialist in Business Intelligence, Consultant and University Lecturer, MSc in Information Science</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Instagram </strong><u><strong>@soninhafields</strong></u></p>\n','Financial Management: The Invisible Foundation of Every Business That Prospers','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. instagram: https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/','inherit','closed','closed','','29191-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:53','2026-09-26 15:27:53','',29191,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29192',0,'revision','',0),(29193,283039559,'2026-03-19 16:59:41','2026-03-19 16:59:41','<p></p><h4><strong>Main Job Sites in Ireland</strong></h4><h4><u>www.IrishJobs.ie</u>: one of Ireland’s largest job portals.<u>www.Jobs.ie</u>: vacancies in many areas, great for all kinds of professionals.<u>www.Indeed.ie</u>: aggregates many vacancies from different sources, with useful filters.<u>www.Monster.ie</u>: another broad site for finding opportunities.<u>www.RecruitIreland.com</u>: focused on recruitment in Ireland.<u>www.JobsIreland.ie</u>: a relevant platform for job searching.</h4><p><strong>💼 WHERE TO LOOK FOR WORK IN LAOIS</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🧑‍💻 Job sites used in Ireland</strong></p><p><strong>These sites are used by companies in Ireland (including vacancies in Laois). You can filter by city/county:</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Indeed – </strong><u><strong>https://ie.indeed.com/jobs?q=&amp;l=Laois</strong></u></p><p><strong>→ One of the sites with the most vacancies in Laois and across Ireland, including part-time and full-time.</strong></p><p><strong>Indeed</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 </strong><strong>IrishJobs.ie</strong><strong> – </strong><u><strong>https://www.irishjobs.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong>→ Plenty of vacancies across several sectors.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 </strong><strong>Jobs.ie</strong><strong> – </strong><u><strong>https://www.jobs.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong>→ Filter by location and type of work.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 </strong><strong>RecruitIreland.com</strong></p><p><strong>→ Another site with many vacancies by category.</strong></p><p><strong>💡 Useful apps for job searching:</strong></p><p><strong>IrishJobs App</strong></p><p><strong>Jobs.ie</strong><strong> App</strong></p><p><strong>LinkedIn Jobs</strong></p><p><strong>Indeed App</strong></p><p><strong>… and other job apps that show vacancies straight on your phone.</strong></p><p><u><strong>novavidanairlanda.com</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🏙️ Public services and local support</strong></p><p><strong>📍 Intreo – Public employment service</strong></p><p><strong>It is the Irish government’s official employment and social support service.</strong></p><p><strong>You can: </strong></p><p><strong>✔ get help looking for work</strong></p><p><strong>✔ get advice on CVs and interviews</strong></p><p><strong>✔ find out about benefits and supports</strong></p><p><strong>👉 Ask at the Intreo office in Portlaoise (part of the DSP – Department of Social Protection service) or on the official website.</strong></p><p><u><strong>laois.ie</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📍 Turas Nua</strong></p><p><strong>Partner organisation of Intreo that helps people find local employment, with personalised support and links with employers.</strong></p><p><strong>Laois Jobs Fair</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📍 SICAP / Laois Partnership Company</strong></p><p><strong>Employment support programme that helps with: </strong></p><p><strong>✔ vocational training</strong></p><p><strong>✔ putting a CV together</strong></p><p><strong>✔ connecting to jobs and courses</strong></p><p><strong>Phone: 057 8661900 (information and services) </strong></p><p><u><strong>laois.ie</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📅 Job fairs and employment events</strong></p><p><strong>🔹 Laois Jobs Fair – an event where several companies offer vacancies, information on training and professional support.</strong></p><p><strong>Even if the main event has already passed, note that it is organised regularly and is a great place to meet employers and find opportunities.</strong></p><p><strong>Laois Jobs Fair</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📱 FACEBOOK GROUPS / JOB-SEEKING COMMUNITIES</strong></p><p><strong>There are groups where workers and companies post vacancies and tips. Common examples (search on Facebook):</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Jobs in Ireland / Jobs in Ireland</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Jobs in Laois / Vacancies in Laois (or Midlands Ireland Jobs)</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Irish Jobs – Hospitality &amp; Retail</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Irish Jobs – Part Time &amp; Full Time</strong></p><p><strong>🔎 Just search Facebook for terms like:</strong></p><p><strong>“Jobs in Laois”, “Jobs Ireland”, “Laois Jobs”, “Ireland Work”, etc.</strong></p><p><strong>💡 Tip: comment or ask to be recommended — sometimes new vacancies appear in groups before official sites.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📱 WHATSAPP / TELEGRAM GROUPS</strong></p><p><strong>WhatsApp or Telegram groups are usually unofficial and change frequently, but you can find invitations in Facebook groups or by asking on Brazilian pages in Ireland.</strong></p><p><strong>👉 Look for groups like:</strong></p><p><strong>“Brazilians in Ireland – Jobs”</strong></p><p><strong>“Brazilians in Laois”</strong></p><p><strong>“Vacancies Ireland – Hospitality / Retail”</strong></p><p><strong>“Job Alerts Ireland”</strong></p><p><strong>These groups usually share daily vacancies and employer recommendations.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🧠 TIPS TO IMPROVE YOUR CHANCES</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>✔ Have a CV adapted to Ireland:</strong></p><p><strong>No photo, recent experience and references. </strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>✔ Register your CV on the job sites:</strong></p><p><strong>Many recruiters look for candidates directly there.</strong></p><p><strong>✔ Visit shops and businesses in person:</strong></p><p><strong>Handing in your CV can make a difference.</strong></p><p><strong>✔ Speak to local recruiters:</strong></p><p><strong>Such as FRS Recruitment, Sigmar Recruitment, Staffline Ireland and others — they often have vacancies that do not appear online. </strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>✔ Look for temporary work (hospitality / retail):</strong></p><p><strong>These sectors often hire more quickly.</strong></p><p><strong>✔️ Let people know in local groups (WhatsApp, Facebook) that you are looking for work. That helps with local recommendations.</strong></p>\n','Working in Laois','Main job sites in Ireland, where to look for work in Laois, public services, jobs fairs, Facebook and WhatsApp groups, and tips to improve your chances.','publish','open','open','','working-in-laois','','','2026-09-26 16:27:54','2026-09-26 15:27:54','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/19/working-in-laois/',0,'post','',0),(29194,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:54','2026-09-26 15:27:54','<p></p><h4><strong>Main Job Sites in Ireland</strong></h4><h4><u>www.IrishJobs.ie</u>: one of Ireland’s largest job portals.<u>www.Jobs.ie</u>: vacancies in many areas, great for all kinds of professionals.<u>www.Indeed.ie</u>: aggregates many vacancies from different sources, with useful filters.<u>www.Monster.ie</u>: another broad site for finding opportunities.<u>www.RecruitIreland.com</u>: focused on recruitment in Ireland.<u>www.JobsIreland.ie</u>: a relevant platform for job searching.</h4><p><strong>💼 WHERE TO LOOK FOR WORK IN LAOIS</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🧑‍💻 Job sites used in Ireland</strong></p><p><strong>These sites are used by companies in Ireland (including vacancies in Laois). You can filter by city/county:</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 Indeed – </strong><u><strong>https://ie.indeed.com/jobs?q=&amp;l=Laois</strong></u></p><p><strong>→ One of the sites with the most vacancies in Laois and across Ireland, including part-time and full-time.</strong></p><p><strong>Indeed</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 </strong><strong>IrishJobs.ie</strong><strong> – </strong><u><strong>https://www.irishjobs.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong>→ Plenty of vacancies across several sectors.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 </strong><strong>Jobs.ie</strong><strong> – </strong><u><strong>https://www.jobs.ie/</strong></u></p><p><strong>→ Filter by location and type of work.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🌐 </strong><strong>RecruitIreland.com</strong></p><p><strong>→ Another site with many vacancies by category.</strong></p><p><strong>💡 Useful apps for job searching:</strong></p><p><strong>IrishJobs App</strong></p><p><strong>Jobs.ie</strong><strong> App</strong></p><p><strong>LinkedIn Jobs</strong></p><p><strong>Indeed App</strong></p><p><strong>… and other job apps that show vacancies straight on your phone.</strong></p><p><u><strong>novavidanairlanda.com</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🏙️ Public services and local support</strong></p><p><strong>📍 Intreo – Public employment service</strong></p><p><strong>It is the Irish government’s official employment and social support service.</strong></p><p><strong>You can: </strong></p><p><strong>✔ get help looking for work</strong></p><p><strong>✔ get advice on CVs and interviews</strong></p><p><strong>✔ find out about benefits and supports</strong></p><p><strong>👉 Ask at the Intreo office in Portlaoise (part of the DSP – Department of Social Protection service) or on the official website.</strong></p><p><u><strong>laois.ie</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📍 Turas Nua</strong></p><p><strong>Partner organisation of Intreo that helps people find local employment, with personalised support and links with employers.</strong></p><p><strong>Laois Jobs Fair</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📍 SICAP / Laois Partnership Company</strong></p><p><strong>Employment support programme that helps with: </strong></p><p><strong>✔ vocational training</strong></p><p><strong>✔ putting a CV together</strong></p><p><strong>✔ connecting to jobs and courses</strong></p><p><strong>Phone: 057 8661900 (information and services) </strong></p><p><u><strong>laois.ie</strong></u></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📅 Job fairs and employment events</strong></p><p><strong>🔹 Laois Jobs Fair – an event where several companies offer vacancies, information on training and professional support.</strong></p><p><strong>Even if the main event has already passed, note that it is organised regularly and is a great place to meet employers and find opportunities.</strong></p><p><strong>Laois Jobs Fair</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📱 FACEBOOK GROUPS / JOB-SEEKING COMMUNITIES</strong></p><p><strong>There are groups where workers and companies post vacancies and tips. Common examples (search on Facebook):</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Jobs in Ireland / Jobs in Ireland</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Jobs in Laois / Vacancies in Laois (or Midlands Ireland Jobs)</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Irish Jobs – Hospitality &amp; Retail</strong></p><p><strong>➡ Irish Jobs – Part Time &amp; Full Time</strong></p><p><strong>🔎 Just search Facebook for terms like:</strong></p><p><strong>“Jobs in Laois”, “Jobs Ireland”, “Laois Jobs”, “Ireland Work”, etc.</strong></p><p><strong>💡 Tip: comment or ask to be recommended — sometimes new vacancies appear in groups before official sites.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>📱 WHATSAPP / TELEGRAM GROUPS</strong></p><p><strong>WhatsApp or Telegram groups are usually unofficial and change frequently, but you can find invitations in Facebook groups or by asking on Brazilian pages in Ireland.</strong></p><p><strong>👉 Look for groups like:</strong></p><p><strong>“Brazilians in Ireland – Jobs”</strong></p><p><strong>“Brazilians in Laois”</strong></p><p><strong>“Vacancies Ireland – Hospitality / Retail”</strong></p><p><strong>“Job Alerts Ireland”</strong></p><p><strong>These groups usually share daily vacancies and employer recommendations.</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>🧠 TIPS TO IMPROVE YOUR CHANCES</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>✔ Have a CV adapted to Ireland:</strong></p><p><strong>No photo, recent experience and references. </strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>✔ Register your CV on the job sites:</strong></p><p><strong>Many recruiters look for candidates directly there.</strong></p><p><strong>✔ Visit shops and businesses in person:</strong></p><p><strong>Handing in your CV can make a difference.</strong></p><p><strong>✔ Speak to local recruiters:</strong></p><p><strong>Such as FRS Recruitment, Sigmar Recruitment, Staffline Ireland and others — they often have vacancies that do not appear online. </strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>✔ Look for temporary work (hospitality / retail):</strong></p><p><strong>These sectors often hire more quickly.</strong></p><p><strong>✔️ Let people know in local groups (WhatsApp, Facebook) that you are looking for work. That helps with local recommendations.</strong></p>\n','Working in Laois','Main job sites in Ireland, where to look for work in Laois, public services, jobs fairs, Facebook and WhatsApp groups, and tips to improve your chances.','inherit','closed','closed','','29193-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:54','2026-09-26 15:27:54','',29193,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29194',0,'revision','',0),(29195,283039559,'2026-03-19 18:21:43','2026-03-19 18:21:43','<h2></h2><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><h2><strong>         </strong></h2><h2><strong>Chef Sonia Campos</strong> <u>@grannymadeireland</u></h2><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Ingredients:</strong></p><p><strong>3 eggs</strong></p><p><strong>Vanilla drops (optional)</strong></p><p><strong>1 tin of condensed milk (395g)</strong></p><p><strong>A pinch of salt</strong></p><p><strong>150g grated or desiccated coconut</strong></p><p><strong>1 small can of coconut milk (200ml)</strong></p><p><strong>1 cup (120g) of cornflour</strong></p><p><strong>1 tablespoon (16g) of baking powder</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Method:</strong></p><p><strong>Preheat the oven to 180°C and grease a medium tin with butter and cornflour or flour.</strong></p><p><strong>In the blender, put the eggs, the condensed milk, the coconut milk, the vanilla (if using) and the pinch of salt. Blend for about 2 minutes until completely smooth.</strong></p><p><strong>Add the grated coconut and the cornflour. Blend again until well combined.</strong></p><p><strong>Add the baking powder last and mix gently with a spoon, or blend quickly just to incorporate it.</strong></p><p><strong>Pour the batter into the prepared tin. Bake for approximately 35 to 45 minutes, or until golden brown on top. Let it cool a little before cutting. It stays slightly creamy on the inside — that is the perfect texture for a moist coconut cake!</strong></p><p><strong>Chef Sonia Campos’s tip — to make it even moister and tastier, mix in: 3 tablespoons of coconut milk and 1 tablespoon of condensed milk.</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Pour the glaze over the cake while it is still warm and finish with grated coconut on top.</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>It is simply irresistible!</strong></p>\n','Moist Coconut Cake','Chef Sonia Campos @grannymadeireland — a wonderfully moist coconut cake that is simple to make.','publish','open','open','','moist-coconut-cake','','','2026-09-26 16:27:54','2026-09-26 15:27:54','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/19/moist-coconut-cake/',0,'post','',0),(29196,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:54','2026-09-26 15:27:54','<h2></h2><p></p><p></p><p></p><p></p><h2><strong>         </strong></h2><h2><strong>Chef Sonia Campos</strong> <u>@grannymadeireland</u></h2><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Ingredients:</strong></p><p><strong>3 eggs</strong></p><p><strong>Vanilla drops (optional)</strong></p><p><strong>1 tin of condensed milk (395g)</strong></p><p><strong>A pinch of salt</strong></p><p><strong>150g grated or desiccated coconut</strong></p><p><strong>1 small can of coconut milk (200ml)</strong></p><p><strong>1 cup (120g) of cornflour</strong></p><p><strong>1 tablespoon (16g) of baking powder</strong></p><p><strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Method:</strong></p><p><strong>Preheat the oven to 180°C and grease a medium tin with butter and cornflour or flour.</strong></p><p><strong>In the blender, put the eggs, the condensed milk, the coconut milk, the vanilla (if using) and the pinch of salt. Blend for about 2 minutes until completely smooth.</strong></p><p><strong>Add the grated coconut and the cornflour. Blend again until well combined.</strong></p><p><strong>Add the baking powder last and mix gently with a spoon, or blend quickly just to incorporate it.</strong></p><p><strong>Pour the batter into the prepared tin. Bake for approximately 35 to 45 minutes, or until golden brown on top. Let it cool a little before cutting. It stays slightly creamy on the inside — that is the perfect texture for a moist coconut cake!</strong></p><p><strong>Chef Sonia Campos’s tip — to make it even moister and tastier, mix in: 3 tablespoons of coconut milk and 1 tablespoon of condensed milk.</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>Pour the glaze over the cake while it is still warm and finish with grated coconut on top.</strong></p><p></p><p><strong>It is simply irresistible!</strong></p>\n','Moist Coconut Cake','Chef Sonia Campos @grannymadeireland — a wonderfully moist coconut cake that is simple to make.','inherit','closed','closed','','29195-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:54','2026-09-26 15:27:54','',29195,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29196',0,'revision','',0),(29197,283039559,'2026-03-25 07:49:48','2026-03-25 07:49:48','<p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Instagram: </strong></em><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>The arrival of spring in the northern hemisphere is not merely a change of season. It is a subtle yet deep invitation for us to cross a state of transition — one of those that are not always visible from the outside, but that intensely move our inner world.</p><p></p><p>After winter’s withdrawal, life begins to lift itself again. The light returns, the days expand, nature awakens. And along with it, something in us also shifts. Spring calls us to blossoming, to the beginning of new cycles, to the opening of what was previously gestating.</p><p></p><p>But to blossom is not only to begin.</p><p>To blossom requires presence, support and maturity.</p><p>There is a time between the initial impulse and the concrete manifestation of what we wish to live. A time that we often neglect, because we were taught to seek quick results, immediate answers and paths without a crossing.</p><p></p><p>Spring teaches us the opposite.</p><p>It reminds us that everything born needs to be cared for. That what begins needs to be nourished. That the beauty of blossoming lies not only in the result, but in the silent process of growth that happens day after day.</p><p></p><p>For us, women, that understanding is even more essential. Because life invites us not only to do, but to gestate. To respect our rhythms, our internal times, our capacity to sustain what we choose to live.</p><p>And perhaps the greatest challenge of this time is precisely that: not to lose ourselves among the many possibilities that arise, but to remain connected to what truly matters.</p><p></p><p>Spring is fertile.</p><p>But not everything needs to bloom.</p><p>Discerning, choosing and sustaining are movements of feminine maturity.</p><p>Returning to a consciousness of the cycles is returning to our place.</p><p></p><p>It is learning to walk with more truth, less urgency and more presence.</p><p>Spring has arrived.</p><p>And with it, the opportunity to bloom — not out of impulse, but out of consciousness.</p><p></p><p>At <em>Escola Mulher Sistêmica</em>, we look at life from this perspective: that we are part of a larger cycle, and that when we disconnect from it, we also lose our sense of direction.</p><p></p><p>Our School is here, close to you, in County Laois, as a living space of reconnection, maturity and belonging to the cycle of life.</p>\n','Spring: The Quiet Call to Bloom with Consciousness','Text: Juliana Gomes, founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/','publish','open','open','','spring-the-quiet-call-to-bloom-with-consciousness','','','2026-09-26 16:27:55','2026-09-26 15:27:55','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/25/spring-the-quiet-call-to-bloom-with-consciousness/',0,'post','',0),(29198,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:55','2026-09-26 15:27:55','<p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Instagram: </strong></em><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>The arrival of spring in the northern hemisphere is not merely a change of season. It is a subtle yet deep invitation for us to cross a state of transition — one of those that are not always visible from the outside, but that intensely move our inner world.</p><p></p><p>After winter’s withdrawal, life begins to lift itself again. The light returns, the days expand, nature awakens. And along with it, something in us also shifts. Spring calls us to blossoming, to the beginning of new cycles, to the opening of what was previously gestating.</p><p></p><p>But to blossom is not only to begin.</p><p>To blossom requires presence, support and maturity.</p><p>There is a time between the initial impulse and the concrete manifestation of what we wish to live. A time that we often neglect, because we were taught to seek quick results, immediate answers and paths without a crossing.</p><p></p><p>Spring teaches us the opposite.</p><p>It reminds us that everything born needs to be cared for. That what begins needs to be nourished. That the beauty of blossoming lies not only in the result, but in the silent process of growth that happens day after day.</p><p></p><p>For us, women, that understanding is even more essential. Because life invites us not only to do, but to gestate. To respect our rhythms, our internal times, our capacity to sustain what we choose to live.</p><p>And perhaps the greatest challenge of this time is precisely that: not to lose ourselves among the many possibilities that arise, but to remain connected to what truly matters.</p><p></p><p>Spring is fertile.</p><p>But not everything needs to bloom.</p><p>Discerning, choosing and sustaining are movements of feminine maturity.</p><p>Returning to a consciousness of the cycles is returning to our place.</p><p></p><p>It is learning to walk with more truth, less urgency and more presence.</p><p>Spring has arrived.</p><p>And with it, the opportunity to bloom — not out of impulse, but out of consciousness.</p><p></p><p>At <em>Escola Mulher Sistêmica</em>, we look at life from this perspective: that we are part of a larger cycle, and that when we disconnect from it, we also lose our sense of direction.</p><p></p><p>Our School is here, close to you, in County Laois, as a living space of reconnection, maturity and belonging to the cycle of life.</p>\n','Spring: The Quiet Call to Bloom with Consciousness','Text: Juliana Gomes, founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/','inherit','closed','closed','','29197-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:55','2026-09-26 15:27:55','',29197,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29198',0,'revision','',0),(29199,283039559,'2026-03-25 13:05:08','2026-03-25 13:05:08','<p>Text: Nutri Marcela</p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p>Spring has arrived, and with it the days get longer… The <em>daffodils</em> (those lovely yellow flowers) start appearing everywhere, and a new energy of renewal after winter begins to emerge here in Ireland.</p><p>And together with that energy, this is also a great moment to look at our eating with more care. To think about how spring can be a great ally for a lighter, more nutritious and balanced diet.</p><p>When we talk about healthy eating, following the seasons makes all the difference. Seasonal foods also count for a lot in that process. So, when you come across produce such as <em>Irish carrots</em> or <em>Irish spinach</em>, choose those options, because they tend to be fresher, tastier and also better for our planet.</p><p>And today I want to show you how, with small steps, we can make the most of this season to make our eating tastier and more nutritious — not only for you, but for your whole family.</p><p> </p><h5>💚 A little about me</h5><p>My name is Marcela Ferreira, I am a nutritionist, I have lived in Ireland for 14 years and I work with a gentle approach to nutrition: based on science and without radicalism.</p><p>My focus is to help you build an eating pattern for life, improve your relationship with food and make more conscious choices, whether your goal is to lose weight, look after your health or improve a test result.</p><p>Because more than numbers, how you feel matters. And caring for your diet is not only about what you do today, but about what you can keep up over the years, creating a healthy and sustainable eating pattern.</p><p>And that is why thinking about how to eat well in each season is also part of that process.</p><p>For example, in winter… who really feels like eating salad? Our body naturally asks for warmer, more comforting meals.</p><p>But that is a subject for another conversation. Today we will focus on spring 🌸 and on how we can make our eating lighter, more nutritious and aligned with this new season.</p><p> </p><p><strong> </strong></p><h5>🌸 How to adjust your eating in spring, in practice</h5><p>Spring here in Ireland still arrives with milder temperatures. It is only around May that we start to feel a little warmth, or rather, to put away our heavier coats.</p><p>We also do not yet have a great variety of local fruit and vegetables at this stage, and that is fine.</p><p>What matters most here is not perfection but a simple principle: the more colours on the plate, the better.</p><p>If you do not usually include vegetables in your meals yet, start gradually. Try adding at least one vegetable at lunch and at dinner.</p><p>And one important point: frozen vegetables are great allies too. They are just as nutritious as fresh ones. The same goes for frozen fruit.</p><p>If you do not like them much, start by choosing the ones you already enjoy. The idea is to gradually bring more colour and variety into your everyday life.</p><p>Remember: progress is better than perfection 💚</p><p></p><p> </p><h5>🥗 Do not forget the legumes</h5><p>Another important point is including more legumes in your diet.</p><p>Our traditional rice and beans, so present in our culture, is an incredible example of nutritious and comforting food, and it can — and should — continue to be part of your routine.</p><p>Here in Ireland you will find a wide variety of beans in supermarkets such as Tesco and Dunnes, as well as in the small Brazilian shops.</p><p>An important tip is to soak the beans for at least twelve hours and change the water at least once. When you cook them, use fresh water, and if you like, add a few drops of vinegar or bay leaves.</p><p>This process helps improve digestion, reduces gas formation and also makes the beans more nutritious.</p><p>And pay attention to the accompaniments. Processed meats such as bacon and sausage should be eaten more occasionally, and not every day.</p><p>You can also vary with other options such as chickpeas, lentils and even hummus. The ready-made supermarket version can be a good option, or you can make your own.</p><p>A practical snack idea is hummus with carrot. Simple, nutritious and many children really like it.</p><p>And yes, tinned legumes can be an option too. Choose versions with no added salt or ones preserved in water. Just remember to rinse them well before eating, as that helps reduce excess sodium and can also lessen digestive discomfort such as gas, which some people report.</p><p>Variety is fundamental to our health, and it does not have to be perfect every day. Think across the week.</p><p></p><p> </p><h5>🐟 Include fish in a simple way</h5><p>Another point that is well worth it in Ireland is making the most of easy access to fish such as salmon.</p><p>A simple way to prepare it is to season with salt and olive oil and bake at two hundred degrees for about twenty minutes.</p><p>You can serve it with potato, carrot and broccoli. Everything can be prepared practically, even in the same pan, with potato and carrot in the water and the broccoli steamed.</p><p></p><p> </p><h5>🫒 And do not forget the olive oil</h5><p>Olive oil is a great ally for health and is very accessible here.</p><p>Prefer to buy it in a dark glass bottle and store it in a cupboard, away from the light.</p><p> </p><h5>💧 Hydration counts too</h5><p>And one simple but essential reminder: drink water.</p><p>Hydration helps the bowel work, helps energy, helps mood and helps the body stay balanced.</p><p>Finally, here is an invitation for you to reflect, with this renewing energy of spring. What could you improve in your eating today, even in small steps?</p><p></p><p>Remember: progress is always better than perfection.</p><p>If you feel you need help organising your eating, looking after your health through nutrition, improving your relationship with food or bringing more balance into your everyday life, I am here to help you.</p><p>Online and in-person appointments in Dublin and Kilcock</p><p></p><p></p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</u></p><p></p><p>With care,</p><p>Nutri Marcela 💚</p>\n','🌿 Spring Has Arrived: An Invitation to Refresh Your Eating Too','Text: Nutri Marcela. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/','publish','open','open','','spring-has-arrived-an-invitation-to-refresh-your-eating-too','','','2026-09-26 16:27:55','2026-09-26 15:27:55','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/25/spring-has-arrived-an-invitation-to-refresh-your-eating-too/',0,'post','',0),(29200,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:55','2026-09-26 15:27:55','<p>Text: Nutri Marcela</p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p>Spring has arrived, and with it the days get longer… The <em>daffodils</em> (those lovely yellow flowers) start appearing everywhere, and a new energy of renewal after winter begins to emerge here in Ireland.</p><p>And together with that energy, this is also a great moment to look at our eating with more care. To think about how spring can be a great ally for a lighter, more nutritious and balanced diet.</p><p>When we talk about healthy eating, following the seasons makes all the difference. Seasonal foods also count for a lot in that process. So, when you come across produce such as <em>Irish carrots</em> or <em>Irish spinach</em>, choose those options, because they tend to be fresher, tastier and also better for our planet.</p><p>And today I want to show you how, with small steps, we can make the most of this season to make our eating tastier and more nutritious — not only for you, but for your whole family.</p><p> </p><h5>💚 A little about me</h5><p>My name is Marcela Ferreira, I am a nutritionist, I have lived in Ireland for 14 years and I work with a gentle approach to nutrition: based on science and without radicalism.</p><p>My focus is to help you build an eating pattern for life, improve your relationship with food and make more conscious choices, whether your goal is to lose weight, look after your health or improve a test result.</p><p>Because more than numbers, how you feel matters. And caring for your diet is not only about what you do today, but about what you can keep up over the years, creating a healthy and sustainable eating pattern.</p><p>And that is why thinking about how to eat well in each season is also part of that process.</p><p>For example, in winter… who really feels like eating salad? Our body naturally asks for warmer, more comforting meals.</p><p>But that is a subject for another conversation. Today we will focus on spring 🌸 and on how we can make our eating lighter, more nutritious and aligned with this new season.</p><p> </p><p><strong> </strong></p><h5>🌸 How to adjust your eating in spring, in practice</h5><p>Spring here in Ireland still arrives with milder temperatures. It is only around May that we start to feel a little warmth, or rather, to put away our heavier coats.</p><p>We also do not yet have a great variety of local fruit and vegetables at this stage, and that is fine.</p><p>What matters most here is not perfection but a simple principle: the more colours on the plate, the better.</p><p>If you do not usually include vegetables in your meals yet, start gradually. Try adding at least one vegetable at lunch and at dinner.</p><p>And one important point: frozen vegetables are great allies too. They are just as nutritious as fresh ones. The same goes for frozen fruit.</p><p>If you do not like them much, start by choosing the ones you already enjoy. The idea is to gradually bring more colour and variety into your everyday life.</p><p>Remember: progress is better than perfection 💚</p><p></p><p> </p><h5>🥗 Do not forget the legumes</h5><p>Another important point is including more legumes in your diet.</p><p>Our traditional rice and beans, so present in our culture, is an incredible example of nutritious and comforting food, and it can — and should — continue to be part of your routine.</p><p>Here in Ireland you will find a wide variety of beans in supermarkets such as Tesco and Dunnes, as well as in the small Brazilian shops.</p><p>An important tip is to soak the beans for at least twelve hours and change the water at least once. When you cook them, use fresh water, and if you like, add a few drops of vinegar or bay leaves.</p><p>This process helps improve digestion, reduces gas formation and also makes the beans more nutritious.</p><p>And pay attention to the accompaniments. Processed meats such as bacon and sausage should be eaten more occasionally, and not every day.</p><p>You can also vary with other options such as chickpeas, lentils and even hummus. The ready-made supermarket version can be a good option, or you can make your own.</p><p>A practical snack idea is hummus with carrot. Simple, nutritious and many children really like it.</p><p>And yes, tinned legumes can be an option too. Choose versions with no added salt or ones preserved in water. Just remember to rinse them well before eating, as that helps reduce excess sodium and can also lessen digestive discomfort such as gas, which some people report.</p><p>Variety is fundamental to our health, and it does not have to be perfect every day. Think across the week.</p><p></p><p> </p><h5>🐟 Include fish in a simple way</h5><p>Another point that is well worth it in Ireland is making the most of easy access to fish such as salmon.</p><p>A simple way to prepare it is to season with salt and olive oil and bake at two hundred degrees for about twenty minutes.</p><p>You can serve it with potato, carrot and broccoli. Everything can be prepared practically, even in the same pan, with potato and carrot in the water and the broccoli steamed.</p><p></p><p> </p><h5>🫒 And do not forget the olive oil</h5><p>Olive oil is a great ally for health and is very accessible here.</p><p>Prefer to buy it in a dark glass bottle and store it in a cupboard, away from the light.</p><p> </p><h5>💧 Hydration counts too</h5><p>And one simple but essential reminder: drink water.</p><p>Hydration helps the bowel work, helps energy, helps mood and helps the body stay balanced.</p><p>Finally, here is an invitation for you to reflect, with this renewing energy of spring. What could you improve in your eating today, even in small steps?</p><p></p><p>Remember: progress is always better than perfection.</p><p>If you feel you need help organising your eating, looking after your health through nutrition, improving your relationship with food or bringing more balance into your everyday life, I am here to help you.</p><p>Online and in-person appointments in Dublin and Kilcock</p><p></p><p></p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</u></p><p></p><p>With care,</p><p>Nutri Marcela 💚</p>\n','🌿 Spring Has Arrived: An Invitation to Refresh Your Eating Too','Text: Nutri Marcela. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/','inherit','closed','closed','','29199-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:55','2026-09-26 15:27:55','',29199,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29200',0,'revision','',0),(29201,283039559,'2026-03-26 07:58:44','2026-03-26 07:58:44','<p><em><strong>By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence.</strong></em></p><p></p><p>instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p>In the world of entrepreneurship, there is a clear difference between having a business and building a business that PROSPERS. Many entrepreneurs start with energy, dedication and good ideas, but few truly understand the right moment to scale and, above all, how to do it in a structured and sustainable way.</p><p></p><p>The truth is that every business has a ripening time. Just as a tree does not bear fruit the day after it is planted, companies also need time to put down roots: validating the product, understanding the audience, adjusting processes and consolidating positioning. Skipping that stage can be expensive. Respecting that time, on the other hand, is what prepares the ground for solid growth.</p><p></p><p>But there comes a moment when not growing becomes the greatest risk.</p><p></p><p>Scaling is not just selling more. It is growing with strategy, with predictability and with the structure to sustain that growth. And here an essential point comes in: nobody scales alone.</p><p></p><p>One of the biggest mistakes an entrepreneur makes is believing they have to cope with everything. They do not, and nor should they. Strategic partnerships, smart hiring and the support of qualified professionals are fundamental to minimising risk and accelerating results. Having someone who has already walked the path beside you can avoid expensive mistakes and shorten the time between where you are and where you want to be.</p><p></p><p>Besides that, scaling requires clarity of goals. It is not enough to want to grow; you have to define how, how much and in how much time. A well-structured scalability goal guides decisions, directs investment and keeps the focus on what really matters.</p><p></p><p>And speaking of growth, we cannot ignore the power of digital. PAID TRAFFIC, for example, is today one of the most effective tools for scaling a business with speed and precision. It allows you to reach the right audience, at the right time, with a strategic message. But it is not the only way.</p><p></p><p>Other ways of scaling include:</p><p></p><p>* Building authority through content;</p><p>* Strategic positioning on social media;</p><p>* Process automation;</p><p>* Well-structured sales funnels;</p><p>* Partnerships and collaborations with other professionals and brands.</p><p></p><p>Each of these strategies, well applied, helps turn effort into scalable results.</p><p></p><p>Throughout my career as an entrepreneur, digital marketing teacher, mentor and influencer, I have learned that growing is not about doing more — it is about doing better, with direction and intelligence. I love technology, I believe in the power of education, and my mission is to teach other entrepreneurs to see beyond the operational, to step into a more strategic level of their businesses.</p><p></p><p>If there is one thing I can say with conviction, it is this: growth is part of the process, but it has to be built.</p><p></p><p>Respect the time of your business, but do not settle into it. Seek strategy, surround yourself with the right people and have the courage to take the next step.</p><p></p><p>Because in entrepreneurship, growing is not luck. It is a decision.</p>\n','SCALING IS NECESSARY! Growing with Consistency Is the Next Level of Your Business!','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. instagram: https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/','publish','open','open','','scaling-is-necessary-growing-with-consistency-is-the-next-level','','','2026-09-26 16:27:56','2026-09-26 15:27:56','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/03/26/scaling-is-necessary-growing-with-consistency-is-the-next-level/',0,'post','',0),(29202,0,'2026-09-26 16:27:56','2026-09-26 15:27:56','<p><em><strong>By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence.</strong></em></p><p></p><p>instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/</u></p><p></p><p></p><p>In the world of entrepreneurship, there is a clear difference between having a business and building a business that PROSPERS. Many entrepreneurs start with energy, dedication and good ideas, but few truly understand the right moment to scale and, above all, how to do it in a structured and sustainable way.</p><p></p><p>The truth is that every business has a ripening time. Just as a tree does not bear fruit the day after it is planted, companies also need time to put down roots: validating the product, understanding the audience, adjusting processes and consolidating positioning. Skipping that stage can be expensive. Respecting that time, on the other hand, is what prepares the ground for solid growth.</p><p></p><p>But there comes a moment when not growing becomes the greatest risk.</p><p></p><p>Scaling is not just selling more. It is growing with strategy, with predictability and with the structure to sustain that growth. And here an essential point comes in: nobody scales alone.</p><p></p><p>One of the biggest mistakes an entrepreneur makes is believing they have to cope with everything. They do not, and nor should they. Strategic partnerships, smart hiring and the support of qualified professionals are fundamental to minimising risk and accelerating results. Having someone who has already walked the path beside you can avoid expensive mistakes and shorten the time between where you are and where you want to be.</p><p></p><p>Besides that, scaling requires clarity of goals. It is not enough to want to grow; you have to define how, how much and in how much time. A well-structured scalability goal guides decisions, directs investment and keeps the focus on what really matters.</p><p></p><p>And speaking of growth, we cannot ignore the power of digital. PAID TRAFFIC, for example, is today one of the most effective tools for scaling a business with speed and precision. It allows you to reach the right audience, at the right time, with a strategic message. But it is not the only way.</p><p></p><p>Other ways of scaling include:</p><p></p><p>* Building authority through content;</p><p>* Strategic positioning on social media;</p><p>* Process automation;</p><p>* Well-structured sales funnels;</p><p>* Partnerships and collaborations with other professionals and brands.</p><p></p><p>Each of these strategies, well applied, helps turn effort into scalable results.</p><p></p><p>Throughout my career as an entrepreneur, digital marketing teacher, mentor and influencer, I have learned that growing is not about doing more — it is about doing better, with direction and intelligence. I love technology, I believe in the power of education, and my mission is to teach other entrepreneurs to see beyond the operational, to step into a more strategic level of their businesses.</p><p></p><p>If there is one thing I can say with conviction, it is this: growth is part of the process, but it has to be built.</p><p></p><p>Respect the time of your business, but do not settle into it. Seek strategy, surround yourself with the right people and have the courage to take the next step.</p><p></p><p>Because in entrepreneurship, growing is not luck. It is a decision.</p>\n','SCALING IS NECESSARY! Growing with Consistency Is the Next Level of Your Business!','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. instagram: https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/','inherit','closed','closed','','29201-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:27:56','2026-09-26 15:27:56','',29201,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29202',0,'revision','',0),(29204,283039559,'2026-04-05 21:31:02','2026-04-05 21:31:02','<p><em><strong>Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer</strong></em></p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/</u></p><p></p><p>It is very common to see “12 weeks to lose X kilos” programmes. And along with them come extreme solutions: exhausting workouts every day and severe food restriction. Yes, that may well produce rapid weight loss… but the more important question is: could you keep that lifestyle?</p><p></p><p>Probably not. Because it is not sustainable.</p><p></p><p>And here is a truth based on my own experience: I lost 35 kg and have maintained that result for more than 7 years. What many people do not notice is that the habits that made me lose weight are, for the most part, the same ones that help me maintain it today. Of course, I make adjustments according to each stage of life, but the base stays the same.</p><p></p><p>There is no shortcut. There is consistency.</p><p></p><p>Another important point: physical activity should not be seen only as a tool to “burn calories”. And above all, cardio alone is not the best strategy for weight loss.</p><p></p><p>Strength training is fundamental in that process. It helps preserve and increase muscle mass, which improves the metabolism and contributes to more lasting results. Cardio, meanwhile, has an essential role for cardiovascular health, energy and wellbeing, and should not be seen only as a means of losing weight.</p><p></p><p>If you want to improve something in this process, start by changing your mindset:</p><p>instead of chasing quick results, chase habits you can sustain.</p><p></p><p>Because in the end, what brings results is not what you do for 12 weeks — it is what you can keep for life!</p><p></p><p><em><strong>Movement is medicine!</strong></em></p><p><strong>To age with health, autonomy and quality of life, we need to stay strong.</strong></p>\n','Physical Activity Should Not Be a Summer Project — It Should Be a Lifetime Project.','Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/','publish','open','open','','physical-activity-should-not-be-a-summer-project-but-a-lifetime-project','','','2026-09-26 16:28:06','2026-09-26 15:28:06','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/04/05/physical-activity-should-not-be-a-summer-project-but-a-lifetime-project/',0,'post','',0),(29205,0,'2026-09-26 16:28:06','2026-09-26 15:28:06','<p><em><strong>Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer</strong></em></p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/</u></p><p></p><p>It is very common to see “12 weeks to lose X kilos” programmes. And along with them come extreme solutions: exhausting workouts every day and severe food restriction. Yes, that may well produce rapid weight loss… but the more important question is: could you keep that lifestyle?</p><p></p><p>Probably not. Because it is not sustainable.</p><p></p><p>And here is a truth based on my own experience: I lost 35 kg and have maintained that result for more than 7 years. What many people do not notice is that the habits that made me lose weight are, for the most part, the same ones that help me maintain it today. Of course, I make adjustments according to each stage of life, but the base stays the same.</p><p></p><p>There is no shortcut. There is consistency.</p><p></p><p>Another important point: physical activity should not be seen only as a tool to “burn calories”. And above all, cardio alone is not the best strategy for weight loss.</p><p></p><p>Strength training is fundamental in that process. It helps preserve and increase muscle mass, which improves the metabolism and contributes to more lasting results. Cardio, meanwhile, has an essential role for cardiovascular health, energy and wellbeing, and should not be seen only as a means of losing weight.</p><p></p><p>If you want to improve something in this process, start by changing your mindset:</p><p>instead of chasing quick results, chase habits you can sustain.</p><p></p><p>Because in the end, what brings results is not what you do for 12 weeks — it is what you can keep for life!</p><p></p><p><em><strong>Movement is medicine!</strong></em></p><p><strong>To age with health, autonomy and quality of life, we need to stay strong.</strong></p>\n','Physical Activity Should Not Be a Summer Project — It Should Be a Lifetime Project.','Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/','inherit','closed','closed','','29204-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:28:06','2026-09-26 15:28:06','',29204,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29205',0,'revision','',0),(29206,283039559,'2026-04-17 19:58:23','2026-04-17 19:58:23','<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p></p><p>“It works fast... but did your heart get the memo?”</p><p></p><p>In recent months I have heard the same sentence in many sessions:</p><p>“Aria, I am thinking about using the pen... they say it works very fast.”</p><p>And yes — it does work. But the question almost nobody asks is: what happens to the nervous system, to the emotions and to identity when the body changes too quickly?</p><p></p><p>I am not speaking against medicine — I speak as someone who closely accompanies what</p><p>happens inside people when the body changes before the mind and the heart</p><p>are ready.</p><p>Because losing weight quickly is not just losing weight. It is going through a transformation that</p><p>involves the body, the nervous system and the way you see yourself in the world.</p><p>And that deserves respect — not haste.</p><p></p><p>“Less hunger on the plate... more silence in the emotions?”</p><p>Medications such as Mounjaro (tirzepatide) were created to treat type 2 diabetes.</p><p>They imitate hormones that reduce appetite, increase satiety and stabilise</p><p>blood glucose.</p><p>In practice, you eat less — and you lose weight.</p><p>But there is something deeper here: hunger is not only in the stomach. It is also in the heart.</p><p>Many people eat to relieve anxiety, to compensate for sadness or to fill emotional</p><p>emptiness. When food drops away quickly, that emotional route is affected too.</p><p>And the nervous system feels it.</p><p></p><p>“Your nervous system did not get the instruction manual”</p><p>When the body starts eating much less, quickly, it enters adaptation.</p><p>During use, many people report:</p><p>● intense satiety</p><p>● reduced pleasure in food</p><p>● nausea and fatigue</p><p>● mood changes</p><p>● a feeling of apathy or “switching off”</p><p></p><p>This happens because food also regulates emotions.</p><p>When that regulation changes too quickly, the nervous system has to find new</p><p>forms of balance — and that does not always happen gently.</p><p>Some people become anxious. Others irritable. Others feel a silent emptiness.</p><p>It is not weakness. It is the body trying to reorganise itself.</p><p></p><p>“And when the pen leaves the scene... who holds the emotions?”</p><p>When use is stopped, the body tries to recover the previous balance.</p><p>And that can bring:</p><p>● increased hunger</p><p>● return of emotional appetite</p><p>● weight regain</p><p>● a feeling of loss of control</p><p>But there is something even deeper: if the weight changed fast but the emotional patterns remained, the body can return to the old behaviour — not out of failure, but out of survival.</p><p>The body tries to protect the old identity.</p><p>And that can generate shame, guilt, fear of gaining weight again — and even emotional</p><p>dependence on the medication.</p><p>I have seen people who lost many kilos... but still feel trapped inside themselves.</p><p></p><p>“New body, old inner story?”</p><p>Losing weight quickly changes the body — and changes how the world looks at you.</p><p>More attention. More comments. More expectations.</p><p>And the heart is not always ready for that.</p><p>Some people feel proud. Others are unaccustomed to their own reflection.</p><p></p><p>Because the body changes fast — but identity needs time.</p><p>There is also something delicate: for many people, weight functions as emotional</p><p>protection.</p><p>Protection against exposure. Against vulnerability. Against old memories.</p><p>When the weight drops quickly, that protection can disappear — and old emotions</p><p>emerge.</p><p>And that needs to be held with care.</p><p></p><p>“Not every heart is ready to lose weight fast”</p><p>Not every emotional story supports rapid change.</p><p>Profiles that need more caution include:</p><p>● people with a history of compulsive eating</p><p>● intense anxiety or emotional instability</p><p>● a history of trauma or abuse</p><p>● a very critical body relationship</p><p>● people who lose weight under external pressure</p><p>It is not about forbidding. It is about understanding whether your system is prepared.</p><p></p><p>“When the body loses weight... the soul needs to update”</p><p>From a therapeutic and energetic point of view, losing weight quickly is a change of identity.</p><p>The mind needs to update the image it has of itself.</p><p>The heart needs to deal with new emotions.</p><p>The soul needs to integrate a new way of being.</p><p>When that does not happen, misalignment appears.</p><p>The body changes — but inside, the feeling stays old.</p><p>Because weight is not only fat. It is history. It is memory. It is accumulated emotion.</p><p>If you are considering using the pen, perhaps the most important question is not:</p><p>“Will this work?”</p><p>but rather:</p><p>“Do I have support to sustain who I am going to become?”</p><p>Accompanying that process with therapeutic and emotional support helps sustain:</p><p>● new forms of regulation</p><p>● new habits</p><p>● the reorganisation of identity</p><p>● the integration of the new body image</p><p>Not as something mystical — but as conscious care.</p><p>“It is not about fear... it is about consciousness”</p><p>If you are using, considering using, or have already used — it is not about guilt.</p><p>It is about consciousness.</p><p>Medicine has its place. But real transformation happens when body,</p><p>mind and heart walk together.</p><p>Because losing weight is not only about numbers going down on the scale.</p><p>It is going through a deep internal process.</p><p>And when that is done with support, presence and listening — the result is not only a</p><p>lighter body.</p><p>It is a lighter life.</p><p>And that... yes... is real transformation.</p>\n','The Weight-Loss Pen... and Does the Spirit Keep Up?','Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist. It works fast... but did your heart get the memo?','publish','open','open','','the-weight-loss-pen-and-the-spirit-running-along-behind','','','2026-09-26 16:28:06','2026-09-26 15:28:06','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/04/17/the-weight-loss-pen-and-the-spirit-running-along-behind/',0,'post','',0),(29207,0,'2026-09-26 16:28:06','2026-09-26 15:28:06','<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p></p><p>“It works fast... but did your heart get the memo?”</p><p></p><p>In recent months I have heard the same sentence in many sessions:</p><p>“Aria, I am thinking about using the pen... they say it works very fast.”</p><p>And yes — it does work. But the question almost nobody asks is: what happens to the nervous system, to the emotions and to identity when the body changes too quickly?</p><p></p><p>I am not speaking against medicine — I speak as someone who closely accompanies what</p><p>happens inside people when the body changes before the mind and the heart</p><p>are ready.</p><p>Because losing weight quickly is not just losing weight. It is going through a transformation that</p><p>involves the body, the nervous system and the way you see yourself in the world.</p><p>And that deserves respect — not haste.</p><p></p><p>“Less hunger on the plate... more silence in the emotions?”</p><p>Medications such as Mounjaro (tirzepatide) were created to treat type 2 diabetes.</p><p>They imitate hormones that reduce appetite, increase satiety and stabilise</p><p>blood glucose.</p><p>In practice, you eat less — and you lose weight.</p><p>But there is something deeper here: hunger is not only in the stomach. It is also in the heart.</p><p>Many people eat to relieve anxiety, to compensate for sadness or to fill emotional</p><p>emptiness. When food drops away quickly, that emotional route is affected too.</p><p>And the nervous system feels it.</p><p></p><p>“Your nervous system did not get the instruction manual”</p><p>When the body starts eating much less, quickly, it enters adaptation.</p><p>During use, many people report:</p><p>● intense satiety</p><p>● reduced pleasure in food</p><p>● nausea and fatigue</p><p>● mood changes</p><p>● a feeling of apathy or “switching off”</p><p></p><p>This happens because food also regulates emotions.</p><p>When that regulation changes too quickly, the nervous system has to find new</p><p>forms of balance — and that does not always happen gently.</p><p>Some people become anxious. Others irritable. Others feel a silent emptiness.</p><p>It is not weakness. It is the body trying to reorganise itself.</p><p></p><p>“And when the pen leaves the scene... who holds the emotions?”</p><p>When use is stopped, the body tries to recover the previous balance.</p><p>And that can bring:</p><p>● increased hunger</p><p>● return of emotional appetite</p><p>● weight regain</p><p>● a feeling of loss of control</p><p>But there is something even deeper: if the weight changed fast but the emotional patterns remained, the body can return to the old behaviour — not out of failure, but out of survival.</p><p>The body tries to protect the old identity.</p><p>And that can generate shame, guilt, fear of gaining weight again — and even emotional</p><p>dependence on the medication.</p><p>I have seen people who lost many kilos... but still feel trapped inside themselves.</p><p></p><p>“New body, old inner story?”</p><p>Losing weight quickly changes the body — and changes how the world looks at you.</p><p>More attention. More comments. More expectations.</p><p>And the heart is not always ready for that.</p><p>Some people feel proud. Others are unaccustomed to their own reflection.</p><p></p><p>Because the body changes fast — but identity needs time.</p><p>There is also something delicate: for many people, weight functions as emotional</p><p>protection.</p><p>Protection against exposure. Against vulnerability. Against old memories.</p><p>When the weight drops quickly, that protection can disappear — and old emotions</p><p>emerge.</p><p>And that needs to be held with care.</p><p></p><p>“Not every heart is ready to lose weight fast”</p><p>Not every emotional story supports rapid change.</p><p>Profiles that need more caution include:</p><p>● people with a history of compulsive eating</p><p>● intense anxiety or emotional instability</p><p>● a history of trauma or abuse</p><p>● a very critical body relationship</p><p>● people who lose weight under external pressure</p><p>It is not about forbidding. It is about understanding whether your system is prepared.</p><p></p><p>“When the body loses weight... the soul needs to update”</p><p>From a therapeutic and energetic point of view, losing weight quickly is a change of identity.</p><p>The mind needs to update the image it has of itself.</p><p>The heart needs to deal with new emotions.</p><p>The soul needs to integrate a new way of being.</p><p>When that does not happen, misalignment appears.</p><p>The body changes — but inside, the feeling stays old.</p><p>Because weight is not only fat. It is history. It is memory. It is accumulated emotion.</p><p>If you are considering using the pen, perhaps the most important question is not:</p><p>“Will this work?”</p><p>but rather:</p><p>“Do I have support to sustain who I am going to become?”</p><p>Accompanying that process with therapeutic and emotional support helps sustain:</p><p>● new forms of regulation</p><p>● new habits</p><p>● the reorganisation of identity</p><p>● the integration of the new body image</p><p>Not as something mystical — but as conscious care.</p><p>“It is not about fear... it is about consciousness”</p><p>If you are using, considering using, or have already used — it is not about guilt.</p><p>It is about consciousness.</p><p>Medicine has its place. But real transformation happens when body,</p><p>mind and heart walk together.</p><p>Because losing weight is not only about numbers going down on the scale.</p><p>It is going through a deep internal process.</p><p>And when that is done with support, presence and listening — the result is not only a</p><p>lighter body.</p><p>It is a lighter life.</p><p>And that... yes... is real transformation.</p>\n','The Weight-Loss Pen... and Does the Spirit Keep Up?','Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist. It works fast... but did your heart get the memo?','inherit','closed','closed','','29206-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:28:06','2026-09-26 15:28:06','',29206,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29207',0,'revision','',0),(29208,283039559,'2026-05-09 17:29:19','2026-05-09 17:29:19','<p><strong>Text: Nutri Marcela</strong></p><p><strong>Instagram: </strong><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>A reflection on the physical, nutritional and emotional impacts of using these medications</p><p></p><p>Today I want to talk about the so-called “weight-loss injections”, a subject that is high on the agenda everywhere… on social media, in consultations and even in conversations among friends and family.</p><p></p><p>The movement is so strong that in some cases people end up feeling excluded for not using them, as if it were necessary to be part of society, often without thinking, about their own health.</p><p></p><p>This ends up reinforcing a thinness ideal that is often unattainable, unrealistic and, above all, unsustainable for most people.</p><p></p><p>💉 What are the “weight-loss injections”?</p><p></p><p>In fact, that name is not the most accurate. These medications were developed for the treatment of obesity and type 2 diabetes, with medical indication and proper follow-up.</p><p></p><p>They work by imitating a hormone naturally produced by our body, called GLP-1, which acts mainly in the intestine, helping to regulate hunger, increase satiety and improve blood glucose control.</p><p></p><p>And did you know that some of these mechanisms can also be stimulated naturally? Food quality, mindful eating and even the order in which we eat can influence satiety and our body’s response.</p><p></p><p>👩‍⚕️ Who are these medications indicated for?</p><p></p><p>These medications, known as GLP-1 analogues, are important and bring real benefits when properly indicated.</p><p></p><p>According to international guidelines, the indication is generally for people with obesity (BMI ≥ 30) or overweight (BMI ≥ 27) associated with comorbidities such as type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance, hypertension, high cholesterol or cardiovascular disease.</p><p></p><p>And it is important to be clear: I am not against the use of these medications. On the contrary, they represent an important advance in health and can bring more quality of life to people who genuinely need them.</p><p></p><p>But one reflection remains: is everyone who is using them actually indicated?</p><p></p><p>⚠️ Points of attention in use</p><p></p><p>We live in an intense search for quick results, especially when we talk about aesthetics. And in that context it is essential to look beyond the weight on the scale.</p><p></p><p>Mental health, for instance, deserves attention. The relationship with the body, with food and with one’s own image can be deeply affected.</p><p></p><p>Another warning sign is the inappropriate use of these medications, without medical follow-up, obtained inappropriately or even counterfeit. This represents a real risk to health.</p><p></p><p>🥗 The role of nutrition in this process</p><p></p><p>One of the effects of these medications is a reduced appetite, besides possible side effects such as nausea, queasiness and constipation.</p><p></p><p>But that raises an important question: how do you guarantee nutritionally adequate eating if you are eating much less?</p><p></p><p>Without proper guidance, intake can become insufficient in important nutrients such as protein, vitamins and minerals.</p><p></p><p>And then consequences begin to appear, such as hair loss, fatigue, nutritional deficiencies and a worse quality of life.</p><p></p><p>Besides that, reduced eating can lead to loss of lean mass, that is, loss of muscle, which directly impacts metabolism and long-term health. This process can increase the risk of sarcopenia and also contribute to more pronounced weight regain in the future.</p><p></p><p>🧠 Hunger, behaviour and “food noise”</p><p></p><p>Many people report that with the medication, the so-called “food noise”, or food noise, decreases.</p><p></p><p>But it is important to remember that hunger is an important physiological signal from our body. We should not be afraid of it. The goal is not to eliminate hunger, but to learn to understand and regulate it.</p><p></p><p>And more: these medications do not directly treat emotional hunger. If there is no work on eating behaviour, those patterns tend to return when use is stopped.</p><p></p><p>🔄 What happens afterwards?</p><p></p><p>One point that needs considering is what happens after the weight loss.</p><p></p><p>The body, naturally, tends to reduce the metabolism as an adaptation mechanism. In other words, maintaining the lost weight may become a bigger challenge.</p><p></p><p>Besides that, when there is significant loss of muscle mass, the risk of weight regain increases even further.</p><p></p><p>And that leads us to an important reflection: if we are talking about medications used for chronic conditions, are we not perhaps facing treatments that may be needed for a lifetime?</p><p></p><p>💚 A final reflection</p><p></p><p>In the search for quick solutions, we often forget that health is a process.</p><p></p><p>Caring for the body involves eating, movement, mental health and behaviour.</p><p></p><p>There is nothing wrong in wanting to lose weight. But it is important to reflect: is this being done for health, or only for aesthetic pressure?</p><p></p><p>Being thin is not a synonym for health.</p><p></p><p>Real transformation happens when we manage to build a more balanced relationship with our body and with food.</p><p></p><p>As Carl Jung once said:</p><p>“That to which you resist, persists. That which you accept, transforms.”</p><p>If you are indicated for these medications, talk to your doctor and seek proper follow-up.</p><p></p><p>Now, if the motivation is purely aesthetic, </p><p>it is worth reflecting more carefully. Not everything that seems easy at the beginning is sustainable in the long term.</p><p>If you would like to understand better how to look after your eating in a balanced, sustainable and more conscious way, I am here to help you.</p><p></p><p>Online and in-person appointments in Dublin and Kilcock</p><p>@marcela.holisticnutrition</p><p></p><p>With care,</p><p>Nutri Marcela 💚</p>\n','Weight-Loss Injections: Losing Weight Became Easier... but What About Health?','Text: Nutri Marcela. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/','publish','open','open','','weight-loss-injections-losing-weight-got-easier-but-what-about-health','','','2026-09-26 16:28:07','2026-09-26 15:28:07','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/05/09/weight-loss-injections-losing-weight-got-easier-but-what-about-health/',0,'post','',0),(29209,0,'2026-09-26 16:28:07','2026-09-26 15:28:07','<p><strong>Text: Nutri Marcela</strong></p><p><strong>Instagram: </strong><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>A reflection on the physical, nutritional and emotional impacts of using these medications</p><p></p><p>Today I want to talk about the so-called “weight-loss injections”, a subject that is high on the agenda everywhere… on social media, in consultations and even in conversations among friends and family.</p><p></p><p>The movement is so strong that in some cases people end up feeling excluded for not using them, as if it were necessary to be part of society, often without thinking, about their own health.</p><p></p><p>This ends up reinforcing a thinness ideal that is often unattainable, unrealistic and, above all, unsustainable for most people.</p><p></p><p>💉 What are the “weight-loss injections”?</p><p></p><p>In fact, that name is not the most accurate. These medications were developed for the treatment of obesity and type 2 diabetes, with medical indication and proper follow-up.</p><p></p><p>They work by imitating a hormone naturally produced by our body, called GLP-1, which acts mainly in the intestine, helping to regulate hunger, increase satiety and improve blood glucose control.</p><p></p><p>And did you know that some of these mechanisms can also be stimulated naturally? Food quality, mindful eating and even the order in which we eat can influence satiety and our body’s response.</p><p></p><p>👩‍⚕️ Who are these medications indicated for?</p><p></p><p>These medications, known as GLP-1 analogues, are important and bring real benefits when properly indicated.</p><p></p><p>According to international guidelines, the indication is generally for people with obesity (BMI ≥ 30) or overweight (BMI ≥ 27) associated with comorbidities such as type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance, hypertension, high cholesterol or cardiovascular disease.</p><p></p><p>And it is important to be clear: I am not against the use of these medications. On the contrary, they represent an important advance in health and can bring more quality of life to people who genuinely need them.</p><p></p><p>But one reflection remains: is everyone who is using them actually indicated?</p><p></p><p>⚠️ Points of attention in use</p><p></p><p>We live in an intense search for quick results, especially when we talk about aesthetics. And in that context it is essential to look beyond the weight on the scale.</p><p></p><p>Mental health, for instance, deserves attention. The relationship with the body, with food and with one’s own image can be deeply affected.</p><p></p><p>Another warning sign is the inappropriate use of these medications, without medical follow-up, obtained inappropriately or even counterfeit. This represents a real risk to health.</p><p></p><p>🥗 The role of nutrition in this process</p><p></p><p>One of the effects of these medications is a reduced appetite, besides possible side effects such as nausea, queasiness and constipation.</p><p></p><p>But that raises an important question: how do you guarantee nutritionally adequate eating if you are eating much less?</p><p></p><p>Without proper guidance, intake can become insufficient in important nutrients such as protein, vitamins and minerals.</p><p></p><p>And then consequences begin to appear, such as hair loss, fatigue, nutritional deficiencies and a worse quality of life.</p><p></p><p>Besides that, reduced eating can lead to loss of lean mass, that is, loss of muscle, which directly impacts metabolism and long-term health. This process can increase the risk of sarcopenia and also contribute to more pronounced weight regain in the future.</p><p></p><p>🧠 Hunger, behaviour and “food noise”</p><p></p><p>Many people report that with the medication, the so-called “food noise”, or food noise, decreases.</p><p></p><p>But it is important to remember that hunger is an important physiological signal from our body. We should not be afraid of it. The goal is not to eliminate hunger, but to learn to understand and regulate it.</p><p></p><p>And more: these medications do not directly treat emotional hunger. If there is no work on eating behaviour, those patterns tend to return when use is stopped.</p><p></p><p>🔄 What happens afterwards?</p><p></p><p>One point that needs considering is what happens after the weight loss.</p><p></p><p>The body, naturally, tends to reduce the metabolism as an adaptation mechanism. In other words, maintaining the lost weight may become a bigger challenge.</p><p></p><p>Besides that, when there is significant loss of muscle mass, the risk of weight regain increases even further.</p><p></p><p>And that leads us to an important reflection: if we are talking about medications used for chronic conditions, are we not perhaps facing treatments that may be needed for a lifetime?</p><p></p><p>💚 A final reflection</p><p></p><p>In the search for quick solutions, we often forget that health is a process.</p><p></p><p>Caring for the body involves eating, movement, mental health and behaviour.</p><p></p><p>There is nothing wrong in wanting to lose weight. But it is important to reflect: is this being done for health, or only for aesthetic pressure?</p><p></p><p>Being thin is not a synonym for health.</p><p></p><p>Real transformation happens when we manage to build a more balanced relationship with our body and with food.</p><p></p><p>As Carl Jung once said:</p><p>“That to which you resist, persists. That which you accept, transforms.”</p><p>If you are indicated for these medications, talk to your doctor and seek proper follow-up.</p><p></p><p>Now, if the motivation is purely aesthetic, </p><p>it is worth reflecting more carefully. Not everything that seems easy at the beginning is sustainable in the long term.</p><p>If you would like to understand better how to look after your eating in a balanced, sustainable and more conscious way, I am here to help you.</p><p></p><p>Online and in-person appointments in Dublin and Kilcock</p><p>@marcela.holisticnutrition</p><p></p><p>With care,</p><p>Nutri Marcela 💚</p>\n','Weight-Loss Injections: Losing Weight Became Easier... but What About Health?','Text: Nutri Marcela. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/','inherit','closed','closed','','29208-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:28:07','2026-09-26 15:28:07','',29208,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29209',0,'revision','',0),(29210,283039559,'2026-05-09 20:00:01','2026-05-09 20:00:01','<p><strong>Chef Sonia Campos</strong> <u><strong>@grannymadeireland</strong></u></p><p></p><p>Thinking about the cost of the ingredients, I developed this perfect batter! Economical, soft, moist and super simple to make.</p><p>It can be made in a blender or even mixed by hand with a whisk.</p><p></p><p></p><p><em>The children are going to love it!!!</em></p><p></p><h5>WRITE DOWN THE RECIPE: </h5><p></p><p>1 large egg (60g), 250g whole milk</p><p>1 teacup (115g) of soya or sunflower oil</p><p>Half a teacup (240g) of wheat flour</p><p>2 teacups (180g) of sugar</p><p>1 teacup (30g) of 50% cocoa powder</p><p>2 tablespoons (15g) of baking powder</p><p>1 tablespoon of cornflour</p><p></p><p>Tip: TO MAKE THE WHITE BATTER, JUST LEAVE OUT THE COCOA</p><p>POWDER AND ADD 15ML OF VANILLA ESSENCE OR</p><p>GRATED VANILLA POD.</p><p></p><p>Blend everything in the blender for 5 min. </p><p>Pour into a bowl and gradually add the sifted wheat flour, beat well and last of all</p><p>add the baking powder. Bake in a preheated oven at 180 degrees.</p><p></p><p>TOPPING MADE WITH GOURMET BRIGADEIRO: </p><p>1 tin of</p><p>condensed milk, </p><p>200g of cream, </p><p>20g of cocoa powder, </p><p>100g of chocolate, milk or dark chocolate, </p><p>15g of unsalted butter (it gives the topping extra shine). </p><p>Put it on the heat, stirring slowly until it reaches a soft stage. Let it cool and cover your</p><p>cupcakes.</p><p></p><p>Another option, instead of topping, is to fill your cupcakes</p><p>with this brigadeiro.</p>\n','GRANNY’S CUPCAKE','Chef Sonia Campos @grannymadeireland — an economical, soft, moist and super simple cupcake batter the children will love.','publish','open','open','','grannys-cupcake','','','2026-09-26 16:28:07','2026-09-26 15:28:07','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/05/09/grannys-cupcake/',0,'post','',0),(29211,0,'2026-09-26 16:28:07','2026-09-26 15:28:07','<p><strong>Chef Sonia Campos</strong> <u><strong>@grannymadeireland</strong></u></p><p></p><p>Thinking about the cost of the ingredients, I developed this perfect batter! Economical, soft, moist and super simple to make.</p><p>It can be made in a blender or even mixed by hand with a whisk.</p><p></p><p></p><p><em>The children are going to love it!!!</em></p><p></p><h5>WRITE DOWN THE RECIPE: </h5><p></p><p>1 large egg (60g), 250g whole milk</p><p>1 teacup (115g) of soya or sunflower oil</p><p>Half a teacup (240g) of wheat flour</p><p>2 teacups (180g) of sugar</p><p>1 teacup (30g) of 50% cocoa powder</p><p>2 tablespoons (15g) of baking powder</p><p>1 tablespoon of cornflour</p><p></p><p>Tip: TO MAKE THE WHITE BATTER, JUST LEAVE OUT THE COCOA</p><p>POWDER AND ADD 15ML OF VANILLA ESSENCE OR</p><p>GRATED VANILLA POD.</p><p></p><p>Blend everything in the blender for 5 min. </p><p>Pour into a bowl and gradually add the sifted wheat flour, beat well and last of all</p><p>add the baking powder. Bake in a preheated oven at 180 degrees.</p><p></p><p>TOPPING MADE WITH GOURMET BRIGADEIRO: </p><p>1 tin of</p><p>condensed milk, </p><p>200g of cream, </p><p>20g of cocoa powder, </p><p>100g of chocolate, milk or dark chocolate, </p><p>15g of unsalted butter (it gives the topping extra shine). </p><p>Put it on the heat, stirring slowly until it reaches a soft stage. Let it cool and cover your</p><p>cupcakes.</p><p></p><p>Another option, instead of topping, is to fill your cupcakes</p><p>with this brigadeiro.</p>\n','GRANNY’S CUPCAKE','Chef Sonia Campos @grannymadeireland — an economical, soft, moist and super simple cupcake batter the children will love.','inherit','closed','closed','','29210-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:28:07','2026-09-26 15:28:07','',29210,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29211',0,'revision','',0),(29212,283039559,'2026-05-19 22:31:10','2026-05-19 22:31:10','<p></p><h5>1. Who are you?</h5><p>My name is Cristiane Lima, I am a nail designer, manicure and pedicure technician. My journey in the world of nails began in 2015, in Brazil, when I decided to turn my passion for beauty into a profession.</p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/espacocris.nails/</u></p><p></p><p>It was in Brazil that I did my first training in the field and started building my professional experience. From the very beginning, I always saw the world of nails as something much more than aesthetics. For me, every appointment represents self-esteem, care, confidence and a connection with each client.</p><p></p><p>Over these 10 years I have kept evolving, learning new techniques and understanding more and more the importance of delivering work with excellence, attention to detail and professionalism.</p><p></p><p>In 2022 I arrived in Portugal and started taking on a few clients, trying to restart my career in a new country. But at that moment I faced insecurities, fear and the natural challenges of starting from zero far from home. I ended up giving up for a while, but my passion for the profession never stopped existing inside me.</p><p></p><p>Today, living in Ireland, I decided to come back with everything and start again even stronger and more professionally. Here in Ireland I have also done new training to update my techniques, sharpen my knowledge and offer an increasingly qualified service.</p><p></p><p>And my journey does not stop here: soon I will do another course in Portugal, because I believe a professional of excellence never stops evolving. I want to keep growing, learning and keeping up with new trends and techniques to always deliver the best to my clients.</p><p></p><p>My goal is to build a body of work that is seen, recognised and admired for its professionalism, its quality and its attention to every detail — not only for my personal growth, but above all to provide a special experience for every woman who sits in my chair. </p><p></p><h5>2. At what moment did you realise you wanted to start a business, and how did you choose this niche?</h5><p>I realised I wanted to start a business when I understood that I could build something of my own, with my own identity, my quality standard and my way of serving. I chose the beauty niche because I have always liked turning small details into self-esteem.</p><p></p><p>The world of nails enchanted me because it brings together technique, creativity, care and connection with people. Every appointment is an opportunity to make someone feel more beautiful, more confident and happier.</p><p></p><h5>3. What was your greatest fear or challenge in this process?</h5><p>My greatest challenge was believing that I could start again in another country. Leaving the comfort zone, facing insecurities and starting over again takes a lot of courage. But I learned that every difficult phase is also part of building our story.</p><p></p><h5>4. What was the biggest mishap of your entrepreneurial journey that you now laugh about? 😂</h5><p>One of the biggest mishaps was realising that being an entrepreneur is not just about doing beautiful nails. There is a diary, materials, promotion, service, organisation, invoicing and still keeping your emotions balanced. Today I laugh, but at the beginning I almost went mad trying to be the manicurist, the social media manager, the accountant and the receptionist all at once. 😂</p><h5></h5><h5>5. If you did not have this business, which other niche would you have tried?</h5><p>Probably something connected to fashion, aesthetics or creative communication. I have always liked everything involving beauty, self-esteem and connection with people.</p><p></p><h5>6. What is your life motto?</h5><p>“Do everything with love, dedication and excellence, because the details speak for you.”</p><p></p><h5>7. Today, what does success mean to you?</h5><p>Success, for me, is balance. It is having financial results, professional recognition, freedom and, above all, knowing that my work makes a positive impact on people’s lives.</p><p></p><h5>8. Which small everyday victory gives you that quiet happiness?</h5><p>When a client looks at the final result and smiles spontaneously. It is a simple detail, but it reminds me every day why I chose this profession.</p><p></p><h5>9. Which habit or change of mindset transformed your business?</h5><p>Understanding that I needed to value myself professionally. When I started looking at my work more seriously, investing in knowledge and stopping being afraid of charging what my work is worth, everything began to change.</p><p></p><h5>10. What do you think could be improved in your business?</h5><p>I always believe I can improve my digital presence, my organisation and my clients’ experience. Being an entrepreneur means being in constant evolution.</p><p></p><h5>11. What honest advice would you give to someone who wants to start a business?</h5><p>Do not romanticise the journey. Being an entrepreneur is wonderful, but it is also tiring, and it demands discipline, consistency and a lot of patience. Not every day will be motivating, but carrying on even on the hard days makes all the difference.</p>\n','Entrepreneur Chat: A Creative, Light and Motivating Talk to Inspire Anyone Thinking of Starting a Business — and to Provoke Good Laughs and Deep Reflections 😉','Cristiane Lima, nail designer, manicure and pedicure, on restarting her business in Ireland — her fears, her lessons and her motto.','publish','open','open','','entrepreneur-chat-a-creative-light-and-motivating-talk','','','2026-09-26 16:28:08','2026-09-26 15:28:08','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/05/19/entrepreneur-chat-a-creative-light-and-motivating-talk/',0,'post','',0),(29213,0,'2026-09-26 16:28:08','2026-09-26 15:28:08','<p></p><h5>1. Who are you?</h5><p>My name is Cristiane Lima, I am a nail designer, manicure and pedicure technician. My journey in the world of nails began in 2015, in Brazil, when I decided to turn my passion for beauty into a profession.</p><p><u>https://www.instagram.com/espacocris.nails/</u></p><p></p><p>It was in Brazil that I did my first training in the field and started building my professional experience. From the very beginning, I always saw the world of nails as something much more than aesthetics. For me, every appointment represents self-esteem, care, confidence and a connection with each client.</p><p></p><p>Over these 10 years I have kept evolving, learning new techniques and understanding more and more the importance of delivering work with excellence, attention to detail and professionalism.</p><p></p><p>In 2022 I arrived in Portugal and started taking on a few clients, trying to restart my career in a new country. But at that moment I faced insecurities, fear and the natural challenges of starting from zero far from home. I ended up giving up for a while, but my passion for the profession never stopped existing inside me.</p><p></p><p>Today, living in Ireland, I decided to come back with everything and start again even stronger and more professionally. Here in Ireland I have also done new training to update my techniques, sharpen my knowledge and offer an increasingly qualified service.</p><p></p><p>And my journey does not stop here: soon I will do another course in Portugal, because I believe a professional of excellence never stops evolving. I want to keep growing, learning and keeping up with new trends and techniques to always deliver the best to my clients.</p><p></p><p>My goal is to build a body of work that is seen, recognised and admired for its professionalism, its quality and its attention to every detail — not only for my personal growth, but above all to provide a special experience for every woman who sits in my chair. </p><p></p><h5>2. At what moment did you realise you wanted to start a business, and how did you choose this niche?</h5><p>I realised I wanted to start a business when I understood that I could build something of my own, with my own identity, my quality standard and my way of serving. I chose the beauty niche because I have always liked turning small details into self-esteem.</p><p></p><p>The world of nails enchanted me because it brings together technique, creativity, care and connection with people. Every appointment is an opportunity to make someone feel more beautiful, more confident and happier.</p><p></p><h5>3. What was your greatest fear or challenge in this process?</h5><p>My greatest challenge was believing that I could start again in another country. Leaving the comfort zone, facing insecurities and starting over again takes a lot of courage. But I learned that every difficult phase is also part of building our story.</p><p></p><h5>4. What was the biggest mishap of your entrepreneurial journey that you now laugh about? 😂</h5><p>One of the biggest mishaps was realising that being an entrepreneur is not just about doing beautiful nails. There is a diary, materials, promotion, service, organisation, invoicing and still keeping your emotions balanced. Today I laugh, but at the beginning I almost went mad trying to be the manicurist, the social media manager, the accountant and the receptionist all at once. 😂</p><h5></h5><h5>5. If you did not have this business, which other niche would you have tried?</h5><p>Probably something connected to fashion, aesthetics or creative communication. I have always liked everything involving beauty, self-esteem and connection with people.</p><p></p><h5>6. What is your life motto?</h5><p>“Do everything with love, dedication and excellence, because the details speak for you.”</p><p></p><h5>7. Today, what does success mean to you?</h5><p>Success, for me, is balance. It is having financial results, professional recognition, freedom and, above all, knowing that my work makes a positive impact on people’s lives.</p><p></p><h5>8. Which small everyday victory gives you that quiet happiness?</h5><p>When a client looks at the final result and smiles spontaneously. It is a simple detail, but it reminds me every day why I chose this profession.</p><p></p><h5>9. Which habit or change of mindset transformed your business?</h5><p>Understanding that I needed to value myself professionally. When I started looking at my work more seriously, investing in knowledge and stopping being afraid of charging what my work is worth, everything began to change.</p><p></p><h5>10. What do you think could be improved in your business?</h5><p>I always believe I can improve my digital presence, my organisation and my clients’ experience. Being an entrepreneur means being in constant evolution.</p><p></p><h5>11. What honest advice would you give to someone who wants to start a business?</h5><p>Do not romanticise the journey. Being an entrepreneur is wonderful, but it is also tiring, and it demands discipline, consistency and a lot of patience. Not every day will be motivating, but carrying on even on the hard days makes all the difference.</p>\n','Entrepreneur Chat: A Creative, Light and Motivating Talk to Inspire Anyone Thinking of Starting a Business — and to Provoke Good Laughs and Deep Reflections 😉','Cristiane Lima, nail designer, manicure and pedicure, on restarting her business in Ireland — her fears, her lessons and her motto.','inherit','closed','closed','','29212-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:28:08','2026-09-26 15:28:08','',29212,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29213',0,'revision','',0),(29214,283039559,'2026-05-21 15:59:33','2026-05-21 15:59:33','<p></p><p><em><strong>Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer</strong></em></p><p><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>Medication can help open the door, but training and the change of habits are what will keep the results. First I want to say that I do not believe that Mounjaro/Ozempic, or GLP-1 class medication in general, is only a “shortcut” — but I also do not believe it should be used by anyone who simply thinks they need to lose weight! </p><p></p><p>For some people these medications can change lives, especially those who have been struggling for years with obesity, compulsive eating or insulin resistance. </p><p></p><p>And science has already shown that the benefits go beyond weight loss. The SELECT study, published in the New England Journal of Medicine, demonstrated that semaglutide (Ozempic/Wegovy) significantly reduced the risk of cardiovascular events in people with obesity or excess weight, even without diabetes. </p><p></p><p>But I also have to say this: it is not a magic solution. </p><p></p><p>I see a lot of people assuming the pen does the whole job. The reality is that these medications can help a great deal with weight loss, BUT without strength training, sufficient protein and a change of habits, there is a risk of losing muscle mass as well, not just fat. </p><p></p><p>And this is important: recent studies on body composition in GLP-1 users show that part of the weight loss can come from lean mass, which makes strength training even more important during the process. </p><p></p><p>If you are using Ozempic or Mounjaro — and I hope under medical supervision — strength training becomes even more important. </p><p></p><p>The goal should not just be to “weigh less”. The goal should be: </p><p>✔ lose fat </p><p>✔ maintain (or gain) muscle mass </p><p>✔ improve metabolic health </p><p>✔ build sustainable habits </p><p></p><p>Another important point: studies show that when the medication is combined with physical exercise, the results tend to be more sustainable and less likely to be regained. </p><p></p><p>The truth is this: </p><p>Medication can help open the door, but training and the change of habits will help keep the results. </p><p></p><p>As a personal trainer, my opinion is simple: I do not judge those who use it, but I always reinforce the importance of using it with medical accompaniment and genuinely in necessary cases. </p><p></p><p>Now imagine that, out of nowhere, you decide you want to use it to lose 2kg because you think you need to, and to stop eating compulsively, or to silence the “food noise” as we call it in English? </p><p></p><p>I do not believe that is the best solution, and when you stop the medication? I believe the best solution and the most efficient way to lose the weight in that case and keep it off would be to work on the reasons why you eat compulsively and on the reasons behind the “food noise”. </p><p></p><p>To finish, my view is this: these tools can be extremely useful when properly indicated, but what really sustains results in the long term continues to be the basics done well: consistency, training, eating and a change of behaviour. </p><p></p><h5>References: </h5><p>1. Rubino D. et al. Tirzepatide Once Weekly for the Treatment of Obesity (SURMOUNT-1). New England Journal of Medicine. 2. Lincoff AM. et al. Semaglutide and Cardiovascular Outcomes in Obesity without Diabetes (SELECT). New England Journal of Medicine. 3. Recent reviews on body composition and lean mass loss with GLP-1 therapies (lean mass preservation). PubMed Central. 4. Studies on weight loss maintenance with exercise + GLP-1. The Lancet Diabetes &amp; Endocrinology.</p>\n','Medication Can Help Open the Door, but Training and Habit Change Are What Will Keep the Results','Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/','publish','open','open','','medication-can-open-the-door-but-training-and-habit-change-keep-the-results','','','2026-09-26 16:28:08','2026-09-26 15:28:08','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/05/21/medication-can-open-the-door-but-training-and-habit-change-keep-the-results/',0,'post','',0),(29215,0,'2026-09-26 16:28:08','2026-09-26 15:28:08','<p></p><p><em><strong>Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer</strong></em></p><p><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>Medication can help open the door, but training and the change of habits are what will keep the results. First I want to say that I do not believe that Mounjaro/Ozempic, or GLP-1 class medication in general, is only a “shortcut” — but I also do not believe it should be used by anyone who simply thinks they need to lose weight! </p><p></p><p>For some people these medications can change lives, especially those who have been struggling for years with obesity, compulsive eating or insulin resistance. </p><p></p><p>And science has already shown that the benefits go beyond weight loss. The SELECT study, published in the New England Journal of Medicine, demonstrated that semaglutide (Ozempic/Wegovy) significantly reduced the risk of cardiovascular events in people with obesity or excess weight, even without diabetes. </p><p></p><p>But I also have to say this: it is not a magic solution. </p><p></p><p>I see a lot of people assuming the pen does the whole job. The reality is that these medications can help a great deal with weight loss, BUT without strength training, sufficient protein and a change of habits, there is a risk of losing muscle mass as well, not just fat. </p><p></p><p>And this is important: recent studies on body composition in GLP-1 users show that part of the weight loss can come from lean mass, which makes strength training even more important during the process. </p><p></p><p>If you are using Ozempic or Mounjaro — and I hope under medical supervision — strength training becomes even more important. </p><p></p><p>The goal should not just be to “weigh less”. The goal should be: </p><p>✔ lose fat </p><p>✔ maintain (or gain) muscle mass </p><p>✔ improve metabolic health </p><p>✔ build sustainable habits </p><p></p><p>Another important point: studies show that when the medication is combined with physical exercise, the results tend to be more sustainable and less likely to be regained. </p><p></p><p>The truth is this: </p><p>Medication can help open the door, but training and the change of habits will help keep the results. </p><p></p><p>As a personal trainer, my opinion is simple: I do not judge those who use it, but I always reinforce the importance of using it with medical accompaniment and genuinely in necessary cases. </p><p></p><p>Now imagine that, out of nowhere, you decide you want to use it to lose 2kg because you think you need to, and to stop eating compulsively, or to silence the “food noise” as we call it in English? </p><p></p><p>I do not believe that is the best solution, and when you stop the medication? I believe the best solution and the most efficient way to lose the weight in that case and keep it off would be to work on the reasons why you eat compulsively and on the reasons behind the “food noise”. </p><p></p><p>To finish, my view is this: these tools can be extremely useful when properly indicated, but what really sustains results in the long term continues to be the basics done well: consistency, training, eating and a change of behaviour. </p><p></p><h5>References: </h5><p>1. Rubino D. et al. Tirzepatide Once Weekly for the Treatment of Obesity (SURMOUNT-1). New England Journal of Medicine. 2. Lincoff AM. et al. Semaglutide and Cardiovascular Outcomes in Obesity without Diabetes (SELECT). New England Journal of Medicine. 3. Recent reviews on body composition and lean mass loss with GLP-1 therapies (lean mass preservation). PubMed Central. 4. Studies on weight loss maintenance with exercise + GLP-1. The Lancet Diabetes &amp; Endocrinology.</p>\n','Medication Can Help Open the Door, but Training and Habit Change Are What Will Keep the Results','Text: Ana Paula, Personal Trainer https://www.instagram.com/fitandstrongwithana/','inherit','closed','closed','','29214-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:28:08','2026-09-26 15:28:08','',29214,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29215',0,'revision','',0),(29219,283039559,'2026-05-28 15:01:39','2026-05-28 15:01:39','<p>Hello, my name is Tatiane and this subject really spoke to me when I started Dunamase Hampers, and it matched Cristiane’s Mini Interview (I will leave the link here):</p><p><u>https://tdcriativo.wixsite.com/brasileirosnairlanda/post/papo-de-empreendedor-com-um-toque-criativo-leve-e-motivador-para-inspirar-quem-pensa-em-empreende-13</u></p><p></p><p>Being very honest, I loved making gift baskets, but I discovered that I love everything involving this world of entrepreneurship, and so TD Criativo was born, <u>https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/</u> and then the ConexãoBr project <u>https://www.instagram.com/conexaobr.ie/</u>, which is an arm of our Virtual Magazine (a special mention to Patricia Vidal:) <u>https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/</u></p><p></p><p><em>Entrepreneurship</em> is, for many, the fulfilment of a dream. The possibility of turning a skill, a passion or a market need into income makes thousands of people start small businesses every year. However, there is a reality that is rarely shown on social media: at the beginning, the entrepreneur has to learn to perform several roles at the same time.</p><p></p><p>Before being able to hire specialised professionals or build a team, it is common for the entrepreneur to be the finance person, the salesperson, the person responsible for marketing, purchasing, customer service and even for solving daily problems.</p><p></p><p>Although challenging, understanding the basics of each area is not only a necessity — it is a survival and growth strategy for the business.</p><p></p><p>According to Michael Gerber (2001), many small businesses fail because the entrepreneur masters only the technical part of the service or product, but never learns to run the company as a system. In other words, knowing how to do something well does not necessarily mean knowing how to manage a business.</p><p></p><h5>1. Financial Control: Without Numbers, There Is No Management</h5><p></p><p>One of the greatest difficulties small entrepreneurs face is financial management.</p><p>Controlling income, expenses, profit, costs and cash flow is essential to the health of the company. Many businesses close not because they do not sell, but because they cannot correctly manage the money that comes in.</p><p>According to Dornelas (2022), financial control allows the entrepreneur to make strategic decisions, reduce waste and have the predictability needed to grow.</p><p></p><p>Cash flow helps answer important questions:</p><p>- How much is my business really making?</p><p>- Am I charging enough?</p><p>- Can I invest at this moment?</p><p>- Am I mixing personal money with the company’s?</p><p></p><p>As Chiavenato (2021) says, “what is not controlled cannot be improved”.</p><p></p><p>Practical tip:</p><p>Even without hiring an accountant at the start, simple spreadsheets or finance apps can help with day-to-day tracking.</p><p></p><h5>2. Pricing: Charging Correctly Is Surviving</h5><p></p><p>One of the most common mistakes among small entrepreneurs is setting prices based on a competitor or on a hunch.</p><p>Many charge only so as to “not lose the customer”, without calculating costs, time invested, materials, taxes, travel and profit margin.</p><p>According to Kotler and Keller (2019), pricing should take three main factors into account:</p><p>Costs</p><p>How much it costs to produce or deliver the service.</p><p>Perceived value</p><p>How much value the customer perceives in what you offer.</p><p>Positioning strategy</p><p>What image you want your brand to convey to the market.</p><p>When the price is miscalculated, the entrepreneur can work very hard and still make no profit.</p><p>As Sebrae (2023) highlights, financially sustainable companies need a clear pricing policy.</p><p></p><p>More information in this article with dear Sonia</p><p><u>https://tdcriativo.wixsite.com/brasileirosnairlanda/post/gest%C3%A3o-financeira-o-alicerce-invis%C3%ADvel-de-todo-neg%C3%B3cio-que-prospera</u></p><p></p><h5>3. Sales Strategy: Whoever Does Not Learn to Sell, Suffers</h5><p></p><p>Selling does not mean insisting on or pressuring people.</p><p>In fact, selling means understanding needs and offering solutions.</p><p>Daniel Pink (2013) argues that in today’s world we are all sellers in some way — entrepreneurs included.</p><p>A good sales strategy involves:</p><p>Attracting</p><p>Showing your product or service to the right people.</p><p>Conversing</p><p>Understanding the customer’s pains and needs.</p><p>Closing</p><p>Presenting solutions and making the buying decision easy.</p><p>Companies that invest in the customer relationship tend to sell more and build authority.</p><p></p><h5>4. Invoicing: Professionalism Also Means Knowing How to Charge</h5><p>Many small entrepreneurs feel embarrassed or uncomfortable charging their clients.</p><p>However, charging is part of business management.</p><p>According to Robbins and Coulter (2020), organised processes reduce conflict and strengthen the professional image of the business.</p><p>Having contracts, defined deadlines, organised messages and clear communication avoids future friction.</p><p>Charging is not rudeness.</p><p>Charging is respecting your own work.</p><p></p><p>5. Purchasing and Stock Control</p><p>Buying too much can create waste. Buying too little can cost you sales.</p><p>Balance is essential.</p><p>According to Slack and Brandon-Jones (2022), good stock management reduces operating costs and improves company efficiency.</p><p>The entrepreneur needs to learn:</p><p></p><p>- How much to buy</p><p>- When to buy</p><p>- Which supplier offers the best value for money</p><p>- What really sells</p><p></p><p>Being organised in this area avoids money sitting still and increases profitability.</p><p></p><p>6. Marketing and Social Media: Your Company Needs to Be Seen</p><p>Today, many companies have social media as their main shop window.</p><p>However, posting randomly does not mean doing marketing.</p><p>Kotler, Kartajaya and Setiawan (2021) argue that modern marketing involves relationship, positioning and value creation.</p><p>Important questions:</p><p></p><p>- Does my brand convey trust?</p><p>- Does my profile show professionalism?</p><p>- Am I creating content that answers customer questions?</p><p></p><p>Companies that position themselves well on social media achieve greater reach, credibility and sales.</p><p></p><h5>7. Photos of the Product or Service: The First Impression Matters</h5><p></p><p>On social media, the image sells.</p><p>A good photo conveys quality, organisation and professionalism.</p><p>According to Solomon (2020), consumers are highly influenced by visual stimuli during the purchase decision process.</p><p>You do not have to start with expensive equipment.</p><p>But it is important to invest in:</p><p></p><p>- Good lighting</p><p>- An organised setting</p><p>- Sharp images</p><p>- Before and after (when applicable)</p><p>- Professional visual presentation</p><p></p><p>Many times, the customer buys with their eyes first.</p><p></p><h5>8. Service and After-sales: Good Customers Come Back</h5><p>Winning a customer costs more than keeping a satisfied customer.</p><p>According to Kotler and Keller (2019), customer retention increases profit and strengthens brand reputation.</p><p>After-sales can include:</p><p></p><p>- A thank-you message</p><p>- A request for feedback</p><p>- Promotions for existing customers</p><p>- Follow-up after the service</p><p></p><p>Well-served customers not only come back — they recommend you.</p><p>And a recommendation is still one of the most powerful forms of marketing.</p><p></p><h5>9. Organisation and Strategy: Growing Without Direction Is Difficult</h5><p>Companies do not grow on effort alone.</p><p>They grow through organisation.</p><p>Peter Drucker (2006) said that efficiency is doing things right; strategy is doing the right things.</p><p>Having goals, planning and a vision helps the entrepreneur move out of survival mode.</p><p>Small consistent actions produce big results over time.</p><p></p><h5>Conclusion</h5><p></p><p>Entrepreneurship requires courage, learning and adaptation.</p><p>At the beginning, it is normal to need to take on several roles. That does not mean failure — it means construction.</p><p>The good news is that nobody needs to know everything at once.</p><p>The secret lies in learning continuously, seeking training and improving one step at a time.</p><p></p><p>As Peter Drucker once said:</p><p>“The best way to predict the future is to create it.”</p><p>If you are a small entrepreneur, keep learning. The growth of your business begins with the knowledge you decide to acquire today.</p><p></p><h5>Bibliography</h5><p></p><p>CHIAVENATO, Idalberto. Introdução à Teoria Geral da Administração. 10. ed. São Paulo: Atlas, 2021.</p><p>DORNELAS, José Carlos Assis. Empreendedorismo: Transformando Ideias em Negócios. 8. ed. São Paulo: Atlas, 2022.</p><p>DRUCKER, Peter F. The Effective Executive. New York: Harper Business, 2006.</p><p>GERBER, Michael E. O Mito do Empreendedor. São Paulo: Fundamento, 2001.</p><p>KOTLER, Philip; KELLER, Kevin Lane. Administração de Marketing. 15. ed. São Paulo: Pearson, 2019.</p><p>KOTLER, Philip; KARTAJAYA, Hermawan; SETIAWAN, Iwan. Marketing 5.0. Rio de Janeiro: Sextante, 2021.</p><p>PINK, Daniel H. To Sell is Human. New York: Riverhead Books, 2013.</p><p>ROBBINS, Stephen P.; COULTER, Mary. Administração. São Paulo: Pearson, 2020.</p><p>SEBRAE. Gestão Financeira para Pequenos Negócios. Brasília: Sebrae, 2023.</p><p>SLACK, Nigel; BRANDON-JONES, Alistair. Operations and Process Management. 7. ed. London: Pearson, 2022.</p><p>SOLOMON, Michael. Consumer Behavior: Buying, Having and Being. 13. ed. New York: Pearson, 2020.</p><p>I think this format will come out very strong in the Virtual Magazine: professional, educational and authoritative, but still accessible to the small entrepreneur in our community.</p>\n','What Every Small Entrepreneur Needs to Learn to Make the Business Grow. Why understanding every area of the company can be the difference between growing or closing the doors?','Nine areas every small entrepreneur has to wear at the same time — from financial control and pricing to customer service, photos and strategy.','publish','open','open','','what-every-small-entrepreneur-needs-to-learn-to-grow-the-business','','','2026-09-26 16:30:14','2026-09-26 15:30:14','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/05/28/what-every-small-entrepreneur-needs-to-learn-to-grow-the-business/',0,'post','',0),(29220,0,'2026-09-26 16:30:14','2026-09-26 15:30:14','<p>Hello, my name is Tatiane and this subject really spoke to me when I started Dunamase Hampers, and it matched Cristiane’s Mini Interview (I will leave the link here):</p><p><u>https://tdcriativo.wixsite.com/brasileirosnairlanda/post/papo-de-empreendedor-com-um-toque-criativo-leve-e-motivador-para-inspirar-quem-pensa-em-empreende-13</u></p><p></p><p>Being very honest, I loved making gift baskets, but I discovered that I love everything involving this world of entrepreneurship, and so TD Criativo was born, <u>https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/</u> and then the ConexãoBr project <u>https://www.instagram.com/conexaobr.ie/</u>, which is an arm of our Virtual Magazine (a special mention to Patricia Vidal:) <u>https://www.instagram.com/coachpatriciavidal/</u></p><p></p><p><em>Entrepreneurship</em> is, for many, the fulfilment of a dream. The possibility of turning a skill, a passion or a market need into income makes thousands of people start small businesses every year. However, there is a reality that is rarely shown on social media: at the beginning, the entrepreneur has to learn to perform several roles at the same time.</p><p></p><p>Before being able to hire specialised professionals or build a team, it is common for the entrepreneur to be the finance person, the salesperson, the person responsible for marketing, purchasing, customer service and even for solving daily problems.</p><p></p><p>Although challenging, understanding the basics of each area is not only a necessity — it is a survival and growth strategy for the business.</p><p></p><p>According to Michael Gerber (2001), many small businesses fail because the entrepreneur masters only the technical part of the service or product, but never learns to run the company as a system. In other words, knowing how to do something well does not necessarily mean knowing how to manage a business.</p><p></p><h5>1. Financial Control: Without Numbers, There Is No Management</h5><p></p><p>One of the greatest difficulties small entrepreneurs face is financial management.</p><p>Controlling income, expenses, profit, costs and cash flow is essential to the health of the company. Many businesses close not because they do not sell, but because they cannot correctly manage the money that comes in.</p><p>According to Dornelas (2022), financial control allows the entrepreneur to make strategic decisions, reduce waste and have the predictability needed to grow.</p><p></p><p>Cash flow helps answer important questions:</p><p>- How much is my business really making?</p><p>- Am I charging enough?</p><p>- Can I invest at this moment?</p><p>- Am I mixing personal money with the company’s?</p><p></p><p>As Chiavenato (2021) says, “what is not controlled cannot be improved”.</p><p></p><p>Practical tip:</p><p>Even without hiring an accountant at the start, simple spreadsheets or finance apps can help with day-to-day tracking.</p><p></p><h5>2. Pricing: Charging Correctly Is Surviving</h5><p></p><p>One of the most common mistakes among small entrepreneurs is setting prices based on a competitor or on a hunch.</p><p>Many charge only so as to “not lose the customer”, without calculating costs, time invested, materials, taxes, travel and profit margin.</p><p>According to Kotler and Keller (2019), pricing should take three main factors into account:</p><p>Costs</p><p>How much it costs to produce or deliver the service.</p><p>Perceived value</p><p>How much value the customer perceives in what you offer.</p><p>Positioning strategy</p><p>What image you want your brand to convey to the market.</p><p>When the price is miscalculated, the entrepreneur can work very hard and still make no profit.</p><p>As Sebrae (2023) highlights, financially sustainable companies need a clear pricing policy.</p><p></p><p>More information in this article with dear Sonia</p><p><u>https://tdcriativo.wixsite.com/brasileirosnairlanda/post/gest%C3%A3o-financeira-o-alicerce-invis%C3%ADvel-de-todo-neg%C3%B3cio-que-prospera</u></p><p></p><h5>3. Sales Strategy: Whoever Does Not Learn to Sell, Suffers</h5><p></p><p>Selling does not mean insisting on or pressuring people.</p><p>In fact, selling means understanding needs and offering solutions.</p><p>Daniel Pink (2013) argues that in today’s world we are all sellers in some way — entrepreneurs included.</p><p>A good sales strategy involves:</p><p>Attracting</p><p>Showing your product or service to the right people.</p><p>Conversing</p><p>Understanding the customer’s pains and needs.</p><p>Closing</p><p>Presenting solutions and making the buying decision easy.</p><p>Companies that invest in the customer relationship tend to sell more and build authority.</p><p></p><h5>4. Invoicing: Professionalism Also Means Knowing How to Charge</h5><p>Many small entrepreneurs feel embarrassed or uncomfortable charging their clients.</p><p>However, charging is part of business management.</p><p>According to Robbins and Coulter (2020), organised processes reduce conflict and strengthen the professional image of the business.</p><p>Having contracts, defined deadlines, organised messages and clear communication avoids future friction.</p><p>Charging is not rudeness.</p><p>Charging is respecting your own work.</p><p></p><p>5. Purchasing and Stock Control</p><p>Buying too much can create waste. Buying too little can cost you sales.</p><p>Balance is essential.</p><p>According to Slack and Brandon-Jones (2022), good stock management reduces operating costs and improves company efficiency.</p><p>The entrepreneur needs to learn:</p><p></p><p>- How much to buy</p><p>- When to buy</p><p>- Which supplier offers the best value for money</p><p>- What really sells</p><p></p><p>Being organised in this area avoids money sitting still and increases profitability.</p><p></p><p>6. Marketing and Social Media: Your Company Needs to Be Seen</p><p>Today, many companies have social media as their main shop window.</p><p>However, posting randomly does not mean doing marketing.</p><p>Kotler, Kartajaya and Setiawan (2021) argue that modern marketing involves relationship, positioning and value creation.</p><p>Important questions:</p><p></p><p>- Does my brand convey trust?</p><p>- Does my profile show professionalism?</p><p>- Am I creating content that answers customer questions?</p><p></p><p>Companies that position themselves well on social media achieve greater reach, credibility and sales.</p><p></p><h5>7. Photos of the Product or Service: The First Impression Matters</h5><p></p><p>On social media, the image sells.</p><p>A good photo conveys quality, organisation and professionalism.</p><p>According to Solomon (2020), consumers are highly influenced by visual stimuli during the purchase decision process.</p><p>You do not have to start with expensive equipment.</p><p>But it is important to invest in:</p><p></p><p>- Good lighting</p><p>- An organised setting</p><p>- Sharp images</p><p>- Before and after (when applicable)</p><p>- Professional visual presentation</p><p></p><p>Many times, the customer buys with their eyes first.</p><p></p><h5>8. Service and After-sales: Good Customers Come Back</h5><p>Winning a customer costs more than keeping a satisfied customer.</p><p>According to Kotler and Keller (2019), customer retention increases profit and strengthens brand reputation.</p><p>After-sales can include:</p><p></p><p>- A thank-you message</p><p>- A request for feedback</p><p>- Promotions for existing customers</p><p>- Follow-up after the service</p><p></p><p>Well-served customers not only come back — they recommend you.</p><p>And a recommendation is still one of the most powerful forms of marketing.</p><p></p><h5>9. Organisation and Strategy: Growing Without Direction Is Difficult</h5><p>Companies do not grow on effort alone.</p><p>They grow through organisation.</p><p>Peter Drucker (2006) said that efficiency is doing things right; strategy is doing the right things.</p><p>Having goals, planning and a vision helps the entrepreneur move out of survival mode.</p><p>Small consistent actions produce big results over time.</p><p></p><h5>Conclusion</h5><p></p><p>Entrepreneurship requires courage, learning and adaptation.</p><p>At the beginning, it is normal to need to take on several roles. That does not mean failure — it means construction.</p><p>The good news is that nobody needs to know everything at once.</p><p>The secret lies in learning continuously, seeking training and improving one step at a time.</p><p></p><p>As Peter Drucker once said:</p><p>“The best way to predict the future is to create it.”</p><p>If you are a small entrepreneur, keep learning. The growth of your business begins with the knowledge you decide to acquire today.</p><p></p><h5>Bibliography</h5><p></p><p>CHIAVENATO, Idalberto. Introdução à Teoria Geral da Administração. 10. ed. São Paulo: Atlas, 2021.</p><p>DORNELAS, José Carlos Assis. Empreendedorismo: Transformando Ideias em Negócios. 8. ed. São Paulo: Atlas, 2022.</p><p>DRUCKER, Peter F. The Effective Executive. New York: Harper Business, 2006.</p><p>GERBER, Michael E. O Mito do Empreendedor. São Paulo: Fundamento, 2001.</p><p>KOTLER, Philip; KELLER, Kevin Lane. Administração de Marketing. 15. ed. São Paulo: Pearson, 2019.</p><p>KOTLER, Philip; KARTAJAYA, Hermawan; SETIAWAN, Iwan. Marketing 5.0. Rio de Janeiro: Sextante, 2021.</p><p>PINK, Daniel H. To Sell is Human. New York: Riverhead Books, 2013.</p><p>ROBBINS, Stephen P.; COULTER, Mary. Administração. São Paulo: Pearson, 2020.</p><p>SEBRAE. Gestão Financeira para Pequenos Negócios. Brasília: Sebrae, 2023.</p><p>SLACK, Nigel; BRANDON-JONES, Alistair. Operations and Process Management. 7. ed. London: Pearson, 2022.</p><p>SOLOMON, Michael. Consumer Behavior: Buying, Having and Being. 13. ed. New York: Pearson, 2020.</p><p>I think this format will come out very strong in the Virtual Magazine: professional, educational and authoritative, but still accessible to the small entrepreneur in our community.</p>\n','What Every Small Entrepreneur Needs to Learn to Make the Business Grow. Why understanding every area of the company can be the difference between growing or closing the doors?','Nine areas every small entrepreneur has to wear at the same time — from financial control and pricing to customer service, photos and strategy.','inherit','closed','closed','','29219-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:30:14','2026-09-26 15:30:14','',29219,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29220',0,'revision','',0),(29221,283039559,'2026-06-14 22:41:30','2026-06-14 22:41:30','<h3>When the purpose stops being to survive together and becomes living with consciousness</h3><p></p><p></p><p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Instagram: </strong></em><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</strong></u></p><p></p><p><u>https://mulhersistemica.my.canva.site/adultecer-para-amar</u></p><p></p><p>We are in Valentine’s Week.</p><p>A time when shop windows fill with hearts, social media floods with declarations of love and, inevitably, we are invited to reflect on our relationships.</p><p>But perhaps the most important question is not whether we are loving or being loved.</p><p>Perhaps the question is:</p><h5>What are couple relationships for in the time we are living through?</h5><p></p><p>This is a question that has accompanied my work with women in recent years. And the more I observe their stories, their challenges and their processes of transformation, the more I see that we are living through a profound change in the way we understand love, partnership and life as a couple.</p><p>For thousands of years, relationships had a very clear function: to guarantee the continuity of life.</p><p>Our ancestors united in order to survive.</p><p>Marriages were, above all, structures supporting the family, the domestic economy, protection and reproduction. Love might exist, but it was not necessarily the centre of the choice.</p><p>Survival was.</p><p></p><p>It was through that model that life reached us.</p><p>And it is important to recognise that with respect.</p><p>Life went through wars, scarcity, illness, loss, inequality and countless challenges in order to keep existing. Our parents, grandparents and great-grandparents carried weights we can barely imagine.</p><p>That is why we also inherit deep beliefs about relationships.</p><p>The idea that love demands sacrifice.</p><p>The idea that you have to put up with it.</p><p>The idea that individual happiness should be put second in order to preserve the family.</p><p></p><p>The idea that staying is always better than leaving.</p><p>But something is changing.</p><p>For the first time in history, millions of people are asking not only how to survive, but how to live.</p><p>The search for therapy, self-knowledge, spirituality, personal development and emotional health has never been so intense.</p><p>People want to understand who they are.</p><p>They want to discover their purposes.</p><p>They want to find meaning.</p><p></p><p>And when individuals change, relationships inevitably change too.</p><p>Perhaps that is why we see so many questionings emerge.</p><p>Couples who choose not to have children.</p><p>Women who decide to postpone motherhood.</p><p>New family configurations.</p><p>Separations that once seemed unthinkable.</p><p>People seeking different ways of building a shared life.</p><p>I do not believe we are watching the end of love.</p><p>I believe we are watching the end of a relationship model that no longer answers the needs of a new consciousness.</p><p>The data reinforces that perception.</p><p>Brazil has been recording record numbers of divorces, and a significant share of those separations happen before even ten years of relationship.</p><p>But perhaps the question is not why relationships are ending.</p><p>Perhaps the question is: what did they come to teach us?</p><p>In my experience, couple relationships remain one of the greatest paths of self-knowledge available to a human being.</p><p></p><p>It is in the meeting with the other that our wounds appear.</p><p>It is in the everyday life of living together that our lacks, fears, expectations and unconscious patterns are revealed.</p><p>The other becomes a mirror.</p><p>And we do not always like what we see reflected.</p><p>Perhaps that is why so many women begin their therapeutic processes motivated by relationship pain.</p><p>Many arrive seeking to save a relationship.</p><p>But end up finding themselves.</p><p>They discover that some questions are not about the partner.</p><p>They are about them.</p><p>About their limits.</p><p>About their self-esteem.</p><p>About their family history.</p><p>About the way they learned to love.</p><p>About what they believe they deserve.</p><p>And here arises one of the greatest challenges of contemporary relationships.</p><p>What happens when one person grows and the other stays where they are?</p><p>What happens when a woman begins to broaden her consciousness, look at her pain, revise her beliefs and build a new relationship with herself?</p><p>That is a delicate but necessary question.</p><p>Because relationships are not made only of love.</p><p>They are made of exchanges.</p><p></p><p>Of shared responsibility.</p><p>Of common purpose.</p><p>Of a willingness to grow.</p><p>No relationship can evolve beyond the level of consciousness of the people who make it up.</p><p>Perhaps that is why New Era relationships demand something that was not previously so necessary: deep conversations about purpose.</p><p>Not only about children.</p><p>Not only about money.</p><p>Not only about where to live or which goals to reach.</p><p>But about who we are becoming.</p><p>About what kind of life we want to build.</p><p>About which values we wish to sustain.</p><p></p><p>About what consciousness we want to cultivate together.</p><p>I believe the new purpose of couple relationships is not only to guarantee that life continues.</p><p>I believe it is to help life to flourish.</p><p>To create spaces where two people can grow, mature, awaken and expand their consciousness without having to abandon themselves in order to stay together.</p><p>Perhaps our ancestors united to guarantee the survival of life.</p><p>Perhaps our generation is being invited to learn something different.</p><p>To learn to live this life with more truth.</p><p>More presence.</p><p>More freedom.</p><p>And more consciousness.</p><p></p><p>Because, in the end, perhaps the love of the New Era is not about finding someone who completes us.</p><p></p><p>Perhaps it is about finding someone willing to walk at our side while both of us go on transforming.</p><p></p><p><u>https://mulhersistemica.my.canva.site/adultecer-para-amar</u></p>\n','Couple Relationships in the New Era','When the purpose stops being to survive together and becomes living together with consciousness. Text: Juliana Gomes, founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica.','publish','open','open','','couple-relationships-in-the-new-era','','','2026-09-26 16:30:15','2026-09-26 15:30:15','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/06/14/couple-relationships-in-the-new-era/',0,'post','',0),(29222,0,'2026-09-26 16:30:15','2026-09-26 15:30:15','<h3>When the purpose stops being to survive together and becomes living with consciousness</h3><p></p><p></p><p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong></em></p><p><em><strong>Instagram: </strong></em><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</strong></u></p><p></p><p><u>https://mulhersistemica.my.canva.site/adultecer-para-amar</u></p><p></p><p>We are in Valentine’s Week.</p><p>A time when shop windows fill with hearts, social media floods with declarations of love and, inevitably, we are invited to reflect on our relationships.</p><p>But perhaps the most important question is not whether we are loving or being loved.</p><p>Perhaps the question is:</p><h5>What are couple relationships for in the time we are living through?</h5><p></p><p>This is a question that has accompanied my work with women in recent years. And the more I observe their stories, their challenges and their processes of transformation, the more I see that we are living through a profound change in the way we understand love, partnership and life as a couple.</p><p>For thousands of years, relationships had a very clear function: to guarantee the continuity of life.</p><p>Our ancestors united in order to survive.</p><p>Marriages were, above all, structures supporting the family, the domestic economy, protection and reproduction. Love might exist, but it was not necessarily the centre of the choice.</p><p>Survival was.</p><p></p><p>It was through that model that life reached us.</p><p>And it is important to recognise that with respect.</p><p>Life went through wars, scarcity, illness, loss, inequality and countless challenges in order to keep existing. Our parents, grandparents and great-grandparents carried weights we can barely imagine.</p><p>That is why we also inherit deep beliefs about relationships.</p><p>The idea that love demands sacrifice.</p><p>The idea that you have to put up with it.</p><p>The idea that individual happiness should be put second in order to preserve the family.</p><p></p><p>The idea that staying is always better than leaving.</p><p>But something is changing.</p><p>For the first time in history, millions of people are asking not only how to survive, but how to live.</p><p>The search for therapy, self-knowledge, spirituality, personal development and emotional health has never been so intense.</p><p>People want to understand who they are.</p><p>They want to discover their purposes.</p><p>They want to find meaning.</p><p></p><p>And when individuals change, relationships inevitably change too.</p><p>Perhaps that is why we see so many questionings emerge.</p><p>Couples who choose not to have children.</p><p>Women who decide to postpone motherhood.</p><p>New family configurations.</p><p>Separations that once seemed unthinkable.</p><p>People seeking different ways of building a shared life.</p><p>I do not believe we are watching the end of love.</p><p>I believe we are watching the end of a relationship model that no longer answers the needs of a new consciousness.</p><p>The data reinforces that perception.</p><p>Brazil has been recording record numbers of divorces, and a significant share of those separations happen before even ten years of relationship.</p><p>But perhaps the question is not why relationships are ending.</p><p>Perhaps the question is: what did they come to teach us?</p><p>In my experience, couple relationships remain one of the greatest paths of self-knowledge available to a human being.</p><p></p><p>It is in the meeting with the other that our wounds appear.</p><p>It is in the everyday life of living together that our lacks, fears, expectations and unconscious patterns are revealed.</p><p>The other becomes a mirror.</p><p>And we do not always like what we see reflected.</p><p>Perhaps that is why so many women begin their therapeutic processes motivated by relationship pain.</p><p>Many arrive seeking to save a relationship.</p><p>But end up finding themselves.</p><p>They discover that some questions are not about the partner.</p><p>They are about them.</p><p>About their limits.</p><p>About their self-esteem.</p><p>About their family history.</p><p>About the way they learned to love.</p><p>About what they believe they deserve.</p><p>And here arises one of the greatest challenges of contemporary relationships.</p><p>What happens when one person grows and the other stays where they are?</p><p>What happens when a woman begins to broaden her consciousness, look at her pain, revise her beliefs and build a new relationship with herself?</p><p>That is a delicate but necessary question.</p><p>Because relationships are not made only of love.</p><p>They are made of exchanges.</p><p></p><p>Of shared responsibility.</p><p>Of common purpose.</p><p>Of a willingness to grow.</p><p>No relationship can evolve beyond the level of consciousness of the people who make it up.</p><p>Perhaps that is why New Era relationships demand something that was not previously so necessary: deep conversations about purpose.</p><p>Not only about children.</p><p>Not only about money.</p><p>Not only about where to live or which goals to reach.</p><p>But about who we are becoming.</p><p>About what kind of life we want to build.</p><p>About which values we wish to sustain.</p><p></p><p>About what consciousness we want to cultivate together.</p><p>I believe the new purpose of couple relationships is not only to guarantee that life continues.</p><p>I believe it is to help life to flourish.</p><p>To create spaces where two people can grow, mature, awaken and expand their consciousness without having to abandon themselves in order to stay together.</p><p>Perhaps our ancestors united to guarantee the survival of life.</p><p>Perhaps our generation is being invited to learn something different.</p><p>To learn to live this life with more truth.</p><p>More presence.</p><p>More freedom.</p><p>And more consciousness.</p><p></p><p>Because, in the end, perhaps the love of the New Era is not about finding someone who completes us.</p><p></p><p>Perhaps it is about finding someone willing to walk at our side while both of us go on transforming.</p><p></p><p><u>https://mulhersistemica.my.canva.site/adultecer-para-amar</u></p>\n','Couple Relationships in the New Era','When the purpose stops being to survive together and becomes living together with consciousness. Text: Juliana Gomes, founder of Escola Mulher Sistêmica.','inherit','closed','closed','','29221-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:30:15','2026-09-26 15:30:15','',29221,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29222',0,'revision','',0),(29223,283039559,'2026-06-20 09:02:23','2026-06-20 09:02:23','<p><em><strong>By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence.</strong></em></p><p></p><p><strong>instagram: </strong><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>In an increasingly competitive market, many entrepreneurs believe that the secret to success is simply selling more. However, the sustainability of a business depends on factors far deeper than a simple commercial transaction. Among them, two stand out for their capacity to generate loyalty, referrals and continuous growth: welcoming the customer and the buying experience.</p><p></p><h5>The first contact is decisive. Before even evaluating prices or conditions, the customer evaluates people. Kindness, courtesy, lightness, good humour and genuine attention create an emotional connection that opens the door to trust. Demonstrating command of the product or service conveys safety, but just as important is knowing how to listen. Many entrepreneurs make the mistake of presenting their solutions before understanding the real needs of the customer. </h5><p></p><p>When we ask questions, listen attentively and seek to understand the challenges of the person in front of us, we manage to offer a more appropriate and personalised solution. People like buying from those who convey positive energy, credibility and a genuine interest in helping them. </p><p>An excellent service may not generate an immediate sale, but it is a guarantee of a future sale! </p><p></p><h5>The second factor is the buying experience. The relationship with the customer does not end when the sale is completed. On the contrary, that is when loyalty begins to be built. Every detail matters: the communication, the service, the delivery, the follow-up and the way the customer feels throughout the whole journey.</h5><p></p><p>Companies that delight their customers create a lasting memory. Delivering more than was promised does not necessarily mean spending more, but rather adding value through attention, care and excellence in the details. A small gesture can turn a satisfied customer into a true promoter of your brand.</p><p></p><p>Ultimately, consistent businesses are built by people who understand that selling is not just offering a product or service, but creating relationships and memorable experiences. Customers may forget what they bought, but they hardly ever forget how they were treated.</p><p>That, in the end, is what differentiates a simple sale from a sustainable company! </p><p></p><p></p><p>Sonia Campos</p><p>Entrepreneur and MSc in Market Intelligence.</p>\n','TWO FACTORS THAT GIVE YOUR BUSINESS CONSISTENCY IN SALES.','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. instagram: https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/','publish','open','open','','two-factors-that-give-your-business-sales-consistency','','','2026-09-26 16:30:15','2026-09-26 15:30:15','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/06/20/two-factors-that-give-your-business-sales-consistency/',0,'post','',0),(29224,0,'2026-09-26 16:30:15','2026-09-26 15:30:15','<p><em><strong>By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence.</strong></em></p><p></p><p><strong>instagram: </strong><u><strong>https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/</strong></u></p><p></p><p>In an increasingly competitive market, many entrepreneurs believe that the secret to success is simply selling more. However, the sustainability of a business depends on factors far deeper than a simple commercial transaction. Among them, two stand out for their capacity to generate loyalty, referrals and continuous growth: welcoming the customer and the buying experience.</p><p></p><h5>The first contact is decisive. Before even evaluating prices or conditions, the customer evaluates people. Kindness, courtesy, lightness, good humour and genuine attention create an emotional connection that opens the door to trust. Demonstrating command of the product or service conveys safety, but just as important is knowing how to listen. Many entrepreneurs make the mistake of presenting their solutions before understanding the real needs of the customer. </h5><p></p><p>When we ask questions, listen attentively and seek to understand the challenges of the person in front of us, we manage to offer a more appropriate and personalised solution. People like buying from those who convey positive energy, credibility and a genuine interest in helping them. </p><p>An excellent service may not generate an immediate sale, but it is a guarantee of a future sale! </p><p></p><h5>The second factor is the buying experience. The relationship with the customer does not end when the sale is completed. On the contrary, that is when loyalty begins to be built. Every detail matters: the communication, the service, the delivery, the follow-up and the way the customer feels throughout the whole journey.</h5><p></p><p>Companies that delight their customers create a lasting memory. Delivering more than was promised does not necessarily mean spending more, but rather adding value through attention, care and excellence in the details. A small gesture can turn a satisfied customer into a true promoter of your brand.</p><p></p><p>Ultimately, consistent businesses are built by people who understand that selling is not just offering a product or service, but creating relationships and memorable experiences. Customers may forget what they bought, but they hardly ever forget how they were treated.</p><p>That, in the end, is what differentiates a simple sale from a sustainable company! </p><p></p><p></p><p>Sonia Campos</p><p>Entrepreneur and MSc in Market Intelligence.</p>\n','TWO FACTORS THAT GIVE YOUR BUSINESS CONSISTENCY IN SALES.','By Sonia Campos, MSc in Information Science and Specialist in Business Intelligence. instagram: https://www.instagram.com/soninhafields/','inherit','closed','closed','','29223-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:30:15','2026-09-26 15:30:15','',29223,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29224',0,'revision','',0),(29225,283039559,'2026-06-20 09:10:30','2026-06-20 09:10:30','<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/ariaenergyhealing/</u></p><p></p><p>There is a silent trap in the immigrant experience.</p><p>It does not happen at the airport.</p><p>It does not happen when you say goodbye to your family.</p><p>Nor when you realise you have missed birthdays, weddings or important moments of</p><p>those who stayed behind.</p><p>It happens little by little.</p><p>So slowly that it often goes unnoticed.</p><p>Because emigrating does not only mean changing country.</p><p></p><p>It means inevitably losing part of the system that regulated your emotional life. And that is something few immigrants understand when they arrive.</p><p>When we lived close to family, friends or people who knew us</p><p>deeply, there was an invisible network supporting our emotional balance.</p><p>A spontaneous conversation. A Sunday lunch. An unexpected visit. A hug</p><p>without needing to book it in the diary.</p><p>All of that helped our nervous system remember that we were safe.</p><p>When we emigrate, that network disappears.</p><p></p><p>And however many new friendships we build, there is a truth few people like to</p><p>admit:</p><p>Part of the isolation is inevitable. No matter how sociable you are. No matter</p><p>how many friends you make. No matter how much you love the country you live in.</p><p>There is a period that lasts months for some people and years for others, in which the</p><p>sense of not fully belonging anywhere is part of the journey.</p><p>You no longer live the life you left behind. But you also do not yet feel</p><p>fully part of the new reality.</p><p>It is an emotional no-man’s-land.</p><p>And it is precisely in that space that some of the immigrant’s greatest</p><p>vulnerabilities begin to appear.</p><p>The first of them is projection.</p><p>Without noticing, we start expecting new relationships to fill spaces that were</p><p>previously occupied by an entire community.</p><p></p><p>We expect a partner to be best friend, therapist, family and safe harbour all at</p><p>the same time.</p><p>We expect recent friendships to have the depth of decades.</p><p>We expect people we met a few months ago to understand pains built up</p><p>over a whole lifetime.</p><p>And when that does not happen, the sense of abandonment appears.</p><p>But often we are not suffering because of the relationship itself. We are suffering from</p><p>the absence of the network that existed before it.</p><p>The problem is that there is a second factor that makes all of this even more intense. And it</p><p>rarely gets the attention it deserves.</p><p>The body. Especially when we talk about physical activity.</p><p>Many immigrants, especially those who come from sunny countries, go through</p><p>a drastic change of lifestyle.</p><p>Less time outdoors. Less exposure to the sun. Less movement. Fewer spontaneous</p><p>walks. Fewer in-person meetings. Less human contact.</p><p>And without noticing, they enter a dangerous cycle.</p><p></p><h5>They feel more isolated.</h5><p>They go out less. They move less.</p><p>And the less they move, the worse they feel.</p><p>What few people know is that movement is not only about losing weight or</p><p>improving physical health.</p><p></p><h5>Movement is a tool for emotional regulation.</h5><p>When we walk, run, train or simply put the body in</p><p>movement, we help the nervous system process stress, anxiety, frustration and</p><p>sadness.</p><p>Physical activity acts as a kind of biological pressure valve.</p><p>It reduces stress levels. It improves mood. It promotes sleep. It increases the sense of</p><p>energy.</p><p>And, perhaps most important for the immigrant, it creates an internal sense of stability.</p><p>Because there is something nobody tells us about immigration: not every feeling of</p><p>longing is emotional.</p><p></p><p>Sometimes what we are interpreting as longing is exhaustion. Sometimes what seems like</p><p>deep sadness is a lack of sunlight. Sometimes what seems like an existential crisis is a</p><p>nervous system that has gone months without releasing accumulated tension.</p><p>And no, that does not mean the pain of the distance is not real.</p><p>It is.</p><p>The longing exists. The losses exist. The missed birthdays exist. The absences</p><p>exist.</p><p>But when the body is exhausted, sedentary and deprived of natural light, everything becomes bigger.</p><p>Rejection seems more painful.</p><p>Loneliness seems deeper.</p><p>Anxiety seems more intense.</p><p>Conflicts seem more serious.</p><p>Farewells seem more final.</p><p>That is why one of the biggest mistakes an immigrant can make is believing that all of</p><p>their emotional challenges will be solved through the mind alone.</p><p>Analysis is essential, but it does not nourish the needs of the physical body.</p><p>In the vast majority of cases, the only need is for more movement.</p><p>More walking. More nature. More fresh air. More contact with the physical world.</p><p>Especially during the long winter months.</p><p>Because winter does not only affect mood. It affects the perception we have of life.</p><p>For someone who grew up surrounded by sun, consecutive months of grey sky can</p><p>reduce energy, motivation and the emotional capacity to deal with challenges.</p><p>And when we add that to the inevitable isolation of immigration, the effects become even</p><p>more evident.</p><p>That is why creating a movement routine is not a luxury. It is not vanity. It is not just</p><p>a matter of health.</p><p>It is an emotional survival strategy.</p><h5>It is a way of telling the nervous system: “We are far from home, but we are</h5><h5>safe.”</h5><p></p><p>Perhaps the greatest lesson of immigration is realising that belonging is not born only from</p><p>the people around us.</p><p>It is also born from the relationship we build with ourselves.</p><p>And that relationship is not created only through thoughts.</p><p>It is created through the small daily choices that help body, mind and</p><p>heart walk together.</p><p>So the next time you feel the longing is crushing your chest, ask one simple</p><p>question before concluding that something is wrong:</p><p>Are you really missing home...</p><p>Or has it been weeks since your body has been begging to get out of the chair, see the daylight and</p><p>remind your nervous system that there is still life happening out there?</p><p>With love,</p><p>Aria Milioni</p>\n','Who Have You Become Far from Home?','Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/ariaenergyhealing/','publish','open','open','','who-you-became-far-from-home','','','2026-09-26 16:30:16','2026-09-26 15:30:16','',0,'http://localhost:8080/2026/06/20/who-you-became-far-from-home/',0,'post','',0),(29226,0,'2026-09-26 16:30:16','2026-09-26 15:30:16','<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p><p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p><p></p><p>Instagram: <u>https://www.instagram.com/ariaenergyhealing/</u></p><p></p><p>There is a silent trap in the immigrant experience.</p><p>It does not happen at the airport.</p><p>It does not happen when you say goodbye to your family.</p><p>Nor when you realise you have missed birthdays, weddings or important moments of</p><p>those who stayed behind.</p><p>It happens little by little.</p><p>So slowly that it often goes unnoticed.</p><p>Because emigrating does not only mean changing country.</p><p></p><p>It means inevitably losing part of the system that regulated your emotional life. And that is something few immigrants understand when they arrive.</p><p>When we lived close to family, friends or people who knew us</p><p>deeply, there was an invisible network supporting our emotional balance.</p><p>A spontaneous conversation. A Sunday lunch. An unexpected visit. A hug</p><p>without needing to book it in the diary.</p><p>All of that helped our nervous system remember that we were safe.</p><p>When we emigrate, that network disappears.</p><p></p><p>And however many new friendships we build, there is a truth few people like to</p><p>admit:</p><p>Part of the isolation is inevitable. No matter how sociable you are. No matter</p><p>how many friends you make. No matter how much you love the country you live in.</p><p>There is a period that lasts months for some people and years for others, in which the</p><p>sense of not fully belonging anywhere is part of the journey.</p><p>You no longer live the life you left behind. But you also do not yet feel</p><p>fully part of the new reality.</p><p>It is an emotional no-man’s-land.</p><p>And it is precisely in that space that some of the immigrant’s greatest</p><p>vulnerabilities begin to appear.</p><p>The first of them is projection.</p><p>Without noticing, we start expecting new relationships to fill spaces that were</p><p>previously occupied by an entire community.</p><p></p><p>We expect a partner to be best friend, therapist, family and safe harbour all at</p><p>the same time.</p><p>We expect recent friendships to have the depth of decades.</p><p>We expect people we met a few months ago to understand pains built up</p><p>over a whole lifetime.</p><p>And when that does not happen, the sense of abandonment appears.</p><p>But often we are not suffering because of the relationship itself. We are suffering from</p><p>the absence of the network that existed before it.</p><p>The problem is that there is a second factor that makes all of this even more intense. And it</p><p>rarely gets the attention it deserves.</p><p>The body. Especially when we talk about physical activity.</p><p>Many immigrants, especially those who come from sunny countries, go through</p><p>a drastic change of lifestyle.</p><p>Less time outdoors. Less exposure to the sun. Less movement. Fewer spontaneous</p><p>walks. Fewer in-person meetings. Less human contact.</p><p>And without noticing, they enter a dangerous cycle.</p><p></p><h5>They feel more isolated.</h5><p>They go out less. They move less.</p><p>And the less they move, the worse they feel.</p><p>What few people know is that movement is not only about losing weight or</p><p>improving physical health.</p><p></p><h5>Movement is a tool for emotional regulation.</h5><p>When we walk, run, train or simply put the body in</p><p>movement, we help the nervous system process stress, anxiety, frustration and</p><p>sadness.</p><p>Physical activity acts as a kind of biological pressure valve.</p><p>It reduces stress levels. It improves mood. It promotes sleep. It increases the sense of</p><p>energy.</p><p>And, perhaps most important for the immigrant, it creates an internal sense of stability.</p><p>Because there is something nobody tells us about immigration: not every feeling of</p><p>longing is emotional.</p><p></p><p>Sometimes what we are interpreting as longing is exhaustion. Sometimes what seems like</p><p>deep sadness is a lack of sunlight. Sometimes what seems like an existential crisis is a</p><p>nervous system that has gone months without releasing accumulated tension.</p><p>And no, that does not mean the pain of the distance is not real.</p><p>It is.</p><p>The longing exists. The losses exist. The missed birthdays exist. The absences</p><p>exist.</p><p>But when the body is exhausted, sedentary and deprived of natural light, everything becomes bigger.</p><p>Rejection seems more painful.</p><p>Loneliness seems deeper.</p><p>Anxiety seems more intense.</p><p>Conflicts seem more serious.</p><p>Farewells seem more final.</p><p>That is why one of the biggest mistakes an immigrant can make is believing that all of</p><p>their emotional challenges will be solved through the mind alone.</p><p>Analysis is essential, but it does not nourish the needs of the physical body.</p><p>In the vast majority of cases, the only need is for more movement.</p><p>More walking. More nature. More fresh air. More contact with the physical world.</p><p>Especially during the long winter months.</p><p>Because winter does not only affect mood. It affects the perception we have of life.</p><p>For someone who grew up surrounded by sun, consecutive months of grey sky can</p><p>reduce energy, motivation and the emotional capacity to deal with challenges.</p><p>And when we add that to the inevitable isolation of immigration, the effects become even</p><p>more evident.</p><p>That is why creating a movement routine is not a luxury. It is not vanity. It is not just</p><p>a matter of health.</p><p>It is an emotional survival strategy.</p><h5>It is a way of telling the nervous system: “We are far from home, but we are</h5><h5>safe.”</h5><p></p><p>Perhaps the greatest lesson of immigration is realising that belonging is not born only from</p><p>the people around us.</p><p>It is also born from the relationship we build with ourselves.</p><p>And that relationship is not created only through thoughts.</p><p>It is created through the small daily choices that help body, mind and</p><p>heart walk together.</p><p>So the next time you feel the longing is crushing your chest, ask one simple</p><p>question before concluding that something is wrong:</p><p>Are you really missing home...</p><p>Or has it been weeks since your body has been begging to get out of the chair, see the daylight and</p><p>remind your nervous system that there is still life happening out there?</p><p>With love,</p><p>Aria Milioni</p>\n','Who Have You Become Far from Home?','Text written by: Aria Milioni, Systemic Therapist. Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/ariaenergyhealing/','inherit','closed','closed','','29225-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:30:16','2026-09-26 15:30:16','',29225,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29226',0,'revision','',0),(29234,0,'2026-09-26 16:37:27','2026-09-26 15:37:27','<p><u><em>Texto: Ana Luiza A Duarte</em></u></p><p><u><em>Terapeuta Integrativa</em></u></p><p></p><p>O inverno chega com seu ar denso, seus dias curtos e um certo recolhimento inevitável. E, enquanto o mundo parece adormecer, algo em nós começa a despertar. É o tempo de ouvir o que o corpo pede, o que o coração sussurra, o que a alma quer conservar.</p><p>O frio, às vezes, é uma forma de cuidado: desacelera, preserva, protege o que ainda vai florescer. E, já que é tempo de conservar e preparar, talvez o inverno também seja um convite à escolha.</p><p>Antes de lançar as sementes, é preciso saber <strong>o que se deseja ver crescer</strong>. Quais sonhos merecem germinar quando o sol voltar a aquecer a terra? Quais ideias, afetos ou planos você</p><p>quer nutrir agora, no silêncio, para que brotem com força quando a estação mudar?</p><p>Porque o calor interno não se limita ao corpo — ele pode tomar forma de <strong>ideias que acendem</strong>, de <strong>intenções que se aquecem</strong>, de <strong>planos que ganham contorno </strong>mesmo sob o frio.</p><p>São pequenas brasas que mantêm viva a esperança de um novo florescer.</p><p>Enquanto lá fora tudo parece quieto, dentro de nós algo se reorganiza. O que parece pausa é, na verdade, preparo. O descanso é o terreno fértil de tudo o que virá.</p><p>Há um tipo de calor que não vem do fogo — ele nasce devagar, em silêncio, quando o frio nos convida a voltar para dentro.</p><p>É um calor que brota do coração e precisa ser nutrido com cuidado. A mente, quando alimentada por pensamentos prósperos, por ideias que inspiram e por gestos que despertam a criatividade, ajuda a manter essa chama acesa. Esse calor nos cura, nos desperta e nos permite dar o <strong>primeiro voo às ideias novas</strong> — pequenas centelhas que podem aproximar-nos de nós mesmos e das pessoas ao nosso redor.</p><p>Talvez seja justamente esse recolhimento que nos permita <strong>acessar a nossa fonte criativa.</strong></p><p>Para criar, precisamos nos sensibilizar para o que nos cerca, perceber as possibilidades à nossa volta, escolher o que nos inspira e transformar isso em algo único — uma ação, uma expressão, uma ideia que carregue paixão e determinação. Quando o ruído lá fora diminui, conseguimos ouvir essas vozes sutis dentro de nós, aquelas que pedem espaço, forma e cor.</p><p>O inverno, com sua calma, cria o terreno perfeito para imaginar e recriar o que queremos viver.</p><p>Então, acenda suas pequenas fogueiras: um livro, uma canção, uma sopa quente, uma lembrança boa. Permita-se esse calor que nasce de dentro e se espalha devagar.</p><p><em>O inverno é um lembrete de que o calor verdadeiro não está apenas no sol — está naquilo que escolhemos nutrir quando o mundo esfria.</em></p><p></p><p></p><p><u><strong>Ana Luiza A Duarte</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Terapeuta Integrativa</strong></u></p><p></p>','O calor que nasce do inverno [PROBE]','Texto: Ana Luiza A Duarte Terapeuta Integrativa O inverno chega com seu ar denso, seus dias curtos e um certo recolhimento inevitável. E, enquanto o mundo parece adormecer, algo em nós começa a despertar. É o tempo de ouvir o que o corpo pede, o que o coração sussurra, o que a alma quer conservar. O frio, às vezes, é uma forma de cuidado: desacelera, preserva, protege o que ainda vai florescer. E, já que é tempo de conservar e preparar, talvez o inverno também seja um convite à escolha. Antes...','inherit','closed','closed','','10352-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:37:27','2026-09-26 15:37:27','',10352,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29234',0,'revision','',0),(29235,0,'2026-09-26 16:37:29','2026-09-26 15:37:29','<p><u><em>Texto: Ana Luiza A Duarte</em></u></p><p><u><em>Terapeuta Integrativa</em></u></p><p></p><p>O inverno chega com seu ar denso, seus dias curtos e um certo recolhimento inevitável. E, enquanto o mundo parece adormecer, algo em nós começa a despertar. É o tempo de ouvir o que o corpo pede, o que o coração sussurra, o que a alma quer conservar.</p><p>O frio, às vezes, é uma forma de cuidado: desacelera, preserva, protege o que ainda vai florescer. E, já que é tempo de conservar e preparar, talvez o inverno também seja um convite à escolha.</p><p>Antes de lançar as sementes, é preciso saber <strong>o que se deseja ver crescer</strong>. Quais sonhos merecem germinar quando o sol voltar a aquecer a terra? Quais ideias, afetos ou planos você</p><p>quer nutrir agora, no silêncio, para que brotem com força quando a estação mudar?</p><p>Porque o calor interno não se limita ao corpo — ele pode tomar forma de <strong>ideias que acendem</strong>, de <strong>intenções que se aquecem</strong>, de <strong>planos que ganham contorno </strong>mesmo sob o frio.</p><p>São pequenas brasas que mantêm viva a esperança de um novo florescer.</p><p>Enquanto lá fora tudo parece quieto, dentro de nós algo se reorganiza. O que parece pausa é, na verdade, preparo. O descanso é o terreno fértil de tudo o que virá.</p><p>Há um tipo de calor que não vem do fogo — ele nasce devagar, em silêncio, quando o frio nos convida a voltar para dentro.</p><p>É um calor que brota do coração e precisa ser nutrido com cuidado. A mente, quando alimentada por pensamentos prósperos, por ideias que inspiram e por gestos que despertam a criatividade, ajuda a manter essa chama acesa. Esse calor nos cura, nos desperta e nos permite dar o <strong>primeiro voo às ideias novas</strong> — pequenas centelhas que podem aproximar-nos de nós mesmos e das pessoas ao nosso redor.</p><p>Talvez seja justamente esse recolhimento que nos permita <strong>acessar a nossa fonte criativa.</strong></p><p>Para criar, precisamos nos sensibilizar para o que nos cerca, perceber as possibilidades à nossa volta, escolher o que nos inspira e transformar isso em algo único — uma ação, uma expressão, uma ideia que carregue paixão e determinação. Quando o ruído lá fora diminui, conseguimos ouvir essas vozes sutis dentro de nós, aquelas que pedem espaço, forma e cor.</p><p>O inverno, com sua calma, cria o terreno perfeito para imaginar e recriar o que queremos viver.</p><p>Então, acenda suas pequenas fogueiras: um livro, uma canção, uma sopa quente, uma lembrança boa. Permita-se esse calor que nasce de dentro e se espalha devagar.</p><p><em>O inverno é um lembrete de que o calor verdadeiro não está apenas no sol — está naquilo que escolhemos nutrir quando o mundo esfria.</em></p><p></p><p></p><p><u><strong>Ana Luiza A Duarte</strong></u></p><p><u><strong>Terapeuta Integrativa</strong></u></p><p></p>','O calor que nasce do inverno','Texto: Ana Luiza A Duarte Terapeuta Integrativa O inverno chega com seu ar denso, seus dias curtos e um certo recolhimento inevitável. E, enquanto o mundo parece adormecer, algo em nós começa a despertar. É o tempo de ouvir o que o corpo pede, o que o coração sussurra, o que a alma quer conservar. O frio, às vezes, é uma forma de cuidado: desacelera, preserva, protege o que ainda vai florescer. E, já que é tempo de conservar e preparar, talvez o inverno também seja um convite à escolha. Antes...','inherit','closed','closed','','10352-revision-v1','','','2026-09-26 16:37:29','2026-09-26 15:37:29','',10352,'http://localhost:8080/?p=29235',0,'revision','',0),(29244,0,'2026-09-26 16:39:46','2026-09-26 15:39:46','<p><u><em>Texto: Ana Luiza A Duarte</em></u></